on."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The condition is the average age continuing to rise."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The age is that of these nations'' populations."}, {"n": 7, "text": "A reason is given for the prediction."}, {"n": 8, "text": "Older people have fewer reasons to save than younger people."}]}], "the_read": "What if older people have much better reasons to save, even if they have fewer?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q11", "qnum": 11, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q12", "prompt": "The term “pit bull” does not designate a breed of dog, as do the terms “German shepherd” and “poodle.” It is like the terms “Seeing-Eye dog” and “police dog,” which designate dogs according to what they do. If you take two German shepherds and place them side by side, you cannot tell by appearance alone which is the police dog and which is the Seeing-Eye dog.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about the term pit bull. · That term does not designate a breed. · A contrast is drawn with other terms. · German shepherd is one of those terms. · Poodle is the other. · Those terms do designate breeds. (2) A comparison is being made. · The comparison is with two other terms. · One term is Seeing-Eye dog. · The other is police dog. · Those terms designate dogs by what they do. (3) This sets up a situation. · The situation involves two German shepherds. · They are placed side by side. · You cannot tell which is which. · Appearance alone is not enough. · One is the police dog. · The other is the Seeing-Eye dog.", "category": "Conclusion", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The term “pit bull” does not designate a breed of dog, as do the terms “German shepherd” and “poodle.”", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the term pit bull."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That term does not designate a breed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A contrast is drawn with other terms."}, {"n": 4, "text": "German shepherd is one of those terms."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Poodle is the other."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those terms do designate breeds."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is like the terms “Seeing-Eye dog” and “police dog,” which designate dogs according to what they do.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A comparison is being made."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The comparison is with two other terms."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One term is Seeing-Eye dog."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other is police dog."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those terms designate dogs by what they do."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "If you take two German shepherds and place them side by side, you cannot tell by appearance alone which is the police dog and which is the Seeing-Eye dog.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This sets up a situation."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The situation involves two German shepherds."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are placed side by side."}, {"n": 4, "text": "You cannot tell which is which."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Appearance alone is not enough."}, {"n": 6, "text": "One is the police dog."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The other is the Seeing-Eye dog."}]}], "the_read": "What if a dog''s breed can be determined by what it does? What if dog breed has nothing to do with appearance?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q12", "qnum": 12, "question_type": "Conclusion", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 13, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q13", "prompt": "Historically, monetary systems have developed only in population centers with marketplaces. Through the fourth century B.C., Mesopotamian cities engaged in trade, but had never had marketplaces. By that period, however, Greek cities all had marketplaces, or agorae. The Greek cities’ agorae were centrally located and goods were traded there either for money or for commodities.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is a historical claim. · It is about monetary systems. · Those systems have developed in only one kind of place. · The places are population centers. · Those centers have marketplaces. (2) This covers a period of time. · The period runs through the fourth century B.C. · It is about Mesopotamian cities. · Those cities engaged in trade. · A contrast follows that. · They had never had marketplaces. (3) This is a contrast with the Mesopotamian cities. · It is about Greek cities. · It refers to the same period. · All of those cities had marketplaces. · The marketplaces were called agorae. (4) This is about the Greek agorae. · They were centrally located. · Goods were traded there. · There were two things goods were traded for. · One of them was money. · The other was commodities.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Historically, monetary systems have developed only in population centers with marketplaces.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a historical claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about monetary systems."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those systems have developed in only one kind of place."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The places are population centers."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those centers have marketplaces."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Through the fourth century B.C., Mesopotamian cities engaged in trade, but had never had marketplaces.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This covers a period of time."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The period runs through the fourth century B.C."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about Mesopotamian cities."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those cities engaged in trade."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A contrast follows that."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They had never had marketplaces."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "By that period, however, Greek cities all had marketplaces, or agorae.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with the Mesopotamian cities."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about Greek cities."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It refers to the same period."}, {"n": 4, "text": "All of those cities had marketplaces."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The marketplaces were called agorae."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "The Greek cities’ agorae were centrally located and goods were traded there either for money or for commodities.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the Greek agorae."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They were centrally located."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Goods were traded there."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There were two things goods were traded for."}, {"n": 5, "text": "One of them was money."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The other was commodities."}]}], "the_read": "Fourth century Mesopotamian cities did not have monetary systems.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 14, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q14", "prompt": "Computer operating system software has become increasingly standardized. But when a large business with multiple, linked computer systems uses identical operating system software on all of its computers, a computer vandal who gains access to one computer automatically has access to the data on all the computers. Using a program known as a “virus,” the vandal can then destroy much of the data on all the computers. If such a business introduced minor variations into its operating system software, unauthorized access to all the computers at the same time could be virtually eliminated. Furthermore, variations in operating system software can be created without any loss of computer compatibility to the business. Therefore, it is advisable for businesses to implement such variations.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about computer operating system software. · That software has become standardized. · The standardization is increasing. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · It sets up a condition. · The condition involves a large business. · The business has multiple computer systems. · Those systems are linked. · It uses identical operating system software. · The software is on all of its computers. (3) This gives the result of that condition. · It is about a computer vandal. · The vandal gains access to one computer. · That gives access to all the computers. · What is accessed is the data. · The access is granted automatically. (4) This is about a program. · The program is known as a virus. · The vandal uses it. · The vandal can then destroy data. · Much of the data can be destroyed. · The data is on all the computers. (5) This is a conditional statement. · The condition is the business introducing variations. · The variations are minor ones. · They go into its operating system software. · The result concerns unauthorized access. · The access is to all the computers at the same time. · That access could be eliminated. · The elimination is virtual rather than total. (6) This is an additional point. · It is about variations in operating system software. · Those variations can be created. · Nothing is lost by creating them. · What is not lost is computer compatibility. · The compatibility is the business''s own. (7) This is the conclusion. · It is about businesses. · Something is advisable for them. · What is advisable is implementing such variations.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Computer operating system software has become increasingly standardized.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about computer operating system software."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That software has become standardized."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The standardization is increasing."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But when a large business with multiple, linked computer systems uses identical operating system software on all of its computers,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It sets up a condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The condition involves a large business."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The business has multiple computer systems."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those systems are linked."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It uses identical operating system software."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The software is on all of its computers."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "a computer vandal who gains access to one computer automatically has access to the data on all the computers.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives the result of that condition."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about a computer vandal."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The vandal gains access to one computer."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That gives access to all the computers."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What is accessed is the data."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The access is granted automatically."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Using a program known as a “virus,” the vandal can then destroy much of the data on all the computers.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a program."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The program is known as a virus."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The vandal uses it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The vandal can then destroy data."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Much of the data can be destroyed."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The data is on all the computers."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "If such a business introduced minor variations into its operating system software, unauthorized access to all the computers at the same time could be virtually eliminated.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a conditional statement."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The condition is the business introducing variations."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The variations are minor ones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They go into its operating system software."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The result concerns unauthorized access."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The access is to all the computers at the same time."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That access could be eliminated."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The elimination is virtual rather than total."}]}, {"n": 6, "text": "Furthermore, variations in operating system software can be created without any loss of computer compatibility to the business.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about variations in operating system software."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those variations can be created."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Nothing is lost by creating them."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What is not lost is computer compatibility."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The compatibility is the business''s own."}]}, {"n": 7, "text": "Therefore, it is advisable for businesses to implement such variations.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about businesses."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Something is advisable for them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What is advisable is implementing such variations."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s a huge downside to variations in operating system software, like it''s very expensive to purchase and maintain, and it''s hard for employees to use?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "B"}, {"citation": "PT1.S3.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q16", "prompt": "It is the mark of a superior conductor that he or she has the authority to insist, even with a top orchestra, that rehearsal work must be intensified. This authority cannot simply be claimed; the conductor must earn it by winning the orchestra’s respect for the artistic interpretations he or she is currently pursuing.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This describes the mark of something. · It is about a superior conductor. · The conductor has authority. · The authority is to insist on something. · What is insisted on is intensified rehearsal work. · This holds even with a top orchestra. (2) This is about that authority. · The authority cannot simply be claimed. · The conductor must earn it instead. · It is earned by winning respect. · The respect comes from the orchestra. · The respect is for artistic interpretations. · Those are the ones the conductor is currently pursuing.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "It is the mark of a superior conductor that he or she has the authority to insist, even with a top orchestra, that rehearsal work must be intensified.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This describes the mark of something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about a superior conductor."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The conductor has authority."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The authority is to insist on something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What is insisted on is intensified rehearsal work."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This holds even with a top orchestra."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This authority cannot simply be claimed; the conductor must earn it by winning the orchestra’s respect for the artistic interpretations he or she is currently pursuing.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about that authority."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The authority cannot simply be claimed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The conductor must earn it instead."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is earned by winning respect."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The respect comes from the orchestra."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The respect is for artistic interpretations."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those are the ones the conductor is currently pursuing."}]}], "the_read": "What if the orchestra pays zero attention to the conductor and cannot/will not recognize any conductor''s interpretation whatsoever? What if respecting a conductor''s artistic decisions will not sway the orchestra to work harder?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT1.S3.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT1_S3_Q17", "prompt": "In the United States proven oil reserves—the amount of oil considered extractable from known fields—are at the same level as they were ten years ago. Yet over this same period no new oil fields of any consequence have been discovered, and the annual consumption of domestically p