ed by lenient judges and the kind dispensed by severe ones.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives the reason for that claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There are no longer two kinds of justice."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One kind was dispensed by lenient judges."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other was dispensed by severe judges."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Public advocate: But with judges stripped of discretionary powers, there can be no leniency even where it would be appropriate.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The second speaker is a public advocate."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is a contrast with the politician."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Judges have been stripped of discretionary powers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There can be no leniency."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That holds even where leniency would be appropriate."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "So juries now sometimes acquit a given defendant solely because the jurors feel that the mandatory sentence would be too harsh.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This follows from what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about juries."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They acquit a defendant."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This happens sometimes rather than always."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is happening now."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The reason is the jurors'' feeling."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They feel the mandatory sentence would be too harsh."}, {"n": 8, "text": "That feeling is the sole reason."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those juries, then, do not return an accurate verdict on the defendant’s guilt.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This follows from what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about those juries."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They do not return an accurate verdict."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The verdict concerns the defendant''s guilt."}]}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is why it is imperative that the legislation instituting mandatory jail sentences be repealed.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on what came before."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Something is described as imperative."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The legislation should be repealed."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The legislation instituted mandatory jail sentences."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether mandatory sentencing has been a positive addition to the legal system.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "D"}, {"citation": "PT21.S3.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Counter", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 14, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q16", "prompt": "Researchers studying artificial sweeteners have long claimed that the perception of sweetness is determined by the activation of a single type of receptor on the tongue, called a sweetness receptor. They have also claimed that any given individual molecule of substance can activate at most one sweetness receptor and that the fewer molecules that are required to activate a receptor, the sweeter that substance will be perceived to be. Now, the researchers claim to have discovered a substance of which only one molecule is needed to activate any sweetness receptor.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about researchers. · They study artificial sweeteners. · They have claimed this for a long time. · The perception of sweetness is determined by something. · It is determined by the activation of a receptor. · There is a single type of receptor. · The receptor is on the tongue. · It is called a sweetness receptor. (2) This is an additional claim by the researchers. · It is about any individual molecule. · Such a molecule can activate a receptor. · It can activate at most one of them. · A second part of the claim follows. · It is about how many molecules are required. · Fewer molecules are what matters here. (3) This completes the previous claim. · It is about how the substance is perceived. · The substance will be perceived as sweeter. (4) This is about the present. · The researchers claim a discovery. · What they discovered is a substance. · Only one molecule of it is needed. · One molecule can activate any sweetness receptor.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Researchers studying artificial sweeteners have long claimed that the perception of sweetness is determined by the activation of a single type of receptor on the tongue, called a sweetness receptor.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about researchers."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They study artificial sweeteners."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They have claimed this for a long time."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The perception of sweetness is determined by something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is determined by the activation of a receptor."}, {"n": 6, "text": "There is a single type of receptor."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The receptor is on the tongue."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It is called a sweetness receptor."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "They have also claimed that any given individual molecule of substance can activate at most one sweetness receptor and that the fewer molecules that are required to activate a receptor,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional claim by the researchers."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about any individual molecule."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Such a molecule can activate a receptor."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It can activate at most one of them."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A second part of the claim follows."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It is about how many molecules are required."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Fewer molecules are what matters here."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "the sweeter that substance will be perceived to be.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This completes the previous claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about how the substance is perceived."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The substance will be perceived as sweeter."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Now, the researchers claim to have discovered a substance of which only one molecule is needed to activate any sweetness receptor.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the present."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The researchers claim a discovery."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they discovered is a substance."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Only one molecule of it is needed."}, {"n": 5, "text": "One molecule can activate any sweetness receptor."}]}], "the_read": "The newly discovered substance will be perceived to be very sweet.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q17", "prompt": "An editorial in the _Grandburg Daily Herald_ claims that Grandburg’s voters would generally welcome the defeat of the political party now in control of the Grandburg City Council. The editorial bases its claim on a recent survey that found that 59 percent of Grandburg’s registered voters think that the party will definitely be out of power after next year’s city council elections.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about an editorial. · The editorial is in the Grandburg Daily Herald. · It makes a claim about Grandburg''s voters. · Those voters would welcome something. · They would generally welcome it rather than all of them. · What they would welcome is the defeat of a political party. · That party is now in control. · It controls the Grandburg City Council. (2) This is the basis for the editorial''s claim. · The basis is a survey. · The survey is a recent one. · It found a figure of 59 percent. · The percentage is of registered voters in Grandburg. · Those voters think something. · They think the party will be out of power. · It would happen after next year''s city council elections.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "An editorial in the _Grandburg Daily Herald_ claims that Grandburg’s voters would generally welcome the defeat of the political party now in control of the Grandburg City Council.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about an editorial."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The editorial is in the Grandburg Daily Herald."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It makes a claim about Grandburg''s voters."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those voters would welcome something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They would generally welcome it rather than all of them."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What they would welcome is the defeat of a political party."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That party is now in control."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It controls the Grandburg City Council."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The editorial bases its claim on a recent survey that found that 59 percent of Grandburg’s registered voters think that the party will definitely be out of power after next year’s city council elections.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the basis for the editorial''s claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The basis is a survey."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The survey is a recent one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It found a figure of 59 percent."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The percentage is of registered voters in Grandburg."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those voters think something."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They think the party will be out of power."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It would happen after next year''s city council elections."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because a majority expects something to happen, that doens''t mean a majority would be happy about that thing happening?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q17", "qnum": 17, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q18", "prompt": "Prolonged exposure to nonionizing radiation— electromagnetic radiation at or below the frequency of visible light—increases a person’s chances of developing soft-tissue cancer. Electric power lines as well as such electrical appliances as electric blankets and video-display terminals are sources of nonionizing radiation.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about exposure to radiation. · The exposure is prolonged. · The radiation is nonionizing. · Nonionizing radiation is electromagnetic. · It is at or below the frequency of visible light. · The exposure increases a person''s chances. · What the chances are of is developing cancer. · The cancer is soft-tissue cancer. (2) This names sources of that radiation. · Electric power lines are one source. · Electrical appliances are also sources. · Electric blankets are given as an example. · Video-display terminals are another example.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Prolonged exposure to nonionizing radiation— electromagnetic radiation at or below the frequency of visible light—increases a person’s chances of developing soft-tissue cancer.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about exposure to radiation."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The exposure is prolonged."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The radiation is nonionizing."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Nonionizing radiation is electromagnetic."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is at or below the frequency of visible light."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The exposure increases a person''s chances."}, {"n": 7, "text": "What the chances are of is developing cancer."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The cancer is soft-tissue cancer."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Electric power lines as well as such electrical appliances as electric blankets and video-display terminals are sources of nonionizing radiation.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This names sources of that radiation."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Electric power lines are one source."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Electrical appliances are also sources."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Electric blankets are given as an example."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Video-display terminals are another example."}]}], "the_read": "Electric power lines and some appliances may increase risk of cancer.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q18", "qnum": 18, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 17, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q19", "prompt": "In the first decade following the founding of the British Labour party, the number of people regularly voting for Labour increased fivefold. The number of committed Labour voters increased a further fivefold during the party’s second decade. Since the increase was thus the same in the first as in the second decade, the often-made claim that the Labour party gained more voters in the party’s second decade than in its first is clearly false.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about a decade. · The decade followed the founding of a party. · The party is the British Labour party. · It is about people voting for Labour. · Those people voted regularly. · Their number increased fivefold. (2) This is about committed Labour voters. · Their number increased again. · The increase was a further fivefold. · It happened during the second decade. · The decade is the party''s own second. (3) This gives a reason for what follows. · The increase is treated as the same in both decades. · The comparison is between the first and the second. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is about a claim other people make. · The claim is made often. · The claim is that Labour gained more voters in its second decade. · The comparison is with its first decade. · The conclusion says the claim is false. · It says so clearly.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In the first decade following the founding of the British Labour party, the number of people regularly voting for Labour increased fivefold.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a decade."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The decade followed the founding of a party."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The party is the British Labour party."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is about people voting for Labour."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those people voted regularly."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Their number increased fivefold."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The number of committed Labour voters increased a further fivefold during the party’s second decade.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about committed Labour voters."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Their number increased again."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The increase was a further fivefold."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It happened during the second decade."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The decade is the party''s own second."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Since the increase was thus the same in the first as in the second decade,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason for what follows."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The increase is treated as the same in both decades."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The comparison is between the first and the second."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "the often-made claim that the Labour party gained more voters in the party’s second decade than in its first is clearly false.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about a claim other people make."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The claim is made often."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The claim is that Labour gained more voters in its second decade."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with its first decade."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The conclusion says the claim is false."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It says so clearly."}]}], "the_read": "What if a fivefold increase of a greater number is larger than a fivefold increase of a smaller number?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q19", "qnum": 19, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 18, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q20", "prompt": "A number of seriously interested amateur astronomers have tested the new Exodus refractor telescope. With it, they were able to observe in crisp detail planetary features that were seen only as fuzzy images in their 8-inch (approximately 20-centimeter) Newtonian telescopes, even though the 8-inch telescopes, with their wider apertures, gather more light than the 4-inch (approximately 10-centimeter) Exodus. Given these amateur astronomers’ observational findings, any serious amateur astronomer ought to choose the Exodus if she or he is buying a telescope for planetary observation.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about amateur astronomers. · They are seriously interested ones. · A number of them did something. · They tested a telescope. · The telescope is the Exodus refractor. · The telescope is new. (2) This is about using that telescope. · They were able to observe planetary features. · The detail was crisp. · The same features looked different before. · They were seen only as fuzzy images. · That was in their Newtonian telescopes. · Those telescopes are 8-inch ones. · Eight inches is about 20 centimeters. (3) This is a contrast with what came before. · It is about the 8-inch telescopes. · Those telescopes have wider apertures. · They gather more light. · The comparison is with the Exodus. · The Exodus is a 4-inch telescope. · Four inches is about 10 centimeters. (4) This is the conclusion. · It rests on those observational findings. · The findings belong to these amateur astronomers. · It is about any serious amateur astronomer. · Such a person ought to choose the Exodus. · This applies under a condition. · The condition is buying a telescope for planetary observation.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A number of seriously interested amateur astronomers have tested the new Exodus refractor telescope.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about amateur astronomers."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are seriously interested ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A number of them did something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They tested a telescope."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The telescope is the Exodus refractor."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The telescope is new."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "With it, they were able to observe in crisp detail planetary features that were seen only as fuzzy images in their 8-inch (approximately 20-centimeter) Newtonian telescopes,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about using that telescope."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They were able to observe planetary features."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The detail was crisp."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The same features looked different before."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They were seen only as fuzzy images."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That was in their Newtonian telescopes."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those telescopes are 8-inch ones."}, {"n": 8, "text": "Eight inches is about 20 centimeters."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "even though the 8-inch telescopes, with their wider apertures, gather more light than the 4-inch (approximately 10-centimeter) Exodus.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the 8-inch telescopes."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those telescopes have wider apertures."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They gather more light."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with the Exodus."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The Exodus is a 4-inch telescope."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Four inches is about 10 centimeters."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Given these amateur astronomers’ observational findings, any serious amateur astronomer ought to choose the Exodus if she or he is buying a telescope for planetary observation.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on those observational findings."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The findings belong to these amateur astronomers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is about any serious amateur astronomer."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Such a person ought to choose the Exodus."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This applies under a condition."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The condition is buying a telescope for planetary observation."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s a downside to Exodus refractor telescopes? What if it''s too expensive for any amateur?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q20", "qnum": 20, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "D"}, {"citation": "PT21.S3.Q21", "qnum": 21, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 19, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q22", "prompt": "Anatomical bilateral symmetry is a common trait. It follows, therefore, that it confers survival advantages on organisms. After all, if bilateral symmetry did not confer such advantages, it would not be common.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about a trait. · The trait is anatomical bilateral symmetry. · The trait is common. (2) This is the conclusion. · It is drawn from what came before. · The symmetry confers advantages. · The advantages are survival advantages. · They are conferred on organisms. (3) This supports the conclusion. · It is a conditional statement. · The condition is symmetry not conferring such advantages. · In that case it would not be common.", "category": "Parallel Reasoning", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Anatomical bilateral symmetry is a common trait.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a trait."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The trait is anatomical bilateral symmetry."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The trait is common."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "It follows, therefore, that it confers survival advantages on organisms.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is drawn from what came before."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The symmetry confers advantages."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The advantages are survival advantages."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They are conferred on organisms."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "After all, if bilateral symmetry did not confer such advantages, it would not be common.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This supports the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is a conditional statement."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The condition is symmetry not conferring such advantages."}, {"n": 4, "text": "In that case it would not be common."}]}], "the_read": "What if the rules of conditional reasoning don''t apply?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q22", "qnum": 22, "question_type": "Parallel Reasoning", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 20, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q23", "prompt": "Electrical engineers have repeatedly demonstrated that the best solid-state amplifiers are indistinguishable from the best vacuum-tube amplifiers with respect to the characteristics commonly measured in evaluating the quality of an amplifier’s musical reproduction. Therefore, those music lovers who insist that recorded music sounds better when played with the best vacuum-tube amplifier than when played with the best solid-state amplifier must be imagining the difference in quality that they claim to hear.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about electrical engineers. · They have demonstrated something repeatedly. · It concerns the best solid-state amplifiers. · Those are indistinguishable from the best vacuum-tube amplifiers. · The indistinguishability is limited to certain characteristics. · Those characteristics are the ones commonly measured. · They are measured to evaluate quality. · The quality is of musical reproduction. (2) This is the conclusion. · It is about certain music lovers. · Those people insist on something. · They say recorded music sounds better with a vacuum-tube amplifier. · The comparison is with the best solid-state amplifier. · The conclusion says they must be imagining it. · What they imagine is the difference in quality. · It is a difference they claim to hear.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Electrical engineers have repeatedly demonstrated that the best solid-state amplifiers are indistinguishable from the best vacuum-tube amplifiers with respect to the characteristics commonly measured in evaluating the quality of an amplifier’s musical reproduction.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about electrical engineers."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They have demonstrated something repeatedly."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It concerns the best solid-state amplifiers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those are indistinguishable from the best vacuum-tube amplifiers."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The indistinguishability is limited to certain characteristics."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those characteristics are the ones commonly measured."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They are measured to evaluate quality."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The quality is of musical reproduction."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Therefore, those music lovers who insist that recorded music sounds better when played with the best vacuum-tube amplifier than when played with the best solid-state amplifier must be imagining the difference in quality that they claim to hear.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about certain music lovers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those people insist on something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They say recorded music sounds better with a vacuum-tube amplifier."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with the best solid-state amplifier."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The conclusion says they must be imagining it."}, {"n": 7, "text": "What they imagine is the difference in quality."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It is a difference they claim to hear."}]}], "the_read": "What if they are distinguishable with respect to characteristics not commonly measured?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q23", "qnum": 23, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 21, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q24", "prompt": "Explanation must be distinguished from justification. Every human action potentially has an explanation; that is, with sufficient knowledge it would be possible to give an accurate description of the causes of that action. An action is justified only when the person performing the action has sufficient reasons for the action. According to many psychologists, even when there is a justification for an action, that justification often forms no part of the explanation. The general principle, however, is that only an action whose justification, that is, the reasons for the action, forms an essential part of its explanation is rational.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about two things. · One of them is explanation. · The other is justification. · The two must be distinguished. (2) This is about every human action. · Each one potentially has an explanation. · The rest restates what that means. · Sufficient knowledge would be needed. · It would be possible to give a description. · The description would be accurate. · What is described is the causes of the action. (3) This is about when an action is justified. · It states a necessary condition. · The person performing the action must have reasons. · The reasons must be sufficient. (4) This is attributed to psychologists. · It is the view of many of them. · It applies even when a justification exists. · The justification forms no part of the explanation. · This is often so rather than always. (5) This is a contrast with what came before. · It states a general principle. · The principle is about which actions are rational. · An action''s justification is its reasons. · Those reasons must form part of its explanation. · The part must be an essential one. · Only such an action is rational.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Explanation must be distinguished from justification.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about two things."}, {"n": 2, "text": "One of them is explanation."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The other is justification."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The two must be distinguished."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Every human action potentially has an explanation; that is, with sufficient knowledge it would be possible to give an accurate description of the causes of that action.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about every human action."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Each one potentially has an explanation."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The rest restates what that means."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Sufficient knowledge would be needed."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It would be possible to give a description."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The description would be accurate."}, {"n": 7, "text": "What is described is the causes of the action."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "An action is justified only when the person performing the action has sufficient reasons for the action.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about when an action is justified."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It states a necessary condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The person performing the action must have reasons."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The reasons must be sufficient."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "According to many psychologists, even when there is a justification for an action, that justification often forms no part of the explanation.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is attributed to psychologists."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is the view of many of them."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It applies even when a justification exists."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The justification forms no part of the explanation."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is often so rather than always."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "The general principle, however, is that only an action whose justification, that is, the reasons for the action, forms an essential part of its explanation is rational.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It states a general principle."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The principle is about which actions are rational."}, {"n": 4, "text": "An action''s justification is its reasons."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those reasons must form part of its explanation."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The part must be an essential one."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Only such an action is rational."}]}], "the_read": "According to many psychologists, there are some human actions that are not rational.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q24", "qnum": 24, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 22, "slug": "PT21_S3_Q25", "prompt": "At the company picnic, all of the employees who participated in more than four of the scheduled events, and only those employees, were eligible for the raffle held at the end of the day. Since only a small proportion of the employees were eligible for the raffle, most of the employees must have participated in fewer than four of the scheduled events.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about a company picnic. · It is about employees. · Those employees participated in scheduled events. · They participated in more than four of them. (2) This limits the group to those employees only. · Those employees were eligible for something. · What they were eligible for is a raffle. · The raffle was held at the end of the day. (3) This is the conclusion. · It rests on a given. · The given is that few employees were eligible. · The proportion eligible was small. · The conclusion is about most of the employees. · They must have participated in fewer than four events. · The events are the scheduled ones.", "category": "Parallel Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "At the company picnic, all of the employees who participated in more than four of the scheduled events,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a company picnic."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about employees."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those employees participated in scheduled events."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They participated in more than four of them."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "and only those employees, were eligible for the raffle held at the end of the day.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This limits the group to those employees only."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those employees were eligible for something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they were eligible for is a raffle."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The raffle was held at the end of the day."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Since only a small proportion of the employees were eligible for the raffle, most of the employees must have participated in fewer than four of the scheduled events.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on a given."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The given is that few employees were eligible."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The proportion eligible was small."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The conclusion is about most of the employees."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They must have participated in fewer than four events."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The events are the scheduled ones."}]}], "the_read": "What if most employees participated in exactly four events?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT21.S3.Q25", "qnum": 25, "question_type": "Parallel Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}]'::jsonb,
    'section',
    '{"standard": 25, "accommodation_53": 38, "accommodation_70": 50}'::jsonb,
    'PT21.S3'
  ),
  (
    'read-recall-colour-pt101-s2',
    'Read, Recall, Colour · PT101 Section 2',
    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, and react to it. Once you finish the stimulus, say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, then give it a reaction. After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears, and you recall the whole argument out loud from memory.", "rules": ["Read each piece silently. Everything you say back, including your reaction and final recall, is out loud.", "Read about a sentence at a time. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said.", "Then colour it: react to that specific piece with a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling.", "Repeat these steps through the whole stimulus.", "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and stays gone. Say the entire argument back from memory, with every detail you can still hold.", "Press space again to move on. The whole section records in one take, with no pauses or breaks."], "briefing": {"lede": "Read these instructions once before your first section. After that, come back when you need a reminder.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still reach the answers remembering almost none of it.", "body": ["You can follow a stimulus sentence by sentence and understand every part. Then you reach answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and that is what this drill trains.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. Understanding the stimulus is not enough if it is gone by the time you reach the answers.", "Most everyday reading trains you to reach the end, not retain every detail. You scan names, drop connecting words, and turn a list of three things into one vague label. That works for a phone bill. It does not work for the LSAT."]}, {"heading": "How the method works", "body": ["For each piece, read it, say it back, and colour it. Once you finish the stimulus, recall the whole argument. You decide how much to read at a time, and nothing on the screen decides for you.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it", "body": "Read about a sentence silently. Give every word enough attention to hear the sentence naturally in your head. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less. If you take on too much, the beginning is usually what disappears first."}, {"label": "Tell it back", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it", "body": "React to what you just said. Give it a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling. Keep it short, but make it specific. If the same reaction could fit any sentence, it does not count."}, {"label": "Recall it", "body": "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Say the entire argument out loud from memory, with every detail you can still hold. This is what gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter", "body": ["Do not give back only the gist or what seemed important. You cannot know which details will matter until you have read the whole stimulus. Filtering as you read is how useful information disappears.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. Names, numbers, and small words carrying the load, such as all, most, only, and probably, have to come back too.", "**Being efficient does not mean leaving information out.**"]}, {"heading": "When nothing comes to mind", "body": ["You may try to colour a sentence and find that nothing comes to mind. Usually, the hidden thought is that nothing feels good enough to say.", "Do not judge the reaction before you say it. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count when they connect to what you just read.", "The reaction makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away from the text", "body": ["The text stays visible while you say each piece back. You have to look away from it yourself.", "If you read while you recall, you stop using your short-term memory and slide back into the author''s exact words. Looking away forces you to rebuild the meaning in your own words.", "This is also the version that works on paper and on test day."]}, {"heading": "Build toward twenty-five minutes", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section. It only counts when you read every stimulus, say each piece back, colour it, and recall the whole argument. Finishing early by skipping parts is not finishing early. If you receive extra time on test day, use your full allotted time here too.", "The clock counts up so you can track your pace without watching your time disappear.", "Do the method correctly before you try to do it quickly. Go slower while it is new. Speed follows once the method becomes automatic."]}], "closer": "One section keeps the method steady. Improvement starts with the second."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, tells it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives The Recall of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Recall against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q1", "prompt": "Press release: A comprehensive review evaluating the medical studies done up to the present time has found no reason to think that drinking coffee in normal amounts harms the coffee-drinker''s heart. So coffee drinkers can relax and enjoy their beverage—it is safe to drink coffee.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is a press release. · A review has been done. · The review is a comprehensive one. · The review evaluated medical studies. · Those studies were done up to the present time. · The review found no reason to think something. · The thing is that drinking coffee harms the heart. · The amounts of coffee are normal ones. (2) This is the conclusion. · Coffee drinkers can relax. · They can enjoy their beverage. · It is safe to drink coffee.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Press release: A comprehensive review evaluating the medical studies done up to the present time has found no reason to think that drinking coffee in normal amounts harms the coffee-drinker''s heart.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a press release."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A review has been done."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The review is a comprehensive one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The review evaluated medical studies."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those studies were done up to the present time."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The review found no reason to think something."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The thing is that drinking coffee harms the heart."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The amounts of coffee are normal ones."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "So coffee drinkers can relax and enjoy their beverage—it is safe to drink coffee.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Coffee drinkers can relax."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They can enjoy their beverage."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is safe to drink coffee."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because coffee isn''t bad for your heart that doesn''t mean it''s safe overall? What if coffee makes your hair fall out?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q2", "prompt": "All people prefer colors that they can distinguish easily to colors that they have difficulty distinguishing. Infants can easily distinguish bright colors but, unlike adults, have difficulty distinguishing subtle shades. A brightly colored toy for infants sells better than the same toy in subtle shades at the same price.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about all people. · People prefer some colors to others. · The preferred colors are ones they can distinguish easily. · The other colors are ones they have difficulty distinguishing. (2) This is about infants. · Infants can distinguish bright colors easily. · There is a contrast in this sentence. · Infants have difficulty distinguishing subtle shades. · Adults are unlike infants in this. (3) This is about a toy for infants. · The toy is brightly colored. · That toy sells better than another version. · The other version is the same toy in subtle shades. · The price of both is the same.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "All people prefer colors that they can distinguish easily to colors that they have difficulty distinguishing.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about all people."}, {"n": 2, "text": "People prefer some colors to others."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The preferred colors are ones they can distinguish easily."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other colors are ones they have difficulty distinguishing."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Infants can easily distinguish bright colors but, unlike adults, have difficulty distinguishing subtle shades.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about infants."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Infants can distinguish bright colors easily."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There is a contrast in this sentence."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Infants have difficulty distinguishing subtle shades."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Adults are unlike infants in this."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "A brightly colored toy for infants sells better than the same toy in subtle shades at the same price.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a toy for infants."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The toy is brightly colored."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That toy sells better than another version."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other version is the same toy in subtle shades."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The price of both is the same."}]}], "the_read": "Infant toys may be purchased in color shades the infant prefers.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q3", "prompt": "A group of unusual meteorites was found in Shergotty, India. Their structure indicates that they originated on one of the geologically active planets, Mercury, Venus, or Mars. Because of Mercury''s proximity to the Sun, any material dislodged from that planet''s surface would have been captured by the Sun, rather than falling to Earth as meteorites. Nor could Venus be the source of the meteorites, because its gravity would have prevented dislodged material from escaping into space. The meteorites, therefore, probably fell to Earth after being dislodged from Mars, perhaps as the result of a collision with a large object.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A group of meteorites was found. · The meteorites are unusual ones. · They were found in Shergotty. · Shergotty is in India. (2) This is about the meteorites'' structure. · The structure indicates where they originated. · They originated on one planet. · That planet is geologically active. · Mercury is one of the named planets. · Venus is another named planet. · Mars is the third named planet. (3) This gives a reason. · Mercury is close to the Sun. · Material might be dislodged from that planet''s surface. · This holds for any such material. · That material would have been captured by the Sun. · It would not have fallen to Earth. · Falling to Earth would have made it meteorites. (4) Venus could not be the source either. · The source would be of the meteorites. · A reason is given for this. · Venus has its own gravity. · Its gravity would have prevented something. · Dislodged material could not escape into space. (5) This is the conclusion. · The meteorites fell to Earth. · That is probably what happened. · They were dislodged from Mars first. · A possible cause is offered. · The cause is a collision. · The collision was with a large object.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A group of unusual meteorites was found in Shergotty, India.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A group of meteorites was found."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The meteorites are unusual ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They were found in Shergotty."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Shergotty is in India."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Their structure indicates that they originated on one of the geologically active planets, Mercury, Venus, or Mars.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the meteorites'' structure."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The structure indicates where they originated."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They originated on one planet."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That planet is geologically active."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Mercury is one of the named planets."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Venus is another named planet."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Mars is the third named planet."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Because of Mercury''s proximity to the Sun, any material dislodged from that planet''s surface would have been captured by the Sun, rather than falling to Earth as meteorites.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Mercury is close to the Sun."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Material might be dislodged from that planet''s surface."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This holds for any such material."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That material would have been captured by the Sun."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It would not have fallen to Earth."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Falling to Earth would have made it meteorites."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Nor could Venus be the source of the meteorites, because its gravity would have prevented dislodged material from escaping into space.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Venus could not be the source either."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The source would be of the meteorites."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A reason is given for this."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Venus has its own gravity."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Its gravity would have prevented something."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Dislodged material could not escape into space."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "The meteorites, therefore, probably fell to Earth after being dislodged from Mars, perhaps as the result of a collision with a large object.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The meteorites fell to Earth."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That is probably what happened."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They were dislodged from Mars first."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A possible cause is offered."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The cause is a collision."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The collision was with a large object."}]}], "the_read": "What if the structural indications are misleading? What if Mars'' gravity would also prevent meteors from escaping into space?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q4", "prompt": "Because quitting smoking is very stressful and leads to weight gain, it is difficult to do. The key to quitting, however, may be as simple as replacing an unhealthy activity with a healthy one. In one study, half of those attempting to quit were assigned to a smoking-cessation program alone, and the other half were assigned to the same program plus fifteen weeks of aerobic exercise. At the one-month mark, none in the first group had quit, but 40 percent of those in the second group had not smoked.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This gives a reason. · Quitting smoking is stressful. · It is very stressful. · Quitting smoking leads to weight gain. · Quitting smoking is difficult to do. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · There is a key to quitting. · The key may be simple. · It may be as simple as a replacement. · What is replaced is an unhealthy activity. · What replaces it is a healthy activity. (3) There was one study. · The study involved people attempting to quit. · Half of them were assigned to something. · What they were assigned to was a smoking-cessation program. · That group got the program alone. (4) This is about the other half. · They were assigned to the same program. · They also got aerobic exercise. · The exercise lasted fifteen weeks. (5) This is at the one-month mark. · None in the first group had quit. · There is a contrast in this sentence. · Some of the second group had not smoked. · That portion was 40 percent.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Because quitting smoking is very stressful and leads to weight gain, it is difficult to do.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Quitting smoking is stressful."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is very stressful."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Quitting smoking leads to weight gain."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Quitting smoking is difficult to do."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The key to quitting, however, may be as simple as replacing an unhealthy activity with a healthy one.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a key to quitting."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The key may be simple."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It may be as simple as a replacement."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What is replaced is an unhealthy activity."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What replaces it is a healthy activity."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "In one study, half of those attempting to quit were assigned to a smoking-cessation program alone,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There was one study."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The study involved people attempting to quit."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Half of them were assigned to something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What they were assigned to was a smoking-cessation program."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That group got the program alone."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "and the other half were assigned to the same program plus fifteen weeks of aerobic exercise.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the other half."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They were assigned to the same program."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They also got aerobic exercise."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The exercise lasted fifteen weeks."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "At the one-month mark, none in the first group had quit, but 40 percent of those in the second group had not smoked.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is at the one-month mark."}, {"n": 2, "text": "None in the first group had quit."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There is a contrast in this sentence."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Some of the second group had not smoked."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That portion was 40 percent."}]}], "the_read": "What if the aerobics group stopped smoking because they had less addictive personalities, and not because of the exercise?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q5", "prompt": "Altogether, the students in Ms. Tarnowski''s Milton Elementary School class collected more aluminum cans than did the students in any of the school''s other classes. Therefore, the Milton student who collected the most aluminum cans was in Ms. Tarnowski''s class.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This counts things altogether. · The students are in Ms. Tarnowski''s class. · The class is at Milton Elementary School. · Those students collected aluminum cans. · They collected more than other students did. · The comparison is with students in the school''s other classes. · It holds for any of those other classes. (2) This is the conclusion. · One Milton student collected the most cans. · The cans are aluminum ones. · That student was in Ms. Tarnowski''s class.", "category": "Parallel Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Altogether, the students in Ms. Tarnowski''s Milton Elementary School class collected more aluminum cans than did the students in any of the school''s other classes.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This counts things altogether."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The students are in Ms. Tarnowski''s class."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The class is at Milton Elementary School."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those students collected aluminum cans."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They collected more than other students did."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with students in the school''s other classes."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It holds for any of those other classes."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Therefore, the Milton student who collected the most aluminum cans was in Ms. Tarnowski''s class.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "One Milton student collected the most cans."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The cans are aluminum ones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That student was in Ms. Tarnowski''s class."}]}], "the_read": "What if what is true of the class isn''t necessarily true of the students in the class? What if one student in a low-overall-performing class collected more cans than any other individual?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Parallel Flaw", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q6", "prompt": "Several excellent candidates have been proposed for the presidency of United Wire, and each candidate would bring to the job different talents and experience. If the others are compared with Jones, however, it will be apparent that none of them has her unique set of qualifications. Jones, therefore, is best qualified to be the new president of United Wire.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Several candidates have been proposed. · The candidates are excellent ones. · They were proposed for a presidency. · The presidency is of United Wire. · Each candidate would bring something to the job. · What each would bring is different talents and experience. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · This states a condition. · The condition is that the others are compared with Jones. · Then something will be apparent. · None of the others has her qualifications. · Her set of qualifications is unique. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about Jones. · She is best qualified. · What she is qualified for is the presidency. · She would be the new president of United Wire.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Several excellent candidates have been proposed for the presidency of United Wire, and each candidate would bring to the job different talents and experience.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Several candidates have been proposed."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The candidates are excellent ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They were proposed for a presidency."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The presidency is of United Wire."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Each candidate would bring something to the job."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What each would bring is different talents and experience."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "If the others are compared with Jones, however, it will be apparent that none of them has her unique set of qualifications.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This states a condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The condition is that the others are compared with Jones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Then something will be apparent."}, {"n": 5, "text": "None of the others has her qualifications."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Her set of qualifications is unique."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Jones, therefore, is best qualified to be the new president of United Wire.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about Jones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "She is best qualified."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What she is qualified for is the presidency."}, {"n": 5, "text": "She would be the new president of United Wire."}]}], "the_read": "What if unique qualifications aren''t necessarily the best qualification?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q7", "prompt": "A neighborhood group plans to protest the closing of the neighborhood''s only recreation center on the grounds that to do so would leave the neighborhood without local access to a recreation center. \"Our neighborhood already has the most residents per center of any neighborhood in the city,\" complained one resident, \"and closing this center would make the situation unacceptable since access to recreational facilities is a necessity for this neighborhood.\"", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A neighborhood group has a plan. · The plan is to protest a closing. · What would close is a recreation center. · It is the neighborhood''s only one. · The protest has stated grounds. · The grounds are that closing would leave the neighborhood without access. · The access in question is local access. (2) This is spoken by one resident. · The resident is complaining. · The neighborhood has the most residents per center. · It already has that. · The comparison is with any neighborhood in the city. (3) This is an additional point. · Closing this center would change the situation. · The situation would become unacceptable. · A reason is given for that. · The reason is that access is a necessity. · The access is to recreational facilities. · The necessity is for this neighborhood.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A neighborhood group plans to protest the closing of the neighborhood''s only recreation center on the grounds that to do so would leave the neighborhood without local access to a recreation center.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A neighborhood group has a plan."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The plan is to protest a closing."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What would close is a recreation center."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is the neighborhood''s only one."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The protest has stated grounds."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The grounds are that closing would leave the neighborhood without access."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The access in question is local access."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "\"Our neighborhood already has the most residents per center of any neighborhood in the city,\" complained one resident,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is spoken by one resident."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The resident is complaining."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The neighborhood has the most residents per center."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It already has that."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with any neighborhood in the city."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "\"and closing this center would make the situation unacceptable since access to recreational facilities is a necessity for this neighborhood.\"", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Closing this center would change the situation."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The situation would become unacceptable."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A reason is given for that."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The reason is that access is a necessity."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The access is to recreational facilities."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The necessity is for this neighborhood."}]}], "the_read": "What if the community needs fulfilled by the rec center will be filled by another facility (ie the library) once the center closes?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q8", "prompt": "Sociologist: The claim that there is a large number of violent crimes in our society is false, for this claim is based upon the large number of stories in newspapers about violent crimes. But since violent crimes are very rare occurrences, newspapers are likely to print stories about them.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A sociologist is speaking. · There is a claim at issue. · The claim is that violent crimes are numerous. · The number is said to be large. · The crimes are in our society. · The sociologist says the claim is false. (2) This gives a reason. · The claim is based on something. · It is based on stories in newspapers. · The number of stories is large. · The stories are about violent crimes. (3) This is a contrast with what came before. · Violent crimes are rare occurrences. · They are very rare. · Newspapers are likely to print stories. · The stories would be about violent crimes.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Sociologist: The claim that there is a large number of violent crimes in our society is false,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A sociologist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a claim at issue."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The claim is that violent crimes are numerous."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The number is said to be large."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The crimes are in our society."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The sociologist says the claim is false."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "for this claim is based upon the large number of stories in newspapers about violent crimes.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The claim is based on something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is based on stories in newspapers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The number of stories is large."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The stories are about violent crimes."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "But since violent crimes are very rare occurrences, newspapers are likely to print stories about them.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Violent crimes are rare occurrences."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are very rare."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Newspapers are likely to print stories."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The stories would be about violent crimes."}]}], "the_read": "What if we can''t assume the truth of our conclusion?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q8", "qnum": 8, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 9, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q9", "prompt": "Historian: Anyone who thinks that the terrors of the ancient regime of Q were exclusively the work of fanatics is overlooking a basic truth: the regime was made up primarily of ordinary people enthusiastically seeking paradise. The regime executed many people in pursuit of its goal; but it later became clear that paradise, as they defined it, is unrealizable. So at least some of the ordinary people of Q were in fact murderers.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A historian is speaking. · This is about anyone with a certain thought. · The thought is that the terrors were the work of fanatics. · The terrors belong to the regime of Q. · That regime is an ancient one. · The thought is that fanatics were exclusively responsible. · Such a person is overlooking a truth. · The truth is a basic one. (2) This is about the regime. · The regime was made up of ordinary people. · That was primarily so. · Those people were seeking paradise. · They sought it enthusiastically. (3) The regime executed people. · It executed many of them. · The executions were in pursuit of its goal. · There is a contrast in this sentence. · It later became clear that paradise is unrealizable. · Paradise here is as they defined it. (4) This is the conclusion. · Some of the ordinary people were murderers. · It is at least some of them. · Those people are the ordinary people of Q.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Historian: Anyone who thinks that the terrors of the ancient regime of Q were exclusively the work of fanatics is overlooking a basic truth:", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A historian is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is about anyone with a certain thought."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The thought is that the terrors were the work of fanatics."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The terrors belong to the regime of Q."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That regime is an ancient one."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The thought is that fanatics were exclusively responsible."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Such a person is overlooking a truth."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The truth is a basic one."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "the regime was made up primarily of ordinary people enthusiastically seeking paradise.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the regime."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The regime was made up of ordinary people."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That was primarily so."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those people were seeking paradise."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They sought it enthusiastically."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The regime executed many people in pursuit of its goal; but it later became clear that paradise, as they defined it, is unrealizable.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The regime executed people."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It executed many of them."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The executions were in pursuit of its goal."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There is a contrast in this sentence."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It later became clear that paradise is unrealizable."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Paradise here is as they defined it."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "So at least some of the ordinary people of Q were in fact murderers.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some of the ordinary people were murderers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is at least some of them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those people are the ordinary people of Q."}]}], "the_read": "What if executing people in pursuit of paradise isn''t murder?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q9", "qnum": 9, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 10, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q10", "prompt": "Economist: The economy seems to be heading out of recession. Recent figures show that consumers are buying more durable goods than before, indicating that they expect economic growth in the near future.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) An economist is speaking. · The economy seems to be doing something. · It seems to be heading out of recession. (2) There are figures on this. · The figures are recent ones. · The figures show what consumers are buying. · Consumers are buying durable goods. · They are buying more than before. · That indicates something about consumers. · Consumers expect economic growth. · The growth would come in the near future.", "category": "Argument Part", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Economist: The economy seems to be heading out of recession.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "An economist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The economy seems to be doing something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It seems to be heading out of recession."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Recent figures show that consumers are buying more durable goods than before, indicating that they expect economic growth in the near future.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There are figures on this."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The figures are recent ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The figures show what consumers are buying."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Consumers are buying durable goods."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They are buying more than before."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That indicates something about consumers."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Consumers expect economic growth."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The growth would come in the near future."}]}], "the_read": "What if the expectation of economic growth doesn''t mean the economy is headed out of a recession?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Argument Part", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 11, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q11", "prompt": "Not surprisingly, there are no professors under the age of eighteen. And, as is well known, no one under eighteen can vote legally. Finally, some brilliant people are professors, some are legal voters, and some are under eighteen.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is said to be unsurprising. · There are no professors of one kind. · Those would be under the age of eighteen. (2) This is an additional point. · This is said to be well known. · No one under eighteen can vote. · The voting in question is legal voting. (3) This is the final point. · It is about brilliant people. · Some of them are professors. · Some of them are legal voters. · Some of them are under eighteen.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Not surprisingly, there are no professors under the age of eighteen.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is said to be unsurprising."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There are no professors of one kind."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those would be under the age of eighteen."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "And, as is well known, no one under eighteen can vote legally.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is said to be well known."}, {"n": 3, "text": "No one under eighteen can vote."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The voting in question is legal voting."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Finally, some brilliant people are professors, some are legal voters, and some are under eighteen.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the final point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about brilliant people."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Some of them are professors."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Some of them are legal voters."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Some of them are under eighteen."}]}], "the_read": "There are some people who both cannot vote and are not professors.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q11", "qnum": 11, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q12", "prompt": "For years scientists have been scanning the skies in the hope of finding life on other planets. But in spite of the ever-increasing sophistication of the equipment they employ, some of it costing hundreds of millions of dollars, not the first shred of evidence of such life has been forthcoming. And there is no reason to think that these scientists will be any more successful in the future, no matter how much money is invested in the search. The dream of finding extraterrestrial life is destined to remain a dream, as science''s experience up to this point should indicate.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about scientists. · They have been scanning the skies. · They have done so for years. · They have a hope in doing it. · The hope is to find life. · The life would be on other planets. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · The scientists employ equipment. · The equipment is sophisticated. · Its sophistication is ever-increasing. (3) Some of the equipment costs a lot. · The cost is hundreds of millions of dollars. · No evidence has been forthcoming. · Not the first shred of it has come. · The evidence would be of such life. (4) This is an additional point. · There is no reason to think something. · The thought would be about these scientists. · They would be more successful in the future. · This holds no matter how much money is invested. · The money would be invested in the search. (5) There is a dream at issue. · The dream is of finding extraterrestrial life. · It is destined to remain a dream. · A basis is given for this. · The basis is science''s experience. · That experience runs up to this point. · That experience should indicate it.", "category": "Conclusion", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "For years scientists have been scanning the skies in the hope of finding life on other planets.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about scientists."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They have been scanning the skies."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They have done so for years."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They have a hope in doing it."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The hope is to find life."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The life would be on other planets."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But in spite of the ever-increasing sophistication of the equipment they employ,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The scientists employ equipment."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The equipment is sophisticated."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Its sophistication is ever-increasing."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "some of it costing hundreds of millions of dollars, not the first shred of evidence of such life has been forthcoming.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some of the equipment costs a lot."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The cost is hundreds of millions of dollars."}, {"n": 3, "text": "No evidence has been forthcoming."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Not the first shred of it has come."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The evidence would be of such life."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "And there is no reason to think that these scientists will be any more successful in the future, no matter how much money is invested in the search.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is no reason to think something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The thought would be about these scientists."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They would be more successful in the future."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This holds no matter how much money is invested."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The money would be invested in the search."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "The dream of finding extraterrestrial life is destined to remain a dream, as science''s experience up to this point should indicate.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a dream at issue."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The dream is of finding extraterrestrial life."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is destined to remain a dream."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A basis is given for this."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The basis is science''s experience."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That experience runs up to this point."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That experience should indicate it."}]}], "the_read": "What if extraterrestrial life will come to us?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q12", "qnum": 12, "question_type": "Conclusion", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 13, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q13", "prompt": "Carl''s Coffee Emporium stocks only two decaffeinated coffees: French Roast and Mocha Java. Yusef only serves decaffeinated coffee, and the coffee he served after dinner last night was far too smooth and mellow to have been French Roast. So, if Yusef still gets all his coffee from Carl''s, what he served last night was Mocha Java.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Carl''s Coffee Emporium stocks coffees. · It stocks only two decaffeinated ones. · French Roast is one of them. · Mocha Java is the other one. (2) This is about Yusef. · He serves only decaffeinated coffee. · He served coffee after dinner. · That was last night. · The coffee was smooth and mellow. · It was far too much so. · It could not have been French Roast. (3) This is the conclusion. · It rests on a condition. · The condition is that Yusef still gets his coffee from Carl''s. · He gets all of it there. · What he served last night was Mocha Java.", "category": "Parallel Reasoning", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Carl''s Coffee Emporium stocks only two decaffeinated coffees: French Roast and Mocha Java.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Carl''s Coffee Emporium stocks coffees."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It stocks only two decaffeinated ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "French Roast is one of them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Mocha Java is the other one."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Yusef only serves decaffeinated coffee, and the coffee he served after dinner last night was far too smooth and mellow to have been French Roast.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about Yusef."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He serves only decaffeinated coffee."}, {"n": 3, "text": "He served coffee after dinner."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That was last night."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The coffee was smooth and mellow."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It was far too much so."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It could not have been French Roast."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "So, if Yusef still gets all his coffee from Carl''s, what he served last night was Mocha Java.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on a condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The condition is that Yusef still gets his coffee from Carl''s."}, {"n": 4, "text": "He gets all of it there."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What he served last night was Mocha Java."}]}], "the_read": "What if the rules of conditional reasoning don''t apply?\n\nWhat if the \"Carl''s\" where we''re assuming Yusef still gets his coffee is Carl''s Coffee Super Mart, NOT Carl''s Coffee Emporium?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Parallel Reasoning", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 14, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q14", "prompt": "Steven: The allowable blood alcohol level for drivers should be cut in half. With this reduced limit, social drinkers will be deterred from drinking and driving, resulting in significantly increased highway safety.\n\nMiguel: No, lowering the current allowable blood alcohol level would have little effect on highway safety, because it would not address the most important aspect of the drunken driving problem, which is the danger to the public posed by heavy drinkers, who often drive with a blood alcohol level of twice the current legal limit.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Steven is the first speaker. · There is an allowable blood alcohol level. · The level applies to drivers. · It should be cut in half. (2) This is about the reduced limit. · Social drinkers will be deterred. · What they are deterred from is drinking and driving. · A result would follow from that. · The result is increased highway safety. · The increase would be significant. (3) Miguel is the second speaker. · He disagrees with Steven. · The level could be lowered. · The level is the current allowable one. · Lowering it would have little effect. · The effect would be on highway safety. (4) This gives a reason. · Lowering the level would not address something. · What it misses is an aspect of the problem. · The problem is drunken driving. · That aspect is the most important one. (5) The aspect is a danger. · The danger is to the public. · Heavy drinkers pose that danger. · Heavy drinkers drive at a certain level. · They often do so. · Their blood alcohol level is twice the legal limit. · The limit is the current one.", "category": "Controversy", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Steven: The allowable blood alcohol level for drivers should be cut in half.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Steven is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is an allowable blood alcohol level."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The level applies to drivers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It should be cut in half."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "With this reduced limit, social drinkers will be deterred from drinking and driving, resulting in significantly increased highway safety.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the reduced limit."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Social drinkers will be deterred."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they are deterred from is drinking and driving."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A result would follow from that."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The result is increased highway safety."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The increase would be significant."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Miguel: No, lowering the current allowable blood alcohol level would have little effect on highway safety,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Miguel is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He disagrees with Steven."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The level could be lowered."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The level is the current allowable one."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Lowering it would have little effect."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The effect would be on highway safety."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "because it would not address the most important aspect of the drunken driving problem,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Lowering the level would not address something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What it misses is an aspect of the problem."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The problem is drunken driving."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That aspect is the most important one."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "which is the danger to the public posed by heavy drinkers, who often drive with a blood alcohol level of twice the current legal limit.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The aspect is a danger."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The danger is to the public."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Heavy drinkers pose that danger."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Heavy drinkers drive at a certain level."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They often do so."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Their blood alcohol level is twice the legal limit."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The limit is the current one."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether cutting the allowable blood alcohol in half will improve highway safety.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Controversy", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q15", "prompt": "The authors of a recent article examined warnings of an impending wave of extinctions of animal species within the next 100 years. These authors say that no evidence exists to support the idea that the rate of extinction of animal species is now accelerating. They are wrong, however. Consider only the data on fishes: 40 species and subspecies of North American fishes have vanished in the twentieth century, 13 between 1900 and 1950, and 27 since 1950.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is an article at issue. · The article is a recent one. · Its authors examined warnings. · The warnings are of a wave of extinctions. · The wave is impending. · The extinctions would be of animal species. · They would come within the next 100 years. (2) This is about those authors. · They say no evidence exists. · The evidence would support an idea. · The idea concerns a rate of extinction. · The rate is for animal species. · The rate is now accelerating. (3) This is a contrast with what came before. · This is about those authors. · The authors are wrong. (4) We are asked to consider data. · The data is only about fishes. · Some species and subspecies have vanished. · The number that vanished is 40. · The fishes are North American ones. · They vanished in the twentieth century. · 13 of them vanished between 1900 and 1950. · 27 of them vanished since 1950.", "category": "Conclusion", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The authors of a recent article examined warnings of an impending wave of extinctions of animal species within the next 100 years.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is an article at issue."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The article is a recent one."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Its authors examined warnings."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The warnings are of a wave of extinctions."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The wave is impending."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The extinctions would be of animal species."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They would come within the next 100 years."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "These authors say that no evidence exists to support the idea that the rate of extinction of animal species is now accelerating.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about those authors."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They say no evidence exists."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The evidence would support an idea."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The idea concerns a rate of extinction."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The rate is for animal species."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The rate is now accelerating."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are wrong, however.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is about those authors."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The authors are wrong."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Consider only the data on fishes: 40 species and subspecies of North American fishes have vanished in the twentieth century, 13 between 1900 and 1950, and 27 since 1950.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "We are asked to consider data."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The data is only about fishes."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Some species and subspecies have vanished."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The number that vanished is 40."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The fishes are North American ones."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They vanished in the twentieth century."}, {"n": 7, "text": "13 of them vanished between 1900 and 1950."}, {"n": 8, "text": "27 of them vanished since 1950."}]}], "the_read": "What if a jump from 13 to 27 is normal margin of error? What if only 2 subspecies of fish have gone extinct since 2000? What if the fish subspecies are not representative of all animal species?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Conclusion", "correct_answer": "A"}, {"citation": "PT101.S2.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Evaluate", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q17", "prompt": "After the Second World War, the charter of the newly formed United Nations established an eleven-member Security Council and charged it with taking collective action in response to threats to world peace. The charter further provided that the five nations that were then the major powers would permanently have sole authority to cast vetoes. The reason given for this arrangement was that the burden of maintaining world peace would rest on the world''s major powers, and no nation should be required to assume the burden of enforcing a decision it found repugnant.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This happened after the Second World War. · The United Nations had a charter. · The United Nations was newly formed. · The charter established a Security Council. · The Council had eleven members. · The charter charged the Council with a task. · The task is taking collective action. · The action responds to threats to world peace. (2) This is a further provision of the charter. · Certain nations were then the major powers. · There were five of them. · Those nations would have authority to cast vetoes. · Their authority would be sole authority. · They would have it permanently. (3) A reason was given for the arrangement. · There is a burden at issue. · The burden is maintaining world peace. · That burden would rest on the major powers. · Those are the world''s major powers. (4) This is an additional point. · No nation should be required to assume a burden. · The burden is enforcing a decision. · The nation would find that decision repugnant.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "After the Second World War, the charter of the newly formed United Nations established an eleven-member Security Council and charged it with taking collective action in response to threats to world peace.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This happened after the Second World War."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The United Nations had a charter."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The United Nations was newly formed."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The charter established a Security Council."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The Council had eleven members."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The charter charged the Council with a task."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The task is taking collective action."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The action responds to threats to world peace."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The charter further provided that the five nations that were then the major powers would permanently have sole authority to cast vetoes.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a further provision of the charter."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Certain nations were then the major powers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There were five of them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those nations would have authority to cast vetoes."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Their authority would be sole authority."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They would have it permanently."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The reason given for this arrangement was that the burden of maintaining world peace would rest on the world''s major powers,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A reason was given for the arrangement."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a burden at issue."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The burden is maintaining world peace."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That burden would rest on the major powers."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those are the world''s major powers."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "and no nation should be required to assume the burden of enforcing a decision it found repugnant.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "No nation should be required to assume a burden."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The burden is enforcing a decision."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The nation would find that decision repugnant."}]}], "the_read": "What if nations that were not major powers at the end of WWII later became major powers?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q17", "qnum": 17, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 17, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q18", "prompt": "Environmental scientist: It is true that over the past ten years, there has been a sixfold increase in government funding for the preservation of wetlands, while the total area of wetlands needing such preservation has increased only twofold (although this area was already large ten years ago). Even when inflation is taken into account, the amount of funding now is at least three times what it was ten years ago. Nevertheless, the current amount of government funding for the preservation of wetlands is inadequate and should be augmented.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) An environmental scientist is speaking. · This much is granted as true. · The period is the past ten years. · There has been an increase in funding. · The increase is sixfold. · The funding comes from government. · The funding is for preserving wetlands. (2) This is set against the funding increase. · There is a total area of wetlands. · Those wetlands need such preservation. · That area has increased twofold. · The increase is only twofold. · The area was already large ten years ago. (3) Inflation can be taken into account. · This holds even then. · There is an amount of funding now. · It is compared with the amount ten years ago. · It is at least three times that. (4) This is a contrast with what came before. · There is a current amount of funding. · The funding is from government. · The funding is for preserving wetlands. · That amount is inadequate. · It should be augmented.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Environmental scientist: It is true that over the past ten years, there has been a sixfold increase in government funding for the preservation of wetlands,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "An environmental scientist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This much is granted as true."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The period is the past ten years."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There has been an increase in funding."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The increase is sixfold."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The funding comes from government."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The funding is for preserving wetlands."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "while the total area of wetlands needing such preservation has increased only twofold (although this area was already large ten years ago).", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is set against the funding increase."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a total area of wetlands."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those wetlands need such preservation."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That area has increased twofold."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The increase is only twofold."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The area was already large ten years ago."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Even when inflation is taken into account, the amount of funding now is at least three times what it was ten years ago.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Inflation can be taken into account."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This holds even then."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There is an amount of funding now."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is compared with the amount ten years ago."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is at least three times that."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Nevertheless, the current amount of government funding for the preservation of wetlands is inadequate and should be augmented.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a current amount of funding."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The funding is from government."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The funding is for preserving wetlands."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That amount is inadequate."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It should be augmented."}]}], "the_read": "What if it has become much more expensive to maintain a given area of wetlands in the past ten years? What if wetland funding ten years ago was a total joke, and even this big increase still won''t cut it?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q18", "qnum": 18, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 18, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q19", "prompt": "In Australia the population that is of driving age has grown larger over the last five years, but the annual number of traffic fatalities has declined. This leads to the conclusion that, overall, the driving-age population of Australia consists of more skillful drivers now than five years ago.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about Australia. · There is a population of driving age. · That population has grown larger. · This happened over the last five years. · There is a contrast in this sentence. · The number of traffic fatalities has declined. · That number is an annual one. (2) This is the conclusion. · It is about the driving-age population. · That population is Australia''s. · It consists of more skillful drivers now. · The comparison is with five years ago. · This is said to hold overall.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In Australia the population that is of driving age has grown larger over the last five years, but the annual number of traffic fatalities has declined.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about Australia."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a population of driving age."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That population has grown larger."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This happened over the last five years."}, {"n": 5, "text": "There is a contrast in this sentence."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The number of traffic fatalities has declined."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That number is an annual one."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This leads to the conclusion that, overall, the driving-age population of Australia consists of more skillful drivers now than five years ago.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the driving-age population."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That population is Australia''s."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It consists of more skillful drivers now."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with five years ago."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is said to hold overall."}]}], "the_read": "What if the drop in fatalities has nothing to do with skill? What if cars are made much more safely now? What if speed limits were lowered?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q19", "qnum": 19, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 19, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q20", "prompt": "Anthropological studies indicate that distinct cultures differ in their moral codes. Thus, as long as there are distinct cultures, there are no values shared across cultures.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There are studies on this. · The studies are anthropological ones. · The studies indicate something about cultures. · The cultures are distinct ones. · They differ in their moral codes. (2) This is the conclusion. · It holds under a condition. · The condition is that distinct cultures exist. · There are no shared values. · The sharing would be across cultures.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Anthropological studies indicate that distinct cultures differ in their moral codes.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There are studies on this."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The studies are anthropological ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The studies indicate something about cultures."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The cultures are distinct ones."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They differ in their moral codes."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Thus, as long as there are distinct cultures, there are no values shared across cultures.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It holds under a condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The condition is that distinct cultures exist."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There are no shared values."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The sharing would be across cultures."}]}], "the_read": "What if there are common values among many different moral codes?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q20", "qnum": 20, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 20, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q21", "prompt": "Newspaper editor: Law enforcement experts, as well as most citizens, have finally come to recognize that legal prohibitions against gambling all share a common flaw: no matter how diligent the effort, the laws are impossible to enforce. Ethical qualms notwithstanding, when a law fails to be effective, it should not be a law. That is why there should be no legal prohibition against gambling.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A newspaper editor is speaking. · Law enforcement experts have recognized something. · Most citizens have recognized it too. · They have finally come to recognize it. · There are legal prohibitions against gambling. · All of them share a flaw. · The flaw is a common one. (2) The laws are impossible to enforce. · This holds regardless of the effort. · The effort might be a diligent one. (3) Ethical qualms are set aside here. · This states a condition about a law. · The condition is that the law fails to be effective. · Then it should not be a law. (4) This is the conclusion. · It follows from what came before. · There should be no prohibition against gambling. · The prohibition would be a legal one.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Newspaper editor: Law enforcement experts, as well as most citizens, have finally come to recognize that legal prohibitions against gambling all share a common flaw:", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A newspaper editor is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Law enforcement experts have recognized something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Most citizens have recognized it too."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They have finally come to recognize it."}, {"n": 5, "text": "There are legal prohibitions against gambling."}, {"n": 6, "text": "All of them share a flaw."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The flaw is a common one."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "no matter how diligent the effort, the laws are impossible to enforce.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The laws are impossible to enforce."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This holds regardless of the effort."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The effort might be a diligent one."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Ethical qualms notwithstanding, when a law fails to be effective, it should not be a law.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Ethical qualms are set aside here."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This states a condition about a law."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The condition is that the law fails to be effective."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Then it should not be a law."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "That is why there should be no legal prohibition against gambling.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It follows from what came before."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There should be no prohibition against gambling."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The prohibition would be a legal one."}]}], "the_read": "What if unenforceable laws are still effective? What if the law''s mere existence is enough to deter people from gambling?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q21", "qnum": 21, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 21, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q22", "prompt": "Copernicus''s astronomical system is superior to Ptolemy''s and was so at the time it was proposed, even though at that time all observational evidence was equally consistent with both theories. Ptolemy believed that the stars revolved around the earth at great speeds. This struck Copernicus as unlikely; he correctly thought that a simpler theory is that the earth rotates on its axis.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Copernicus had an astronomical system. · Ptolemy had one too. · Copernicus''s system is superior. · The comparison is with Ptolemy''s system. · It was superior at the time it was proposed. (2) This is a concession. · It concerns that same time. · There was observational evidence then. · All of it was consistent with both theories. · It was equally consistent with each. (3) This is about Ptolemy''s belief. · He believed the stars revolved. · They revolved around the earth. · They did so at great speeds. (4) This struck Copernicus a certain way. · It struck him as unlikely. · He thought of another theory. · His thought was correct. · That theory is a simpler one. · The theory is that the earth rotates. · The rotation is on its axis.", "category": "Principle Conform", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Copernicus''s astronomical system is superior to Ptolemy''s and was so at the time it was proposed,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Copernicus had an astronomical system."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Ptolemy had one too."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Copernicus''s system is superior."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with Ptolemy''s system."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It was superior at the time it was proposed."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "even though at that time all observational evidence was equally consistent with both theories.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a concession."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It concerns that same time."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There was observational evidence then."}, {"n": 4, "text": "All of it was consistent with both theories."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It was equally consistent with each."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Ptolemy believed that the stars revolved around the earth at great speeds.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about Ptolemy''s belief."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He believed the stars revolved."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They revolved around the earth."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They did so at great speeds."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "This struck Copernicus as unlikely; he correctly thought that a simpler theory is that the earth rotates on its axis.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This struck Copernicus a certain way."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It struck him as unlikely."}, {"n": 3, "text": "He thought of another theory."}, {"n": 4, "text": "His thought was correct."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That theory is a simpler one."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The theory is that the earth rotates."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The rotation is on its axis."}]}], "the_read": "What if more complex theories are always better?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q22", "qnum": 22, "question_type": "Principle Conform", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 22, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q23", "prompt": "Essayist: The existence of a moral order in the universe—i.e., an order in which bad is always eventually punished and good rewarded—depends upon human souls being immortal. In some cultures this moral order is regarded as the result of a karma that controls how one is reincarnated, in others it results from the actions of a supreme being who metes out justice to people after their death. But however a moral order is represented, if human souls are immortal, then it follows that the bad will be punished.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) An essayist is speaking. · A moral order might exist in the universe. · That order is defined here. · In that order bad is punished. · The punishment always comes eventually. · In that order good is rewarded. · The existence of that order depends on something. · What it depends on is human souls being immortal. (2) This is about some cultures. · In them the moral order is regarded as a result. · The result comes from a karma. · That karma controls reincarnation. · It controls how one is reincarnated. (3) This is about other cultures. · In those cultures the order is a result of actions. · The actions are those of a supreme being. · That being metes out justice to people. · The justice comes after their death. (4) This is a contrast with what came before. · It holds however a moral order is represented. · This states a condition. · The condition is that human souls are immortal. · Then something is said to follow. · The bad will be punished.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Essayist: The existence of a moral order in the universe—i.e., an order in which bad is always eventually punished and good rewarded—depends upon human souls being immortal.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "An essayist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A moral order might exist in the universe."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That order is defined here."}, {"n": 4, "text": "In that order bad is punished."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The punishment always comes eventually."}, {"n": 6, "text": "In that order good is rewarded."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The existence of that order depends on something."}, {"n": 8, "text": "What it depends on is human souls being immortal."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "In some cultures this moral order is regarded as the result of a karma that controls how one is reincarnated,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about some cultures."}, {"n": 2, "text": "In them the moral order is regarded as a result."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The result comes from a karma."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That karma controls reincarnation."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It controls how one is reincarnated."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "in others it results from the actions of a supreme being who metes out justice to people after their death.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about other cultures."}, {"n": 2, "text": "In those cultures the order is a result of actions."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The actions are those of a supreme being."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That being metes out justice to people."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The justice comes after their death."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "But however a moral order is represented, if human souls are immortal, then it follows that the bad will be punished.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It holds however a moral order is represented."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This states a condition."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The condition is that human souls are immortal."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Then something is said to follow."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The bad will be punished."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because immortal souls are necessary for moral order, that doesn''t mean they guarantee moral order?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q23", "qnum": 23, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 23, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q24", "prompt": "No mathematical proposition can be proven true by observation. It follows that it is impossible to know any mathematical proposition to be true.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about mathematical propositions. · No such proposition can be proven true. · The proving would be by observation. (2) This is the conclusion. · It follows from what came before. · Something is said to be impossible. · It is impossible to know a proposition to be true. · This applies to any mathematical proposition.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "No mathematical proposition can be proven true by observation.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about mathematical propositions."}, {"n": 2, "text": "No such proposition can be proven true."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The proving would be by observation."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "It follows that it is impossible to know any mathematical proposition to be true.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It follows from what came before."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Something is said to be impossible."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is impossible to know a proposition to be true."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This applies to any mathematical proposition."}]}], "the_read": "What if we can know a mathematical proposition to be true without proving it through true observation?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q24", "qnum": 24, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 24, "slug": "PT101_S2_Q25", "prompt": "The publisher of a best-selling self-help book had, in some promotional material, claimed that it showed readers how to become exceptionally successful. Of course, everyone knows that no book can deliver to the many what, by definition, must remain limited to the few: exceptional success. Thus, although it is clear that the publisher knowingly made a false claim, doing so should not be considered unethical in this case.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a publisher at issue. · The publisher published a self-help book. · The book is best-selling. · The publisher made a claim. · The claim was in some promotional material. · The claim is that the book showed readers something. · What it showed is how to become exceptionally successful. (2) This is said to be known by everyone. · No book can deliver a certain thing. · The delivery would be to the many. · That thing must remain limited to the few. · It is limited by definition. · The thing is exceptional success. (3) This is the conclusion. · This part is a concession. · It is clear that the publisher made a false claim. · The publisher made it knowingly. · Making the claim should not be considered unethical. · This is so in this case.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The publisher of a best-selling self-help book had, in some promotional material, claimed that it showed readers how to become exceptionally successful.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a publisher at issue."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The publisher published a self-help book."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The book is best-selling."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The publisher made a claim."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The claim was in some promotional material."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The claim is that the book showed readers something."}, {"n": 7, "text": "What it showed is how to become exceptionally successful."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Of course, everyone knows that no book can deliver to the many what, by definition, must remain limited to the few: exceptional success.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is said to be known by everyone."}, {"n": 2, "text": "No book can deliver a certain thing."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The delivery would be to the many."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That thing must remain limited to the few."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is limited by definition."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The thing is exceptional success."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Thus, although it is clear that the publisher knowingly made a false claim, doing so should not be considered unethical in this case.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This part is a concession."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is clear that the publisher made a false claim."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The publisher made it knowingly."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Making the claim should not be considered unethical."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is so in this case."}]}], "the_read": "What if knowingly making a false claim is always unethical, whether or not people know it''s false?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT101.S2.Q25", "qnum": 25, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "A"}]}]'::jsonb,
