  (
    'read-recall-colour-pt102-s3',
    'Read, Recall, Colour · PT102 Section 3',
    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, and react to it. Once you finish the stimulus, say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, then give it a reaction. After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears, and you recall the whole argument out loud from memory.", "rules": ["Read each piece silently. Everything you say back, including your reaction and final recall, is out loud.", "Read about a sentence at a time. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said.", "Then colour it: react to that specific piece with a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling.", "Repeat these steps through the whole stimulus.", "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and stays gone. Say the entire argument back from memory, with every detail you can still hold.", "Press space again to move on. The whole section records in one take, with no pauses or breaks."], "briefing": {"lede": "Read these instructions once before your first section. After that, come back when you need a reminder.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still reach the answers remembering almost none of it.", "body": ["You can follow a stimulus sentence by sentence and understand every part. Then you reach answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and that is what this drill trains.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. Understanding the stimulus is not enough if it is gone by the time you reach the answers.", "Most everyday reading trains you to reach the end, not retain every detail. You scan names, drop connecting words, and turn a list of three things into one vague label. That works for a phone bill. It does not work for the LSAT."]}, {"heading": "How the method works", "body": ["For each piece, read it, say it back, and colour it. Once you finish the stimulus, recall the whole argument. You decide how much to read at a time, and nothing on the screen decides for you.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it", "body": "Read about a sentence silently. Give every word enough attention to hear the sentence naturally in your head. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less. If you take on too much, the beginning is usually what disappears first."}, {"label": "Tell it back", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it", "body": "React to what you just said. Give it a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling. Keep it short, but make it specific. If the same reaction could fit any sentence, it does not count."}, {"label": "Recall it", "body": "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Say the entire argument out loud from memory, with every detail you can still hold. This is what gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter", "body": ["Do not give back only the gist or what seemed important. You cannot know which details will matter until you have read the whole stimulus. Filtering as you read is how useful information disappears.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. Names, numbers, and small words carrying the load, such as all, most, only, and probably, have to come back too.", "**Being efficient does not mean leaving information out.**"]}, {"heading": "When nothing comes to mind", "body": ["You may try to colour a sentence and find that nothing comes to mind. Usually, the hidden thought is that nothing feels good enough to say.", "Do not judge the reaction before you say it. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count when they connect to what you just read.", "The reaction makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away from the text", "body": ["The text stays visible while you say each piece back. You have to look away from it yourself.", "If you read while you recall, you stop using your short-term memory and slide back into the author''s exact words. Looking away forces you to rebuild the meaning in your own words.", "This is also the version that works on paper and on test day."]}, {"heading": "Build toward twenty-five minutes", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section. It only counts when you read every stimulus, say each piece back, colour it, and recall the whole argument. Finishing early by skipping parts is not finishing early. If you receive extra time on test day, use your full allotted time here too.", "The clock counts up so you can track your pace without watching your time disappear.", "Do the method correctly before you try to do it quickly. Go slower while it is new. Speed follows once the method becomes automatic."]}], "closer": "One section keeps the method steady. Improvement starts with the second."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, tells it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives The Recall of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Recall against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q1", "prompt": "Director of Ace Manufacturing Company: Our management consultant proposes that we reassign staff so that all employees are doing both what they like to do and what they do well. This, she says, will \"increase productivity by fully exploiting our available resources.\" But Ace Manufacturing has a long-standing commitment not to exploit its workers. Therefore, implementing her recommendations would cause us to violate our own policy.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A director is speaking. · The director is at Ace Manufacturing Company. · A management consultant makes a proposal. · The consultant is theirs. · The proposal is to reassign staff. · The aim is that employees do what they like to do. · They should also be doing what they do well. · This applies to all employees. (2) This is what she says it will do. · It will increase productivity. · The way is by exploiting resources. · The resources are their available ones. · The exploiting is full. (3) This is a contrast. · Ace Manufacturing has a commitment. · The commitment is long-standing. · The commitment is not to exploit its workers. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is about implementing her recommendations. · Doing so would cause a violation. · What would be violated is their own policy.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Director of Ace Manufacturing Company: Our management consultant proposes that we reassign staff so that all employees are doing both what they like to do and what they do well.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A director is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The director is at Ace Manufacturing Company."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A management consultant makes a proposal."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The consultant is theirs."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The proposal is to reassign staff."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The aim is that employees do what they like to do."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They should also be doing what they do well."}, {"n": 8, "text": "This applies to all employees."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This, she says, will \"increase productivity by fully exploiting our available resources.\"", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is what she says it will do."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It will increase productivity."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The way is by exploiting resources."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The resources are their available ones."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The exploiting is full."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "But Ace Manufacturing has a long-standing commitment not to exploit its workers.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Ace Manufacturing has a commitment."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The commitment is long-standing."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The commitment is not to exploit its workers."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Therefore, implementing her recommendations would cause us to violate our own policy.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about implementing her recommendations."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Doing so would cause a violation."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What would be violated is their own policy."}]}], "the_read": "What if \"exploit\" is used in two different senses?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q2", "prompt": "A large number of drivers routinely violate highway speed limits. Since driving at speeds that exceed posted limits is a significant factor in most accidents, installing devices in all cars that prevent those cars from traveling faster than the speed limit would prevent most accidents.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Drivers violate speed limits. · The limits are highway speed limits. · A large number of drivers do this. · They do it routinely. (2) This is the reason given. · It is about driving above the posted limits. · That is a factor in accidents. · The factor is significant. · This holds for most accidents. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about installing devices. · The devices would go in all cars. · The devices prevent cars traveling faster than the speed limit. · Doing this would prevent accidents. · It would prevent most of them.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A large number of drivers routinely violate highway speed limits.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Drivers violate speed limits."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The limits are highway speed limits."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A large number of drivers do this."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They do it routinely."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Since driving at speeds that exceed posted limits is a significant factor in most accidents,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about driving above the posted limits."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That is a factor in accidents."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The factor is significant."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This holds for most accidents."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "installing devices in all cars that prevent those cars from traveling faster than the speed limit would prevent most accidents.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about installing devices."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The devices would go in all cars."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The devices prevent cars traveling faster than the speed limit."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Doing this would prevent accidents."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It would prevent most of them."}]}], "the_read": "What if the devices are dangerous, and would cause more accidents than speeding?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q3", "prompt": "In a recession, a decrease in consumer spending causes many businesses to lay off workers or even to close. Workers who lose their jobs in a recession usually cannot find new jobs. The result is an increase in the number of people who are jobless. Recovery from a recession is defined by an increase in consumer spending and an expansion of business activity that creates a need for additional workers. But businesspeople generally have little confidence in the economy after a recession and therefore delay hiring additional workers as long as possible.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about a recession. · Consumer spending goes down. · The decrease causes an effect on businesses. · Many businesses lay off workers. · Or they even close. (2) This is about workers who lose their jobs. · They lose them in a recession. · They cannot find new jobs. · This is usually so. (3) This is the result. · There is an increase. · What increases is the number of people who are jobless. (4) This defines recovery from a recession. · Consumer spending goes up. · There is an expansion of business activity. · The expansion creates a need. · The need is for additional workers. (5) This is a contrast. · Businesspeople have little confidence in the economy. · This is after a recession. · It is generally so. · They therefore delay hiring additional workers. · They delay as long as possible.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In a recession, a decrease in consumer spending causes many businesses to lay off workers or even to close.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a recession."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Consumer spending goes down."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The decrease causes an effect on businesses."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Many businesses lay off workers."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Or they even close."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Workers who lose their jobs in a recession usually cannot find new jobs.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about workers who lose their jobs."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They lose them in a recession."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They cannot find new jobs."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is usually so."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The result is an increase in the number of people who are jobless.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the result."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is an increase."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What increases is the number of people who are jobless."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Recovery from a recession is defined by an increase in consumer spending and an expansion of business activity that creates a need for additional workers.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This defines recovery from a recession."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Consumer spending goes up."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There is an expansion of business activity."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The expansion creates a need."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The need is for additional workers."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "But businesspeople generally have little confidence in the economy after a recession and therefore delay hiring additional workers as long as possible.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Businesspeople have little confidence in the economy."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is after a recession."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is generally so."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They therefore delay hiring additional workers."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They delay as long as possible."}]}], "the_read": "Recovery from a recession may be delayed.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q4", "prompt": "Scientists analyzing air bubbles that had been trapped in Antarctic ice during the Earth''s last ice age found that the ice-age atmosphere had contained unusually large amounts of ferrous material and surprisingly small amounts of carbon dioxide. One scientist noted that algae absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere. The scientist hypothesized that the ferrous material, which was contained in atmospheric dust, had promoted a great increase in the population of Antarctic algae such as diatoms.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Scientists analyzed air bubbles. · The bubbles were trapped in Antarctic ice. · They were trapped during the Earth''s last ice age. · The scientists found something about the ice-age atmosphere. · It contained large amounts of ferrous material. · The amounts were unusually large. · It contained small amounts of carbon dioxide. · The small amounts were surprising. (2) One scientist noted something. · It is about algae. · Algae absorb carbon dioxide. · They absorb it from the atmosphere. (3) The scientist made a hypothesis. · It is about the ferrous material. · The material was contained in atmospheric dust. · The material promoted an increase. · The increase was great. · What increased was the population of Antarctic algae. · Diatoms are given as an example.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Scientists analyzing air bubbles that had been trapped in Antarctic ice during the Earth''s last ice age found that the ice-age atmosphere had contained unusually large amounts of ferrous material and surprisingly small amounts of carbon dioxide.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Scientists analyzed air bubbles."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The bubbles were trapped in Antarctic ice."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They were trapped during the Earth''s last ice age."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The scientists found something about the ice-age atmosphere."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It contained large amounts of ferrous material."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The amounts were unusually large."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It contained small amounts of carbon dioxide."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The small amounts were surprising."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "One scientist noted that algae absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "One scientist noted something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about algae."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Algae absorb carbon dioxide."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They absorb it from the atmosphere."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The scientist hypothesized that the ferrous material, which was contained in atmospheric dust, had promoted a great increase in the population of Antarctic algae such as diatoms.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The scientist made a hypothesis."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the ferrous material."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The material was contained in atmospheric dust."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The material promoted an increase."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The increase was great."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What increased was the population of Antarctic algae."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Diatoms are given as an example."}]}], "the_read": "What if we have evidence that there was little or no algae at this time? What if something else is responsible for the low levels of carbon dioxide?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q5", "prompt": "Adults who work outside the home spend, on average, 100 minutes less time each week in preparing dinner than adults who do not work outside the home. But, contrary to expectation, comparisons show that the dinners eaten at home by the two groups of adults do not differ significantly with respect to nutritional value, variety of menus, or number of courses.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about adults who work outside the home. · They spend less time preparing dinner. · The amount less is 100 minutes. · The period is each week. · This is an average. · The comparison is with adults who do not work outside the home. (2) This is a contrast. · The result runs contrary to expectation. · The dinners of the two groups are compared. · The dinners are eaten at home. · They do not differ significantly. · One respect is nutritional value. · Another is variety of menus. · Another is the number of courses.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Adults who work outside the home spend, on average, 100 minutes less time each week in preparing dinner than adults who do not work outside the home.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about adults who work outside the home."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They spend less time preparing dinner."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The amount less is 100 minutes."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The period is each week."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is an average."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with adults who do not work outside the home."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But, contrary to expectation, comparisons show that the dinners eaten at home by the two groups of adults do not differ significantly with respect to nutritional value, variety of menus, or number of courses.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The result runs contrary to expectation."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The dinners of the two groups are compared."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The dinners are eaten at home."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They do not differ significantly."}, {"n": 6, "text": "One respect is nutritional value."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Another is variety of menus."}, {"n": 8, "text": "Another is the number of courses."}]}], "the_read": "What if adults who work outside the home are eating out more?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q6", "prompt": "Legislator: Your agency is responsible for regulating an industry shaken by severe scandals. You were given funds to hire 500 investigators to examine the scandals, but you hired no more than 400. I am forced to conclude that you purposely limited hiring in an attempt to prevent the full extent of the scandals from being revealed.\n\nRegulator: We tried to hire the 500 investigators but the starting salaries for these positions had been frozen so low by the legislature that it was impossible to attract enough qualified applicants.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A legislator is speaking. · The legislator addresses an agency. · The agency is responsible for regulating an industry. · The industry has been shaken by scandals. · The scandals are severe. (2) The agency was given funds. · The funds were to hire investigators. · The number funded was 500. · The investigators were to examine the scandals. · This is a contrast. · The agency hired no more than 400. (3) This is the legislator''s conclusion. · The legislator claims to be forced to it. · The agency limited hiring. · The limiting was purposeful. · It was an attempt to prevent something. · What would be prevented is the full extent of the scandals being revealed. (4) A regulator answers the legislator. · They tried to hire the 500 investigators. · This is a contrast with that attempt. · The starting salaries had been frozen. · The legislature froze them. · They were frozen very low. · It was impossible to attract enough qualified applicants.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Legislator: Your agency is responsible for regulating an industry shaken by severe scandals.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A legislator is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The legislator addresses an agency."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The agency is responsible for regulating an industry."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The industry has been shaken by scandals."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The scandals are severe."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "You were given funds to hire 500 investigators to examine the scandals, but you hired no more than 400.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The agency was given funds."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The funds were to hire investigators."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The number funded was 500."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The investigators were to examine the scandals."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The agency hired no more than 400."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "I am forced to conclude that you purposely limited hiring in an attempt to prevent the full extent of the scandals from being revealed.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the legislator''s conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The legislator claims to be forced to it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The agency limited hiring."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The limiting was purposeful."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It was an attempt to prevent something."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What would be prevented is the full extent of the scandals being revealed."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Regulator: We tried to hire the 500 investigators but the starting salaries for these positions had been frozen so low by the legislature that it was impossible to attract enough qualified applicants.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A regulator answers the legislator."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They tried to hire the 500 investigators."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is a contrast with that attempt."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The starting salaries had been frozen."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The legislature froze them."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They were frozen very low."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It was impossible to attract enough qualified applicants."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over the reason why the regulator did not hire 500 investigators.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q7", "prompt": "A commonly accepted myth is that left-handed people are more prone to cause accidents than are right-handed people. But this is, in fact, just a myth, as is indicated by the fact that more household accidents are caused by right-handed people than are caused by left-handed people.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a myth. · The myth is commonly accepted. · It is about left-handed people. · They are said to be more prone to cause accidents. · The comparison is with right-handed people. (2) This is a contrast. · The claim is just a myth. · Something is offered as indicating this. · More household accidents are caused by right-handed people. · The comparison is with accidents caused by left-handed people.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A commonly accepted myth is that left-handed people are more prone to cause accidents than are right-handed people.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a myth."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The myth is commonly accepted."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about left-handed people."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are said to be more prone to cause accidents."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with right-handed people."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But this is, in fact, just a myth, as is indicated by the fact that more household accidents are caused by right-handed people than are caused by left-handed people.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The claim is just a myth."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Something is offered as indicating this."}, {"n": 4, "text": "More household accidents are caused by right-handed people."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with accidents caused by left-handed people."}]}], "the_read": "What if there are far more right handed people in the world than lefties?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q8", "prompt": "Ornithologist: The curvature of the claws of modern tree-dwelling birds enables them to perch in trees. The claws of Archeopteryx, the earliest known birdlike creature, show similar curvature that must have enabled the creature to perch on tree limbs. Therefore, Archeopteryx was probably a tree-dwelling creature.\n\nPaleontologist: No, the ability to perch in trees is not good evidence that Archeopteryx was a tree-dwelling bird. Chickens also spend time perched in trees, yet chickens are primarily ground-dwelling.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) An ornithologist is speaking. · It is about the claws of modern birds. · Those birds are tree-dwelling. · The claws have a curvature. · The curvature enables them to perch in trees. (2) This is about the claws of Archeopteryx. · Archeopteryx is the earliest known birdlike creature. · Its claws show a similar curvature. · The curvature must have enabled it to perch. · It would perch on tree limbs. (3) This is the conclusion. · Archeopteryx was a tree-dwelling creature. · This is hedged as probable. (4) A paleontologist answers the ornithologist. · The answer is no. · It is about the ability to perch in trees. · That ability is not good evidence. · What it would be evidence for is Archeopteryx being a tree-dwelling bird. (5) Chickens spend time perched in trees. · They do this too. · This is a contrast. · Chickens are ground-dwelling. · They are primarily so.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Ornithologist: The curvature of the claws of modern tree-dwelling birds enables them to perch in trees.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "An ornithologist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the claws of modern birds."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those birds are tree-dwelling."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The claws have a curvature."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The curvature enables them to perch in trees."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The claws of Archeopteryx, the earliest known birdlike creature, show similar curvature that must have enabled the creature to perch on tree limbs.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the claws of Archeopteryx."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Archeopteryx is the earliest known birdlike creature."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Its claws show a similar curvature."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The curvature must have enabled it to perch."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It would perch on tree limbs."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, Archeopteryx was probably a tree-dwelling creature.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Archeopteryx was a tree-dwelling creature."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is hedged as probable."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Paleontologist: No, the ability to perch in trees is not good evidence that Archeopteryx was a tree-dwelling bird.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A paleontologist answers the ornithologist."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The answer is no."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about the ability to perch in trees."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That ability is not good evidence."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What it would be evidence for is Archeopteryx being a tree-dwelling bird."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Chickens also spend time perched in trees, yet chickens are primarily ground-dwelling.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Chickens spend time perched in trees."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They do this too."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Chickens are ground-dwelling."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They are primarily so."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether the archeopteryx''s claw shape is good evidence that it was tree dwelling.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q8", "qnum": 8, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "C"}, {"citation": "PT102.S3.Q9", "qnum": 9, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 9, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q10", "prompt": "There are rumors that the Premier will reshuffle the cabinet this week. However, every previous reshuffle that the Premier has made was preceded by meetings between the Premier and senior cabinet members. No such meetings have occurred or are planned. Therefore the rumors are most likely false.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulat