mors are false."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is hedged as most likely."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because meetings preceded reshuffling in the past, that doesn''t mean they have to precede reshuffling in the future?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Principle Conform", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 10, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q11", "prompt": "Carl: Researchers who perform operations on animals for experimental purposes are legally required to complete detailed pain protocols indicating whether the animals will be at risk of pain and, if so, what steps will be taken to minimize or alleviate it. Yet when human beings undergo operations, such protocols are never required. If lawmakers were as concerned about human beings as they seem to be about animals, there would be pain protocols for human beings too.\n\nDebbie: But consider this: a person for whom a doctor wants to schedule surgery can simply be told what pain to expect and can then decide whether or not to undergo the operation. So you see, pain protocols are unnecessary for human beings.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Carl is the first speaker. · It is about researchers who operate on animals. · The operations are for experimental purposes. · Those researchers are legally required to do something. · They must complete pain protocols. · The protocols are detailed. · The protocols indicate whether the animals will be at risk of pain. (2) This is conditional on the animals being at risk. · The protocol also indicates steps. · The steps would be taken to minimize pain. · They could instead alleviate it. (3) This is a contrast. · It is about human beings undergoing operations. · Such protocols are not required. · They are never required. (4) This is a conditional claim. · It is about lawmakers'' concern for human beings. · The comparison is with how concerned they seem about animals. · There would then be pain protocols for human beings. · There would be those as well as for animals. (5) Debbie is the second speaker. · This is a contrast with Carl''s point. · It is about a person whose doctor wants to schedule surgery. · That person can be told what pain to expect. · Being told is simple. · The person can then decide. · The decision is whether or not to undergo the operation. (6) This is Debbie''s conclusion. · It is about pain protocols. · Those protocols are unnecessary. · They are unnecessary for human beings.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Carl: Researchers who perform operations on animals for experimental purposes are legally required to complete detailed pain protocols indicating whether the animals will be at risk of pain and,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Carl is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about researchers who operate on animals."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The operations are for experimental purposes."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those researchers are legally required to do something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They must complete pain protocols."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The protocols are detailed."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The protocols indicate whether the animals will be at risk of pain."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "if so, what steps will be taken to minimize or alleviate it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is conditional on the animals being at risk."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The protocol also indicates steps."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The steps would be taken to minimize pain."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They could instead alleviate it."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Yet when human beings undergo operations, such protocols are never required.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about human beings undergoing operations."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Such protocols are not required."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are never required."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "If lawmakers were as concerned about human beings as they seem to be about animals, there would be pain protocols for human beings too.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a conditional claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about lawmakers'' concern for human beings."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The comparison is with how concerned they seem about animals."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There would then be pain protocols for human beings."}, {"n": 5, "text": "There would be those as well as for animals."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Debbie: But consider this: a person for whom a doctor wants to schedule surgery can simply be told what pain to expect and can then decide whether or not to undergo the operation.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Debbie is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is a contrast with Carl''s point."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about a person whose doctor wants to schedule surgery."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That person can be told what pain to expect."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Being told is simple."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The person can then decide."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The decision is whether or not to undergo the operation."}]}, {"n": 6, "text": "So you see, pain protocols are unnecessary for human beings.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is Debbie''s conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about pain protocols."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those protocols are unnecessary."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are unnecessary for human beings."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether the lack of pain protocols for humans indicates that lawmakers do not care as much about humans as they seem to care about animals.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q11", "qnum": 11, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "B"}, {"citation": "PT102.S3.Q12", "qnum": 12, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 11, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q13", "prompt": "A company with long-outstanding bills owed by its customers can assign those bills to a collection agency that pays the company a fraction of their amount and then tries to collect payment from the customers. Since these agencies pay companies only 15 percent of the total amount of the outstanding bills, a company interested in reducing losses from long-outstanding bills would be well advised to pursue its debtors on its own.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about a company with outstanding bills. · The bills have been outstanding a long time. · The bills are owed by its customers. · The company can assign those bills. · They are assigned to a collection agency. · The agency pays the company a fraction of the amount. · The agency then tries to collect from the customers. (2) This is the reason given. · The agencies pay companies a percentage. · The figure is 15 percent. · The word only marks it as small. · The percentage is of the total amount of the outstanding bills. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about a company interested in reducing losses. · The losses come from long-outstanding bills. · Such a company would be well advised to pursue its debtors. · It should pursue them on its own.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A company with long-outstanding bills owed by its customers can assign those bills to a collection agency that pays the company a fraction of their amount and then tries to collect payment from the customers.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a company with outstanding bills."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The bills have been outstanding a long time."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The bills are owed by its customers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The company can assign those bills."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They are assigned to a collection agency."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The agency pays the company a fraction of the amount."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The agency then tries to collect from the customers."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Since these agencies pay companies only 15 percent of the total amount of the outstanding bills,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The agencies pay companies a percentage."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The figure is 15 percent."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The word only marks it as small."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The percentage is of the total amount of the outstanding bills."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "a company interested in reducing losses from long-outstanding bills would be well advised to pursue its debtors on its own.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about a company interested in reducing losses."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The losses come from long-outstanding bills."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Such a company would be well advised to pursue its debtors."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It should pursue them on its own."}]}], "the_read": "What if it''s much more expensive to pursue debtors on your own? What if at least with collections there''s a guarantee you''ll get some money?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q14", "prompt": "Herbalist: Many of my customers find that their physical coordination improves after drinking juice containing certain herbs. A few doctors assert that the herbs are potentially harmful, but doctors are always trying to maintain a monopoly over medical therapies. So there is no reason not to try my herb juice.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A herbalist is speaking. · Many of the herbalist''s customers find something. · Their physical coordination improves. · The improvement follows drinking juice. · The juice contains certain herbs. (2) Some doctors make an assertion. · Only a few do. · They say the herbs are potentially harmful. · This is a contrast. · Doctors try to maintain a monopoly. · The monopoly is over medical therapies. · They always do this. (3) This is the conclusion. · There is no reason against it. · What there is no reason against is trying the herbalist''s herb juice.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Herbalist: Many of my customers find that their physical coordination improves after drinking juice containing certain herbs.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A herbalist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Many of the herbalist''s customers find something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Their physical coordination improves."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The improvement follows drinking juice."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The juice contains certain herbs."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "A few doctors assert that the herbs are potentially harmful, but doctors are always trying to maintain a monopoly over medical therapies.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some doctors make an assertion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Only a few do."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They say the herbs are potentially harmful."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Doctors try to maintain a monopoly."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The monopoly is over medical therapies."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They always do this."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "So there is no reason not to try my herb juice.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is no reason against it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What there is no reason against is trying the herbalist''s herb juice."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because doctors want a monopoly that doesn''t mean they''re wrong about herb danger?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 13, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q15", "prompt": "Because of the lucrative but illegal trade in rhinoceros horns, a certain rhinoceros species has been hunted nearly to extinction. Therefore an effective way to ensure the survival of that species would be to periodically trim off the horns of all rhinoceroses, thereby eliminating the motivation for poaching.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is the reason given. · There is a trade in rhinoceros horns. · The trade is lucrative. · The trade is illegal. · A rhinoceros species has been hunted. · It has been hunted nearly to extinction. (2) This is the conclusion. · There would be an effective way to ensure the species survives. · The way is to trim off the horns. · The trimming would be periodic. · It would be done to all rhinoceroses. · Doing so would eliminate the motivation for poaching.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Because of the lucrative but illegal trade in rhinoceros horns, a certain rhinoceros species has been hunted nearly to extinction.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a trade in rhinoceros horns."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The trade is lucrative."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The trade is illegal."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A rhinoceros species has been hunted."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It has been hunted nearly to extinction."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Therefore an effective way to ensure the survival of that species would be to periodically trim off the horns of all rhinoceroses, thereby eliminating the motivation for poaching.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There would be an effective way to ensure the species survives."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The way is to trim off the horns."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The trimming would be periodic."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It would be done to all rhinoceroses."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Doing so would eliminate the motivation for poaching."}]}], "the_read": "What if rhinos can''t survive without their horns?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT102.S3.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 14, "slug": "PT102_S3_Q16", "prompt": "Motorcoach driver: Professional drivers spend much more time driving, on average, than do other people and hence are more competent drivers than are other, less experienced drivers. Therefore, the speed limit on major highways should not be reduced, because that action would have the undesirable effect of forcing some people who are now both law-abiding and competent drivers to break the law.\n\nPolice officer: All drivers can drive within the legal speed limit if they wish, so it is not true to say that reducing the speed limit would be the cause of such illegal behavior.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A motorcoach driver is speaking. · It is about professional drivers. · They spend more time driving. · The difference is large. · This is an average. (2) The comparison is with other people. · A conclusion is drawn from that. · Professional drivers are more competent. · The second comparison is with other drivers. · Those other drivers are less experienced. (3) This is the driver''s conclusion. · It is about the speed limit. · The limit is on major highways. · The limit should not be reduced. (4) This is the driver''s reason. · Reducing the limit would have an effect. · The effect is undesirable. · It would force some people to break the law. · Those people are law-abiding now. · They are also competent drivers. (5) A police officer answers. · All drivers can drive within the legal speed limit. · They can do so if they wish. · A conclusion is drawn from that ability. · It is not true that reducing the limit would cause the behavior. · The behavior meant is the illegal one.", "category": "Controversy", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Motorcoach driver: Professional drivers spend much more time driving, on average,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A motorcoach driver is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about professional drivers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They spend more time 