  (
    'read-recall-colour-pt103-s1',
    'Read, Recall, Colour · PT103 Section 1',
    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, and react to it. Once you finish the stimulus, say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, then give it a reaction. After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears, and you recall the whole argument out loud from memory.", "rules": ["Read each piece silently. Everything you say back, including your reaction and final recall, is out loud.", "Read about a sentence at a time. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said.", "Then colour it: react to that specific piece with a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling.", "Repeat these steps through the whole stimulus.", "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and stays gone. Say the entire argument back from memory, with every detail you can still hold.", "Press space again to move on. The whole section records in one take, with no pauses or breaks."], "briefing": {"lede": "Read these instructions once before your first section. After that, come back when you need a reminder.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still reach the answers remembering almost none of it.", "body": ["You can follow a stimulus sentence by sentence and understand every part. Then you reach answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and that is what this drill trains.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. Understanding the stimulus is not enough if it is gone by the time you reach the answers.", "Most everyday reading trains you to reach the end, not retain every detail. You scan names, drop connecting words, and turn a list of three things into one vague label. That works for a phone bill. It does not work for the LSAT."]}, {"heading": "How the method works", "body": ["For each piece, read it, say it back, and colour it. Once you finish the stimulus, recall the whole argument. You decide how much to read at a time, and nothing on the screen decides for you.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it", "body": "Read about a sentence silently. Give every word enough attention to hear the sentence naturally in your head. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less. If you take on too much, the beginning is usually what disappears first."}, {"label": "Tell it back", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it", "body": "React to what you just said. Give it a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling. Keep it short, but make it specific. If the same reaction could fit any sentence, it does not count."}, {"label": "Recall it", "body": "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Say the entire argument out loud from memory, with every detail you can still hold. This is what gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter", "body": ["Do not give back only the gist or what seemed important. You cannot know which details will matter until you have read the whole stimulus. Filtering as you read is how useful information disappears.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. Names, numbers, and small words carrying the load, such as all, most, only, and probably, have to come back too.", "**Being efficient does not mean leaving information out.**"]}, {"heading": "When nothing comes to mind", "body": ["You may try to colour a sentence and find that nothing comes to mind. Usually, the hidden thought is that nothing feels good enough to say.", "Do not judge the reaction before you say it. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count when they connect to what you just read.", "The reaction makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away from the text", "body": ["The text stays visible while you say each piece back. You have to look away from it yourself.", "If you read while you recall, you stop using your short-term memory and slide back into the author''s exact words. Looking away forces you to rebuild the meaning in your own words.", "This is also the version that works on paper and on test day."]}, {"heading": "Build toward twenty-five minutes", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section. It only counts when you read every stimulus, say each piece back, colour it, and recall the whole argument. Finishing early by skipping parts is not finishing early. If you receive extra time on test day, use your full allotted time here too.", "The clock counts up so you can track your pace without watching your time disappear.", "Do the method correctly before you try to do it quickly. Go slower while it is new. Speed follows once the method becomes automatic."]}], "closer": "One section keeps the method steady. Improvement starts with the second."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, tells it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives The Recall of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Recall against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q1", "prompt": "Three-year-old Sara and her playmate Michael are both ill and have the same symptoms. Since they play together every afternoon, Sara probably has the same illness as Michael does. Since Michael definitely does not have a streptococcal infection, despite his having some symptoms of one, the illness that Sara has is definitely not a streptococcal infection either.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Sara is three years old. · Michael is Sara''s playmate. · Sara and Michael are both ill. · The two have the same symptoms. (2) This gives a reason. · The reason is that the two play together. · They play together every afternoon. · Sara has the same illness as Michael. · This is stated as only probable. (3) This gives another reason. · Michael does not have a streptococcal infection. · That is definitely so. · He does have some symptoms of one. · The word despite marks a concession. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is about the illness Sara has. · Her illness is not a streptococcal infection. · That is definitely so. · The word either ties this to Michael.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Three-year-old Sara and her playmate Michael are both ill and have the same symptoms.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Sara is three years old."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Michael is Sara''s playmate."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Sara and Michael are both ill."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The two have the same symptoms."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Since they play together every afternoon, Sara probably has the same illness as Michael does.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The reason is that the two play together."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They play together every afternoon."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Sara has the same illness as Michael."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is stated as only probable."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Since Michael definitely does not have a streptococcal infection, despite his having some symptoms of one,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives another reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Michael does not have a streptococcal infection."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That is definitely so."}, {"n": 4, "text": "He does have some symptoms of one."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The word despite marks a concession."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "the illness that Sara has is definitely not a streptococcal infection either.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the illness Sara has."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Her illness is not a streptococcal infection."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That is definitely so."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The word either ties this to Michael."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because they have the same symptoms that doesn''t mean they have the same illness? What if Sara has two illnesses?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q2", "prompt": "Lambert: The proposal to raise gasoline taxes to support mass transit networks is unfair. Why should drivers who will never use train or bus lines be forced to pay for them?\n\nKeziah: You have misunderstood. The government has always spent far more, per user, from general revenue sources to fund highways than to fund mass transit. The additional revenue from the gasoline tax will simply allow the government to make its distribution of transportation funds more equitable.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Lambert is the first speaker. · There is a proposal at issue. · The proposal is to raise gasoline taxes. · The taxes would support mass transit networks. · Lambert says the proposal is unfair. (2) This is asked as a question. · It is about certain drivers. · Those drivers will never use train lines. · They will never use bus lines either. · The question is why they should be forced to pay. · The paying would be for those lines. (3) Keziah is the second speaker. · She responds to what Lambert said. · She says he has misunderstood. (4) This is about government spending. · The government has spent more on highways. · It has spent far more. · The comparison is with funding for mass transit. · The spending is counted per user. · The money comes from general revenue sources. · This has always been so. (5) The gasoline tax would bring additional revenue. · That revenue would allow the government to act. · The government would change how it distributes transportation funds. · The distribution would become more equitable. · The word simply downplays what the tax does.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Lambert: The proposal to raise gasoline taxes to support mass transit networks is unfair.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Lambert is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a proposal at issue."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The proposal is to raise gasoline taxes."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The taxes would support mass transit networks."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Lambert says the proposal is unfair."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Why should drivers who will never use train or bus lines be forced to pay for them?", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is asked as a question."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about certain drivers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those drivers will never use train lines."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They will never use bus lines either."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The question is why they should be forced to pay."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The paying would be for those lines."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Keziah: You have misunderstood.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Keziah is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "She responds to what Lambert said."}, {"n": 3, "text": "She says he has misunderstood."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "The government has always spent far more, per user, from general revenue sources to fund highways than to fund mass transit.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about government spending."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The government has spent more on highways."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It has spent far more."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with funding for mass transit."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The spending is counted per user."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The money comes from general revenue sources."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This has always been so."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "The additional revenue from the gasoline tax will simply allow the government to make its distribution of transportation funds more equitable.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The gasoline tax would bring additional revenue."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That revenue would allow the government to act."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The government would change how it distributes transportation funds."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The distribution would become more equitable."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The word simply downplays what the tax does."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether raising gas taxes to support public transit is unfair.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q3", "prompt": "The number of calories in a gram of refined cane sugar is the same as in an equal amount of fructose, the natural sugar found in fruits and vegetables. Therefore, a piece of candy made with a given amount of refined cane sugar is no higher in calories than a piece of fruit that contains an equal amount of fructose.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about a number of calories. · A gram of refined cane sugar is at issue. · That number is the same for fructose. · The amount of fructose is equal. · Fructose is a natural sugar. · Fructose is found in fruits and vegetables. (2) This is the conclusion. · It is about a piece of candy. · The candy is made with refined cane sugar. · The amount of that sugar is a given one. · The candy is no higher in calories. · The comparison is with a piece of fruit. · That fruit contains an equal amount of fructose.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The number of calories in a gram of refined cane sugar is the same as in an equal amount of fructose, the natural sugar found in fruits and vegetables.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a number of calories."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A gram of refined cane sugar is at issue."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That number is the same for fructose."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The amount of fructose is equal."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Fructose is a natural sugar."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Fructose is found in fruits and vegetables."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Therefore, a piece of candy made with a given amount of refined cane sugar is no higher in calories than a piece of fruit that contains an equal amount of fructose.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about a piece of candy."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The candy is made with refined cane sugar."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The amount of that sugar is a given one."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The candy is no higher in calories."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with a piece of fruit."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That fruit contains an equal amount of fructose."}]}], "the_read": "What if all the other ingredients in candy have way higher calorie counts than the other stuff in fruit?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q4", "prompt": "In order to increase production, ABC Company should implement a flextime schedule, which would allow individual employees some flexibility in deciding when to begin and end their workday. Studies have shown that working under flextime schedules is associated with increased employee morale.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is a recommendation. · The recommendation is for ABC Company. · ABC Company should implement a flextime schedule. · The purpose is to increase production. (2) This is about the flextime schedule. · The schedule would allow employees flexibility. · The employees are individual ones. · The flexibility is only some. · They would decide when to begin their workday. · They would decide when to end it. (3) Studies have shown something. · The studies are about working under flextime schedules. · That work is associated with employee morale. · The morale is increased. · The link is an association.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In order to increase production, ABC Company should implement a flextime schedule,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a recommendation."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The recommendation is for ABC Company."}, {"n": 3, "text": "ABC Company should implement a flextime schedule."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The purpose is to increase production."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "which would allow individual employees some flexibility in deciding when to begin and end their workday.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the flextime schedule."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The schedule would allow employees flexibility."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The employees are individual ones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The flexibility is only some."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They would decide when to begin their workday."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They would decide when to end it."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Studies have shown that working under flextime schedules is associated with increased employee morale.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Studies have shown something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The studies are about working under flextime schedules."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That work is associated with employee morale."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The morale is increased."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The link is an association."}]}], "the_read": "What if increased morale doesn''t lead to increased productivity?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q5", "prompt": "Attorneys for a criminal defendant charged that the government, in a coverup, had destroyed evidence that would have supported the defendant in a case. The government replied that there is no evidence that would even tend to support the defendant in the case.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Attorneys made a charge. · The attorneys act for a criminal defendant. · The charge is against the government. · The government had destroyed evidence. · The destroying was part of a coverup. · The evidence would have supported the defendant. · The support would have come in a case. (2) The government made a reply. · The reply says there is no such evidence. · No evidence would tend to support the defendant. · The word even strengthens that denial. · The case is the one at issue.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Attorneys for a criminal defendant charged that the government, in a coverup, had destroyed evidence that would have supported the defendant in a case.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Attorneys made a charge."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The attorneys act for a criminal defendant."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The charge is against the government."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The government had destroyed evidence."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The destroying was part of a coverup."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The evidence would have supported the defendant."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The support would have come in a case."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The government replied that there is no evidence that would even tend to support the defendant in the case.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The government made a reply."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The reply says there is no such evidence."}, {"n": 3, "text": "No evidence would tend to support the defendant."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The word even strengthens that denial."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The case is the one at issue."}]}], "the_read": "What if that doesn''t mean they didn''t destroy evidence?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q6", "prompt": "Videocassette recorders (VCRs) enable people to watch movies at home on videotape. People who own VCRs go to movie theaters more often than do people who do not own VCRs. Contrary to popular belief, therefore, owning a VCR actually stimulates people to go to movie theaters more often than they otherwise would.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Videocassette recorders are called VCRs. · They enable people to watch movies. · The watching happens at home. · The movies are on videotape. (2) This is about people who own VCRs. · Those people go to movie theaters. · They go more often. · The comparison is with people who do not own VCRs. (3) This is the conclusion. · It runs contrary to popular belief. · Owning a VCR stimulates people. · They are stimulated to go to movie theaters. · They go more often. · The comparison is with how often they otherwise would.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Videocassette recorders (VCRs) enable people to watch movies at home on videotape.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Videocassette recorders are called VCRs."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They enable people to watch movies."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The watching happens at home."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The movies are on videotape."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "People who own VCRs go to movie theaters more often than do people who do not own VCRs.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about people who own VCRs."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those people go to movie theaters."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They go more often."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with people who do not own VCRs."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Contrary to popular belief, therefore, owning a VCR actually stimulates people to go to movie theaters more often than they otherwise would.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It runs contrary to popular belief."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Owning a VCR stimulates people."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are stimulated to go to movie theaters."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They go more often."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with how often they otherwise would."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s no causal relationship between VCRs and going to the movies? What if these people always went to the movies more often, and that''s why they bought VCRs? What if these people are addicted to synthetic butter popcorn and that''s why they go to the movies a lot?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q7", "prompt": "The cumbersome spears that were the principal weapons used by certain tribes in the early Bronze Age precluded widespread casualties during intertribal conflicts. But the comparatively high number of warrior tombs found in recent excavations of the same tribes'' late Bronze Age settlements indicates that in the late Bronze Age, wars between these tribes were frequent, and the casualty rate was high. Hence some archaeologists claim that by the late Bronze Age, these tribes had developed new methods of warfare designed to inflict many casualties.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about spears. · The spears were cumbersome. · They were the principal weapons of certain tribes. · This was in the early Bronze Age. · The spears precluded widespread casualties. · Those casualties would have come in intertribal conflicts. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · It is about a number of warrior tombs. · That number is comparatively high. · The tombs were found in recent excavations. · The excavations were of the same tribes'' settlements. · Those settlements are from the late Bronze Age. · The tombs indicate something about the late Bronze Age. (3) This is what the tombs indicate. · Wars happened between these tribes. · The wars were frequent. · The casualty rate was high. (4) This is drawn from what came before. · Some archaeologists make a claim. · The claim is about these tribes. · They had developed new methods of warfare. · This was so by the late Bronze Age. · The methods were designed to inflict casualties. · The casualties would be many.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The cumbersome spears that were the principal weapons used by certain tribes in the early Bronze Age precluded widespread casualties during intertribal conflicts.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about spears."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The spears were cumbersome."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They were the principal weapons of certain tribes."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This was in the early Bronze Age."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The spears precluded widespread casualties."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those casualties would have come in intertribal conflicts."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But the comparatively high number of warrior tombs found in recent excavations of the same tribes'' late Bronze Age settlements indicates that in the late Bronze Age,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about a number of warrior tombs."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That number is comparatively high."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The tombs were found in recent excavations."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The excavations were of the same tribes'' settlements."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those settlements are from the late Bronze Age."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The tombs indicate something about the late Bronze Age."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "wars between these tribes were frequent, and the casualty rate was high.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is what the tombs indicate."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Wars happened between these tribes."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The wars were frequent."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The casualty rate was high."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Hence some archaeologists claim that by the late Bronze Age, these tribes had developed new methods of warfare designed to inflict many casualties.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is drawn from what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some archaeologists make a claim."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The claim is about these tribes."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They had developed new methods of warfare."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This was so by the late Bronze Age."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The methods were designed to inflict casualties."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The casualties would be many."}]}], "the_read": "What if the warriors in the tombs died by some means other than mass-casualty weapons? What if there was a pandemic or famine during this time?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q8", "prompt": "Based on data collected from policyholders, life insurance companies have developed tables that list standard weight ranges for various heights. Policyholders whose weight fell within the range given for their height lived longer than those whose weight fell outside their given range. Therefore, if people whose weight falls outside their given range modified their weight to fall within that range, their overall life expectancies would improve.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Life insurance companies have developed tables. · The tables list weight ranges. · Those ranges are standard ones. · The ranges are given for various heights. · The tables are based on data. · The data was collected from policyholders. (2) This is about policyholders. · Some had weight within the range for their height. · Those policyholders lived longer. · The comparison is with policyholders outside their given range. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is a conditional claim. · Some people have weight outside their given range. · Those people might modify their weight. · The modified weight would fall within that range. · Their life expectancies would improve. · The expectancies are overall ones.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Based on data collected from policyholders, life insurance companies have developed tables that list standard weight ranges for various heights.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Life insurance companies have developed tables."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The tables list weight ranges."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those ranges are standard ones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The ranges are given for various heights."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The tables are based on data."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The data was collected from policyholders."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Policyholders whose weight fell within the range given for their height lived longer than those whose weight fell outside their given range.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about policyholders."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some had weight within the range for their height."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those policyholders lived longer."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with policyholders outside their given range."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, if people whose weight falls outside their given range modified their weight to fall within that range, their overall life expectancies would improve.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is a conditional claim."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Some people have weight outside their given range."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those people might modify their weight."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The modified weight would fall within that range."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Their life expectancies would improve."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The expectancies are overall ones."}]}], "the_read": "What if people outside of the weight range losing weight would have a detrimental effect on their health?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q8", "qnum": 8, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 9, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q9", "prompt": "Measurements of the motion of the planet Uranus seem to show Uranus being tugged by a force pulling it away from the Sun and the inner planets. Neptune and Pluto, the two known planets whose orbits are farther from the Sun than is the orbit of Uranus, do not have enough mass to exert the force that the measurements indicate. Therefore, in addition to the known planets, there must be at least one planet in our solar system that we have yet to discover.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There are measurements of motion. · The motion is that of the planet Uranus. · The measurements seem to show something. · Uranus is being tugged by a force. · The force pulls it away from the Sun. · It also pulls Uranus away from the inner planets. (2) This is about Neptune and Pluto. · They are two known planets. · Their orbits are farther from the Sun. · The comparison is with the orbit of Uranus. (3) This is still about Neptune and Pluto. · They do not have enough mass. · The mass would be needed to exert a force. · That force is the one the measurements indicate. (4) This is the conclusion. · There must be another planet. · That planet is in our solar system. · There is at least one such planet. · It comes in addition to the known planets. · We have yet to discover it.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Measurements of the motion of the planet Uranus seem to show Uranus being tugged by a force pulling it away from the Sun and the inner planets.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There are measurements of motion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The motion is that of the planet Uranus."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The measurements seem to show something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Uranus is being tugged by a force."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The force pulls it away from the Sun."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It also pulls Uranus away from the inner planets."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Neptune and Pluto, the two known planets whose orbits are farther from the Sun than is the orbit of Uranus,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about Neptune and Pluto."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are two known planets."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Their orbits are farther from the Sun."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with the orbit of Uranus."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "do not have enough mass to exert the force that the measurements indicate.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is still about Neptune and Pluto."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They do not have enough mass."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The mass would be needed to exert a force."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That force is the one the measurements indicate."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Therefore, in addition to the known planets, there must be at least one planet in our solar system that we have yet to discover.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There must be another planet."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That planet is in our solar system."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There is at least one such planet."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It comes in addition to the known planets."}, {"n": 6, "text": "We have yet to discover it."}]}], "the_read": "What if Uranus is being pulled by a non-planetary force?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q9", "qnum": 9, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 10, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q10", "prompt": "Audiences find a speaker more convincing if the speaker begins a speech by arguing briefly against his or her position before providing reasons for accepting it. The reason this technique is so effective is that it makes the speaker appear fair-minded and trustworthy. Therefore, candidates for national political office who wish to be successful in winning votes should use this argumentative technique in their speeches.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about audiences. · Audiences find a speaker more convincing. · This holds under a condition. · The speaker begins a speech by arguing against his own position. · That arguing against it is brief. · It comes before the reasons for accepting the position. (2) This gives the reason for the effect. · The technique is very effective. · It makes the speaker appear fair-minded. · It also makes the speaker appear trustworthy. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about candidates for office. · The office is national political office. · Those candidates wish to be successful in winning votes. · They should use this argumentative technique. · They should use it in their speeches.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Audiences find a speaker more convincing if the speaker begins a speech by arguing briefly against his or her position before providing reasons for accepting it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about audiences."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Audiences find a speaker more convincing."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This holds under a condition."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The speaker begins a speech by arguing against his own position."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That arguing against it is brief."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It comes before the reasons for accepting the position."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The reason this technique is so effective is that it makes the speaker appear fair-minded and trustworthy.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives the reason for the effect."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The technique is very effective."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It makes the speaker appear fair-minded."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It also makes the speaker appear trustworthy."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, candidates for national political office who wish to be successful in winning votes should use this argumentative technique in their speeches.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about candidates for office."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The office is national political office."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those candidates wish to be successful in winning votes."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They should use this argumentative technique."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They should use it in their speeches."}]}], "the_read": "What if appearing fair-minded and trustworthy has nothing to do with winning political votes?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 11, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q11", "prompt": "Five thousand of the 50,000 books published in country Z in 1991 were novels. Exactly 25 of the films released in country Z in 1992 were based on those novels. Since 100 films were released in country Z in 1992, no more than one-quarter of them were based on books published in country Z in 1991.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Fifty thousand books were published. · The publishing happened in country Z. · It happened in 1991. · Five thousand of those books were novels. (2) This is about films. · The films were released in country Z. · They were released in 1992. · Exactly 25 of them were based on those novels. (3) This is the conclusion. · It rests on a given. · One hundred films were released in 1992. · Those releases were in country Z. · No more than one-quarter of them were based on books. · Those books were published in country Z in 1991.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Five thousand of the 50,000 books published in country Z in 1991 were novels.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Fifty thousand books were published."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The publishing happened in country Z."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It happened in 1991."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Five thousand of those books were novels."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Exactly 25 of the films released in country Z in 1992 were based on those novels.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about films."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The films were released in country Z."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They were released in 1992."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Exactly 25 of them were based on those novels."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Since 100 films were released in country Z in 1992, no more than one-quarter of them were based on books published in country Z in 1991.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on a given."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One hundred films were released in 1992."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those releases were in country Z."}, {"n": 5, "text": "No more than one-quarter of them were based on books."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those books were published in country Z in 1991."}]}], "the_read": "What if some of the movies were based on nonfiction books published in Z in 1991?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q11", "qnum": 11, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q12", "prompt": "On their way from their nest to a food source, ants of most species leave a trail of chemicals called pheromones. The ants use the scent of the pheromones to guide themselves between the food and their nest. All pheromones evaporate without a trace almost immediately when temperatures rise above 45 degrees Celsius (113 degrees Fahrenheit), as is typical during afternoons in places such as the Sahara Desert.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about ants. · It applies to ants of most species. · The ants leave a trail of chemicals. · Those chemicals are called pheromones. · The trail is left on the way from their nest. · The ants are heading to a food source. (2) This is about the ants again. · They use the scent of the pheromones. · The scent guides them. · They guide themselves between the food and their nest. (3) This is about pheromones. · It applies to all of them. · They evaporate without a trace. · They do so almost immediately. · This happens when temperatures rise above a point. · That point is 45 degrees Celsius. · It is also given as 113 degrees Fahrenheit. (4) Those temperatures are typical somewhere. · They are typical during afternoons. · The places include the Sahara Desert. · The Sahara Desert is given as an example.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "On their way from their nest to a food source, ants of most species leave a trail of chemicals called pheromones.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about ants."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It applies to ants of most species."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The ants leave a trail of chemicals."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those chemicals are called pheromones."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The trail is left on the way from their nest."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The ants are heading to a food source."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The ants use the scent of the pheromones to guide themselves between the food and their nest.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the ants again."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They use the scent of the pheromones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The scent guides them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They guide themselves between the food and their nest."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "All pheromones evaporate without a trace almost immediately when temperatures rise above 45 degrees Celsius (113 degrees Fahrenheit),", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about pheromones."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It applies to all of them."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They evaporate without a trace."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They do so almost immediately."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This happens when temperatures rise above a point."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That point is 45 degrees Celsius."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It is also given as 113 degrees Fahrenheit."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "as is typical during afternoons in places such as the Sahara Desert.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Those temperatures are typical somewhere."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are typical during afternoons."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The places include the Sahara Desert."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The Sahara Desert is given as an example."}]}], "the_read": "Ants may not be able to use their trails to guide them back to their nests in the afternoon in the Sahara Desert.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q12", "qnum": 12, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 13, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q13", "prompt": "Some people think that in every barrel of politicians there are only a few rotten ones. But if deceit is a quality of rottenness, I believe all effective politicians are rotten. They must be deceitful in order to do the job properly. Someone who is scrupulously honest about obeying the rules of society will never be an effective politician.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Some people think something. · Their thought is about barrels of politicians. · This applies to every such barrel. · Only a few politicians in it are rotten. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · The speaker states a belief. · The belief rests on a condition. · The condition is that deceit is a quality of rottenness. · All effective politicians are rotten. (3) This is about effective politicians. · They must be deceitful. · The deceit is needed to do the job. · Doing the job properly is what requires it. (4) This is about a certain person. · That person is honest about obeying rules. · The honesty is scrupulous. · The rules are those of society. · Such a person will never be an effective politician.", "category": "Contradiction", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some people think that in every barrel of politicians there are only a few rotten ones.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some people think something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Their thought is about barrels of politicians."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This applies to every such barrel."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Only a few politicians in it are rotten."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But if deceit is a quality of rottenness, I believe all effective politicians are rotten.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The speaker states a belief."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The belief rests on a condition."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The condition is that deceit is a quality of rottenness."}, {"n": 5, "text": "All effective politicians are rotten."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "They must be deceitful in order to do the job properly.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about effective politicians."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They must be deceitful."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The deceit is needed to do the job."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Doing the job properly is what requires it."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Someone who is scrupulously honest about obeying the rules of society will never be an effective politician.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a certain person."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That person is honest about obeying rules."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The honesty is scrupulous."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The rules are those of society."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Such a person will never be an effective politician."}]}], "the_read": "What if doing the job properly is not the same thing as being an effective politician?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Contradiction", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 14, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q14", "prompt": "The Biocarb Company wants to build a sterilization plant to treat contaminated medical waste in a city neighborhood where residents and environmental activists fear that such a facility will pollute the area. Biocarb''s president argues that the operation of the plant cannot cause pollution because the waste would be sterile after processing by the plant. He claims that after exposure for an hour to superheated steam in the autoclave, such refuse would be far cleaner than food prepared in the cleanest kitchen.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The Biocarb Company wants to build a plant. · The plant would sterilize things. · It would treat medical waste. · That waste is contaminated. · The plant would go in a city neighborhood. · Residents there fear the facility. · Environmental activists fear it too. · The fear is that it will pollute the area. (2) Biocarb''s president makes an argument. · The argument is that the plant cannot cause pollution. · What cannot pollute is the operation of the plant. · A reason is given for that. · The waste would be sterile. · It would be sterile after the plant processes it. (3) The president makes a further claim. · The refuse would be exposed to steam. · The steam is superheated. · The exposure lasts an hour. · The exposure happens in the autoclave. · The refuse would then be far cleaner. · The comparison is with food prepared in the cleanest kitchen.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The Biocarb Company wants to build a sterilization plant to treat contaminated medical waste in a city neighborhood where residents and environmental activists fear that such a facility will pollute the area.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The Biocarb Company wants to build a plant."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The plant would sterilize things."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It would treat medical waste."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That waste is contaminated."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The plant would go in a city neighborhood."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Residents there fear the facility."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Environmental activists fear it too."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The fear is that it will pollute the area."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Biocarb''s president argues that the operation of the plant cannot cause pollution because the waste would be sterile after processing by the plant.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Biocarb''s president makes an argument."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The argument is that the plant cannot cause pollution."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What cannot pollute is the operation of the plant."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A reason is given for that."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The waste would be sterile."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It would be sterile after the plant processes it."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "He claims that after exposure for an hour to superheated steam in the autoclave, such refuse would be far cleaner than food prepared in the cleanest kitchen.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The president makes a further claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The refuse would be exposed to steam."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The steam is superheated."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The exposure lasts an hour."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The exposure happens in the autoclave."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The refuse would then be far cleaner."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The comparison is with food prepared in the cleanest kitchen."}]}], "the_read": "What if the act of sterilizing the waste causes pollution?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT103.S1.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT103_S1_Q15", "prompt": "Grow-Again ointment is a proven treatment for reversing male hereditary baldness. Five drops daily is the recommended dose, and exceeding this quantity does not increase the product''s effectiveness. Therefore, offering a manufacturer''s rebate on the purchase price of Grow-Again will not increase sales and consequently would be unprofitable for the manufacturer.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Grow-Again is an ointment. · It is a treatment. · The treatment is proven. · It reverses a kind of baldness. · The baldness is male and hereditary. (2) The recommended dose is five drops. · The dose is taken daily. · Someone might exceed that quantity. · Exceeding it does not increase effectiveness. · The effectiveness is the product''s. (3) This is the conclusion. · A rebate might be offered. · The rebate would come from the manufacturer. · It would apply to the purchase price of Grow-Again. · The rebate will not increase sales. · It would consequently be unprofitable. · The manufacturer is the one who would lose.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Grow-Again ointment is a proven treatment for reversing male hereditary baldness.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Grow-Again is an ointment."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is a treatment."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The treatment is proven."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It reverses a kind of baldness."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The baldness is male and hereditary."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Five drops daily is the recommended dose, and exceeding this quantity does not increase the product''s effectiveness.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The recommended dose is five drops."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The dose is taken daily."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Someone might exceed that quantity."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Exceeding it does not increase effectiveness."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The effectiveness is the product''s."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, offering a manufacturer''s rebate on the purchase price of Grow-Again will not increase sales and consequently would be unprofitable for the manufacturer.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A rebate might be offered."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The rebate would come from the manufacturer."}, {"n": 4, "tex