  ),
  (
    'read-recall-colour-pt104-s1',
    'Read, Recall, Colour · PT104 Section 1',
    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, and react to it. Once you finish the stimulus, say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, then give it a reaction. After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears, and you recall the whole argument out loud from memory.", "rules": ["Read each piece silently. Everything you say back, including your reaction and final recall, is out loud.", "Read about a sentence at a time. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said.", "Then colour it: react to that specific piece with a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling.", "Repeat these steps through the whole stimulus.", "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and stays gone. Say the entire argument back from memory, with every detail you can still hold.", "Press space again to move on. The whole section records in one take, with no pauses or breaks."], "briefing": {"lede": "Read these instructions once before your first section. After that, come back when you need a reminder.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still reach the answers remembering almost none of it.", "body": ["You can follow a stimulus sentence by sentence and understand every part. Then you reach answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and that is what this drill trains.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. Understanding the stimulus is not enough if it is gone by the time you reach the answers.", "Most everyday reading trains you to reach the end, not retain every detail. You scan names, drop connecting words, and turn a list of three things into one vague label. That works for a phone bill. It does not work for the LSAT."]}, {"heading": "How the method works", "body": ["For each piece, read it, say it back, and colour it. Once you finish the stimulus, recall the whole argument. You decide how much to read at a time, and nothing on the screen decides for you.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it", "body": "Read about a sentence silently. Give every word enough attention to hear the sentence naturally in your head. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less. If you take on too much, the beginning is usually what disappears first."}, {"label": "Tell it back", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it", "body": "React to what you just said. Give it a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling. Keep it short, but make it specific. If the same reaction could fit any sentence, it does not count."}, {"label": "Recall it", "body": "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Say the entire argument out loud from memory, with every detail you can still hold. This is what gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter", "body": ["Do not give back only the gist or what seemed important. You cannot know which details will matter until you have read the whole stimulus. Filtering as you read is how useful information disappears.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. Names, numbers, and small words carrying the load, such as all, most, only, and probably, have to come back too.", "**Being efficient does not mean leaving information out.**"]}, {"heading": "When nothing comes to mind", "body": ["You may try to colour a sentence and find that nothing comes to mind. Usually, the hidden thought is that nothing feels good enough to say.", "Do not judge the reaction before you say it. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count when they connect to what you just read.", "The reaction makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away from the text", "body": ["The text stays visible while you say each piece back. You have to look away from it yourself.", "If you read while you recall, you stop using your short-term memory and slide back into the author''s exact words. Looking away forces you to rebuild the meaning in your own words.", "This is also the version that works on paper and on test day."]}, {"heading": "Build toward twenty-five minutes", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section. It only counts when you read every stimulus, say each piece back, colour it, and recall the whole argument. Finishing early by skipping parts is not finishing early. If you receive extra time on test day, use your full allotted time here too.", "The clock counts up so you can track your pace without watching your time disappear.", "Do the method correctly before you try to do it quickly. Go slower while it is new. Speed follows once the method becomes automatic."]}], "closer": "One section keeps the method steady. Improvement starts with the second."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, tells it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives The Recall of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Recall against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q1", "prompt": "Powell: Private waste-removal companies spend 60 percent of what public waste-removal companies spend per customer, yet give their customers at least as good service. Private waste-removal companies, therefore, work more efficiently.\n\nFreeman: Your conclusion is unwarranted. Different customers have different waste-removal needs. Since private companies, unlike their public counterparts, can select which customers to serve, they choose to exclude the potential customers whom they judge to be the most costly to serve.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Powell is the first speaker. · Private waste-removal companies spend a fraction of what public ones spend. · That fraction is 60 percent. · The spending is measured per customer. · This is a contrast. · Private companies give service at least as good. · The service goes to their own customers. (2) This is the conclusion. · It is about private waste-removal companies. · Those companies work more efficiently. (3) Freeman is the second speaker. · Freeman replies to Powell''s conclusion. · Freeman says that conclusion is unwarranted. (4) This is about customers. · Customers are different from one another. · Their needs are different too. · The needs in question are waste-removal needs. (5) This part gives a reason. · It is about private companies. · Private companies can select which customers to serve. · Their public counterparts cannot select. (6) This is about the private companies. · They choose to exclude certain potential customers. · The excluded ones are judged most costly to serve. · The companies make that judgment themselves.", "category": "Controversy", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Powell: Private waste-removal companies spend 60 percent of what public waste-removal companies spend per customer, yet give their customers at least as good service.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Powell is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Private waste-removal companies spend a fraction of what public ones spend."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That fraction is 60 percent."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The spending is measured per customer."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Private companies give service at least as good."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The service goes to their own customers."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Private waste-removal companies, therefore, work more efficiently.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about private waste-removal companies."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those companies work more efficiently."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Freeman: Your conclusion is unwarranted.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Freeman is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Freeman replies to Powell''s conclusion."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Freeman says that conclusion is unwarranted."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Different customers have different waste-removal needs.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about customers."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Customers are different from one another."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Their needs are different too."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The needs in question are waste-removal needs."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Since private companies, unlike their public counterparts, can select which customers to serve,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This part gives a reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about private companies."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Private companies can select which customers to serve."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Their public counterparts cannot select."}]}, {"n": 6, "text": "they choose to exclude the potential customers whom they judge to be the most costly to serve.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the private companies."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They choose to exclude certain potential customers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The excluded ones are judged most costly to serve."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The companies make that judgment themselves."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over why private waste-removal companies are able to offer comparable service at a lower price when compared to public companies.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Controversy", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q2", "prompt": "Although 90 percent of the population believes itself to be well informed about health care, only 20 percent knows enough about DNA to understand a news story about DNA. So apparently at least 80 percent of the population does not know enough about medical concepts to make well-informed personal medical choices or to make good public policy decisions about health care.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is a concession. · Most of the population believes it is well informed. · That share is 90 percent. · What they feel informed about is health care. · A smaller share knows enough about DNA. · That share is only 20 percent. · Knowing that much would let them understand a news story. · The news story is about DNA. (2) This is the conclusion. · The conclusion is hedged with apparently. · Part of the population does not know enough about medical concepts. · That part is at least 80 percent. · They could not make well-informed personal medical choices. · They could not make good public policy decisions. · Those decisions are about health care.", "category": "Gap Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Although 90 percent of the population believes itself to be well informed about health care, only 20 percent knows enough about DNA to understand a news story about DNA.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a concession."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Most of the population believes it is well informed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That share is 90 percent."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What they feel informed about is health care."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A smaller share knows enough about DNA."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That share is only 20 percent."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Knowing that much would let them understand a news story."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The news story is about DNA."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "So apparently at least 80 percent of the population does not know enough about medical concepts to make well-informed personal medical choices or to make good public policy decisions about health care.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The conclusion is hedged with apparently."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Part of the population does not know enough about medical concepts."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That part is at least 80 percent."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They could not make well-informed personal medical choices."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They could not make good public policy decisions."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those decisions are about health care."}]}], "the_read": "What if people don''t need to know anything about DNA in order to make well-informed personal medical choices or health policy?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Gap Flaw", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q3", "prompt": "In Yasukawa''s month-long study of blackbirds, the percentage of smaller birds that survived the duration of the study exceeded the percentage of larger birds that survived. However, Yasukawa''s conclusion that size is a determinant of a blackbird''s chances of survival over a month-long period is probably mistaken, since smaller blackbirds are generally younger than larger ones.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Yasukawa conducted a study. · The study was of blackbirds. · The study lasted a month. · A percentage of smaller birds survived the study. · A percentage of larger birds survived as well. · The percentage for the smaller birds was higher. (2) This is a contrast. · Yasukawa drew a conclusion. · The conclusion was that size is a determinant. · What size would determine is a blackbird''s chances of survival. · The survival is over a month-long period. · That conclusion is probably mistaken. (3) This gives a reason. · It is about smaller blackbirds. · Smaller blackbirds are younger than larger ones. · This is generally the case.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In Yasukawa''s month-long study of blackbirds, the percentage of smaller birds that survived the duration of the study exceeded the percentage of larger birds that survived.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Yasukawa conducted a study."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The study was of blackbirds."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The study lasted a month."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A percentage of smaller birds survived the study."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A percentage of larger birds survived as well."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The percentage for the smaller birds was higher."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "However, Yasukawa''s conclusion that size is a determinant of a blackbird''s chances of survival over a month-long period is probably mistaken,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Yasukawa drew a conclusion."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The conclusion was that size is a determinant."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What size would determine is a blackbird''s chances of survival."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The survival is over a month-long period."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That conclusion is probably mistaken."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "since smaller blackbirds are generally younger than larger ones.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about smaller blackbirds."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Smaller blackbirds are younger than larger ones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is generally the case."}]}], "the_read": "What if Yasukawa only studied birds that were the same age?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "D"}, {"citation": "PT104.S1.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q5", "prompt": "During the 1980s, Japanese collectors were very active in the market for European art, especially as purchasers of nineteenth-century Impressionist paintings. This striking pattern surely reflects a specific preference on the part of many Japanese collectors for certain aesthetic attributes they found in nineteenth-century Impressionist paintings.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about the 1980s. · Japanese collectors were active in a market. · They were very active in it. · The market was for European art. · They were especially active as purchasers. · What they purchased was Impressionist paintings. · Those paintings were nineteenth-century ones. (2) This is the conclusion. · The pattern just described is striking. · The pattern reflects a preference. · The claim is made with certainty. · The preference is a specific one. · Many Japanese collectors hold that preference. · The preference is for certain aesthetic attributes. · They found those attributes in nineteenth-century Impressionist paintings.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "During the 1980s, Japanese collectors were very active in the market for European art, especially as purchasers of nineteenth-century Impressionist paintings.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the 1980s."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Japanese collectors were active in a market."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They were very active in it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The market was for European art."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They were especially active as purchasers."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What they purchased was Impressionist paintings."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those paintings were nineteenth-century ones."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This striking pattern surely reflects a specific preference on the part of many Japanese collectors for certain aesthetic attributes they found in nineteenth-century Impressionist paintings.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The pattern just described is striking."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The pattern reflects a preference."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The claim is made with certainty."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The preference is a specific one."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Many Japanese collectors hold that preference."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The preference is for certain aesthetic attributes."}, {"n": 8, "text": "They found those attributes in nineteenth-century Impressionist paintings."}]}], "the_read": "What if Japanese collectors bought nineteenth-century Impressionist paintings for some reason other than aesthetic pleasure?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q6", "prompt": "Frankie: If jelly makers were given incentives to make a certain percentage of their jellies from cloudberries, income for cloudberry gatherers would increase.\n\nAnna: That plan would fail. Cacao, like cloudberries, was once harvested from wild plants. When chocolate became popular in Europe, the cacao gatherers could not supply enough to meet the increased demand, and farmers began to grow large quantities of it at low cost. Now all cacao used in commercial chocolate production is grown on farms. Likewise, if the demand for cloudberries increases, domesticated berries grown on farms will completely supplant berries gathered in the wild.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Frankie is the first speaker. · This is a conditional proposal. · Jelly makers would be given incentives. · The incentives concern a certain percentage of their jellies. · Those jellies would be made from cloudberries. · Income would increase under that condition. · The income belongs to cloudberry gatherers. (2) Anna is the second speaker. · Anna refers to Frankie''s plan. · Anna says the plan would fail. (3) This is about cacao. · Cacao was harvested from wild plants. · That was once the case. · Cloudberries are like cacao in this respect. (4) This is about a time in Europe. · Chocolate became popular there. · The cacao gatherers could not supply enough. · What they could not meet was the increased demand. · Farmers began to grow cacao. · They grew large quantities of it. · They grew it at low cost. (5) This is about the present. · Cacao is grown on farms. · All of that cacao is grown there. · The cacao is used in chocolate production. · That production is commercial. (6) This is a parallel case. · The demand for cloudberries might increase. · Domesticated berries would supplant other berries. · The domesticated berries are grown on farms. · The supplanting would be complete. · The other berries are gathered in the wild.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Frankie: If jelly makers were given incentives to make a certain percentage of their jellies from cloudberries, income for cloudberry gatherers would increase.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Frankie is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is a conditional proposal."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Jelly makers would be given incentives."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The incentives concern a certain percentage of their jellies."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those jellies would be made from cloudberries."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Income would increase under that condition."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The income belongs to cloudberry gatherers."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Anna: That plan would fail.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Anna is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Anna refers to Frankie''s plan."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Anna says the plan would fail."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Cacao, like cloudberries, was once harvested from wild plants.", "details": [{"n": 