would come in the next few years.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Problems caused by the leaching of pollutants from dumps and landfills are worst in countries with an annual per capita economic output of $4,000 to $5,000,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some problems are caused by leaching."}, {"n": 2, "text": "What leaches is pollutants."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The pollutants come from dumps."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They come from landfills as well."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The problems are worst in certain countries."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those countries have an economic output of $4,000 to $5,000."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That output is annual and per capita."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "and less severe for considerably poorer and considerably richer countries.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The problems are less severe elsewhere."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are less severe for poorer countries."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are less severe for richer countries too."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those countries are considerably poorer or richer."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is so because pollution problems increase during the early stages of a country''s industrial development but then diminish as increasing industrial development generates adequate resources to tackle such problems.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives the reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Pollution problems increase at first."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The increase comes during the early stages of industrial development."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The development is a country''s own."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The problems then diminish."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Industrial development keeps increasing."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That development generates adequate resources."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The resources go to tackling such problems."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Therefore, problems caused by such leaching in Country X, where the annual per capita economic output is now $5,000, should begin to diminish in the next few years.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about Country X."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Country X has an annual per capita economic output of $5,000."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That is its output now."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Problems there are caused by such leaching."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those problems should begin to diminish."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The diminishing would come in the next few years."}]}], "the_read": "What if industrial development in Country X will not increase any time soon?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q14", "prompt": "Critic: Many popular psychological theories are poor theories in that they are inelegant and do not help to dispel the mystery that surrounds our psyche. However, this is not really important. The theories produce the right results: therapeutically, they tend to have greater success than their more scientific rivals.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The critic is the speaker. · Some psychological theories are popular. · Many of those theories are poor ones. · They are poor in that they are inelegant. · They also do not dispel a mystery. · The mystery surrounds our psyche. (2) This is a contrast. · It refers to what was just said. · What was just said is not really important. (3) This is about the theories. · The theories produce the right results. · They tend to have greater success. · The success is therapeutic. · The comparison is with their rivals. · Those rivals are more scientific.", "category": "Argument Part", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Critic: Many popular psychological theories are poor theories in that they are inelegant and do not help to dispel the mystery that surrounds our psyche.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The critic is the speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some psychological theories are popular."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Many of those theories are poor ones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are poor in that they are inelegant."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They also do not dispel a mystery."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The mystery surrounds our psyche."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "However, this is not really important.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It refers to what was just said."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What was just said is not really important."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The theories produce the right results: therapeutically, they tend to have greater success than their more scientific rivals.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the theories."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The theories produce the right results."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They tend to have greater success."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The success is therapeutic."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with their rivals."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those rivals are more scientific."}]}], "the_read": "What if the quality of psychological theory is defined by its elegance and ability to dispel mysteries of the psyche, not therapeutic success?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Argument Part", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 13, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q15", "prompt": "Tony: Few anarchists have ever performed violent actions. These few are vastly outnumbered by the violent adherents of other political ideologies. Therefore, the special association in the public mind between anarchism and political violence is unwarranted.\n\nKeisha: Anarchists have always been few in number, whereas other ideologies have often spawned mass movements. Therefore, the proportion of anarchists who are violent is possibly greater than the proportion of adherents of other ideologies who are violent.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Tony is the first speaker. · This is about anarchists. · Few anarchists have ever performed violent actions. (2) This is about those few anarchists. · They are outnumbered by other people. · The outnumbering is vast. · Those others adhere to other ideologies. · Those ideologies are political ones. · Those adherents are violent. (3) This is the conclusion. · There is an association in the public mind. · The association is a special one. · It links anarchism with political violence. · That association is unwarranted. (4) Keisha is the second speaker. · Anarchists have been few in number. · That has always been so. · This is a contrast. · Other ideologies have spawned mass movements. · They have often done so. (5) This is Keisha''s conclusion. · Some proportion of anarchists is violent. · That proportion may be greater. · The comparison is with adherents of other ideologies. · What is compared is the violent proportion. · The claim is only that this is possible.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Tony: Few anarchists have ever performed violent actions.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Tony is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is about anarchists."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Few anarchists have ever performed violent actions."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "These few are vastly outnumbered by the violent adherents of other political ideologies.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about those few anarchists."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are outnumbered by other people."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The outnumbering is vast."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those others adhere to other ideologies."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those ideologies are political ones."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those adherents are violent."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, the special association in the public mind between anarchism and political violence is unwarranted.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is an association in the public mind."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The association is a special one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It links anarchism with political violence."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That association is unwarranted."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Keisha: Anarchists have always been few in number, whereas other ideologies have often spawned mass movements.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Keisha is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Anarchists have been few in number."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That has always been so."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Other ideologies have spawned mass movements."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They have often done so."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Therefore, the proportion of anarchists who are violent is possibly greater than the proportion of adherents of other ideologies who are violent.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is Keisha''s conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some proportion of anarchists is violent."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That proportion may be greater."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with adherents of other ideologies."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What is compared is the violent proportion."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The claim is only that this is possible."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether the raw number of violent anarchists is telling.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 14, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q16", "prompt": "Recent research shows that sound change (pronunciation shift) in a language is not gradual. New sounds often emerge suddenly. This confounds the classical account of sound change, whose central tenet is gradualness. Since this classical account must be discarded, sound-change theory in general must also be.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Research shows something about sound change. · The research is recent. · Sound change is not gradual. · Sound change is a shift in pronunciation. · The change happens in a language. (2) This is about new sounds. · New sounds emerge suddenly. · They often do so. (3) This is about what was just said. · It confounds an account. · The account is the classical one. · The account is of sound change. · The account has a central tenet. · That tenet is gradualness. (4) This is the conclusion. · It rests on the classical account being discarded. · Sound-change theory must also be discarded. · The theory is meant in general.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Recent research shows that sound change (pronunciation shift) in a language is not gradual.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Research shows something about sound change."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The research is recent."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Sound change is not gradual."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Sound change is a shift in pronunciation."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The change happens in a language."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "New sounds often emerge suddenly.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about new sounds."}, {"n": 2, "text": "New sounds emerge suddenly."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They often do so."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "This confounds the classical account of sound change, whose central tenet is gradualness.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about what was just said."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It confounds an account."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The account is the classical one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The account is of sound change."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The account has a central tenet."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That tenet is gradualness."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Since this classical account must be discarded, sound-change theory in general must also be.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on the classical account being discarded."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Sound-change theory must also be discarded."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The theory is meant in general."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because one part of a theory is wrong that doesn''t mean the whole theory is wrong?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q17", "prompt": "The stable functioning of a society depends upon the relatively long-term stability of the goals of its citizens. This is clear from the fact that unless the majority of individuals have a predictable and enduring set of aspirations, it will be impossible for a legislature to craft laws that will augment the satisfaction of the citizenry, and it should be obvious that a society is stable only if its laws tend to increase the happiness of its citizens.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about a society''s functioning. · The functioning in question is stable. · It depends on the stability of certain goals. · That stability is relatively long-term. · The goals belong to the society''s citizens. (2) This gives the support. · The support makes the claim clear. · It sets up a condition. · The condition is that most individuals have a set of aspirations. · The set would be predictable. · The set would be enduring as well. (3) Something will be impossible. · It will be impossible for a legislature. · The legislature would craft laws. · Those laws would augment satisfaction. · The satisfaction is the citizenry''s. (4) This is an additional point. · The point should be obvious. · A society is stable only under a condition. · The condition concerns the society''s laws. · Those laws tend to increase happiness. · The happiness is that of its citizens.", "category": "Argument Part", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The stable functioning of a society depends upon the relatively long-term stability of the goals of its citizens.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a society''s functioning."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The functioning in question is stable."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It depends on the stability of certain goals."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That stability is relatively long-term."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The goals belong to the society''s citizens."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is clear from the fact that unless the majority of individuals have a predictable and enduring set of aspirations,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives the support."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The support makes the claim clear."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It sets up a condition."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The condition is that most individuals have a set of aspirations."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The set would be predictable."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The set would be enduring as well."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "it will be impossible for a legislature to craft laws that will augment the satisfaction of the citizenry,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Something will be impossible."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It will be impossible for a legislature."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The legislature would craft laws."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those laws would augment satisfaction."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The satisfaction is the citizenry''s."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "and it should be obvious that a society is stable only if its laws tend to increase the happiness of its citizens.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The point should be obvious."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A society is stable only under a condition."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The condition concerns the society''s laws."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those laws tend to increase happiness."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The happiness is that of its citizens."}]}], "the_read": "What if a society can end up with laws that tend to increase the happiness of citizens without involving the legislature at all? What if some societies are monarchies?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q17", "qnum": 17, "question_type": "Argument Part", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q18", "prompt": "Astronauts who experience weightlessness frequently get motion sickness. The astronauts see their own motion relative to passing objects, but while the astronauts are weightless their inner ears indicate that their bodies are not moving. The astronauts'' experience is best explained by the hypothesis that conflicting information received by the brain about the body''s motion causes motion sickness.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about astronauts. · Those astronauts experience weightlessness. · They get motion sickness. · They get it frequently. (2) This is about the astronauts. · The astronauts see their own motion. · They see it relative to passing objects. · This is a contrast. · The astronauts are weightless at the time. · Their inner ears indicate something. · What the ears indicate is that their bodies are not moving. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about the astronauts'' experience. · A hypothesis explains that experience. · The hypothesis explains it best. · The brain receives information about the body''s motion. · That information is conflicting. · The conflicting information causes motion sickness.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Astronauts who experience weightlessness frequently get motion sickness.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about astronauts."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those astronauts experience weightlessness."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They get motion sickness."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They get it frequently."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The astronauts see their own motion relative to passing objects, but while the astronauts are weightless their inner ears indicate that their bodies are not moving.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the astronauts."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The astronauts see their own motion."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They see it relative to passing objects."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The astronauts are weightless at the time."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Their inner ears indicate something."}, {"n": 7, "text": "What the ears indicate is that their bodies are not moving."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The astronauts'' experience is best explained by the hypothesis that conflicting information received by the brain about the body''s motion causes motion sickness.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the astronauts'' experience."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A hypothesis explains that experience."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The hypothesis explains it best."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The brain receives information about the body''s motion."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That information is conflicting."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The conflicting information causes motion sickness."}]}], "the_read": "What if something else, like the air quality in space, gives astronauts motion sickness?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q18", "qnum": 18, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 17, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q19", "prompt": "Pollen and other allergens can cause cells in the nose to release histamine, a chemical that inflames nasal tissue and causes runny nose, congestion, and sneezing. Antihistamines minimize these allergy symptoms by blocking the action of histamine. In addition, antihistamines have other effects, including drowsiness. However, histamine plays no role in the processes by which colds produce their symptoms.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Pollen can cause a release of histamine. · Other allergens can cause it too. · The release happens in cells in the nose. · Histamine is a chemical. · Histamine inflames nasal tissue. · It causes a runny nose. · It also causes congestion. · It causes sneezing as well. (2) This is about antihistamines. · Antihistamines minimize those symptoms. · The symptoms are allergy symptoms. · They work by blocking an action. · The action blocked is histamine''s. (3) This is an additional point. · Antihistamines have other effects. · Drowsiness is one of them. (4) This is a contrast. · It is about histamine. · Histamine plays no role in certain processes. · Those processes are how colds produce symptoms. · The symptoms are the colds'' own.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Pollen and other allergens can cause cells in the nose to release histamine, a chemical that inflames nasal tissue and causes runny nose, congestion, and sneezing.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Pollen can cause a release of histamine."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Other allergens can cause it too."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The release happens in cells in the nose."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Histamine is a chemical."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Histamine inflames nasal tissue."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It causes a runny nose."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It also causes congestion."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It causes sneezing as well."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Antihistamines minimize these allergy symptoms by blocking the action of histamine.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about antihistamines."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Antihistamines minimize those symptoms."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The symptoms are allergy symptoms."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They work by blocking an action."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The action blocked is histamine''s."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "In addition, antihistamines have other effects, including drowsiness.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Antihistamines have other effects."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Drowsiness is one of them."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "However, histamine plays no role in the processes by which colds produce their symptoms.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about histamine."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Histamine plays no role in certain processes."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those processes are how colds produce symptoms."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The symptoms are the colds'' own."}]}], "the_read": "If antihistamines only reduce symptoms by blocking histamine action, they do not help relieve cold symptoms.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q19", "qnum": 19, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 18, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q20", "prompt": "A poem is any work of art that exploits some of the musical characteristics of language, such as meter, rhythm, euphony, and rhyme. A novel, though it may be a work of art in language, does not usually exploit the musical characteristics of language. A symphony, though it may be a work of art that exploits the musical characteristics of sounds, rarely involves language. A limerick, though it may exploit some musical characteristics of language, is not, strictly speaking, art.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A poem is a work of art. · Any such work counts as a poem. · The work exploits musical characteristics. · It exploits only some of them. · The characteristics belong to language. · Meter and rhythm are examples. · Euphony is another example. · Rhyme is another example. (2) This is about a novel. · A novel may be a work of art. · That art would be in language. · This is a concession. · A novel does not exploit the musical characteristics of language. · That is usually the case. (3) This is about a symphony. · A symphony may be a work of art. · It may exploit the musical characteristics of sounds. · This is a concession. · A symphony rarely involves language. (4) This is about a limerick. · A limerick may exploit musical characteristics. · It exploits only some of them. · Those characteristics belong to language. · This is a concession. · A limerick is not art. · That holds strictly speaking.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A poem is any work of art that exploits some of the musical characteristics of language, such as meter, rhythm, euphony, and rhyme.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A poem is a work of art."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Any such work counts as a poem."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The work exploits musical characteristics."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It exploits only some of them."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The characteristics belong to language."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Meter and rhythm are examples."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Euphony is another example."}, {"n": 8, "text": "Rhyme is another example."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "A novel, though it may be a work of art in language, does not usually exploit the musical characteristics of language.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a novel."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A novel may be a work of art."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That art would be in language."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a concession."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A novel does not exploit the musical characteristics of language."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That is usually the case."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "A symphony, though it may be a work of art that exploits the musical characteristics of sounds, rarely involves language.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a symphony."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A symphony may be a work of art."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It may exploit the musical characteristics of sounds."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a concession."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A symphony rarely involves language."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "A limerick, though it may exploit some musical characteristics of language, is not, strictly speaking, art.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a limerick."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A limerick may exploit musical characteristics."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It exploits only some of them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those characteristics belong to language."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is a concession."}, {"n": 6, "text": "A limerick is not art."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That holds strictly speaking."}]}], "the_read": "Works that exploit the musical characteristics of language are not necessarily art.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q20", "qnum": 20, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 19, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q21", "prompt": "In order to pressure the government of Country S to become less repressive, some legislators in Country R want to ban all exports from R to S. Companies in R that manufacture telecommunication equipment such as telephones and fax machines have argued that exports of their products should be exempted from the ban, on the grounds that it is impossible for a country to remain repressive when telecommunication equipment is widely available to the population of that country.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This states a purpose. · The purpose is to pressure a government. · The government is Country S''s. · The government would become less repressive. · Some legislators want a ban. · Those legislators are in Country R. · The ban would cover all exports from R to S. (2) Certain companies are in Country R. · Those companies manufacture telecommunication equipment. · Telephones and fax machines are given as examples. · The companies have argued for an exemption. · The exemption would cover exports of their products. · The exemption would be from the ban. (3) This gives their grounds. · Something is impossible for a country. · What is impossible is remaining repressive. · This holds under a condition. · The condition is that telecommunication equipment is available. · The equipment is widely available. · It is available to that country''s population.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In order to pressure the government of Country S to become less repressive, some legislators in Country R want to ban all exports from R to S.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This states a purpose."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The purpose is to pressure a government."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The government is Country S''s."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The government would become less repressive."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Some legislators want a ban."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those legislators are in Country R."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The ban would cover all exports from R to S."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Companies in R that manufacture telecommunication equipment such as telephones and fax machines have argued that exports of their products should be exempted from the ban,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Certain companies are in Country R."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those companies manufacture telecommunication equipment."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Telephones and fax machines are given as examples."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The companies have argued for an exemption."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The exemption would cover exports of their products."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The exemption would be from the ban."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "on the grounds that it is impossible for a country to remain repressive when telecommunication equipment is widely available to the population of that country.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives their grounds."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Something is impossible for a country."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What is impossible is remaining repressive."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This holds under a condition."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The condition is that telecommunication equipment is available."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The equipment is widely available."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It is available to that country''s population."}]}], "the_read": "What if allowing exports of a product to a country doesn''t mean the product will be widely available in the country?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q21", "qnum": 21, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 20, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q22", "prompt": "Some people believe that saying that an organization is hierarchical says everything there is to say about how that organization operates. All bureaucratically controlled organizations are hierarchical. Yet the Public Works Department, although bureaucratically controlled, operates quite differently than most other bureaucratically controlled organizations operate.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Some people hold a belief. · The belief is about calling an organization hierarchical. · Saying that would say everything about the organization. · What it would cover is how the organization operates. (2) This is about certain organizations. · Those organizations are bureaucratically controlled. · All of them are hierarchical. (3) This is a contrast. · It is about the Public Works Department. · The department is bureaucratically controlled. · The department operates differently. · The difference is considerable. · The comparison is with most other such organizations. · Those organizations are bureaucratically controlled too.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some people believe that saying that an organization is hierarchical says everything there is to say about how that organization operates.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some people hold a belief."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The belief is about calling an organization hierarchical."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Saying that would say everything about the organization."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What it would cover is how the organization operates."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "All bureaucratically controlled organizations are hierarchical.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about certain organizations."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those organizations are bureaucratically controlled."}, {"n": 3, "text": "All of them are hierarchical."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Yet the Public Works Department, although bureaucratically controlled, operates quite differently than most other bureaucratically controlled organizations operate.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the Public Works Department."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The department is bureaucratically controlled."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The department operates differently."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The difference is considerable."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with most other such organizations."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those organizations are bureaucratically controlled too."}]}], "the_read": "Not every heirarchical organization operates in the same way.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q22", "qnum": 22, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 21, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q23", "prompt": "Research indicates that 90 percent of extreme insomniacs consume large amounts of coffee. Since Tom drinks a lot of coffee, it is quite likely that he is an extreme insomniac.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This finding comes from research. · Most extreme insomniacs consume coffee. · That share is 90 percent. · The amounts of coffee are large. (2) This is the conclusion. · It rests on a reason about Tom. · Tom drinks a lot of coffee. · Tom is likely an extreme insomniac. · The likelihood is put as quite likely.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Research indicates that 90 percent of extreme insomniacs consume large amounts of coffee.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This finding comes from research."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Most extreme insomniacs consume coffee."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That share is 90 percent."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The amounts of coffee are large."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Since Tom drinks a lot of coffee, it is quite likely that he is an extreme insomniac.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on a reason about Tom."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Tom drinks a lot of coffee."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Tom is likely an extreme insomniac."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The likelihood is put as quite likely."}]}], "the_read": "What if we can''t use bad conditional reasoning?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q23", "qnum": 23, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 22, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q24", "prompt": "Folklorist: Oral traditions are often preferable to written ones. Exclusive dependence on speech improves the memory; literate populations grow sluggish in recall, running to written sources whenever they need information. Because writing has no limits, it can proliferate to the point where writer and reader both become confused. Since oral traditions are dependent on memory, what is useless and irrelevant is quickly eradicated.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The folklorist is the speaker. · This is the main claim. · Oral traditions are preferable. · The comparison is with written traditions. · This is often so. (2) Dependence on speech improves the memory. · That dependence is exclusive. · Literate populations grow sluggish. · What they are sluggish at is recall. · They run to written sources. · They do this whenever they need information. (3) This gives a reason. · Writing has no limits. · Writing can proliferate to a certain point. · The writer becomes confused at that point. · The reader becomes confused too. (4) This gives a reason. · Oral traditions depend on memory. · Some material is useless. · That material is irrelevant as well. · Such material is eradicated. · The eradication is quick.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Folklorist: Oral traditions are often preferable to written ones.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The folklorist is the speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is the main claim."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Oral traditions are preferable."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with written traditions."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is often so."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Exclusive dependence on speech improves the memory; literate populations grow sluggish in recall, running to written sources whenever they need information.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Dependence on speech improves the memory."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That dependence is exclusive."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Literate populations grow sluggish."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What they are sluggish at is recall."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They run to written sources."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They do this whenever they need information."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Because writing has no limits, it can proliferate to the point where writer and reader both become confused.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Writing has no limits."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Writing can proliferate to a certain point."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The writer becomes confused at that point."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The reader becomes confused too."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Since oral traditions are dependent on memory, what is useless and irrelevant is quickly eradicated.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Oral traditions depend on memory."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Some material is useless."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That material is irrelevant as well."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Such material is eradicated."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The eradication is quick."}]}], "the_read": "What if we don''t care about having better memories and getting rid of useless and irrelevant information?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q24", "qnum": 24, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 23, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q25", "prompt": "When interviewing job candidates, personnel managers not only evaluate a candidate''s work experience and educational background but also inquire about hobbies. Personnel managers try to justify these inquiries by noting that the enthusiasm someone shows for a hobby may well carry over to enthusiasm for a job. But such enthusiasm may also indicate that the candidate is less concerned with work than with play. Therefore personnel managers should not inquire about a candidate''s hobbies.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about personnel managers. · The managers interview job candidates. · They evaluate a candidate''s work experience. · They evaluate the educational background too. · They also inquire about hobbies. · The inquiry comes in addition to the evaluation. (2) The personnel managers try to justify these inquiries. · They justify them by noting a point. · Someone shows enthusiasm for a hobby. · That enthusiasm may carry over to a job. · It may well do so. (3) This is a contrast. · Such enthusiasm may indicate something else. · It may indicate the candidate is less concerned with work. · The comparison is with play. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is about personnel managers. · They should not inquire about hobbies. · The hobbies are a candidate''s own.", "category": "Gap Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "When interviewing job candidates, personnel managers not only evaluate a candidate''s work experience and educational background but also inquire about hobbies.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about personnel managers."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The managers interview job candidates."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They evaluate a candidate''s work experience."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They evaluate the educational background too."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They also inquire about hobbies."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The inquiry comes in addition to the evaluation."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Personnel managers try to justify these inquiries by noting that the enthusiasm someone shows for a hobby may well carry over to enthusiasm for a job.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The personnel managers try to justify these inquiries."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They justify them by noting a point."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Someone shows enthusiasm for a hobby."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That enthusiasm may carry over to a job."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It may well do so."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "But such enthusiasm may also indicate that the candidate is less concerned with work than with play.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Such enthusiasm may indicate something else."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It may indicate the candidate is less concerned with work."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with play."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Therefore personnel managers should not inquire about a candidate''s hobbies.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about personnel managers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They should not inquire about hobbies."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The hobbies are a candidate''s own."}]}], "the_read": "What if it''s good for personnel managers to know if a candidate is more concerned with play than with work, because it helps them weed out the unserious people?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q25", "qnum": 25, "question_type": "Gap Flaw", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 24, "slug": "PT104_S1_Q26", "prompt": "Researcher: The vast majority of a person''s dreams bear no resemblance whatsoever to real events that follow the dreams. Thus, it is unreasonable to believe that one has extrasensory perception solely on the basis of having had several vivid dreams about events that happen after the dreams.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The researcher is the speaker. · Most of a person''s dreams bear no resemblance to later events. · That majority is vast. · There is no resemblance whatsoever. · The events are real ones. · Those events follow the dreams. (2) This is the conclusion. · A belief would be unreasonable. · The belief is that one has extrasensory perception. · The basis for it would be having had dreams. · Those dreams are several and vivid. · The dreams would be about events. · Those events happen after the dreams. · That basis would be the sole one.", "category": "Parallel Reasoning", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Researcher: The vast majority of a person''s dreams bear no resemblance whatsoever to real events that follow the dreams.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The researcher is the speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Most of a person''s dreams bear no resemblance to later events."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That majority is vast."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There is no resemblance whatsoever."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The events are real ones."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those events follow the dreams."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Thus, it is unreasonable to believe that one has extrasensory perception solely on the basis of having had several vivid dreams about events that happen after the dreams.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A belief would be unreasonable."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The belief is that one has extrasensory perception."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The basis for it would be having had dreams."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those dreams are several and vivid."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The dreams would be about events."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those events happen after the dreams."}, {"n": 8, "text": "That basis would be the sole one."}]}], "the_read": "What if the few instances of dreams imitating reality are examples of ESP?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT104.S1.Q26", "qnum": 26, "question_type": "Parallel Reasoning", "correct_answer": "D"}]}]'::jsonb,
