profit even while offering low prices, because they buy goods in large quantities at favorable cost. This creates a problem for small retailers. If they try to retain their customers by lowering prices to match those of large discount chains, the result is a lower profit margin. But small retailers can retain their customer base without lowering prices if they offer exceptional service. Hence, small retailers that are forced to compete with large discount chains must offer exceptional service in order to retain their level of profitability.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Discount chains can make a profit. · Those chains are large ones. · They make a profit while offering prices. · Those prices are low. · This is the reason for it. · They buy goods in large quantities. · The cost they buy at is favorable. (2) This is about what came before it. · It creates a problem. · The problem is for retailers. · Those retailers are small ones. (3) This is a conditional statement. · Small retailers might try to retain their customers. · The way they would do that is by lowering prices. · The prices would match those of the large discount chains. · The result would be a lower profit margin. (4) This is a contrast with what came before it. · Small retailers can retain their customer base. · They can do it without lowering prices. · This holds if they offer service. · The service is exceptional. (5) This is the conclusion. · It is about small retailers. · Those retailers are forced to compete. · What they compete with is large discount chains. · They must offer exceptional service. · The purpose is to retain their profitability. · What is retained is their level of it.", "category": "Gap Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Large discount chains can make a profit even while offering low prices, because they buy goods in large quantities at favorable cost.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Discount chains can make a profit."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those chains are large ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They make a profit while offering prices."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those prices are low."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is the reason for it."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They buy goods in large quantities."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The cost they buy at is favorable."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This creates a problem for small retailers.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about what came before it."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It creates a problem."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The problem is for retailers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those retailers are small ones."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "If they try to retain their customers by lowering prices to match those of large discount chains, the result is a lower profit margin.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a conditional statement."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Small retailers might try to retain their customers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The way they would do that is by lowering prices."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The prices would match those of the large discount chains."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The result would be a lower profit margin."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "But small retailers can retain their customer base without lowering prices if they offer exceptional service.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Small retailers can retain their customer base."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They can do it without lowering prices."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This holds if they offer service."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The service is exceptional."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Hence, small retailers that are forced to compete with large discount chains must offer exceptional service in order to retain their level of profitability.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about small retailers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those retailers are forced to compete."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What they compete with is large discount chains."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They must offer exceptional service."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The purpose is to retain their profitability."}, {"n": 7, "text": "What is retained is their level of it."}]}], "the_read": "What if there are other ways to retain customers without offering exceptional service, such as cool branding or corporate values alignment?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Gap Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q6", "prompt": "We should do what will make others more virtuous and not do what will make others less virtuous. It is an irony of human existence that praise makes those who are less virtuous more virtuous, while it makes those who are more virtuous less virtuous. And, of course, none except the more virtuous deserve praise.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) We should do certain things. · What we should do is what makes others more virtuous. · We should also refrain from certain things. · What we should not do is what makes others less virtuous. (2) This is called an irony. · The irony is one of human existence. · Praise has an effect on people. · Praise makes the less virtuous more virtuous. · This is contrasted with another effect. · Praise makes the more virtuous less virtuous. (3) This is an additional point. · It is said to be obvious. · Some people deserve praise. · Only the more virtuous deserve it. · No one else deserves it.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "We should do what will make others more virtuous and not do what will make others less virtuous.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "We should do certain things."}, {"n": 2, "text": "What we should do is what makes others more virtuous."}, {"n": 3, "text": "We should also refrain from certain things."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What we should not do is what makes others less virtuous."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is an irony of human existence that praise makes those who are less virtuous more virtuous, while it makes those who are more virtuous less virtuous.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is called an irony."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The irony is one of human existence."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Praise has an effect on people."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Praise makes the less virtuous more virtuous."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is contrasted with another effect."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Praise makes the more virtuous less virtuous."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "And, of course, none except the more virtuous deserve praise.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is said to be obvious."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Some people deserve praise."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Only the more virtuous deserve it."}, {"n": 5, "text": "No one else deserves it."}]}], "the_read": "We should not praise those most deserving of praise.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q7", "prompt": "Generic drugs contain exactly the same active ingredients as their brand-name counterparts, but usually cost much less to develop, produce, and market. So, generic drugs are just as effective as their brand-name counterparts, but cost considerably less.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Generic drugs contain active ingredients. · Those ingredients are the same as the ones in brand-name drugs. · They are exactly the same. · This is a contrast with what came before it. · Generic drugs cost less than the brand-name ones. · They cost much less. · This is usually so. · The costs are for developing, producing, and marketing. (2) This is the conclusion. · Generic drugs are effective. · They are just as effective as the brand-name ones. · This is a contrast within the sentence. · Generic drugs cost less. · The amount less is considerable.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Generic drugs contain exactly the same active ingredients as their brand-name counterparts, but usually cost much less to develop, produce, and market.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Generic drugs contain active ingredients."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those ingredients are the same as the ones in brand-name drugs."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are exactly the same."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Generic drugs cost less than the brand-name ones."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They cost much less."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This is usually so."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The costs are for developing, producing, and marketing."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "So, generic drugs are just as effective as their brand-name counterparts, but cost considerably less.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Generic drugs are effective."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are just as effective as the brand-name ones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a contrast within the sentence."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Generic drugs cost less."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The amount less is considerable."}]}], "the_read": "What if generic drugs are marked up to consumers so that they cost the same as name brand drugs? What if they have the same ingredients but in different quantities and concentrations, so they are not as effective?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q8", "prompt": "Economist: In the interaction between producers and consumers, the only obligation that all parties have is to act in the best interests of their own side. And distribution of information about product defects is in the best interests of the consumer. So consumers are always obligated to report product defects they discover, while producers are never obligated to reveal them.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The economist is speaking. · This is about an interaction. · The interaction is between producers and consumers. · All parties have an obligation. · That is the only obligation they have. · The obligation is to act in the best interests of a side. · The side is their own. (2) This is an additional point. · It is about distributing information. · The information concerns product defects. · Distributing it serves someone''s best interests. · The interests are the consumer''s. (3) This is the conclusion. · Consumers are obligated to report product defects. · The defects are ones they discover. · They are always obligated to do so. · This is contrasted with the producers. · Producers are not obligated to reveal defects. · They are never obligated to.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Economist: In the interaction between producers and consumers, the only obligation that all parties have is to act in the best interests of their own side.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The economist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is about an interaction."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The interaction is between producers and consumers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "All parties have an obligation."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That is the only obligation they have."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The obligation is to act in the best interests of a side."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The side is their own."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "And distribution of information about product defects is in the best interests of the consumer.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about distributing information."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The information concerns product defects."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Distributing it serves someone''s best interests."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The interests are the consumer''s."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "So consumers are always obligated to report product defects they discover, while producers are never obligated to reveal them.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Consumers are obligated to report product defects."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The defects are ones they discover."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are always obligated to do so."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is contrasted with the producers."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Producers are not obligated to reveal defects."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They are never obligated to."}]}], "the_read": "What if it is in the best interest of producers to reveal product defect information?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q8", "qnum": 8, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q9", "prompt": "All potatoes naturally contain solanine, which is poisonous in large quantities. Domesticated potatoes contain only very small amounts of solanine, but many wild potatoes contain poisonous levels of solanine. Since most of the solanine in potatoes is concentrated in the skin, however, peeling wild potatoes makes them at least as safe to eat as unpeeled domesticated potatoes of the same size.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Solanine is contained in potatoes. · All potatoes contain it. · They contain it naturally. · The solanine is poisonous. · It is poisonous in large quantities. (2) Domesticated potatoes contain solanine. · The amounts they contain are small. · The amounts are only very small. · This is a contrast with what came before it. · Wild potatoes contain solanine. · Many wild potatoes do. · The levels in them are poisonous. (3) This gives a reason for what follows. · It is also a contrast with what came before it. · Solanine in potatoes is concentrated. · Most of it is concentrated. · The place it is concentrated is the skin. (4) Peeling wild potatoes makes them safe to eat. · They become at least as safe as another kind. · The comparison is with domesticated potatoes. · Those domesticated potatoes are unpeeled. · The potatoes compared are the same size.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "All potatoes naturally contain solanine, which is poisonous in large quantities.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Solanine is contained in potatoes."}, {"n": 2, "text": "All potatoes contain it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They contain it naturally."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The solanine is poisonous."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is poisonous in large quantities."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Domesticated potatoes contain only very small amounts of solanine, but many wild potatoes contain poisonous levels of solanine.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Domesticated potatoes contain solanine."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The amounts they contain are small."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The amounts are only very small."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Wild potatoes contain solanine."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Many wild potatoes do."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The levels in them are poisonous."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Since most of the solanine in potatoes is concentrated in the skin, however,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason for what follows."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is also a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Solanine in potatoes is concentrated."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Most of it is concentrated."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The place it is concentrated is the skin."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "peeling wild potatoes makes them at least as safe to eat as unpeeled domesticated potatoes of the same size.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Peeling wild potatoes makes them safe to eat."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They become at least as safe as another kind."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The comparison is with domesticated potatoes."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those domesticated potatoes are unpeeled."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The potatoes compared are the same size."}]}], "the_read": "What if a peeled wild potato still has more solanine than a similarly sized unpeeled domesticated potato?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q9", "qnum": 9, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 9, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q10", "prompt": "The consequences of surgical errors can be devastating, and no one would want to risk surgery unless it was performed by someone highly competent to perform surgery. General surgeons have special training and expertise that make them extremely competent to perform surgery. Therefore, surgery at the hands of anyone other than a general surgeon involves highly undesirable risks.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Surgical errors have consequences. · Those consequences can be devastating. · No one would want to risk surgery. · There is an exception to that. · The exception is surgery performed by a competent person. · That person is highly competent to perform surgery. (2) General surgeons have training. · The training is special. · They also have expertise. · Their training and expertise make them competent. · They are extremely competent. · What they are competent to do is perform surgery. (3) This is the conclusion. · Some surgery involves risks. · The risks are undesirable. · They are highly undesirable. · The surgery is at the hands of anyone other than a general surgeon.", "category": "Gap Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The consequences of surgical errors can be devastating, and no one would want to risk surgery unless it was performed by someone highly competent to perform surgery.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Surgical errors have consequences."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those consequences can be devastating."}, {"n": 3, "text": "No one would want to risk surgery."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There is an exception to that."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The exception is surgery performed by a competent person."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That person is highly competent to perform surgery."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "General surgeons have special training and expertise that make them extremely competent to perform surgery.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "General surgeons have training."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The training is special."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They also have expertise."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Their training and expertise make them competent."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They are extremely competent."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What they are competent to do is perform surgery."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, surgery at the hands of anyone other than a general surgeon involves highly undesirable risks.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some surgery involves risks."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The risks are undesirable."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are highly undesirable."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The surgery is at the hands of anyone other than a general surgeon."}]}], "the_read": "What if general surgeons are not the only competent surgeons?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Gap Flaw", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 10, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q11", "prompt": "Although the concept of free will is essential to that of moral responsibility, its role in determining responsibility is not the same in all situations. We hold criminals morally responsible for the damage they cause, assuming that they freely chose their activities. But we do not hold someone who has a heart attack while driving morally responsible for the damage caused, if any, even when we have good reason to believe that the heart attack could have been prevented by eating different foods and that one''s choice of diet is made freely.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This opens with a concession. · Free will is a concept. · It is essential to another concept. · The other concept is moral responsibility. · Free will has a role in determining responsibility. · That role is not the same in all situations. (2) We hold criminals responsible. · The responsibility is a moral one. · What we hold them responsible for is damage. · The damage is what they cause. · This rests on an assumption. · The assumption is that they chose their activities freely. (3) This is a contrast with what came before it. · We do not hold a certain person morally responsible. · That person has a heart attack. · The heart attack happens while the person is driving. · What they are not held responsible for is the damage caused. · There may not be any damage. (4) This holds even in a certain case. · The case is one where we have reason to believe two things. · Our reason is a good one. · The first belief is that the heart attack could have been prevented. · Eating different foods would have prevented it. · The second belief is that a choice of diet is made freely.", "category": "Argument Part", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Although the concept of free will is essential to that of moral responsibility, its role in determining responsibility is not the same in all situations.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This opens with a concession."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Free will is a concept."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is essential to another concept."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other concept is moral responsibility."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Free will has a role in determining responsibility."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That role is not the same in all situations."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "We hold criminals morally responsible for the damage they cause, assuming that they freely chose their activities.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "We hold criminals responsible."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The responsibility is a moral one."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What we hold them responsible for is damage."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The damage is what they cause."}, 