s certain that Alice will not volunteer.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about whether Alice volunteers. · What she would volunteer for is a fund-raising drive. · The drive is the hospital''s. · She will volunteer only if two conditions hold. · The first is that Bruce also volunteers. · Bruce is her brother. · The second is that others promise to select Bruce to manage the drive. · Those promising must be a majority of the others working on the drive. (2) This is a contrast with what came before it. · It grants one thing first. · Bruce is willing to volunteer. · None of the others will make the promise. · Those others are working on the drive. · The promise would be to select Bruce as manager of the drive. (3) This is the conclusion. · Alice will not volunteer. · This outcome is certain.", "category": "Parallel Reasoning", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Alice will volunteer to work on the hospital fund-raising drive only if her brother Bruce also volunteers and a majority of the others working on the drive promise to select Bruce to manage the drive.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about whether Alice volunteers."}, {"n": 2, "text": "What she would volunteer for is a fund-raising drive."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The drive is the hospital''s."}, {"n": 4, "text": "She will volunteer only if two conditions hold."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The first is that Bruce also volunteers."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Bruce is her brother."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The second is that others promise to select Bruce to manage the drive."}, {"n": 8, "text": "Those promising must be a majority of the others working on the drive."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "However, although Bruce is willing to volunteer, none of the others working on the drive will promise to select Bruce to manage the drive.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It grants one thing first."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Bruce is willing to volunteer."}, {"n": 4, "text": "None of the others will make the promise."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those others are working on the drive."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The promise would be to select Bruce as manager of the drive."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Thus it is certain that Alice will not volunteer.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Alice will not volunteer."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This outcome is certain."}]}], "the_read": "What if the rules of conditional reasoning don''t apply?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q12", "qnum": 12, "question_type": "Parallel Reasoning", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q13", "prompt": "If an artist receives a public subsidy to support work on a specific project—e.g., making a film—and if this project then proves successful enough to allow the artist to repay the subsidy, is the artist then morally obliged to do so? The answer is clearly yes, since the money returned to the agency distributing the subsidies will be welcome as a source of support for other artists deserving of public subsidies.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is a conditional statement. · An artist might receive a subsidy. · The subsidy is a public one. · The subsidy supports work on a project. · The project is a specific one. · An example is then given. (2) The example is making a film. · The project might prove successful. · It would be successful enough to allow repayment. · The artist would repay the subsidy. · A question is asked. · The question is whether the artist is obliged to repay. · The obligation would be a moral one. (3) The answer to the question is yes. · This is clearly so. · A reason is given for it. · The money would be returned to an agency. · That agency distributes the subsidies. · The money will be welcome there. · It would be a source of support for other artists. · Those artists deserve public subsidies.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "If an artist receives a public subsidy to support work on a specific project—e.g.,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a conditional statement."}, {"n": 2, "text": "An artist might receive a subsidy."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The subsidy is a public one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The subsidy supports work on a project."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The project is a specific one."}, {"n": 6, "text": "An example is then given."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "making a film—and if this project then proves successful enough to allow the artist to repay the subsidy, is the artist then morally obliged to do so?", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The example is making a film."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The project might prove successful."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It would be successful enough to allow repayment."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The artist would repay the subsidy."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A question is asked."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The question is whether the artist is obliged to repay."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The obligation would be a moral one."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The answer is clearly yes, since the money returned to the agency distributing the subsidies will be welcome as a source of support for other artists deserving of public subsidies.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The answer to the question is yes."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is clearly so."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A reason is given for it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The money would be returned to an agency."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That agency distributes the subsidies."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The money will be welcome there."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It would be a source of support for other artists."}, {"n": 8, "text": "Those artists deserve public subsidies."}]}], "the_read": "What if artists are selfish and don''t care about helping other artists?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Met