 "This led those in charge of the attraction to hypothesize that visitors were illicitly selling or sharing the passes.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about what came before it."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It led some people to a hypothesis."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those people are in charge of the attraction."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The hypothesis is about visitors."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The visitors were selling or sharing the passes."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They were doing so illicitly."}]}], "the_read": "What if people with passes to the attraction come back multiple times per year to visit the attraction?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q17", "prompt": "The difference between manners and morals is that the former are necessarily social in nature whereas the latter are not necessarily social in nature. So the rules of etiquette do not apply when one is alone.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a difference between two things. · The two things are manners and morals. · Manners are social in nature. · Manners are necessarily so. · This is contrasted with morals. · Morals are not necessarily social in nature. (2) This is the conclusion. · There are rules of etiquette. · Those rules do not apply in a certain case. · The case is when one is alone.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The difference between manners and morals is that the former are necessarily social in nature whereas the latter are not necessarily social in nature.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a difference between two things."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The two things are manners and morals."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Manners are social in nature."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Manners are necessarily so."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is contrasted with morals."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Morals are not necessarily social in nature."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "So the rules of etiquette do not apply when one is alone.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There are rules of etiquette."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those rules do not apply in a certain case."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The case is when one is alone."}]}], "the_read": "What if the rules of ettiquette don''t only apply to social situations?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q17", "qnum": 17, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q18", "prompt": "Sociologist: The intended function of news is to give us information on which to act. But in a consumer society, news becomes a product to be manufactured and dispensed to the consumer. An enormous industry for the production and consumption of news has evolved, and we ingest news with an insatiable appetite. Under such circumstances, news is primarily entertaining and cannot, therefore, serve its intended function.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The sociologist is speaking. · News has a function. · That function is the intended one. · The function is to give us information. · The information is something to act on. (2) This is a contrast with what came before it. · The setting is a consumer society. · News becomes a product. · The product is manufactured. · The product is dispensed to the consumer. (3) An industry has evolved. · The industry is enormous. · It is for the production of news. · It is also for the consumption of news. · We ingest the news. · We do so with an appetite. · That appetite is insatiable. (4) This holds under the circumstances described. · The news is entertaining. · It is primarily entertaining. · This is the conclusion. · News cannot serve its function. · The function is the intended one.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Sociologist: The intended function of news is to give us information on which to act.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The sociologist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "News has a function."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That function is the intended one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The function is to give us information."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The information is something to act on."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But in a consumer society, news becomes a product to be manufactured and dispensed to the consumer.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The setting is a consumer society."}, {"n": 3, "text": "News becomes a product."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The product is manufactured."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The product is dispensed to the consumer."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "An enormous industry for the production and consumption of news has evolved, and we ingest news with an insatiable appetite.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "An industry has evolved."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The industry is enormous."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is for the production of news."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is also for the consumption of news."}, {"n": 5, "text": "We ingest the news."}, {"n": 6, "text": "We do so with an appetite."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That appetite is insatiable."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Under such circumstances, news is primarily entertaining and cannot, therefore, serve its intended function.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This holds under the circumstances described."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The news is entertaining."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is primarily entertaining."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 5, "text": "News cannot serve its function."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The function is the intended one."}]}], "the_read": "What if people can get information on which to act from entertainment?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q18", "qnum": 18, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 17, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q19", "prompt": "Paleontologist: It is widely, but falsely, held that life began in the ocean and did not exist on land until half a billion years ago. Traces of carbon 14 have been found throughout certain 1.2-billion-year-old rocks in North America. Carbon 14 is extracted by plants and microbes from the atmosphere to fuel their chemical reactions, and is then released when the plants and microbes die.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The paleontologist is speaking. · Something is widely held. · What is held is false. · The belief is that life began in the ocean. · The belief is also that life did not exist on land. · Life is said to have been absent from land until half a billion years ago. (2) Traces of carbon 14 have been found. · They were found in rocks. · The rocks are 1.2 billion years old. · Only certain rocks are meant. · The traces are found throughout those rocks. · The rocks are in North America. (3) Carbon 14 is extracted by plants. · Microbes extract it as well. · They extract it from the atmosphere. · The purpose is to fuel their chemical reactions. · The carbon 14 is later released. · It is released when the plants and microbes die.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Paleontologist: It is widely, but falsely, held that life began in the ocean and did not exist on land until half a billion years ago.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The paleontologist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Something is widely held."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What is held is false."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The belief is that life began in the ocean."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The belief is also that life did not exist on land."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Life is said to have been absent from land until half a billion years ago."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Traces of carbon 14 have been found throughout certain 1.2-billion-year-old rocks in North America.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Traces of carbon 14 have been found."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They were found in rocks."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The rocks are 1.2 billion years old."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Only certain rocks are meant."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The traces are found throughout those rocks."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The rocks are in North America."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Carbon 14 is extracted by plants and microbes from the atmosphere to fuel their chemical reactions, and is then released when the plants and microbes die.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Carbon 14 is extracted by plants."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Microbes extract it as well."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They extract it from the atmosphere."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The purpose is to fuel their chemical reactions."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The carbon 14 is later released."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It is released when the plants and microbes die."}]}], "the_read": "What if the rocks were initially under water?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q19", "qnum": 19, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 18, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q20", "prompt": "The nature of English literature reflects the rich and diverse vocabulary of the English language, which resulted from the dual influence of the Anglo-Saxon and, later, French languages. The French language, though, is a direct descendant of Latin, with few traces of the Celtic language spoken by the pre-Roman inhabitants of the area; the hallmark of French literature is its simplicity and clarity.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) English literature has a nature. · That nature reflects a vocabulary. · The vocabulary belongs to the English language. · The vocabulary is rich. · It is diverse as well. (2) This is about the English vocabulary. · The vocabulary resulted from an influence. · The influence was a dual one. · One influence was the Anglo-Saxon language. · The other was the French language. · The French influence came later. (3) This is a contrast with what came before it. · The French language descends from Latin. · It is a direct descendant. · French carries traces of the Celtic language. · Those traces are few. · Celtic was spoken by the inhabitants of the area. · Those inhabitants were there before the Romans. (4) This turns to French literature. · French literature has a hallmark. · The hallmark is its simplicity. · The hallmark is also its clarity.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The nature of English literature reflects the rich and diverse vocabulary of the English language,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "English literature has a nature."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That nature reflects a vocabulary."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The vocabulary belongs to the English language."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The vocabulary is rich."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is diverse as well."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "which resulted from the dual influence of the Anglo-Saxon and, later, French languages.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the English vocabulary."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The vocabulary resulted from an influence."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The influence was a dual one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "One influence was the Anglo-Saxon language."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The other was the French language."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The French influence came later."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The French language, though, is a direct descendant of Latin, with few traces of the Celtic language spoken by the pre-Roman inhabitants of the area;", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The French language descends from Latin."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is a direct descendant."}, {"n": 4, "text": "French carries traces of the Celtic language."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those traces are few."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Celtic was spoken by the inhabitants of the area."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those inhabitants were there before the Romans."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "the hallmark of French literature is its simplicity and clarity.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This turns to French literature."}, {"n": 2, "text": "French literature has a hallmark."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The hallmark is its simplicity."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The hallmark is also its clarity."}]}], "the_read": "The English language has distant Latin influence.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q20", "qnum": 20, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 19, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q21", "prompt": "Two doctrines have been greatly influential in this century. The first holds that the explanation of any historical event must appeal to economic factors. The second attempts to account psychologically for all historical events, especially in terms of early childhood experience. Both doctrines, however, are mistaken. Certainly there have been events that were due both to economic forces and to the nature of the early childhood experiences of the major participants in the event.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There are two doctrines. · The doctrines have been influential. · They have been greatly influential. · Their influence is in this century. (2) This is about the first doctrine. · It concerns the explanation of a historical event. · It applies to any historical event. · An explanation must appeal to certain factors. · Those factors are economic ones. (3) This is about the second doctrine. · It attempts to account for historical events. · The accounting is psychological. · It covers all historical events. · It works especially in terms of experience. · The experience is from early childhood. (4) This is a contrast with what came before it. · The doctrines are mistaken. · Both of them are mistaken. (5) This is stated with certainty. · There have been events of a certain kind. · Those events were due to two things at once. · One was economic forces. · The other was the nature of early childhood experiences. · The experiences belong to participants in the event. · Those participants are the major ones.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Two doctrines have been greatly influential in this century.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There are two doctrines."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The doctrines have been influential."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They have been greatly influential."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Their influence is in this century."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The first holds that the explanation of any historical event must appeal to economic factors.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the first doctrine."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It concerns the explanation of a historical event."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It applies to any historical event."}, {"n": 4, "text": "An explanation must appeal to certain factors."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those factors are economic ones."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The second attempts to account psychologically for all historical events, especially in terms of early childhood experience.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the second doctrine."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It attempts to account for historical events."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The accounting is psychological."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It covers all historical events."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It works especially in terms of experience."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The experience is from early childhood."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Both doctrines, however, are mistaken.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The doctrines are mistaken."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Both of them are mistaken."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Certainly there have been events that were due both to economic forces and to the nature of the early childhood experiences of the major participants in the event.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is stated with certainty."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There have been events of a certain kind."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those events were due to two things at once."}, {"n": 4, "text": "One was economic forces."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The other was the nature of early childhood experiences."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The experiences belong to participants in the event."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those participants are the major ones."}]}], "the_read": "What if these doctrines allow for hybrid/dual explanations?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q21", "qnum": 21, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 20, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q22", "prompt": "Editorial: Supporters of the proposed law that would require bicyclists to wear helmets are seriously misguided. The number of pedestrians who die yearly as a result of accidents involving automobiles is five times the number of deaths resulting from bicycle accidents, and drunken driving exacts a much higher toll than both combined. Yet there are no calls for a ban on alcohol or walking down the street.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is an editorial. · There is a proposed law. · The law would require bicyclists to wear helmets. · The law has supporters. · Those supporters are misguided. · They are seriously misguided. (2) Pedestrians die every year. · Their deaths result from accidents. · The accidents involve automobiles. · The comparison is with deaths from bicycle accidents. · The pedestrian number is five times the other. (3) This adds a further comparison. · Drunken driving exacts a toll. · That toll is higher. · It is much higher. · The comparison is with the other two tolls combined. (4) This is a contrast with what came before it. · There are no calls for a ban. · One possible ban would be on alcohol. · Another would be on walking down the street.", "category": "Parallel Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Editorial: Supporters of the proposed law that would require bicyclists to wear helmets are seriously misguided.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an editorial."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a proposed law."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The law would require bicyclists to wear helmets."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The law has supporters."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those supporters are misguided."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They are seriously misguided."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The number of pedestrians who die yearly as a result of accidents involving automobiles is five times the number of deaths resulting from bicycle accidents,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Pedestrians die every year."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Their deaths result from accidents."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The accidents involve automobiles."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with deaths from bicycle accidents."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The pedestrian number is five times the other."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "and drunken driving exacts a much higher toll than both combined.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This adds a further comparison."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Drunken driving exacts a toll."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That toll is higher."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is much higher."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with the other two tolls combined."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Yet there are no calls for a ban on alcohol or walking down the street.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There are no calls for a ban."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One possible ban would be on alcohol."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Another would be on walking down the street."}]}], "the_read": "What if requiring bikers to wear a helmet is not analogous to banning walking or drinking?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q22", "qnum": 22, "question_type": "Parallel Flaw", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 21, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q23", "prompt": "Deep tillage is even more deleterious to the world''s topsoil supply than previously believed. For example, farmers who till deeply are ten times more likely to lose topsoil to erosion than are farmers who use no-till methods. Results like these make it clear that farmers who now till deeply should strive, by using other topsoil aeration techniques, to incorporate no-till methods instead.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Deep tillage is deleterious. · What it harms is a topsoil supply. · The supply is the world''s. · It is even more deleterious than was believed. · The belief being compared to is the previous one. (2) This is an example. · Some farmers till deeply. · Those farmers are more likely to lose topsoil. · They are ten times more likely. · The topsoil is lost to erosion. · The comparison is with other farmers. · Those farmers use no-till methods. (3) This rests on results like the one given. · The results make something clear. · It is about farmers who till deeply. · Those farmers till deeply now. · They should strive to incorporate no-till methods. · The no-till methods would replace deep tillage. · They would do this by using other topsoil aeration techniques.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Deep tillage is even more deleterious to the world''s topsoil supply than previously believed.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Deep tillage is deleterious."}, {"n": 2, "text": "What it harms is a topsoil supply."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The supply is the world''s."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is even more deleterious than was believed."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The belief being compared to is the previous one."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "For example, farmers who till deeply are ten times more likely to lose topsoil to erosion than are farmers who use no-till methods.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an example."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some farmers till deeply."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those farmers are more likely to lose topsoil."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are ten times more likely."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The topsoil is lost to erosion."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with other farmers."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those farmers use no-till methods."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Results like these make it clear that farmers who now till deeply should strive, by using other topsoil aeration techniques, to incorporate no-till methods instead.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This rests on results like the one given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The results make something clear."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about farmers who till deeply."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those farmers till deeply now."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They should strive to incorporate no-till methods."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The no-till methods would replace deep tillage."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They would do this by using other topsoil aeration techniques."}]}], "the_read": "What if there are other, much more harmful, downsides to the no-till methods? What if farmers who don''t till don''t lose topsoil, but all their soil gets devoid of nutrients and can''t grow crops? What if soil grows on trees and we don''t care about losing it to erosion?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q23", "qnum": 23, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 22, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q24", "prompt": "Editorial: Our society has a vested interest in maintaining a political system in which candidates are free to adhere to their principles. Yet campaigning for elected office is extremely costly, and because only the wealthiest individuals are able to finance their own political campaigns, most candidates must seek funding from private sources. In so doing, the candidates are almost invariably obliged to compromise their principles. Thus, government itself should assume the cost of candidates'' campaigns.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is an editorial. · Our society has an interest. · The interest is a vested one. · The interest is in maintaining a political system. · Candidates in that system are free. · What they are free to do is adhere to their principles. (2) This is a contrast with what came before it. · The campaigning is costly. · It is extremely costly. · The campaigning is for elected office. · Some individuals can finance their own political campaigns. · Only the wealthiest can do so. · This is given as a reason. (3) Candidates must seek funding. · Most candidates must do so. · The funding comes from sources. · Those sources are private ones. (4) This is about what happens when they do that. · The candidates are obliged to do something. · What they are obliged to do is compromise their principles. · This happens almost invariably. (5) This is the conclusion. · The government should assume a cost. · The government itself should do it. · The cost is that of campaigns. · The campaigns belong to the candidates.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Editorial: Our society has a vested interest in maintaining a political system in which candidates are free to adhere to their principles.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an editorial."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Our society has an interest."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The interest is a vested one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The interest is in maintaining a political system."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Candidates in that system are free."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What they are free to do is adhere to their principles."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Yet campaigning for elected office is extremely costly, and because only the wealthiest individuals are able to finance their own political campaigns,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The campaigning is costly."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is extremely costly."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The campaigning is for elected office."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Some individuals can finance their own political campaigns."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Only the wealthiest can do so."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This is given as a reason."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "most candidates must seek funding from private sources.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Candidates must seek funding."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Most candidates must do so."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The funding comes from sources."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those sources are private ones."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "In so doing, the candidates are almost invariably obliged to compromise their principles.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about what happens when they do that."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The candidates are obliged to do something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they are obliged to do is compromise their principles."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This happens almost invariably."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Thus, government itself should assume the cost of candidates'' campaigns.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The government should assume a cost."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The government itself should do it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The cost is that of campaigns."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The campaigns belong to the candidates."}]}], "the_read": "What if the government shouldn''t pay for anything, no matter what?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q24", "qnum": 24, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 23, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q25", "prompt": "A clothing manufacturer reports that unsalable garments, those found to be defective by inspectors plus those returned by retailers, total 7 percent of the garments produced. Further, it reports that all of its unsalable garments are recycled as scrap, but the percentage of garments reported as recycled scrap is 9 percent.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A clothing manufacturer makes a report. · The report is about unsalable garments. · Some of those garments were found defective. · Inspectors found them defective. · Others were returned by retailers. · Together they total 7 percent. · The percentage is of the garments produced. (2) This is an additional report. · The unsalable garments are recycled as scrap. · All of them are recycled that way. · This is a contrast with what came before it. · A percentage is reported as recycled scrap. · That percentage is 9 percent.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A clothing manufacturer reports that unsalable garments, those found to be defective by inspectors plus those returned by retailers, total 7 percent of the garments produced.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A clothing manufacturer makes a report."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The report is about unsalable garments."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Some of those garments were found defective."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Inspectors found them defective."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Others were returned by retailers."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Together they total 7 percent."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The percentage is of the garments produced."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Further, it reports that all of its unsalable garments are recycled as scrap, but the percentage of garments reported as recycled scrap is 9 percent.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional report."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The unsalable garments are recycled as scrap."}, {"n": 3, "text": "All of them are recycled that way."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A percentage is reported as recycled scrap."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That percentage is 9 percent."}]}], "the_read": "What if insalable garments are measured differently than recycled scrap? What if more things than just insalable garments are recycled as scrap?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q25", "qnum": 25, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 24, "slug": "PT105_S1_Q26", "prompt": "Marion knows that the bridge on her usual route is closed and that, unless she takes the train instead of driving, she can get to work on time only by leaving at least 45 minutes early. She must go to her neighborhood bank before leaving for work, however, and the bank will not be open in time for her to do so if she leaves 45 minutes early. Therefore, since she hates taking the train, Marion cannot avoid being late for work.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Marion knows certain things. · A bridge is closed. · The bridge is on her usual route. · There is an exception to what follows. · The exception is her taking the train. · The alternative to the train is driving. (2) Marion can get to work on time. · There is only one way to do it. · The way is leaving early. · She would leave at least 45 minutes early. (3) This is a contrast with what came before it. · Marion must go to a bank. · The bank is in her neighborhood. · She must go before leaving for work. · The bank will not be open in time. · That is so if she leaves 45 minutes early. (4) This is the conclusion. · It rests on a reason. · The reason is that she hates taking the train. · Marion cannot avoid being late. · The lateness is for work.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Marion knows that the bridge on her usual route is closed and that, unless she takes the train instead of driving,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Marion knows certain things."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A bridge is closed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The bridge is on her usual route."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There is an exception to what follows."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The exception is her taking the train."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The alternative to the train is driving."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "she can get to work on time only by leaving at least 45 minutes early.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Marion can get to work on time."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is only one way to do it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The way is leaving early."}, {"n": 4, "text": "She would leave at least 45 minutes early."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "She must go to her neighborhood bank before leaving for work, however, and the bank will not be open in time for her to do so if she leaves 45 minutes early.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before it."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Marion must go to a bank."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The bank is in her neighborhood."}, {"n": 4, "text": "She must go before leaving for work."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The bank will not be open in time."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That is so if she leaves 45 minutes early."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Therefore, since she hates taking the train, Marion cannot avoid being late for work.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on a reason."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The reason is that she hates taking the train."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Marion cannot avoid being late."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The lateness is for work."}]}], "the_read": "What if Marion sometimes does things she hates?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S1.Q26", "qnum": 26, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "D"}]}]'::jsonb,
    'section',
    '{"standard": 25, "accommodation_53": 38, "accommodation_70": 50}'::jsonb,
