  ),
  (
    'read-recall-colour-pt105-s2',
    'Read, Recall, Colour · PT105 Section 2',
    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, and react to it. Once you finish the stimulus, say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, then give it a reaction. After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears, and you recall the whole argument out loud from memory.", "rules": ["Read each piece silently. Everything you say back, including your reaction and final recall, is out loud.", "Read about a sentence at a time. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said.", "Then colour it: react to that specific piece with a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling.", "Repeat these steps through the whole stimulus.", "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and stays gone. Say the entire argument back from memory, with every detail you can still hold.", "Press space again to move on. The whole section records in one take, with no pauses or breaks."], "briefing": {"lede": "Read these instructions once before your first section. After that, come back when you need a reminder.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still reach the answers remembering almost none of it.", "body": ["You can follow a stimulus sentence by sentence and understand every part. Then you reach answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and that is what this drill trains.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. Understanding the stimulus is not enough if it is gone by the time you reach the answers.", "Most everyday reading trains you to reach the end, not retain every detail. You scan names, drop connecting words, and turn a list of three things into one vague label. That works for a phone bill. It does not work for the LSAT."]}, {"heading": "How the method works", "body": ["For each piece, read it, say it back, and colour it. Once you finish the stimulus, recall the whole argument. You decide how much to read at a time, and nothing on the screen decides for you.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it", "body": "Read about a sentence silently. Give every word enough attention to hear the sentence naturally in your head. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less. If you take on too much, the beginning is usually what disappears first."}, {"label": "Tell it back", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it", "body": "React to what you just said. Give it a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling. Keep it short, but make it specific. If the same reaction could fit any sentence, it does not count."}, {"label": "Recall it", "body": "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Say the entire argument out loud from memory, with every detail you can still hold. This is what gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter", "body": ["Do not give back only the gist or what seemed important. You cannot know which details will matter until you have read the whole stimulus. Filtering as you read is how useful information disappears.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. Names, numbers, and small words carrying the load, such as all, most, only, and probably, have to come back too.", "**Being efficient does not mean leaving information out.**"]}, {"heading": "When nothing comes to mind", "body": ["You may try to colour a sentence and find that nothing comes to mind. Usually, the hidden thought is that nothing feels good enough to say.", "Do not judge the reaction before you say it. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count when they connect to what you just read.", "The reaction makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away from the text", "body": ["The text stays visible while you say each piece back. You have to look away from it yourself.", "If you read while you recall, you stop using your short-term memory and slide back into the author''s exact words. Looking away forces you to rebuild the meaning in your own words.", "This is also the version that works on paper and on test day."]}, {"heading": "Build toward twenty-five minutes", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section. It only counts when you read every stimulus, say each piece back, colour it, and recall the whole argument. Finishing early by skipping parts is not finishing early. If you receive extra time on test day, use your full allotted time here too.", "The clock counts up so you can track your pace without watching your time disappear.", "Do the method correctly before you try to do it quickly. Go slower while it is new. Speed follows once the method becomes automatic."]}], "closer": "One section keeps the method steady. Improvement starts with the second."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, tells it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives The Recall of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Recall against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q1", "prompt": "French divers recently found a large cave along the coast of the Mediterranean Sea. The cave is accessible only through an underwater tunnel. The interior of the cave is completely filled with seawater and contains numerous large stalagmites, which are stony pillars that form when drops of water fall repeatedly on a single spot on a cave floor, leaving behind mineral deposits that accumulate over time.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Divers found a cave. · The divers were French. · The find was recent. · The cave is large. · The cave is along a coast. · The coast is the Mediterranean Sea''s. (2) There is only one way into the cave. · The way in is a tunnel. · The tunnel is underwater. (3) This is about the inside of the cave. · It is filled with water. · The water is seawater. · It is filled completely. · The cave contains stalagmites. · The stalagmites are numerous. · The stalagmites are large. (4) Stalagmites are a kind of pillar. · The pillars are made of stone. · They form from falling drops of water. · The drops fall repeatedly. · The drops fall on one single spot. · The spot is on a cave floor. · The drops leave mineral deposits behind. · The deposits build up over time.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "French divers recently found a large cave along the coast of the Mediterranean Sea.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Divers found a cave."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The divers were French."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The find was recent."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The cave is large."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cave is along a coast."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The coast is the Mediterranean Sea''s."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The cave is accessible only through an underwater tunnel.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is only one way into the cave."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The way in is a tunnel."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The tunnel is underwater."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The interior of the cave is completely filled with seawater and contains numerous large stalagmites,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the inside of the cave."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is filled with water."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The water is seawater."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is filled completely."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cave contains stalagmites."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The stalagmites are numerous."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The stalagmites are large."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "which are stony pillars that form when drops of water fall repeatedly on a single spot on a cave floor, leaving behind mineral deposits that accumulate over time.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Stalagmites are a kind of pillar."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The pillars are made of stone."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They form from falling drops of water."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The drops fall repeatedly."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The drops fall on one single spot."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The spot is on a cave floor."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The drops leave mineral deposits behind."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The deposits build up over time."}]}], "the_read": "The cave was not always completely filled with water.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q2", "prompt": "A director of the Rexx Pharmaceutical Company argued that the development costs for new vaccines that the health department has requested should be subsidized by the government, since the marketing of vaccines promised to be less profitable than the marketing of any other pharmaceutical product. In support of this claim the director argued that sales of vaccines are likely to be lower since each vaccine is administered to a patient only once, whereas medicines that combat diseases and chronic illnesses are administered many times to each patient.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": 