r argued that sales of vaccines are likely to be lower since each vaccine is administered to a patient only once,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is further support for the claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Vaccine sales are likely to be lower."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The reason is how often a vaccine is given."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A vaccine is given to a patient only once."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "whereas medicines that combat diseases and chronic illnesses are administered many times to each patient.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about other medicines."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those medicines fight diseases and chronic illnesses."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those are given many times to each patient."}]}], "the_read": "What if it''s obligatory for pharmaceutical companies to absorb the costs of developing vaccines as a contribution to the public good? What if the government is not obliged to subsidized less profitable pharmaceutical products?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q3", "prompt": "Manager: Our new computer network, the purpose of which is to increase productivity, can be installed during the day, which would disrupt our employees'' work, or else at night, which would entail much higher installation charges. Since saving money is important, we should have the network installed during the day.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A manager is speaking. · There is a new computer network. · Its purpose is to increase productivity. · It can be installed during the day. (2) That option would disrupt work. · The work disrupted is the employees''. · The other option is installing at night. · Night installation would cost much more. (3) Saving money is important. · That is the reason given. · The conclusion is to install during the day.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Manager: Our new computer network, the purpose of which is to increase productivity, can be installed during the day,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A manager is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a new computer network."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Its purpose is to increase productivity."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It can be installed during the day."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "which would disrupt our employees'' work, or else at night, which would entail much higher installation charges.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "That option would disrupt work."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The work disrupted is the employees''."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The other option is installing at night."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Night installation would cost much more."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Since saving money is important, we should have the network installed during the day.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Saving money is important."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That is the reason given."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The conclusion is to install during the day."}]}], "the_read": "What if the monetary value of lost productivity due to daytime installation is greater than the increased cost of evening installation?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q4", "prompt": "An ingredient in marijuana known as THC has been found to inactivate herpesviruses in experiments. In previous experiments researchers found that inactivated herpesviruses can convert healthy cells into cancer cells. It can be concluded that the use of marijuana can cause cancer.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is an ingredient in marijuana. · The ingredient is called THC. · THC can inactivate herpesviruses. · This was found in experiments. (2) This comes from previous experiments. · Researchers were the ones who found it. · It is about inactivated herpesviruses. · They can convert cells. · The cells converted are healthy ones. · They become cancer cells. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about using marijuana. · Using marijuana can cause cancer.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "An ingredient in marijuana known as THC has been found to inactivate herpesviruses in experiments.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is an ingredient in marijuana."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The ingredient is called THC."}, {"n": 3, "text": "THC can inactivate herpesviruses."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This was found in experiments."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "In previous experiments researchers found that inactivated herpesviruses can convert healthy cells into cancer cells.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This comes from previous experiments."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Researchers were the ones who found it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about inactivated herpesviruses."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They can convert cells."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cells converted are healthy ones."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They become cancer cells."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "It can be concluded that the use of marijuana can cause cancer.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about using marijuana."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Using marijuana can cause cancer."}]}], "the_read": "What if other ingredients in marijuana negate THC''s carcinogenic effects?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q5", "prompt": "Archaeologist: A large corporation has recently offered to provide funding to restore an archaeological site and to construct facilities to make the site readily accessible to the general public. The restoration will conform to the best current theories about how the site appeared at the height of the ancient civilization that occupied it. This offer should be rejected, however, because many parts of the site contain unexamined evidence.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) An archaeologist is speaking. · A corporation has made an offer. · The corporation is large. · The offer is recent. · The offer is funding. · The funding would restore an archaeological site. · It would also construct facilities. · The facilities would make the site readily accessible to the general public. (2) The restoration will follow theories. · They are the best current theories. · They concern how the site appeared. · The moment described is the height of that civilization. · The civilization was ancient. · That civilization occupied the site. (3) This is the conclusion. · The offer should be rejected. · The reason is unexamined evidence. · Many parts of the site contain it.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Archaeologist: A large corporation has recently offered to provide funding to restore an archaeological site and to construct facilities to make the site readily accessible to the general public.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "An archaeologist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A corporation has made an offer."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The corporation is large."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The offer is recent."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The offer is funding."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The funding would restore an archaeological site."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It would also construct facilities."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The facilities would make the site readily accessible to the general public."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The restoration will conform to the best current theories about how the site appeared at the height of the ancient civilization that occupied it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The restoration will follow theories."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are the best current theories."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They concern how the site appeared."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The moment described is the height of that civilization."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The civilization was ancient."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That civilization occupied the site."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "This offer should be rejected, however, because many parts of the site contain unexamined evidence.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The offer should be rejected."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The reason is unexamined evidence."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Many parts of the site contain it."}]}], "the_read": "What if making the site available to the public is more important that preserving unexamined evidence?\nor\nWhat the restoration will not endanger the unexamined evidence?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q6", "prompt": "Besides laying eggs in her own nest, any female wood duck will lay an egg in the nest of another female wood duck if she sees the other duck leaving her nest. Under natural nesting conditions, this parasitic behavior is relatively rare because the ducks'' nests are well hidden. However, when people put up nesting boxes to help the ducks breed, they actually undercut the ducks'' reproductive efforts. These nesting boxes become so crowded with extra eggs that few, if any, of the eggs in those boxes hatch.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A female wood duck lays eggs in her own nest. · She will also lay an egg elsewhere. · The other place is another female wood duck''s nest. · This happens under a condition. · The condition is seeing the other duck leave her nest. · This is true of any female wood duck. (2) This is about natural nesting conditions. · The behavior is called parasitic. · It is relatively rare. · The reason is the nests. · The nests are well hidden. (3) This is a contrast. · People put up nesting boxes. · Their intent is to help the ducks breed. · The actual effect is the opposite. · They undercut the ducks'' reproductive efforts. (4) The boxes become crowded. · What crowds them is extra eggs. · The crowding has a result. · Few of the eggs hatch. · It is possible that none of them hatch. · This is about eggs in those boxes.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Besides laying eggs in her own nest, any female wood duck will lay an egg in the nest of another female wood duck if she sees the other duck leaving her nest.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A female wood duck lays eggs in her own nest."}, {"n": 2, "text": "She will also lay an egg elsewhere."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The other place is another female wood duck''s nest."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This happens under a condition."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The condition is seeing the other duck leave her nest."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is true of any female wood duck."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Under natural nesting conditions, this parasitic behavior is relatively rare because the ducks'' nests are well hidden.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about natural nesting conditions."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The behavior is called parasitic."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is relatively rare."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The reason is the nests."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The nests are well hidden."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "However, when people put up nesting boxes to help the ducks breed, they actually undercut the ducks'' reproductive efforts.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "People put up nesting boxes."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Their intent is to help the ducks breed."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The actual effect is the opposite."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They undercut the ducks'' reproductive efforts."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "These nesting boxes become so crowded with extra eggs that few, if any, of the eggs in those boxes hatch.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The boxes become crowded."}, {"n": 2, "text": "What crowds them is extra eggs."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The crowding has a result."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Few of the eggs hatch."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is possible that none of them hatch."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is about eggs in those boxes."}]}], "the_read": "What if even fewer duck eggs hatch in the wild due to the wood duck predator''s predilection for omelets?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q7", "prompt": "The crux of creativity resides in the ability to manufacture variations on a theme. If we look at the history of science, for instance, we see that every idea is built upon a thousand related ideas. Careful analysis leads us to understand that what we choose to call a new theme or a new discovery is itself always and without exception some sort of variation, on a deep level, of previous themes.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Creativity has a crux. · The crux is an ability. · The ability is to manufacture variations. · The variations are on a theme. (2) This is an example. · The example is the history of science. · Every idea is built on other ideas. · The number given is a thousand. · Those ideas are related. (3) Careful analysis leads to an understanding. · It is about what we call new. · We call things a new theme or a new discovery. · That is our choice of words. · Such a thing is itself a variation. · This is always true, without exception. · The variation is on a deep level. · It varies previous themes.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The crux of creativity resides in the ability to manufacture variations on a theme.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Creativity has a crux."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The crux is an ability."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The ability is to manufacture variations."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The variations are on a theme."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "If we look at the history of science, for instance, we see that every idea is built upon a thousand related ideas.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an example."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The example is the history of science."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Every idea is built on other ideas."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The number given is a thousand."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those ideas are related."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Careful analysis leads us to understand that what we choose to call a new theme or a new discovery is itself always and without exception some sort of variation, on a deep level, of previous themes.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Careful analysis leads to an understanding."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about what we call new."}, {"n": 3, "text": "We call things a new theme or a new discovery."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That is our choice of words."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Such a thing is itself a variation."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is always true, without exception."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The variation is on a deep level."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It varies previous themes."}]}], "the_read": "What if the \"crux of creativity\" has nothing to do with the history of science? What if these new discoveries or themes are not examples of creativity?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q8", "prompt": "Millions of female bats rear their pups in Bracken Cave. Although the mothers all leave the cave nightly, on their return each mother is almost always swiftly reunited with her own pup. Since the bats'' calls are their only means of finding one another, and a bat pup cannot distinguish the call of its mother from that of any other adult bat, it is clear that each mother bat can recognize the call of her pup.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Female bats rear pups. · There are millions of them. · This happens in Bracken Cave. (2) The mothers leave the cave. · All of them leave. · The mothers leave every night. · The mothers come back to the cave. · On return each mother is reunited with a pup. · The pup is her own. · This happens almost always. · The reunion happens swiftly. (3) These are the premises. · The bats have calls of their own. · The calls are how they find one another. · Calls are their only means of doing so. · A bat pup cannot distinguish its mother''s call. · It cannot tell her call from that of any other adult bat. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is stated as clear. · Each mother bat can recognize a call. · The call is her pup''s.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Millions of female bats rear their pups in Bracken Cave.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Female bats rear pups."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There are millions of them."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This happens in Bracken Cave."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Although the mothers all leave the cave nightly, on their return each mother is almost always swiftly reunited with her own pup.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The mothers leave the cave."}, {"n": 2, "text": "All of them leave."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The mothers leave every night."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The mothers come back to the cave."}, {"n": 5, "text": "On return each mother is reunited with a pup."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The pup is her own."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This happens almost always."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The reunion happens swiftly."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Since the bats'' calls are their only means of finding one another, and a bat pup cannot distinguish the call of its mother from that of any other adult bat,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "These are the premises."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The bats have calls of their own."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The calls are how they find one another."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Calls are their only means of doing so."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A bat pup cannot distinguish its mother''s call."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It cannot tell her call from that of any other adult bat."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "it is clear that each mother bat can recognize the call of her pup.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is stated as clear."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Each mother bat can recognize a call."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The call is her pup''s."}]}], "the_read": "What if the child bats never leave the same place and the mothers just fly back to the same exact spot? What if it''s innate and they don''t need to recognize one another to find each other?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q8", "qnum": 8, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 9, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q9", "prompt": "Someone who gets sick from eating a meal will often develop a strong distaste for the one food in the meal that had the most distinctive flavor, whether or not that food caused the sickness. This phenomenon explains why children are especially likely to develop strong aversions to some foods.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A person becomes sick. · The sickness comes from eating a meal. · That person often develops a distaste. · The distaste is strong. · It is for one food in the meal. · That food had the most distinctive flavor. · This holds either way. · It does not matter whether that food caused the sickness. (2) The phenomenon explains something. · It is about children. · Children develop strong aversions. · The aversions are to some foods. · Children are especially likely to do this.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Someone who gets sick from eating a meal will often develop a strong distaste for the one food in the meal that had the most distinctive flavor, whether or not that food caused the sickness.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A person becomes sick."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The sickness comes from eating a meal."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That person often develops a distaste."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The distaste is strong."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is for one food in the meal."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That food had the most distinctive flavor."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This holds either way."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It does not matter whether that food caused the sickness."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This phenomenon explains why children are especially likely to develop strong aversions to some foods.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The phenomenon explains something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about children."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Children develop strong aversions."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The aversions are to some foods."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Children are especially likely to do this."}]}], "the_read": "What if children never get sick from eating meals?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q9", "qnum": 9, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 10, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q10", "prompt": "Premiums for automobile accident insurance are often higher for red cars than for cars of other colors. To justify these higher charges, insurance companies claim that, overall, a greater percentage of red cars are involved in accidents than are cars of any other color. If this claim is true, then lives could undoubtedly be saved by banning red cars from the roads altogether.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about insurance premiums. · The insurance is automobile accident insurance. · Premiums are higher for red cars. · The comparison is with cars of other colors. · This is often the case. (2) Insurance companies want to justify the charges. · They make a claim. · The claim is about a percentage. · A greater percentage of red cars are involved in accidents. · The comparison is with cars of any other color. · This is an overall figure. (3) This is conditional on the claim being true. · The conclusion is that lives could be saved. · The way to save them is banning red cars. · The ban would remove them from the roads. · The ban would be total, not partial. · The claim is made with certainty.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Premiums for automobile accident insurance are often higher for red cars than for cars of other colors.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about insurance premiums."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The insurance is automobile accident insurance."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Premiums are higher for red cars."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with cars of other colors."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is often the case."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "To justify these higher charges, insurance companies claim that, overall, a greater percentage of red cars are involved in accidents than are cars of any other color.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Insurance companies want to justify the charges."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They make a claim."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The claim is about a percentage."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A greater percentage of red cars are involved in accidents."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with cars of any other color."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is an overall figure."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "If this claim is true, then lives could undoubtedly be saved by banning red cars from the roads altogether.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is conditional on the claim being true."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The conclusion is that lives could be saved."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The way to save them is banning red cars."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The ban would remove them from the roads."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The ban would be total, not partial."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The claim is made with certainty."}]}], "the_read": "What if the color red does not cause the accidents? What if red cars tend to get in more accidents because reckless drivers tend to choose red cars?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 11, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q11", "prompt": "A certain credit-card company awards its customers bonus points for using its credit card. Customers can use accumulated points in the purchase of brand name merchandise by mail at prices lower than the manufacturers'' suggested retail prices. At any given time, therefore, customers who purchase merchandise using the bonus points spend less than they would spend if they purchased the same merchandise in retail stores.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a credit-card company. · It awards bonus points. · The points go to its customers. · They are awarded for using its credit card. (2) Customers accumulate the points. · They can use them to purchase merchandise. · The merchandise is brand name. · The purchase is by mail. · The prices are lower. · The comparison is with manufacturers'' suggested retail prices. (3) This is the conclusion. · It holds at any given time. · It is about customers who purchase using the bonus points. · Those customers spend less money. · The comparison is with what they would otherwise spend. · The alternative is buying the same merchandise in retail stores.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A certain credit-card company awards its customers bonus points for using its credit card.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a credit-card company."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It awards bonus points."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The points go to its customers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are awarded for using its credit card."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Customers can use accumulated points in the purchase of brand name merchandise by mail at prices lower than the manufacturers'' suggested retail prices.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Customers accumulate the points."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They can use them to purchase merchandise."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The merchandise is brand name."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The purchase is by mail."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The prices are lower."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with manufacturers'' suggested retail prices."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "At any given time, therefore, customers who purchase merchandise using the bonus points spend less than they would spend if they purchased the same merchandise in retail stores.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It holds at any given time."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about customers who purchase using the bonus points."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those customers spend less money."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with what they would otherwise spend."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The alternative is buying the same merchandise in retail stores."}]}], "the_read": "What if retail stores are always running sales that mark down brand name items lower than MSRP?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q11", "qnum": 11, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q12", "prompt": "It is probably not true that colic in infants is caused by the inability of those infants to tolerate certain antibodies found in cow''s milk, since it is often the case that symptoms of colic are shown by infants that are fed breast milk exclusively.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is the conclusion. · It is hedged as probably not true. · The claim being rejected is about colic in infants. · The claim is that colic is caused by an inability. · The inability is to tolerate certain antibodies. · Those antibodies are found in cow''s milk. (2) This is the reason. · This is often the case. · Symptoms of colic are shown. · They are shown by infants. · Those infants are fed breast milk. · They are fed breast milk exclusively.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "It is probably not true that colic in infants is caused by the inability of those infants to tolerate certain antibodies found in cow''s milk,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is hedged as probably not true."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The claim being rejected is about colic in infants."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The claim is that colic is caused by an inability."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The inability is to tolerate certain antibodies."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those antibodies are found in cow''s milk."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "since it is often the case that symptoms of colic are shown by infants that are fed breast milk exclusively.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is often the case."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Symptoms of colic are shown."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are shown by infants."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those infants are fed breast milk."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They are fed breast milk exclusively."}]}], "the_read": "What if cow''s milk and breast milk contain the same antibodies? What if colic can have multiple causes?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q12", "qnum": 12, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 13, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q13", "prompt": "Yolanda: Gaining access to computers without authorization and manipulating the data and programs they contain is comparable to joyriding in stolen cars; both involve breaking into private property and treating it recklessly. Joyriding, however, is the more dangerous crime because it physically endangers people, whereas only intellectual property is harmed in the case of computer crimes.\n\nArjun: I disagree! For example, unauthorized use of medical records systems in hospitals could damage data systems on which human lives depend, and therefore computer crimes also cause physical harm to people.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Yolanda is the first speaker. · She describes gaining access to computers. · The access is without authorization. · It also includes manipulating data and programs. · Those are what the computers contain. · She compares this to joyriding. · The joyriding is in stolen cars. (2) This is what the two share. · Both involve breaking in. · What is broken into is private property. · Both involve treating it recklessly. (3) This is a contrast. · Joyriding is the more dangerous of the two. · The reason is that it endangers people. · The danger is physical. · With computer crimes only intellectual property is harmed. (4) A second speaker begins. · The speaker is Arjun. · Arjun disagrees with what Yolanda said. (5) This is his example. · It is unauthorized use. · The use is of medical records systems. · Those systems are in hospitals. · Such use could damage data systems. · Human lives depend on those systems. (6) This is his conclusion. · It is about computer crimes. · They cause physical harm. · The harm is to people. · They do this too, not only joyriding.", "category": "Controversy", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Yolanda: Gaining access to computers without authorization and manipulating the data and programs they contain is comparable to joyriding in stolen cars;", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Yolanda is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "She describes gaining access to computers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The access is without authorization."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It also includes manipulating data and programs."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those are what the computers contain."}, {"n": 6, "text": "She compares this to joyriding."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The joyriding is in stolen cars."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "both involve breaking into private property and treating it recklessly.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is what the two share."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Both involve breaking in."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What is broken into is private property."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Both involve treating it recklessly."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Joyriding, however, is the more dangerous crime because it physically endangers people, whereas only intellectual property is harmed in the case of computer crimes.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Joyriding is the more dangerous of the two."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The reason is that it endangers people."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The danger is physical."}, {"n": 5, "text": "With computer crimes only intellectual property is harmed."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Arjun: I disagree!", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A second speaker begins."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The speaker is Arjun."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Arjun disagrees with what Yolanda said."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "For example, unauthorized use of medical records systems in hospitals could damage data systems on which human lives depend,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is his example."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is unauthorized use."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The use is of medical records systems."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those systems are in hospitals."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Such use could damage data systems."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Human lives depend on those systems."}]}, {"n": 6, "text": "and therefore computer crimes also cause physical harm to people.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is his conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about computer crimes."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They cause physical harm."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The harm is to people."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They do this too, not only joyriding."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether computer crimes only ever cause harm to intellectual property.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Controversy", "correct_answer": "D"}, {"citation": "PT105.S2.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 14, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q15", "prompt": "A report of a government survey concluded that Center City was among the ten cities in the nation with the highest dropout rate from its schools. The survey data were obtained by asking all city residents over the age of 19 whether they were high school graduates and computing the proportion who were not. A city school official objected that the result did not seem accurate according to the schools'' figures.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a report. · The report is of a government survey. · It reached a conclusion. · The conclusion is about Center City. · Center City was among ten cities. · Those ten had the highest dropout rate. · The rate is from its schools. · The comparison is nationwide. (2) This is how the data were obtained. · People were asked a question. · Those asked were all city residents. · They were over the age of 19. · The question was whether they were high school graduates. · A proportion was then computed. · The proportion was of those who were not. (3) Someone raised an objection. · The objector is a city school official. · The objection is that the result did not seem accurate. · The basis is the schools'' own figures.", "category": "Gap Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A report of a government survey concluded that Center City was among the ten cities in the nation with the highest dropout rate from its schools.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a report."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The report is of a government survey."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It reached a conclusion."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The conclusion is about Center City."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Center City was among ten cities."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those ten had the highest dropout rate."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The rate is from its schools."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The comparison is nationwide."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The survey data were obtained by asking all city residents over the age of 19 whether they were high school graduates and computing the proportion who were not.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is how the data were obtained."}, {"n": 2, "text": "People were asked a question."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those asked were all city residents."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They were over the age of 19."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The question was whether they were high school graduates."}, {"n": 6, "text": "A proportion was then computed."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The proportion was of those who were not."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "A city school official objected that the result did not seem accurate according to the schools'' figures.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Someone raised an objection."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The objector is a city school official."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The objection is that the result did not seem accurate."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The basis is the schools'' own figures."}]}], "the_read": "What if many of the residents over 19 had gone and dropped out of school in another city? What if most people who didn''t finish high school were expelled?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Gap Flaw", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q16", "prompt": "Brown dwarfs—dim red stars that are too cool to burn hydrogen—are very similar in appearance to red dwarf stars, which are just hot enough to burn hydrogen. Stars, when first formed, contain substantial amounts of the element lithium. All stars but the coolest of the brown dwarfs are hot enough to destroy lithium completely by converting it to helium. Accordingly, any star found that contains no lithium is not one of these coolest brown dwarfs.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Brown dwarfs are stars. · They are dim and red. · They are too cool to burn hydrogen. · They look very similar to red dwarf stars. · Red dwarfs are just hot enough to burn hydrogen. (2) This is about stars when first formed. · Newly formed stars contain lithium. · The amounts are substantial. (3) Almost all stars are hot enough to destroy lithium. · The exception is the coolest of the brown dwarfs. · The destruction is complete. · It happens by converting lithium to helium. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is about any star found to contain no lithium. · Such a star is not one of these coolest brown dwarfs.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Brown dwarfs—dim red stars that are too cool to burn hydrogen—are very similar in appearance to red dwarf stars, which are just hot enough to burn hydrogen.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Brown dwarfs are stars."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are dim and red."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are too cool to burn hydrogen."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They look very similar to red dwarf stars."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Red dwarfs are just hot enough to burn hydrogen."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Stars, when first formed, contain substantial amounts of the element lithium.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about stars when first formed."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Newly formed stars contain lithium."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The amounts are substantial."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "All stars but the coolest of the brown dwarfs are hot enough to destroy lithium completely by converting it to helium.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Almost all stars are hot enough to destroy lithium."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The exception is the coolest of the brown dwarfs."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The destruction is complete."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It happens by converting lithium to helium."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Accordingly, any star found that contains no lithium is not one of these coolest brown dwarfs.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about any star found to contain no lithium."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Such a star is not one of these coolest brown dwarfs."}]}], "the_read": "What if at one point in their lives the coolest brown dwarfs were hot enough to burn lithium? What if the coolest brown dwarfs were not always this way?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q17", "prompt": "Whenever a company loses a major product-liability lawsuit, the value of the company''s stocks falls significantly within hours after the announcement. Cotoy has long been involved in a major product-liability lawsuit, and its stocks fell significantly in value today. Therefore, we can be sure that an unfavorable judgment against Cotoy in that lawsuit was announced earlier today.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is a rule about companies. · The trigger is losing a lawsuit. · The lawsuit is a major product-liability one. · The company''s stock value falls. · The fall is significant. · It happens within hours. · It happens after the announcement. (2) Cotoy is involved in a lawsuit. · The lawsuit is a major product-liability one. · The involvement has been long. · Cotoy''s stocks fell. · The fall was significant. · The fall happened today. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is stated with certainty. · A judgment was announced. · The judgment was unfavorable. · It was against Cotoy. · It was in that lawsuit. · The announcement was earlier today.", "category": "Parallel Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Whenever a company loses a major product-liability lawsuit, the value of the company''s stocks falls significantly within hours after the announcement.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a rule about companies."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The trigger is losing a lawsuit."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The lawsuit is a major product-liability one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The company''s stock value falls."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The fall is significant."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It happens within hours."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It happens after the announcement."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Cotoy has long been involved in a major product-liability lawsuit, and its stocks fell significantly in value today.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Cotoy is involved in a lawsuit."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The lawsuit is a major product-liability one."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The involvement has been long."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Cotoy''s stocks fell."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The fall was significant."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The fall happened today."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, we can be sure that an unfavorable judgment against Cotoy in that lawsuit was announced earlier today.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is stated with certainty."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A judgment was announced."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The judgment was unfavorable."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It was against Cotoy."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It was in that lawsuit."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The announcement was earlier today."}]}], "the_read": "What if other situations can cause a company''s stocks to fall significantly in value? What if we can''t use bad conditional reasoning?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q17", "qnum": 17, "question_type": "Parallel Flaw", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 17, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q18", "prompt": "In recent years the climate has been generally cool in northern Asia. But during periods when the average daily temperature and humidity in northern Asia were slightly higher than their normal levels the yields of most crops grown there increased significantly. In the next century, the increased average daily temperature and humidity attained during those periods are expected to become the norm. Yet scientists predict that the yearly yields of most of the region''s crops will decrease during the next century.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about recent years. · The place is northern Asia. · The climate there has been generally cool. (2) This is a contrast. · There were periods of higher temperature and humidity. · The measure is the average daily level. · The rise above normal was slight. · The yields of crops increased. · The increase was significant. · It applied to most crops grown there. (3) This is about the next century. · Those higher levels are expected to become the norm. · They are the levels reached during those periods. (4) This is the puzzle. · Scientists make a prediction. · The yields will decrease. · The yields are yearly. · It applies to most of the region''s crops. · This is during the next century.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In recent years the climate has been generally cool in northern Asia.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about recent years."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The place is northern Asia."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The climate there has been generally cool."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But during periods when the average daily temperature and humidity in northern Asia were slightly higher than their normal levels the yields of most crops grown there increased significantly.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There were periods of higher temperature and humidity."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The measure is the average daily level."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The rise above normal was slight."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The yields of crops increased."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The increase was significant."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It applied to most crops grown there."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "In the next century, the increased average daily temperature and humidity attained during those periods are expected to become the norm.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the next century."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those higher levels are expected to become the norm."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are the levels reached during those periods."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Yet scientists predict that the yearly yields of most of the region''s crops will decrease during the next century.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the puzzle."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Scientists make a prediction."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The yields will decrease."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The yields are yearly."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It applies to most of the region''s crops."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is during the next century."}]}], "the_read": "What if sustained high temperatures will cause an increase in crop pests?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q18", "qnum": 18, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 18, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q19", "prompt": "No one in the French department to which Professor Alban belongs is allowed to teach more than one introductory level class in any one term. Moreover, the only language classes being taught next term are advanced ones. So it is untrue that both of the French classes Professor Alban will be teaching next term will be introductory level classes.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a rule. · It applies to a French department. · Professor Alban belongs to it. · No one there may teach more than one introductory level class. · The limit is per term. (2) This is a second premise. · It is about next term. · The only language classes being taught are advanced ones. (3) This is the conclusion. · It denies a claim. · The claim is about two French classes. · Professor Alban will teach them next term. · The claim is that both will be introductory level.", "category": "Parallel Reasoning", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "No one in the French department to which Professor Alban belongs is allowed to teach more than one introductory level class in any one term.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a rule."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It applies to a French department."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Professor Alban belongs to it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "No one there may teach more than one introductory level class."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The limit is per term."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Moreover, the only language classes being taught next term are advanced ones.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a second premise."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about next term."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The only language classes being taught are advanced ones."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "So it is untrue that both of the French classes Professor Alban will be teaching next term will be introductory level classes.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It denies a claim."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The claim is about two French classes."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Professor Alban will teach them next term."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The claim is that both will be introductory level."}]}], "the_read": "What if Professor Alban is teaching a language class at a different school next term?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q19", "qnum": 19, "question_type": "Parallel Reasoning", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 19, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q20", "prompt": "Some people have been promoting a new herbal mixture as a remedy for the common cold. The mixture contains, among other things, extracts of the plants purple coneflower and goldenseal. A cold sufferer, skeptical of the claim that the mixture is an effective cold remedy, argued, \"Suppose that the mixture were an effective cold remedy. Since most people with colds wish to recover quickly, it follows that almost everybody with a cold would be using it. Therefore, since there are many people who have colds but do not use the mixture, it is obviously not effective.\"", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Some people are promoting something. · What they promote is a herbal mixture. · The mixture is new. · They promote it as a remedy. · The remedy is for the common cold. (2) The mixture contains extracts. · The extracts are of plants. · One plant is purple coneflower. · The other is goldenseal. · It contains other things too. (3) A cold sufferer makes an argument. · The sufferer is skeptical. · The skepticism is about the claim that the mixture is an effective cold remedy. · The argument opens with a supposition. · The supposition is that the mixture were effective. (4) This is a premise. · Most people with colds wish to recover quickly. · A consequence is drawn from it. · Almost everybody with a cold would be using it. (5) This is the conclusion. · There are many people who have colds. · Those people do not use the mixture. · The mixture is not effective. · The speaker treats this as obvious.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some people have been promoting a new herbal mixture as a remedy for the common cold.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some people are promoting something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "What they promote is a herbal mixture."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The mixture is new."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They promote it as a remedy."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The remedy is for the common cold."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The mixture contains, among other things, extracts of the plants purple coneflower and goldenseal.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The mixture contains extracts."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The extracts are of plants."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One plant is purple coneflower."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other is goldenseal."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It contains other things too."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "A cold sufferer, skeptical of the claim that the mixture is an effective cold remedy, argued, \"Suppose that the mixture were an effective cold remedy.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A cold sufferer makes an argument."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The sufferer is skeptical."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The skepticism is about the claim that the mixture is an effective cold remedy."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The argument opens with a supposition."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The supposition is that the mixture were effective."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Since most people with colds wish to recover quickly, it follows that almost everybody with a cold would be using it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a premise."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Most people with colds wish to recover quickly."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A consequence is drawn from it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Almost everybody with a cold would be using it."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Therefore, since there are many people who have colds but do not use the mixture, it is obviously not effective.\"", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There are many people who have colds."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those people do not use the mixture."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The mixture is not effective."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The speaker treats this as obvious."}]}], "the_read": "What if very few people know about the remedy? What if there are other, better, more widely known remedies?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q20", "qnum": 20, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "C"}, {"citation": "PT105.S2.Q21", "qnum": 21, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 20, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q22", "prompt": "To hold criminals responsible for their crimes involves a failure to recognize that criminal actions, like all actions, are ultimately products of the environment that forged the agent''s character. It is not criminals but people in the law-abiding majority who by their actions do most to create and maintain this environment. Therefore, it is law-abiding people whose actions, and nothing else, make them alone truly responsible for crime.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about holding criminals responsible. · They are held responsible for their crimes. · Doing so involves a failure. · The failure is to recognize something. · What is not recognized concerns criminal actions. (2) Criminal actions are like all actions. · They are products of the environment. · This is ultimately so. · The environment forged the agent''s character. (3) Criminals are not the ones who do most. · The ones who do are people in the law-abiding majority. · They do it by their actions. · What they do most is create and maintain this environment. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is about law-abiding people. · Their actions make them responsible. · Nothing other than their actions does. · They alone are responsible. · The responsibility is true responsibility. · It is responsibility for crime.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "To hold criminals responsible for their crimes involves a failure to recognize that criminal actions,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about holding criminals responsible."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are held responsible for their crimes."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Doing so involves a failure."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The failure is to recognize something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What is not recognized concerns criminal actions."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "like all actions, are ultimately products of the environment that forged the agent''s character.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Criminal actions are like all actions."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are products of the environment."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is ultimately so."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The environment forged the agent''s character."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is not criminals but people in the law-abiding majority who by their actions do most to create and maintain this environment.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Criminals are not the ones who do most."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The ones who do are people in the law-abiding majority."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They do it by their actions."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What they do most is create and maintain this environment."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Therefore, it is law-abiding people whose actions, and nothing else, make them alone truly responsible for crime.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about law-abiding people."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Their actions make them responsible."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Nothing other than their actions does."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They alone are responsible."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The responsibility is true responsibility."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It is responsibility for crime."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because someone is a product of their environment, that doesn''t mean their environment is solely responsible for everything they do?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q22", "qnum": 22, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 21, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q23", "prompt": "Chronic back pain is usually caused by a herniated or degenerated spinal disk. In most cases the disk will have been damaged years before chronic pain develops, and in fact an estimated one in five people over the age of 30 has a herniated or degenerated disk that shows no chronic symptoms. If chronic pain later develops in such a case, it is generally brought about by a deterioration of the abdominal and spinal muscles caused by insufficient exercise.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about chronic back pain. · It usually has one cause. · The cause is a spinal disk. · The disk is herniated or degenerated. (2) This is true in most cases. · The disk was damaged earlier. · The damage came years earlier. · It came before the chronic pain developed. (3) This is an estimate. · It is one in five people. · Those people are over the age of 30. · They have a herniated or degenerated disk. · That disk shows no chronic symptoms. (4) This is conditional on chronic pain developing later. · It concerns such a case. · The pain is generally brought about by a deterioration. · The deterioration is of muscles. · The muscles are abdominal and spinal. · The deterioration is caused by insufficient exercise.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Chronic back pain is usually caused by a herniated or degenerated spinal disk.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about chronic back pain."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It usually has one cause."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The cause is a spinal disk."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The disk is herniated or degenerated."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "In most cases the disk will have been damaged years before chronic pain develops,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is true in most cases."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The disk was damaged earlier."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The damage came years earlier."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It came before the chronic pain developed."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "and in fact an estimated one in five people over the age of 30 has a herniated or degenerated disk that shows no chronic symptoms.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an estimate."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is one in five people."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those people are over the age of 30."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They have a herniated or degenerated disk."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That disk shows no chronic symptoms."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "If chronic pain later develops in such a case, it is generally brought about by a deterioration of the abdominal and spinal muscles caused by insufficient exercise.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is conditional on chronic pain developing later."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It concerns such a case."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The pain is generally brought about by a deterioration."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The deterioration is of muscles."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The muscles are abdominal and spinal."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The deterioration is caused by insufficient exercise."}]}], "the_read": "People may be able to take actions that prevent herniated or degenerated disks from becoming painful.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q23", "qnum": 23, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 22, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q24", "prompt": "Each December 31 in Country Q, a tally is made of the country''s total available coal supplies—that is, the total amount of coal that has been mined throughout the country but not consumed. In 1991 that amount was considerably lower than it had been in 1990. Furthermore, Country Q has not imported or exported coal since 1970.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This happens each December 31. · The place is Country Q. · A tally is made. · The tally is of total available coal supplies. · They are the country''s. · A definition of that term follows. (2) This is the definition. · It is the total amount of coal. · The coal has been mined. · It was mined throughout the country. · The coal has not been consumed. (3) This is about 1991. · The amount was lower. · It was considerably lower than in 1990. (4) This is an additional fact. · Country Q has not imported coal. · It has not exported coal. · This has held since 1970.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Each December 31 in Country Q, a tally is made of the country''s total available coal supplies—that is,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This happens each December 31."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The place is Country Q."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A tally is made."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The tally is of total available coal supplies."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They are the country''s."}, {"n": 6, "text": "A definition of that term follows."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "the total amount of coal that has been mined throughout the country but not consumed.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the definition."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is the total amount of coal."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The coal has been mined."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It was mined throughout the country."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The coal has not been consumed."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "In 1991 that amount was considerably lower than it had been in 1990.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about 1991."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The amount was lower."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It was considerably lower than in 1990."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Furthermore, Country Q has not imported or exported coal since 1970.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional fact."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Country Q has not imported coal."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It has not exported coal."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This has held since 1970."}]}], "the_read": "More coal was consumed than was mined in 1991 in Country Q.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q24", "qnum": 24, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 23, "slug": "PT105_S2_Q25", "prompt": "Tom: Employers complain that people graduating from high school too often lack the vocational skills required for full-time employment. Therefore, since these skills are best acquired on the job, we should require high school students to work at part-time jobs so that they acquire the skills needed for today''s job market.\n\nMary: There are already too few part-time jobs for students who want to work, and simply requiring students to work will not create jobs for them.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Tom is the first speaker. · Employers are making a complaint. · The complaint is about people graduating from high school. · Those people lack vocational skills. · The skills are required for full-time employment. · This happens too often. (2) This begins his conclusion. · It rests on a premise. · The premise is that these skills are best acquired on the job. (3) This is his recommendation. · High school students should be required to work. · The work is part-time jobs. · The purpose is that they acquire skills. · The skills are needed for today''s job market. (4) Mary is the second speaker. · There are already too few part-time jobs. · They are too few for students who want to work. · Simply requiring students to work will not create jobs. · It will not create jobs for them.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Tom: Employers complain that people graduating from high school too often lack the vocational skills required for full-time employment.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Tom is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Employers are making a complaint."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The complaint is about people graduating from high school."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those people lack vocational skills."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The skills are required for full-time employment."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This happens too often."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Therefore, since these skills are best acquired on the job,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This begins his conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on a premise."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The premise is that these skills are best acquired on the job."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "we should require high school students to work at part-time jobs so that they acquire the skills needed for today''s job market.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is his recommendation."}, {"n": 2, "text": "High school students should be required to work."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The work is part-time jobs."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The purpose is that they acquire skills."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The skills are needed for today''s job market."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Mary: There are already too few part-time jobs for students who want to work, and simply requiring students to work will not create jobs for them.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Mary is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There are already too few part-time jobs."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are too few for students who want to work."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Simply requiring students to work will not create jobs."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It will not create jobs for them."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether high school students should be required to work part time.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT105.S2.Q25", "qnum": 25, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "D"}]}]'::jsonb,
    'section',
    '{"standard": 25, "accommodation_53": 38, "accommodation_70": 50}'::jsonb,
    'PT105.S2'
  ),
  (
    'read-recall-colour-pt106-s2',
    'Read, Recall, Colour · PT106 Section 2',
    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, and react to it. Once you finish the stimulus, say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, then give it a reaction. After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears, and you recall the whole argument out loud from memory.", "rules": ["Read each piece silently. Everything you say back, including your reaction and final recall, is out loud.", "Read about a sentence at a time. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said.", "Then colour it: react to that specific piece with a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling.", "Repeat these steps through the whole stimulus.", "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and stays gone. Say the entire argument back from memory, with every detail you can still hold.", "Press space again to move on. The whole section records in one take, with no pauses or breaks."], "briefing": {"lede": "Read these instructions once before your first section. After that, come back when you need a reminder.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still reach the answers remembering almost none of it.", "body": ["You can follow a stimulus sentence by sentence and understand every part. Then you reach answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and that is what this drill trains.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. Understanding the stimulus is not enough if it is gone by the time you reach the answers.", "Most everyday reading trains you to reach the end, not retain every detail. You scan names, drop connecting words, and turn a list of three things into one vague label. That works for a phone bill. It does not work for the LSAT."]}, {"heading": "How the method works", "body": ["For each piece, read it, say it back, and colour it. Once you finish the stimulus, recall the whole argument. You decide how much to read at a time, and nothing on the screen decides for you.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it", "body": "Read about a sentence silently. Give every word enough attention to hear the sentence naturally in your head. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less. If you take on too much, the beginning is usually what disappears first."}, {"label": "Tell it back", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it", "body": "React to what you just said. Give it a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling. Keep it short, but make it specific. If the same reaction could fit any sentence, it does not count."}, {"label": "Recall it", "body": "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Say the entire argument out loud from memory, with every detail you can still hold. This is what gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter", "body": ["Do not give back only the gist or what seemed important. You cannot know which details will matter until you have read the whole stimulus. Filtering as you read is how useful information disappears.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. Names, numbers, and small words carrying the load, such as all, most, only, and probably, have to come back too.", "**Being efficient does not mean leaving information out.**"]}, {"heading": "When nothing comes to mind", "body": ["You may try to colour a sentence and find that nothing comes to mind. Usually, the hidden thought is that nothing feels good enough to say.", "Do not judge the reaction before you say it. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count when they connect to what you just read.", "The reaction makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away from the text", "body": ["The text stays visible while you say each piece back. You have to look away from it yourself.", "If you read while you recall, you stop using your short-term memory and slide back into the author''s exact words. Looking away forces you to rebuild the meaning in your own words.", "This is also the version that works on paper and on test day."]}, {"heading": "Build toward twenty-five minutes", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section. It only counts when you read every stimulus, say each piece back, colour it, and recall the whole argument. Finishing early by skipping parts is not finishing early. If you receive extra time on test day, use your full allotted time here too.", "The clock counts up so you can track your pace without watching your time disappear.", "Do the method correctly before you try to do it quickly. Go slower while it is new. Speed follows once the method becomes automatic."]}], "closer": "One section keeps the method steady. Improvement starts with the second."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, tells it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives The Recall of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Recall against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q1", "prompt": "Critic: People today place an especially high value on respect for others; yet, in their comedy acts, many of today''s most popular comedians display blatant disrespect for others. But when people fail to live up to the very ideals they hold in highest esteem, exaggeration of such failings often forms the basis of successful comedy. Thus the current popularity of comedians who display disrespect in their acts is hardly surprising.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The speaker is a critic. · People today place a value on respect for others. · The value they place is especially high. · This part contrasts with what came before. · Many comedians display disrespect for others. · Those comedians are among today''s most popular ones. · The disrespect they display is blatant. · This happens in their comedy acts. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · People sometimes fail to live up to ideals. · The ideals are ones they hold in highest esteem. · Exaggeration of such failings forms the basis of comedy. · The comedy in question is successful comedy. · This often happens rather than always. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about the popularity of certain comedians. · That popularity is current. · Those comedians display disrespect in their acts. · The popularity is hardly surprising.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Critic: People today place an especially high value on respect for others; yet, in their comedy acts, many of today''s most popular comedians display blatant disrespect for others.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The speaker is a critic."}, {"n": 2, "text": "People today place a value on respect for others."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The value they place is especially high."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This part contrasts with what came before."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Many comedians display disrespect for others."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those comedians are among today''s most popular ones."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The disrespect they display is blatant."}, {"n": 8, "text": "This happens in their comedy acts."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But when people fail to live up to the very ideals they hold in highest esteem, exaggeration of such failings often forms the basis of successful comedy.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "People sometimes fail to live up to ideals."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The ideals are ones they hold in highest esteem."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Exaggeration of such failings forms the basis of comedy."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comedy in question is successful comedy."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This often happens rather than always."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Thus the current popularity of comedians who display disrespect in their acts is hardly surprising.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the popularity of certain comedians."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That popularity is current."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those comedians display disrespect in their acts."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The popularity is hardly surprising."}]}], "the_read": "What if people never fail to live up to the ideal of being respectful towards others?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q2", "prompt": "The law firm of Sutherlin, Pérez, and Associates is one of the most successful law firms whose primary specialization is in criminal defense cases. In fact, the firm has a better than 90 percent acquittal rate in such cases. Dalton is an attorney whose primary specialization is in divorce cases, so Dalton certainly cannot be a member of Sutherlin, Pérez, and Associates.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Sutherlin, Pérez, and Associates is a law firm. · That firm is among the most successful ones. · The firms it is ranked among have a primary specialization. · That specialization is in criminal defense cases. (2) This adds support to the previous point. · The firm has an acquittal rate. · That rate is better than 90 percent. · The rate applies to criminal defense cases. (3) Dalton is an attorney. · Dalton''s primary specialization is in divorce cases. · This is the conclusion drawn from that. · Dalton cannot be a member of the firm. · The firm is Sutherlin, Pérez, and Associates. · The claim is made with certainty.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The law firm of Sutherlin, Pérez, and Associates is one of the most successful law firms whose primary specialization is in criminal defense cases.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Sutherlin, Pérez, and Associates is a law firm."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That firm is among the most successful ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The firms it is ranked among have a primary specialization."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That specialization is in criminal defense cases."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "In fact, the firm has a better than 90 percent acquittal rate in such cases.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This adds support to the previous point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The firm has an acquittal rate."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That rate is better than 90 percent."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The rate applies to criminal defense cases."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Dalton is an attorney whose primary specialization is in divorce cases, so Dalton certainly cannot be a member of Sutherlin, Pérez, and Associates.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Dalton is an attorney."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Dalton''s primary specialization is in divorce cases."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is the conclusion drawn from that."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Dalton cannot be a member of the firm."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The firm is Sutherlin, Pérez, and Associates."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The claim is made with certainty."}]}], "the_read": "What if the firm practices other areas of law as well?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q3", "prompt": "Opponents of allowing triple-trailer trucks to use the national highway system are wrong in claiming that these trucks are more dangerous than other commercial vehicles. In the western part of the country, in areas where triple-trailers are now permitted on some highways, for these vehicles the rate of road accident fatalities per mile of travel is lower than the national rate for other types of commercial vehicles. Clearly, triple-trailers are safer than other commercial vehicles.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Some people oppose allowing triple-trailer trucks on the highways. · The highway system in question is the national one. · Those opponents claim these trucks are more dangerous. · The comparison is with other commercial vehicles. · The opponents are wrong in that claim. (2) This is about the western part of the country. · It concerns certain areas there. · Triple-trailers are permitted in those areas. · They are permitted on some highways only. · This permission holds now. (3) This is about those vehicles. · There is a rate of road accident fatalities. · The rate is measured per mile of travel. · That rate is lower than another rate. · The comparison is with the national rate. · The national rate covers other types of commercial vehicles. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is presented as clear. · Triple-trailers are safer. · The comparison is with other commercial vehicles.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Opponents of allowing triple-trailer trucks to use the national highway system are wrong in claiming that these trucks are more dangerous than other commercial vehicles.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some people oppose allowing triple-trailer trucks on the highways."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The highway system in question is the national one."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those opponents claim these trucks are more dangerous."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with other commercial vehicles."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The opponents are wrong in that claim."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "In the western part of the country, in areas where triple-trailers are now permitted on some highways,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the western part of the country."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It concerns certain areas there."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Triple-trailers are permitted in those areas."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are permitted on some highways only."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This permission holds now."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "for these vehicles the rate of road accident fatalities per mile of travel is lower than the national rate for other types of commercial vehicles.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about those vehicles."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a rate of road accident fatalities."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The rate is measured per mile of travel."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That rate is lower than another rate."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with the national rate."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The national rate covers other types of commercial vehicles."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Clearly, triple-trailers are safer than other commercial vehicles.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is presented as clear."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Triple-trailers are safer."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with other commercial vehicles."}]}], "the_read": "What if there are hardly any cars driven on Western highways, so they''re generally much safer than most national highways?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q4", "prompt": "Whittaker: There can be no such thing as the number of medical school students who drop out before their second year, because if they drop out, they never have a second year.\n\nHudson: By your reasoning I cannot help but become rich, because there is similarly no such thing as my dying before my first million dollars is in the bank.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Whittaker is the first speaker. · He says a certain number cannot exist. · The number would be of medical school students. · Those students drop out before their second year. · This is his reason. · Students who drop out never have a second year. (2) Hudson is the second speaker. · He applies Whittaker''s reasoning. · He says he cannot help becoming rich. · This is his reason. · There is similarly no such thing as his dying. · The dying would come before he had a million dollars. · That would be his first million dollars in the bank.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Whittaker: There can be no such thing as the number of medical school students who drop out before their second year, because if they drop out, they never have a second year.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Whittaker is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He says a certain number cannot exist."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The number would be of medical school students."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those students drop out before their second year."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is his reason."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Students who drop out never have a second year."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Hudson: By your reasoning I cannot help but become rich, because there is similarly no such thing as my dying before my first million dollars is in the bank.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Hudson is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He applies Whittaker''s reasoning."}, {"n": 3, "text": "He says he cannot help becoming rich."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is his reason."}, {"n": 5, "text": "There is similarly no such thing as his dying."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The dying would come before he had a million dollars."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That would be his first million dollars in the bank."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether events may be adequately referred to hypothetically.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q5", "prompt": "A newly developed light bulb is much more cost-effective than conventional light bulbs: it costs only about 3 times what a conventional light bulb costs but it lasts up to 10 times as long as a conventional light bulb. Despite the manufacturer''s intense efforts to publicize the advantages of the new bulb, one analyst predicts that these new bulbs will prove to sell very poorly.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a light bulb that was newly developed. · That bulb is more cost-effective than other bulbs. · It is much more cost-effective. · The comparison is with conventional light bulbs. (2) The new bulb costs about 3 times what a conventional one costs. · That cost is presented as only that much. · This is a contrast with what came before. · The new bulb lasts longer than a conventional one. · It lasts up to 10 times as long. · The comparison is with a conventional light bulb. (3) The manufacturer made efforts to publicize the bulb. · Those efforts were intense. · What is publicized is the advantages of the new bulb. · This is a concession that runs against what follows. · An analyst makes a prediction. · It is one analyst. · The prediction is that the new bulbs will sell poorly. · They will sell very poorly.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A newly developed light bulb is much more cost-effective than conventional light bulbs:", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a light bulb that was newly developed."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That bulb is more cost-effective than other bulbs."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is much more cost-effective."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with conventional light bulbs."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "it costs only about 3 times what a conventional light bulb costs but it lasts up to 10 times as long as a conventional light bulb.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The new bulb costs about 3 times what a conventional one costs."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That cost is presented as only that much."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The new bulb lasts longer than a conventional one."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It lasts up to 10 times as long."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with a conventional light bulb."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Despite the manufacturer''s intense efforts to publicize the advantages of the new bulb, one analyst predicts that these new bulbs will prove to sell very poorly.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The manufacturer made efforts to publicize the bulb."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those efforts were intense."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What is publicized is the advantages of the new bulb."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a concession that runs against what follows."}, {"n": 5, "text": "An analyst makes a prediction."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It is one analyst."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The prediction is that the new bulbs will sell poorly."}, {"n": 8, "text": "They will sell very poorly."}]}], "the_read": "What if the new lightbulb has Secret Downsides like safety concerns or lack of compatibility with most fixtures? What if all consumers care about is the upfront cost of the lightbulb rather than how long it lasts?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q6", "prompt": "The Rienzi, a passenger ship, sank as a result of a hole in its hull, possibly caused by sabotage. Normally, when a holed ship sinks as rapidly as the Rienzi did, water does not enter the ship quickly enough for the ship to be fully flooded when it reaches the ocean floor. Full flooding can be achieved, however, by sabotage. Any ship that sinks deep into the ocean when not fully flooded will implode. Deep-sea photographs, taken of the sunken Rienzi where it rests on the ocean floor, reveal that the Rienzi did not implode.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The Rienzi is a ship. · It is a passenger ship. · The Rienzi sank as a result of something. · There was a hole in its hull. · The hole may have been caused by sabotage. · That cause is only a possibility. (2) This describes what normally happens. · It concerns a ship with a hole in it. · The ship sinks as rapidly as the Rienzi did. · Water does not enter such a ship quickly enough. · The water is not enough to fully flood the ship. · That is its state when it reaches the ocean floor. (3) This is a contrast with what came before. · Full flooding can be achieved. · Sabotage is what can achieve it. (4) This is about ships that sink deep into the ocean. · It applies to any such ship. · The ship in question is not fully flooded. · Such a ship will implode. (5) There are photographs of the sunken Rienzi. · The photographs were taken deep in the sea. · The Rienzi rests on the ocean floor. · The photographs reveal something. · The Rienzi did not implode.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The Rienzi, a passenger ship, sank as a result of a hole in its hull, possibly caused by sabotage.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The Rienzi is a ship."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is a passenger ship."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The Rienzi sank as a result of something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There was a hole in its hull."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The hole may have been caused by sabotage."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That cause is only a possibility."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Normally, when a holed ship sinks as rapidly as the Rienzi did, water does not enter the ship quickly enough for the ship to be fully flooded when it reaches the ocean floor.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This describes what normally happens."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It concerns a ship with a hole in it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The ship sinks as rapidly as the Rienzi did."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Water does not enter such a ship quickly enough."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The water is not enough to fully flood the ship."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That is its state when it reaches the ocean floor."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Full flooding can be achieved, however, by sabotage.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Full flooding can be achieved."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Sabotage is what can achieve it."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Any ship that sinks deep into the ocean when not fully flooded will implode.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about ships that sink deep into the ocean."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It applies to any such ship."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The ship in question is not fully flooded."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Such a ship will implode."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Deep-sea photographs, taken of the sunken Rienzi where it rests on the ocean floor, reveal that the Rienzi did not implode.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There are photographs of the sunken Rienzi."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The photographs were taken deep in the sea."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The Rienzi rests on the ocean floor."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The photographs reveal something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The Rienzi did not implode."}]}], "the_read": "If water did not enter the Rienzi abnormally quickly, it was sabotaged.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q7", "prompt": "For every 50 dogs that contract a certain disease, one will die from it. A vaccine exists that is virtually 100 percent effective in preventing this disease. Since the risk of death from complications of vaccination is one death per 5,000 vaccinations, it is therefore safer for a dog to receive the vaccine than not to receive it.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a certain disease. · Dogs contract that disease. · Out of every 50 such dogs, one will die. · The dog dies from that disease. (2) A vaccine exists for this disease. · The vaccine prevents the disease. · It is effective in virtually all cases. · That is close to 100 percent effective. (3) This gives a reason and then a conclusion. · Vaccination carries a risk of death. · The death would come from complications of vaccination. · The risk is one death per 5,000 vaccinations. · It is safer for a dog to receive the vaccine. · The comparison is with not receiving it.", "category": "Evaluate", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "For every 50 dogs that contract a certain disease, one will die from it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a certain disease."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Dogs contract that disease."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Out of every 50 such dogs, one will die."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The dog dies from that disease."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "A vaccine exists that is virtually 100 percent effective in preventing this disease.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A vaccine exists for this disease."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The vaccine prevents the disease."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is effective in virtually all cases."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That is close to 100 percent effective."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Since the risk of death from complications of vaccination is one death per 5,000 vaccinations, it is therefore safer for a dog to receive the vaccine than not to receive it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason and then a conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Vaccination carries a risk of death."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The death would come from complications of vaccination."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The risk is one death per 5,000 vaccinations."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is safer for a dog to receive the vaccine."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with not receiving it."}]}], "the_read": "What if the vaccine causes debilitating complications in every dog that receives it?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Evaluate", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q8", "prompt": "The symptoms of mental disorders are behavioral, cognitive, or emotional problems. Some patients with mental disorders can be effectively treated with psychotherapy. But it is now known that in some patients mental disorders result from chemical imbalances affecting the brain. Thus these patients can be effectively treated only with medication that will reduce or correct the imbalance.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Mental disorders have symptoms. · Those symptoms are problems. · Some of the problems are behavioral. · Some of them are cognitive. · Some of them are emotional. (2) This is about patients with mental disorders. · Some of those patients can be treated. · The treatment is psychotherapy. · The treatment is effective for them. (3) This is a contrast with what came before. · Something is now known. · It concerns some patients rather than all. · Their mental disorders result from chemical imbalances. · The imbalances affect the brain. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is about those patients. · They can be effectively treated with medication. · Medication is the only thing that works for them. · The medication reduces or corrects the imbalance.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The symptoms of mental disorders are behavioral, cognitive, or emotional problems.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Mental disorders have symptoms."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those symptoms are problems."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Some of the problems are behavioral."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Some of them are cognitive."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Some of them are emotional."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some patients with mental disorders can be effectively treated with psychotherapy.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about patients with mental disorders."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some of those patients can be treated."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The treatment is psychotherapy."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The treatment is effective for them."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "But it is now known that in some patients mental disorders result from chemical imbalances affecting the brain.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Something is now known."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It concerns some patients rather than all."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Their mental disorders result from chemical imbalances."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The imbalances affect the brain."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Thus these patients can be effectively treated only with medication that will reduce or correct the imbalance.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about those patients."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They can be effectively treated with medication."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Medication is the only thing that works for them."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The medication reduces or corrects the imbalance."}]}], "the_read": "What if psychotherapy can correct chemical imbalances in the brain?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q8", "qnum": 8, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 9, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q9", "prompt": "Curator: The decision to restore the cloak of the central figure in Veronese''s painting from its present red to the green found underneath is fully justified. Reliable x-ray and chemical tests show that the red pigment was applied after the painting had been completed, and that the red paint was not mixed in Veronese''s workshop. Hence it appears likely that an artist other than Veronese tampered with Veronese''s painting after its completion.\n\nArt critic: But in a copy of Veronese''s painting made shortly after Veronese died, the cloak is red. It is highly unlikely that a copyist would have made so major a change so soon after Veronese''s death.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The curator is the first speaker. · A decision has been made about a painting. · The painting is Veronese''s. · The decision concerns the cloak of the central figure. · The cloak is presently red. · The restoration would change it to green. · The green was found underneath the red. · The curator says the decision is fully justified. (2) Tests were done on the painting. · The tests were x-ray and chemical ones. · The tests are reliable. · The red pigment was applied to the painting. · It was applied after the painting was completed. · The red paint was not mixed in Veronese''s workshop. (3) This is the conclusion. · Something appears likely rather than certain. · An artist tampered with the painting. · That artist was someone other than Veronese. · The tampering came after the painting was completed. (4) The art critic is the second speaker. · This is a contrast with the curator. · A copy of the painting was made. · The copy was made shortly after Veronese died. · In that copy the cloak is red. (5) A copyist might have made a change. · The change would have been a major one. · It would have come soon after Veronese''s death. · That timing counts as very soon. · Such a change is highly unlikely.", "category": "Argument Part", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Curator: The decision to restore the cloak of the central figure in Veronese''s painting from its present red to the green found underneath is fully justified.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The curator is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A decision has been made about a painting."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The painting is Veronese''s."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The decision concerns the cloak of the central figure."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cloak is presently red."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The restoration would change it to green."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The green was found underneath the red."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The curator says the decision is fully justified."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Reliable x-ray and chemical tests show that the red pigment was applied after the painting had been completed, and that the red paint was not mixed in Veronese''s workshop.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Tests were done on the painting."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The tests were x-ray and chemical ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The tests are reliable."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The red pigment was applied to the painting."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It was applied after the painting was completed."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The red paint was not mixed in Veronese''s workshop."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Hence it appears likely that an artist other than Veronese tampered with Veronese''s painting after its completion.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Something appears likely rather than certain."}, {"n": 3, "text": "An artist tampered with the painting."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That artist was someone other than Veronese."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The tampering came after the painting was completed."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Art critic: But in a copy of Veronese''s painting made shortly after Veronese died, the cloak is red.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The art critic is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is a contrast with the curator."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A copy of the painting was made."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The copy was made shortly after Veronese died."}, {"n": 5, "text": "In that copy the cloak is red."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is highly unlikely that a copyist would have made so major a change so soon after Veronese''s death.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A copyist might have made a change."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The change would have been a major one."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It would have come soon after Veronese''s death."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That timing counts as very soon."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Such a change is highly unlikely."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether the red cloak is evidence of tampering.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q9", "qnum": 9, "question_type": "Argument Part", "correct_answer": "B"}, {"citation": "PT106.S2.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 10, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q11", "prompt": "John works five days each week except when on vacation or during weeks in which national holidays occur. Four days a week he works in an insurance company; on Fridays he works as a blacksmith. Last week there were no holidays, and John was not on vacation. Therefore, he must have worked in the insurance company on Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday last week.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) John works each week. · He works five days a week. · There are exceptions to this. · One exception is when he is on vacation. · Another exception is weeks with national holidays. (2) John works in an insurance company. · He does that four days a week. · He also works as a blacksmith. · The blacksmith work happens on Fridays. (3) This is about last week. · There were no holidays last week. · John was not on vacation then. (4) This is the conclusion. · John must have worked in the insurance company. · He worked there on Monday. · He worked there on Tuesday. · He worked there on Wednesday. · He worked there on Thursday. · This all happened last week.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "John works five days each week except when on vacation or during weeks in which national holidays occur.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "John works each week."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He works five days a week."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There are exceptions to this."}, {"n": 4, "text": "One exception is when he is on vacation."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Another exception is weeks with national holidays."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Four days a week he works in an insurance company; on Fridays he works as a blacksmith.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "John works in an insurance company."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He does that four days a week."}, {"n": 3, "text": "He also works as a blacksmith."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The blacksmith work happens on Fridays."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Last week there were no holidays, and John was not on vacation.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about last week."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There were no holidays last week."}, {"n": 3, "text": "John was not on vacation then."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Therefore, he must have worked in the insurance company on Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday last week.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "John must have worked in the insurance company."}, {"n": 3, "text": "He worked there on Monday."}, {"n": 4, "text": "He worked there on Tuesday."}, {"n": 5, "text": "He worked there on Wednesday."}, {"n": 6, "text": "He worked there on Thursday."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This all happened last week."}]}], "the_read": "What if the five days he works aren''t necessarily weekdays? What if he typically works on the weekend and takes Monday and Tuesday off?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q11", "qnum": 11, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 11, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q12", "prompt": "After several attempts to distract his young parrot from chewing on furniture, George reluctantly took an expert''s advice and gently hit the parrot''s beak whenever the bird started to chew furniture. The bird stopped chewing furniture, but it is now afraid of hands and will sometimes bite. Since chewing on the furniture would not have hurt the bird, George should not have hit it.\n\nWhen Carla''s puppy escaped from her yard, it bounded into a busy street. Although Carla does not generally approve of physical discipline, she hit the puppy sharply with her hand. Now the puppy enters the street only when accompanied by Carla, so Carla was justified in disciplining the puppy.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Several attempts were made at distraction. · The attempts targeted a young parrot. · The parrot belongs to someone. · The parrot was chewing on furniture. · What comes next happened after those attempts. (2) George took some advice. · The advice came from an expert. · He took it reluctantly. · He hit the parrot''s beak. · The hitting was gentle. · He did this whenever the bird started to chew furniture. (3) The bird stopped chewing furniture. · This is a contrast with that result. · The bird is now afraid of hands. · The bird will sometimes bite. · The biting happens only sometimes. (4) A reason is given first. · Chewing on the furniture would not have hurt the bird. · This is the judgment that follows. · George should not have hit the bird. (5) Carla has a puppy. · The puppy escaped from her yard. · The puppy bounded into a street. · The street was busy. (6) This is a concession before the main point. · Carla does not approve of physical discipline. · That disapproval is a general one. · Carla hit the puppy. · She hit it sharply. · She used her hand. (7) The puppy now enters the street. · It does so only when accompanied. · The one accompanying it is Carla. · This is the conclusion drawn from that. · Carla was justified in disciplining the puppy.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "After several attempts to distract his young parrot from chewing on furniture,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Several attempts were made at distraction."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The attempts targeted a young parrot."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The parrot belongs to someone."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The parrot was chewing on furniture."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What comes next happened after those attempts."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "George reluctantly took an expert''s advice and gently hit the parrot''s beak whenever the bird started to chew furniture.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "George took some advice."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The advice came from an expert."}, {"n": 3, "text": "He took it reluctantly."}, {"n": 4, "text": "He hit the parrot''s beak."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The hitting was gentle."}, {"n": 6, "text": "He did this whenever the bird started to chew furniture."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The bird stopped chewing furniture, but it is now afraid of hands and will sometimes bite.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The bird stopped chewing furniture."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is a contrast with that result."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The bird is now afraid of hands."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The bird will sometimes bite."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The biting happens only sometimes."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Since chewing on the furniture would not have hurt the bird, George should not have hit it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A reason is given first."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Chewing on the furniture would not have hurt the bird."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is the judgment that follows."}, {"n": 4, "text": "George should not have hit the bird."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "When Carla''s puppy escaped from her yard, it bounded into a busy street.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Carla has a puppy."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The puppy escaped from her yard."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The puppy bounded into a street."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The street was busy."}]}, {"n": 6, "text": "Although Carla does not generally approve of physical discipline, she hit the puppy sharply with her hand.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a concession before the main point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Carla does not approve of physical discipline."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That disapproval is a general one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Carla hit the puppy."}, {"n": 5, "text": "She hit it sharply."}, {"n": 6, "text": "She used her hand."}]}, {"n": 7, "text": "Now the puppy enters the street only when accompanied by Carla, so Carla was justified in disciplining the puppy.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The puppy now enters the street."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It does so only when accompanied."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The one accompanying it is Carla."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is the conclusion drawn from that."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Carla was justified in disciplining the puppy."}]}], "the_read": "What if physical discipline isn''t necessarily justified or unjustified on the basis of harm coming to the animal?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q12", "qnum": 12, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q13", "prompt": "Mature white pines intercept almost all the sunlight that shines on them. They leave a deep litter that dries readily, and they grow to prodigious height so that, even when there are large gaps in a stand of such trees, little light reaches the forest floor. For this reason white pines cannot regenerate in their own shade. Thus, when in a dense forest a stand of trees consists of nothing but mature white pines, it is a fair bet that _______.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about white pines. · The trees are mature ones. · They intercept sunlight that shines on them. · They intercept almost all of it. (2) This is still about the white pines. · They leave a litter behind. · The litter is deep. · The litter dries readily. · They grow to prodigious height. · A result follows from these facts. (3) This holds even in a certain case. · There can be gaps in a stand of such trees. · The gaps are large. · Little light reaches the forest floor. (4) A reason has just been given. · White pines cannot regenerate. · They cannot do so in their own shade. (5) This is the conclusion. · It concerns a dense forest. · There is a stand of trees in it. · The stand consists of nothing but white pines. · Those pines are mature. · Something is a fair bet in that case.", "category": "Fill In", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Mature white pines intercept almost all the sunlight that shines on them.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about white pines."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The trees are mature ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They intercept sunlight that shines on them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They intercept almost all of it."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "They leave a deep litter that dries readily, and they grow to prodigious height so that,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is still about the white pines."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They leave a litter behind."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The litter is deep."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The litter dries readily."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They grow to prodigious height."}, {"n": 6, "text": "A result follows from these facts."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "even when there are large gaps in a stand of such trees, little light reaches the forest floor.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This holds even in a certain case."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There can be gaps in a stand of such trees."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The gaps are large."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Little light reaches the forest floor."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "For this reason white pines cannot regenerate in their own shade.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A reason has just been given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "White pines cannot regenerate."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They cannot do so in their own shade."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Thus, when in a dense forest a stand of trees consists of nothing but mature white pines, it is a fair bet that _______.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It concerns a dense forest."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There is a stand of trees in it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The stand consists of nothing but white pines."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those pines are mature."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Something is a fair bet in that case."}]}], "the_read": "The trees are relatively similar ages.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Fill In", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 13, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q14", "prompt": "Advertisement: A leading economist has determined that among people who used computers at their place of employment last year, those who also owned portable (\"laptop\") computers earned 25 percent more on average than those who did not. It is obvious from this that owning a laptop computer led to a higher-paying job.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This comes from an advertisement. · An economist determined something. · That economist is a leading one. · The finding is about certain people. · Those people used computers. · They used them at their place of employment. · This happened last year. (2) Some of those people also owned portable computers. · Portable computers are called laptops. · Those people earned more. · They earned 25 percent more. · The figure is an average. · The comparison is with those who did not own laptops. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is drawn from what came before. · The conclusion is called obvious. · Owning a laptop computer led to something. · What it led to is a higher-paying job.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Advertisement: A leading economist has determined that among people who used computers at their place of employment last year,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This comes from an advertisement."}, {"n": 2, "text": "An economist determined something."}, 