uestions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Parallel Reasoning", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q16", "prompt": "Private industry is trying to attract skilled research scientists by offering them high salaries. As a result, most research scientists employed in private industry now earn 50 percent more than do comparably skilled research scientists employed by the government. So, unless government-employed research scientists are motivated more by a sense of public duty than by their own interests, the government is likely to lose its most skilled research scientists to private industry, since none of these scientists would have problems finding private-sector jobs.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Private industry is trying to attract scientists. · The scientists are research scientists. · Those scientists are skilled. · Private industry offers them salaries. · The salaries are high. (2) This is a result of what came before. · Most research scientists in private industry earn more. · They earn 50 percent more. · This is the case now. · The comparison is with government-employed research scientists. · Those scientists are comparably skilled. (3) This introduces the conclusion. · A condition is attached to it. · The condition concerns government-employed research scientists. · They might be motivated by a sense of public duty. · The comparison is with their own interests. · The public duty would have to motivate them more. (4) The government is likely to lose scientists. · The scientists it loses are its most skilled ones. · They would go to private industry. · A reason is given for this. · None of these scientists would have problems finding jobs. · The jobs would be in the private sector.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Private industry is trying to attract skilled research scientists by offering them high salaries.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Private industry is trying to attract scientists."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The scientists are research scientists."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those scientists are skilled."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Private industry offers them salaries."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The salaries are high."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "As a result, most research scientists employed in private industry now earn 50 percent more than do comparably skilled research scientists employed by the government.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a result of what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Most research scientists in private industry earn more."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They earn 50 percent more."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is the case now."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with government-employed research scientists."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those scientists are comparably skilled."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "So, unless government-employed research scientists are motivated more by a sense of public duty than by their own interests,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This introduces the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A condition is attached to it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The condition concerns government-employed research scientists."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They might be motivated by a sense of public duty."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with their own interests."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The public duty would have to motivate them more."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "the government is likely to lose its most skilled research scientists to private industry, since none of these scientists would have problems finding private-sector jobs.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The government is likely to lose scientists."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The scientists it loses are its most skilled ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They would go to private industry."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A reason is given for this."}, {"n": 5, "text": "None of these scientists would have problems finding jobs."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The jobs would be in the private sector."}]}], "the_read": "What if government research jobs provide much better health insurance, 401k plans, and other fringe benefits that outweigh the salary differential?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q17", "prompt": "Using fossil energy more efficiently is in the interest of the nation and the global environment, but major improvements are unlikely unless proposed government standards are implemented to eliminate products or practices that are among the least efficient in their class.\n\nObjection: Decisions on energy use are best left to the operation of the market.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about using fossil energy. · The use in question is more efficient use. · Such use is in the interest of the nation. · It is also in the interest of the environment. · The environment in question is the global one. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · Major improvements are unlikely. · They are unlikely under a condition. · The condition is that standards be implemented. · The standards are government ones that have been proposed. · The standards would eliminate certain products or practices. · Those products or practices are among the least efficient in their class. (3) This is stated as an objection. · It is about decisions on energy use. · Such decisions are best left alone. · They should be left to the operation of the market.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Using fossil energy more efficiently is in the interest of the nation and the global environment,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about using fossil energy."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The use in question is more efficient use."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Such use is in the interest of the nation."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is also in the interest of the environment."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The environment in question is the global one."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "but major improvements are unlikely unless proposed government standards are implemented to eliminate products or practices that are among the least efficient in their class.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Major improvements are unlikely."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are unlikely under a condition."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The condition is that standards be implemented."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The standards are government ones that have been proposed."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The standards would eliminate certain products or practices."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those products or practices are among the least efficient in their class."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Objection: Decisions on energy use are best left to the operation of the market.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is stated as an objection."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about decisions on energy use."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Such decisions are best left alone."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They should be left to the operation of the market."}]}], "the_read": "What if we should only take a course of action that is likely result in the major improvements that serve the interest of the nation and the global environment?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q17", "qnum": 17, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 17, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q18", "prompt": "Dobson: Some historians claim that the people who built a ring of stones thousands of years ago in Britain were knowledgeable about celestial events. The ground for this claim is that two of the stones determine a line pointing directly to the position of the sun at sunrise at the spring equinox. There are many stones in the ring, however, so the chance that one pair will point in a celestially significant direction is large. Therefore, the people who built the ring were not knowledgeable about celestial events.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Dobson is the speaker. · Some historians make a claim. · The claim is about people who built a ring of stones. · The ring was built thousands of years ago. · It was built in Britain. · Those people were knowledgeable about celestial events. (2) The claim has a ground. · Two of the stones determine a line. · The line points to a position of the sun. · It points directly there. · The position is where the sun is at sunrise. · The sunrise is the one at the spring equinox. (3) This is a contrast with the claim. · There are many stones in the ring. · A conclusion is drawn from that. · Some pair of stones may point in a certain direction. · The direction would be celestially significant. · The chance of that is large. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is about the people who built the ring. · They were not knowledgeable. · The knowledge at issue is about celestial events.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Dobson: Some historians claim that the people who built a ring of stones thousands of years ago in Britain were knowledgeable about celestial events.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Dobson is the speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some historians make a claim."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The claim is about people who built a ring of stones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The ring was built thousands of years ago."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It was built in Britain."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those people were knowledgeable about celestial events."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The ground for this claim is that two of the stones determine a line pointing directly to the position of the sun at sunrise at the spring equinox.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The claim has a ground."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Two of the stones determine a line."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The line points to a position of the sun."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It points directly there."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The position is where the sun is at sunrise."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The sunrise is the one at the spring equinox."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "There are many stones in the ring, however, so the chance that one pair will point in a celestially significant direction is large.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with the claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There are many stones in the ring."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A conclusion is drawn from that."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Some pair of stones may point in a certain direction."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The direction would be celestially significant."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The chance of that is large."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Therefore, the people who built the ring were not knowledgeable about celestial events.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the people who built the ring."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They were not knowledgeable."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The knowledge at issue is about celestial events."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because the evidence doesn''t prove they were knowledgeable, that doesn''t mean they definitely weren''t knowledgeable?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q18", "qnum": 18, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 18, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q19", "prompt": "Nearly all mail that is correctly addressed arrives at its destination within two business days of being sent. In fact, correctly addressed mail takes longer than this only when it is damaged in transit. Overall, however, most mail arrives three business days or more after being sent.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about mail that is correctly addressed. · Nearly all of it arrives at its destination. · It arrives within two business days. · The days counted are business days. · The count starts when the mail is sent. (2) This sharpens the previous point. · Correctly addressed mail sometimes takes longer. · The comparison is with two business days. · It takes longer only in one case. · That case is when the mail is damaged in transit. (3) This is a contrast with what came before. · The claim is about mail overall. · Most mail arrives later than that. · It arrives three business days or more after sending.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Nearly all mail that is correctly addressed arrives at its destination within two business days of being sent.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about mail that is correctly addressed."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Nearly all of it arrives at its destination."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It arrives within two business days."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The days counted are business days."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The count starts when the mail is sent."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "In fact, correctly addressed mail takes longer than this only when it is damaged in transit.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This sharpens the previous point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Correctly addressed mail sometimes takes longer."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The comparison is with two business days."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It takes longer only in one case."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That case is when the mail is damaged in transit."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Overall, however, most mail arrives three business days or more after being sent.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The claim is about mail overall."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Most mail arrives later than that."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It arrives three business days or more after sending."}]}], "the_read": "Most mail is either incorrectly addressed or damaged in transit.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q19", "qnum": 19, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 19, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q20", "prompt": "The report released by the interior ministry states that within the past 5 years the national land-reclamation program has resulted in a 19 percent increase in the amount of arable land within the country. If these figures are accurate, the program has been a resounding success. Senator Armand, a distinguished mathematician and a woman of indisputable brilliance, maintains, however, that the reclamation program could not possibly have been successful. Clearly, therefore, the figures cited in the report cannot be accurate.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A report was released. · The interior ministry released it. · The report covers the past 5 years. · There is a land-reclamation program. · The program is a national one. · The program resulted in an increase. · The increase was 19 percent. · What increased was the amount of arable land in the country. (2) This is a conditional claim. · The figures might be accurate. · In that case the program has been a success. · The success would be a resounding one. (3) Senator Armand is named here. · She is a mathematician. · She is described as distinguished. · Her brilliance is indisputable. · This is a contrast with what came before. · She maintains a claim about the reclamation program. · The program could not possibly have been successful. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is presented as clear. · The figures were cited in the report. · Those figures cannot be accurate.", "category": "Parallel Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The report released by the interior ministry states that within the past 5 years the national land-reclamation program has resulted in a 19 percent increase in the amount of arable land within the country.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A report was released."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The interior ministry released it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The report covers the past 5 years."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There is a land-reclamation program."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The program is a national one."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The program resulted in an increase."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The increase was 19 percent."}, {"n": 8, "text": "What increased was the amount of arable land in the country."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "If these figures are accurate, the program has been a resounding success.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a conditional claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The figures might be accurate."}, {"n": 3, "text": "In that case the program has been a success."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The success would be a resounding one."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Senator Armand, a distinguished mathematician and a woman of indisputable brilliance, maintains, however, that the reclamation program could not possibly have been successful.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Senator Armand is named here."}, {"n": 2, "text": "She is a mathematician."}, {"n": 3, "text": "She is described as distinguished."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Her brilliance is indisputable."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 6, "text": "She maintains a claim about the reclamation program."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The program could not possibly have been successful."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Clearly, therefore, the figures cited in the report cannot be accurate.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is presented as clear."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The figures were cited in the report."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those figures cannot be accurate."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because Senator Armand is good at math and really smart, that doesn''t mean she''s correct in her assessment of the program?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q20", "qnum": 20, "question_type": "Parallel Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 20, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q21", "prompt": "Wirth: All efforts to identify a gene responsible for predisposing people to manic-depression have failed. In fact, nearly all researchers now agree that there is no \"manic-depression gene.\" Therefore, if these researchers are right, any claim that some people are genetically predisposed to manic-depression is simply false.\n\nChang: I do not dispute your evidence, but I take issue with your conclusion. Many of the researchers you refer to have found evidence that a set of several genes is involved and that complex interactions among these genes produce a predisposition to manic-depression.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Wirth is the first speaker. · Efforts have been made to identify a gene. · All of those efforts have failed. · The gene would be responsible for a predisposition. · The predisposition is to manic-depression. (2) This strengthens the previous point. · Researchers agree on something. · Nearly all of them agree. · That agreement holds now. · There is no manic-depression gene. (3) This is the conclusion. · It rests on a condition. · The condition is that those researchers are right. · Some people might be claimed to be genetically predisposed. · The predisposition would be to manic-depression. · Any such claim is simply false. (4) Chang is the second speaker. · Chang does not dispute the evidence. · The evidence belongs to Wirth. · This is a contrast with that. · Chang takes issue with the conclusion. (5) This is about the researchers Wirth referred to. · Many of them have found evidence. · The evidence is that a set of genes is involved. · The set contains several genes. · There are interactions among these genes. · Those interactions are complex. · The interactions produce a predisposition to manic-depression.", "category": "Controversy", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Wirth: All efforts to identify a gene responsible for predisposing people to manic-depression have failed.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Wirth is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Efforts have been made to identify a gene."}, {"n": 3, "text": "All of those efforts have failed."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The gene would be responsible for a predisposition."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The predisposition is to manic-depression."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "In fact, nearly all researchers now agree that there is no \"manic-depression gene.\"", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This strengthens the previous point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Researchers agree on something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Nearly all of them agree."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That agreement holds now."}, {"n": 5, "text": "There is no manic-depression gene."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, if these researchers are right, any claim that some people are genetically predisposed to manic-depression is simply false.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on a condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The condition is that those researchers are right."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Some people might be claimed to be genetically predisposed."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The predisposition would be to manic-depression."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Any such claim is simply false."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Chang: I do not dispute your evidence, but I take issue with your conclusion.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Chang is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Chang does not dispute the evidence."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The evidence belongs to Wirth."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a contrast with that."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Chang takes issue with the conclusion."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Many of the researchers you refer to have found evidence that a set of several genes is involved and that complex interactions among these genes produce a predisposition to manic-depression.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the researchers Wirth referred to."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Many of them have found evidence."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The evidence is that a set of genes is involved."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The set contains several genes."}, {"n": 5, "text": "There are interactions among these genes."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those interactions are complex."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The interactions produce a predisposition to manic-depression."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether current research indicates no one is genetically predisoposed to manic-depression.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q21", "qnum": 21, "question_type": "Controversy", "correct_answer": "D"}, {"citation": "PT106.S2.Q22", "qnum": 22, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 21, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q23", "prompt": "Garbage dumps do not harm wildlife. Evidence is furnished by the Masai-Mara reserve in Kenya, where baboons that use the garbage dumps on the reserve as a food source mature faster and have more offspring than do baboons on the reserve that do not scavenge on garbage.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is a claim about garbage dumps. · Garbage dumps do not cause harm. · What they do not harm is wildlife. (2) Evidence is offered for that claim. · The evidence comes from a reserve. · The reserve is the Masai-Mara. · The reserve is in Kenya. (3) There are baboons on the reserve. · Some of them use the garbage dumps. · They use the dumps as a food source. · Those baboons mature faster. · Those baboons have more offspring. · The comparison is with other baboons on the reserve. · Those other baboons do not scavenge on garbage.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Garbage dumps do not harm wildlife.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a claim about garbage dumps."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Garbage dumps do not cause harm."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they do not harm is wildlife."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Evidence is furnished by the Masai-Mara reserve in Kenya,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Evidence is offered for that claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The evidence comes from a reserve."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The reserve is the Masai-Mara."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The reserve is in Kenya."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "where baboons that use the garbage dumps on the reserve as a food source mature faster and have more offspring than do baboons on the reserve that do not scavenge on garbage.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There are baboons on the reserve."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some of them use the garbage dumps."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They use the dumps as a food source."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those baboons mature faster."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those baboons have more offspring."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with other baboons on the reserve."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those other baboons do not scavenge on garbage."}]}], "the_read": "What if there are Secret Downsides of feeding at the garbage dump?\nFor instance, what if the baboons that use trash as a food source have offspring with birth defects and cancer? What if maturing faster decreases the lifespan of the baboons who feed at the garbage dump?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q23", "qnum": 23, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 22, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q24", "prompt": "Marianne is a professional chess player who hums audibly while playing her matches, thereby distracting her opponents. When ordered by chess officials to cease humming or else be disqualified from professional chess, Marianne protested the order. She argued that since she was unaware of her humming, her humming was involuntary and that therefore she should not be held responsible for it.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Marianne is a chess player. · She is a professional. · She hums while playing her matches. · The humming is audible. · The humming distracts her opponents. (2) Chess officials gave Marianne an order. · The order was to cease humming. · The alternative was being disqualified. · The disqualification would be from professional chess. · Marianne protested the order. (3) Marianne made an argument. · She was unaware of her humming. · She gave that as her reason. · Her humming was involuntary. · A conclusion follows from that. · She should not be held responsible for the humming.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Marianne is a professional chess player who hums audibly while playing her matches, thereby distracting her opponents.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Marianne is a chess player."}, {"n": 2, "text": "She is a professional."}, {"n": 3, "text": "She hums while playing her matches."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The humming is audible."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The humming distracts her opponents."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "When ordered by chess officials to cease humming or else be disqualified from professional chess, Marianne protested the order.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Chess officials gave Marianne an order."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The order was to cease humming."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The alternative was being disqualified."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The disqualification would be from professional chess."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Marianne protested the order."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "She argued that since she was unaware of her humming, her humming was involuntary and that therefore she should not be held responsible for it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Marianne made an argument."}, {"n": 2, "text": "She was unaware of her humming."}, {"n": 3, "text": "She gave that as her reason."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Her humming was involuntary."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A conclusion follows from that."}, {"n": 6, "text": "She should not be held responsible for the humming."}]}], "the_read": "What if people can be held responsible for involuntary acts?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q24", "qnum": 24, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "B"}, {"citation": "PT106.S2.Q25", "qnum": 25, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 23, "slug": "PT106_S2_Q26", "prompt": "Smoking in bed has long been the main cause of home fires. Despite a significant decline in cigarette smoking in the last two decades, however, there has been no comparable decline in the number of people killed in home fires.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Smoking in bed causes home fires. · It is the main cause of them. · It has been so for a long time. (2) Cigarette smoking has declined. · The decline is significant. · It happened in the last two decades. · This is a contrast with what follows. · There has been no comparable decline elsewhere. · What has not declined is the number of people killed. · Those people are killed in home fires.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Smoking in bed has long been the main cause of home fires.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Smoking in bed causes home fires."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is the main cause of them."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It has been so for a long time."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Despite a significant decline in cigarette smoking in the last two decades, however, there has been no comparable decline in the number of people killed in home fires.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Cigarette smoking has declined."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The decline is significant."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It happened in the last two decades."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a contrast with what follows."}, {"n": 5, "text": "There has been no comparable decline elsewhere."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What has not declined is the number of people killed."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those people are killed in home fires."}]}], "the_read": "What if there has been an increase in another source of house fires, like amateur deep frying, in the last 20 years? What if home fires caused by smoking weren''t usually deadly?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT106.S2.Q26", "qnum": 26, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "B"}]}]'::jsonb,
