    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, and react to it. Once you finish the stimulus, say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, then give it a reaction. After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears, and you recall the whole argument out loud from memory.", "rules": ["Read each piece silently. Everything you say back, including your reaction and final recall, is out loud.", "Read about a sentence at a time. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said.", "Then colour it: react to that specific piece with a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling.", "Repeat these steps through the whole stimulus.", "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and stays gone. Say the entire argument back from memory, with every detail you can still hold.", "Press space again to move on. The whole section records in one take, with no pauses or breaks."], "briefing": {"lede": "Read these instructions once before your first section. After that, come back when you need a reminder.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still reach the answers remembering almost none of it.", "body": ["You can follow a stimulus sentence by sentence and understand every part. Then you reach answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and that is what this drill trains.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. Understanding the stimulus is not enough if it is gone by the time you reach the answers.", "Most everyday reading trains you to reach the end, not retain every detail. You scan names, drop connecting words, and turn a list of three things into one vague label. That works for a phone bill. It does not work for the LSAT."]}, {"heading": "How the method works", "body": ["For each piece, read it, say it back, and colour it. Once you finish the stimulus, recall the whole argument. You decide how much to read at a time, and nothing on the screen decides for you.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it", "body": "Read about a sentence silently. Give every word enough attention to hear the sentence naturally in your head. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less. If you take on too much, the beginning is usually what disappears first."}, {"label": "Tell it back", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it", "body": "React to what you just said. Give it a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling. Keep it short, but make it specific. If the same reaction could fit any sentence, it does not count."}, {"label": "Recall it", "body": "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Say the entire argument out loud from memory, with every detail you can still hold. This is what gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter", "body": ["Do not give back only the gist or what seemed important. You cannot know which details will matter until you have read the whole stimulus. Filtering as you read is how useful information disappears.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. Names, numbers, and small words carrying the load, such as all, most, only, and probably, have to come back too.", "**Being efficient does not mean leaving information out.**"]}, {"heading": "When nothing comes to mind", "body": ["You may try to colour a sentence and find that nothing comes to mind. Usually, the hidden thought is that nothing feels good enough to say.", "Do not judge the reaction before you say it. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count when they connect to what you just read.", "The reaction makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away from the text", "body": ["The text stays visible while you say each piece back. You have to look away from it yourself.", "If you read while you recall, you stop using your short-term memory and slide back into the author''s exact words. Looking away forces you to rebuild the meaning in your own words.", "This is also the version that works on paper and on test day."]}, {"heading": "Build toward twenty-five minutes", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section. It only counts when you read every stimulus, say each piece back, colour it, and recall the whole argument. Finishing early by skipping parts is not finishing early. If you receive extra time on test day, use your full allotted time here too.", "The clock counts up so you can track your pace without watching your time disappear.", "Do the method correctly before you try to do it quickly. Go slower while it is new. Speed follows once the method becomes automatic."]}], "closer": "One section keeps the method steady. Improvement starts with the second."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, tells it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives The Recall of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Recall against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q1", "prompt": "A government ought to protect and encourage free speech, because free speech is an activity that is conducive to a healthy nation and thus is in the best interest of its people.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A government ought to protect free speech. · A government ought to encourage free speech as well. · This recommendation is given a reason. · Free speech is an activity. · That activity is conducive to a healthy nation. · A further step is drawn from that. · Free speech is in the best interest of the people. · The people are the nation''s own people.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A government ought to protect and encourage free speech, because free speech is an activity that is conducive to a healthy nation and thus is in the best interest of its people.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A government ought to protect free speech."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A government ought to encourage free speech as well."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This recommendation is given a reason."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Free speech is an activity."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That activity is conducive to a healthy nation."}, {"n": 6, "text": "A further step is drawn from that."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Free speech is in the best interest of the people."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The people are the nation''s own people."}]}], "the_read": "What if the government shouldn''t do what''s in the best interest of its people? What if there''s a downside to protecting free speech, like people can voice and encourage extremist violence?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q2", "prompt": "The current theory about earthquakes holds that they are caused by adjoining plates of rock sliding past each other; the plates are pressed together until powerful forces overcome the resistance. As plausible as this may sound, at least one thing remains mysterious on this theory. The overcoming of such resistance should create enormous amounts of heat. But so far no increases in temperature unrelated to weather have been detected following earthquakes.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a theory about earthquakes. · That theory is the current one. · The theory says earthquakes have a cause. · The cause is plates of rock sliding. · The plates slide past each other. · The plates are adjoining ones. (2) This continues the theory about the plates. · The plates are pressed together. · The pressing continues until something happens. · Powerful forces overcome the resistance. · The forces are described as powerful. (3) The theory may sound plausible. · That plausibility is conceded here. · Something still remains mysterious. · At least one thing is mysterious. · The mystery attaches to this theory. (4) This is about overcoming that resistance. · Overcoming it should create heat. · The heat should come in enormous amounts. · The claim is about what should happen. (5) This is a contrast with what came before. · No increases in temperature have been detected. · The increases would be ones unrelated to weather. · Detection would come after earthquakes. · This holds so far.", "category": "Conclusion", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The current theory about earthquakes holds that they are caused by adjoining plates of rock sliding past each other;", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a theory about earthquakes."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That theory is the current one."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The theory says earthquakes have a cause."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The cause is plates of rock sliding."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The plates slide past each other."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The plates are adjoining ones."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "the plates are pressed together until powerful forces overcome the resistance.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This continues the theory about the plates."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The plates are pressed together."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The pressing continues until something happens."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Powerful forces overcome the resistance."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The forces are described as powerful."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "As plausible as this may sound, at least one thing remains mysterious on this theory.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The theory may sound plausible."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That plausibility is conceded here."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Something still remains mysterious."}, {"n": 4, "text": "At least one thing is mysterious."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The mystery attaches to this theory."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "The overcoming of such resistance should create enormous amounts of heat.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about overcoming that resistance."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Overcoming it should create heat."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The heat should come in enormous amounts."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The claim is about what should happen."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "But so far no increases in temperature unrelated to weather have been detected following earthquakes.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "No increases in temperature have been detected."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The increases would be ones unrelated to weather."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Detection would come after earthquakes."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This holds so far."}]}], "the_read": "What if the heat occurs and has been detected before earthquakes? What if our heat measuring tools are bad and this heat exists but we just haven''t detected it yet?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Conclusion", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q3", "prompt": "Legal theorist: It is unreasonable to incarcerate anyone for any other reason than that he or she is a serious threat to the property or lives of other people. The breaking of a law does not justify incarceration, for lawbreaking proceeds either from ignorance of the law or of the effects of one''s actions, or from the free choice of the lawbreaker. Obviously mere ignorance cannot justify incarcerating a lawbreaker, and even free choice on the part of the lawbreaker fails to justify incarceration, for free choice proceeds from the desires of an agent, and the desires of an agent are products of genetics and environmental conditioning, neither of which is controlled by the agent.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A legal theorist is speaking. · Incarcerating someone can be unreasonable. · It is unreasonable for any reason other than one. · The one acceptable reason is being a threat. · The threat must be a serious one. · The threat is to property. · The threat is to lives as well. · The property and lives belong to other people. (2) This is about the breaking of a law. · Breaking a law does not justify incarceration. · That is what the theorist claims. (3) This gives the reason for that claim. · Lawbreaking proceeds from one of some sources. · One source is ignorance of the law. · Another is ignorance of the effects of one''s actions. · Another is the free choice of the lawbreaker. · These are the only sources offered. (4) Ignorance cannot justify incarcerating a lawbreaker. · The ignorance in question is mere ignorance. · This is called obvious. · Free choice also fails to justify incarceration. · The free choice is the lawbreaker''s own. · Both possible sources are being ruled out here. (5) This gives a reason for that. · Free choice proceeds from desires. · The desires belong to an agent. (6) This adds a further step. · The desires of an agent are products of something. · One thing they are products of is genetics. · Another is environmental conditioning. · Neither of those is controlled by the agent.", "category": "Argument Part", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Legal theorist: It is unreasonable to incarcerate anyone for any other reason than that he or she is a serious threat to the property or lives of other people.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A legal theorist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Incarcerating someone can be unreasonable."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is unreasonable for any reason other than one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The one acceptable reason is being a threat."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The threat must be a serious one."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The threat is to property."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The threat is to lives as well."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The property and lives belong to other people."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The breaking of a law does not justify incarceration,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the breaking of a law."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Breaking a law does not justify incarceration."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That is what the theorist claims."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "for lawbreaking proceeds either from ignorance of the law or of the effects of one''s actions, or from the free choice of the lawbreaker.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives the reason for that claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Lawbreaking proceeds from one of some sources."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One source is ignorance of the law."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Another is ignorance of the effects of one''s actions."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Another is the free choice of the lawbreaker."}, {"n": 6, "text": "These are the only sources offered."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Obviously mere ignorance cannot justify incarcerating a lawbreaker, and even free choice on the part of the lawbreaker fails to justify incarceration,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Ignorance cannot justify incarcerating a lawbreaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The ignorance in question is mere ignorance."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is called obvious."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Free choice also fails to justify incarceration."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The free choice is the lawbreaker''s own."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Both possible sources are being ruled out here."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "for free choice proceeds from the desires of an agent,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason for that."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Free choice proceeds from desires."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The desires belong to an agent."}]}, {"n": 6, "text": "and the desires of an agent are products of genetics and environmental conditioning, neither of which is controlled by the agent.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This adds a further step."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The desires of an agent are products of something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One thing they are products of is genetics."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Another is environmental conditioning."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Neither of those is controlled by the agent."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because free choice and ignorance don''t justify incarceration, that doesn''t mean the only reasonable reason to incarcerate someone is when they are a serious threat to property or people?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Argument Part", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q4", "prompt": "A certain gene can be stimulated by chemicals in cigarette smoke, causing lung cells to metabolize the chemicals in a way that makes the cells cancerous. Yet smokers in whom this gene is not stimulated have as high a risk of developing lung cancer from smoking as other smokers do.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a certain gene. · The gene can be stimulated. · Chemicals are what stimulate it. · The chemicals are in cigarette smoke. · Stimulation causes lung cells to metabolize the chemicals. · The metabolizing happens in a particular way. · That way makes the cells cancerous. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · In some smokers this gene is not stimulated. · Those smokers have a risk of developing lung cancer. · The cancer would come from smoking. · Their risk is as high as that of other smokers. · The comparison is with the other smokers.", "category": "Inference", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A certain gene can be stimulated by chemicals in cigarette smoke, causing lung cells to metabolize the chemicals in a way that makes the cells cancerous.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a certain gene."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The gene can be stimulated."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Chemicals are what stimulate it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The chemicals are in cigarette smoke."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Stimulation causes lung cells to metabolize the chemicals."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The metabolizing happens in a particular way."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That way makes the cells cancerous."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Yet smokers in whom this gene is not stimulated have as high a risk of developing lung cancer from smoking as other smokers do.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "In some smokers this gene is not stimulated."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those smokers have a risk of developing lung cancer."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The cancer would come from smoking."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Their risk is as high as that of other smokers."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with the other smokers."}]}], "the_read": "What if smokers who don''t have this gene stimulated tend to smoke cigarettes with a more cancerous concentration of nicotine, and develop lung cancer directly from the nicotine?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Inference", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q5", "prompt": "In a poll of eligible voters conducted on the eve of a mayoral election, more of those polled stated that they favored Panitch than stated that they favored any other candidate. Despite this result, another candidate, Yeung, defeated Panitch by a comfortable margin.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There was a poll. · The poll surveyed eligible voters. · It was conducted on the eve of an election. · The election was a mayoral one. · Some of those polled stated that they favored Panitch. · Panitch was named by more of them than any other candidate was. · The comparison is with every other candidate. (2) This runs contrary to that result. · Another candidate was Yeung. · Yeung was the one who defeated Panitch. · The margin was a comfortable one.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In a poll of eligible voters conducted on the eve of a mayoral election, more of those polled stated that they favored Panitch than stated that they favored any other candidate.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There was a poll."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The poll surveyed eligible voters."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It was conducted on the eve of an election."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The election was a mayoral one."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Some of those polled stated that they favored Panitch."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Panitch was named by more of them than any other candidate was."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The comparison is with every other candidate."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Despite this result, another candidate, Yeung, defeated Panitch by a comfortable margin.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This runs contrary to that result."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Another candidate was Yeung."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Yeung was the one who defeated Panitch."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The margin was a comfortable one."}]}], "the_read": "What if Panitch supporters were over sampled in the poll? What if poll respondents stating that they favor a candidate doesn''t mean they will actually vote?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q6", "prompt": "Commissioner: Budget forecasters project a revenue shortfall of a billion dollars in the coming fiscal year. Since there is no feasible way to increase the available funds, our only choice is to decrease expenditures. The plan before you outlines feasible cuts that would yield savings of a billion dollars over the coming fiscal year. We will be able to solve the problem we face, therefore, only if we adopt this plan.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A commissioner is speaking. · Budget forecasters make a projection. · They project a revenue shortfall. · The shortfall is a billion dollars. · The shortfall falls in the coming fiscal year. (2) This gives a reason first. · There is no feasible way to increase the funds. · The funds are the available ones. · Decreasing expenditures is the only choice. · The choice belongs to the speaker''s group. (3) There is a plan before the audience. · The plan outlines cuts. · The cuts are feasible ones. · The cuts would yield savings. · The savings would be a billion dollars. · The savings would come over the coming fiscal year. (4) This is the conclusion. · The speaker''s group faces a problem. · They will be able to solve it. · Solving it requires adopting this plan. · Adopting the plan is the only way.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Commissioner: Budget forecasters project a revenue shortfall of a billion dollars in the coming fiscal year.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A commissioner is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Budget forecasters make a projection."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They project a revenue shortfall."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The shortfall is a billion dollars."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The shortfall falls in the coming fiscal year."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Since there is no feasible way to increase the available funds, our only choice is to decrease expenditures.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives a reason first."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is no feasible way to increase the funds."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The funds are the available ones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Decreasing expenditures is the only choice."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The choice belongs to the speaker''s group."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The plan before you outlines feasible cuts that would yield savings of a billion dollars over the coming fiscal year.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a plan before the audience."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The plan outlines cuts."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The cuts are feasible ones."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The cuts would yield savings."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The savings would be a billion dollars."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The savings would come over the coming fiscal year."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "We will be able to solve the problem we face, therefore, only if we adopt this plan.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The speaker''s group faces a problem."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They will be able to solve it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Solving it requires adopting this plan."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Adopting the plan is the only way."}]}], "the_read": "What if this particular plan isn''t the only way to reduce expenditures by a billion dollars?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q7", "prompt": "Critic: Emily Dickinson''s poetry demonstrates that meaning cannot reside entirely within a poem itself, but is always the unique result of an interaction between a reader''s system of beliefs and the poem; and, of course, any two readers from different cultures or eras have radically different systems of beliefs.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A critic is speaking. · Emily Dickinson wrote poetry. · Her poetry demonstrates something. · Meaning cannot reside within a poem itself. · It cannot reside there entirely. (2) This is the contrasting half of the claim. · Meaning is the result of an interaction. · That result is unique. · This is always so. · One side of the interaction is a reader''s system of beliefs. · The other side is the poem. (3) This adds a further claim. · It concerns any two readers. · The readers come from different cultures. · They may come from different eras instead. · Such readers have different systems of beliefs. · The difference is a radical one.", "category": "Contradiction", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Critic: Emily Dickinson''s poetry demonstrates that meaning cannot reside entirely within a poem itself,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A critic is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Emily Dickinson wrote poetry."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Her poetry demonstrates something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Meaning cannot reside within a poem itself."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It cannot reside there entirely."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "but is always the unique result of an interaction between a reader''s system of beliefs and the poem;", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the contrasting half of the claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Meaning is the result of an interaction."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That result is unique."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is always so."}, {"n": 5, "text": "One side of the interaction is a reader''s system of beliefs."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The other side is the poem."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "and, of course, any two readers from different cultures or eras have radically different systems of beliefs.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This adds a further claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It concerns any two readers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The readers come from different cultures."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They may come from different eras instead."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Such readers have different systems of beliefs."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The difference is a radical one."}]}], "the_read": "What if poetry evokes the same reaction no matter what beliefs the reader has?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Contradiction", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q8", "prompt": "Archaeologist: The fact that the ancient Egyptians and the Maya both built pyramids is often taken as evidence of a historical link between Old- and New-World civilizations that is earlier than any yet documented. But while these buildings are similar to each other, there are important differences in both design and function. The Egyptian pyramids were exclusively tombs for rulers, whereas the Mayan pyramids were used as temples. This shows conclusively that there was no such link between Old- and New-World civilizations.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) An archaeologist is speaking. · The ancient Egyptians built pyramids. · The Maya built pyramids too. · That fact is taken as evidence. · It is often taken that way. · The evidence would be of a historical link. · The link would join Old- and New-World civilizations. · The link would be earlier than any yet documented. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · The buildings are similar to each other. · That similarity is conceded. · There are differences as well. · The differences are important ones. · Some of the differences are in design. · Others are in function. (3) The Egyptian pyramids were tombs. · The tombs were for rulers. · They served that purpose exclusively. · The Mayan pyramids are set against them. · The Mayan pyramids were used as temples. (4) This is the conclusion. · It is drawn from what came before. · There was no such link. · The link would have been between Old- and New-World civilizations. · The showing is called conclusive.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Archaeologist: The fact that the ancient Egyptians and the Maya both built pyramids is often taken as evidence of a historical link between Old- and New-World civilizations that is earlier than any yet documented.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "An archaeologist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The ancient Egyptians built pyramids."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The Maya built pyramids too."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That fact is taken as evidence."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is often taken that way."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The evidence would be of a historical link."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The link would join Old- and New-World civilizations."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The link would be earlier than any yet documented."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But while these buildings are similar to each other, there are important differences in both design and function.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The buildings are similar to each other."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That similarity is conceded."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There are differences as well."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The differences are important ones."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Some of the differences are in design."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Others are in function."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The Egyptian pyramids were exclusively tombs for rulers, whereas the Mayan pyramids were used as temples.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The Egyptian pyramids were tombs."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The tombs were for rulers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They served that purpose exclusively."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The Mayan pyramids are set against them."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The Mayan pyramids were used as temples."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "This shows conclusively that there was no such link between Old- and New-World civilizations.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is drawn from what came before."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There was no such link."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The link would have been between Old- and New-World civilizations."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The showing is called conclusive."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because the pyramids from each culture served different functions, that doesn''t mean there was absolutely no link whatsoever?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q8", "qnum": 8, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 9, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q9", "prompt": "Manuscripts written by first-time authors generally do not get serious attention by publishers except when these authors happen to be celebrities. My manuscript is unlikely to be taken seriously by publishers for I am a first-time author who is not a celebrity.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about manuscripts. · The manuscripts are written by first-time authors. · They do not get serious attention. · The attention would come from publishers. · This holds as a general rule. · There is an exception to it. · The exception is authors who happen to be celebrities. (2) This is the speaker''s conclusion. · The speaker has a manuscript. · It is unlikely to be taken seriously. · Publishers would be the ones taking it seriously. · A reason is given for this. · The speaker is a first-time author. · The speaker is not a celebrity.", "category": "Parallel Reasoning", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Manuscripts written by first-time authors generally do not get serious attention by publishers except when these authors happen to be celebrities.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about manuscripts."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The manuscripts are written by first-time authors."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They do not get serious attention."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The attention would come from publishers."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This holds as a general rule."}, {"n": 6, "text": "There is an exception to it."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The exception is authors who happen to be celebrities."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "My manuscript is unlikely to be taken seriously by publishers for I am a first-time author who is not a celebrity.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the speaker''s conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The speaker has a manuscript."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is unlikely to be taken seriously."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Publishers would be the ones taking it seriously."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A reason is given for this."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The speaker is a first-time author."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The speaker is not a celebrity."}]}], "the_read": "What if the rules of conditional reasoning don''t apply?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q9", "qnum": 9, "question_type": "Parallel Reasoning", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 10, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q10", "prompt": "Twelve healthy volunteers with the Apo-A-IV-1 gene and twelve healthy volunteers who instead have the Apo-A-IV-2 gene each consumed a standard diet supplemented daily by a high-cholesterol food. A high level of cholesterol in the blood is associated with an increased risk of heart disease. After three weeks, the blood cholesterol levels of the subjects in the second group were unchanged, whereas the blood cholesterol levels of those with the Apo-A-IV-1 gene rose 20 percent.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) One group had twelve volunteers. · Those volunteers had the Apo-A-IV-1 gene. · A second group also had twelve volunteers. · Those volunteers had the Apo-A-IV-2 gene instead. · All the volunteers were healthy. · Each of them consumed a standard diet. · The diet was supplemented daily. · The supplement was a high-cholesterol food. (2) Cholesterol can be at a high level. · The cholesterol is in the blood. · A high level is associated with a risk. · The risk is of heart disease. · The risk is an increased one. (3) Three weeks had passed by then. · This is about the subjects in the second group. · Their blood cholesterol levels were measured. · Those levels were unchanged. (4) This contrasts with the other group. · It is about those with the Apo-A-IV-1 gene. · Their blood cholesterol levels rose. · The rise was 20 percent.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Twelve healthy volunteers with the Apo-A-IV-1 gene and twelve healthy volunteers who instead have the Apo-A-IV-2 gene each consumed a standard diet supplemented daily by a high-cholesterol food.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "One group had twelve volunteers."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those volunteers had the Apo-A-IV-1 gene."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A second group also had twelve volunteers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those volunteers had the Apo-A-IV-2 gene instead."}, {"n": 5, "text": "All the volunteers were healthy."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Each of them consumed a standard diet."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The diet was supplemented daily."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The supplement was a high-cholesterol food."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "A high level of cholesterol in the blood is associated with an increased risk of heart disease.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Cholesterol can be at a high level."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The cholesterol is in the blood."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A high level is associated with a risk."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The risk is of heart disease."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The risk is an increased one."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "After three weeks, the blood cholesterol levels of the subjects in the second group were unchanged,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Three weeks had passed by then."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is about the subjects in the second group."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Their blood cholesterol levels were measured."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those levels were unchanged."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "whereas the blood cholesterol levels of those with the Apo-A-IV-1 gene rose 20 percent.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This contrasts with the other group."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about those with the Apo-A-IV-1 gene."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Their blood cholesterol levels rose."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The rise was 20 percent."}]}], "the_read": "High cholesterol diets may increase the risk of heart disease for people with Apo-A-IV-1 genes more than for people with Apo-A-IV-2 genes unless some other difference between the two study groups caused the difference in blood cholesterol levels.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 11, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q11", "prompt": "High school students who feel that they are not succeeding in school often drop out before graduating and go to work. Last year, however, the city''s high school dropout rate was significantly lower than the previous year''s rate. This is encouraging evidence that the program instituted two years ago to improve the morale of high school students has begun to take effect to reduce dropouts.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about high school students. · Some of them feel that they are not succeeding. · The feeling is about school. · Such students drop out. · They drop out before graduating. · They go to work afterward. · Such dropping out happens often. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · The city has a high school dropout rate. · Last year''s rate was lower. · The comparison is with the previous year''s rate. · The drop was a significant one. (3) This is the conclusion drawn from that. · The fact counts as evidence. · The evidence is called encouraging. · There is a program. · The program was instituted two years ago. · Its aim was to improve morale. · The morale is that of high school students. · The program has begun to take effect and reduce dropouts.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "High school students who feel that they are not succeeding in school often drop out before graduating and go to work.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about high school students."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some of them feel that they are not succeeding."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The feeling is about school."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Such students drop out."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They drop out before graduating."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They go to work afterward."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Such dropping out happens often."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Last year, however, the city''s high school dropout rate was significantly lower than the previous year''s rate.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The city has a high school dropout rate."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Last year''s rate was lower."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with the previous year''s rate."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The drop was a significant one."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is encouraging evidence that the program instituted two years ago to improve the morale of high school students has begun to take effect to reduce dropouts.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion drawn from that."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The fact counts as evidence."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The evidence is called encouraging."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There is a program."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The program was instituted two years ago."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Its aim was to improve morale."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The morale is that of high school students."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The program has begun to take effect and reduce dropouts."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s no causal relationship between the program and the reduced dropout rate? What if there were fewer dropouts because there were no jobs available for them to take? What if the dropout rate had been trending downward, and that''s what prompted the new program?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q11", "qnum": 11, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q12", "prompt": "The television show Henry was not widely watched until it was scheduled for Tuesday evenings immediately after That''s Life, the most popular show on television. During the year after the move, Henry was consistently one of the ten most-watched shows on television. Since Henry''s recent move to Wednesday evenings, however, it has been watched by far fewer people. We must conclude that Henry was widely watched before the move to Wednesday evenings because it followed That''s Life and not because people especially liked it.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Henry is a television show. · Henry was not widely watched at first. · That changed when it was scheduled elsewhere. · The new slot was Tuesday evenings. · The slot came immediately after another show. · That other show is That''s Life. · That''s Life is the most popular show on television. (2) This covers the year after the move. · Henry was one of the most-watched shows on television. · It was among the ten most-watched. · It held that position consistently. (3) This is a contrast with what came before. · Henry has now moved again. · The new night is Wednesday evenings. · The move was a recent one. · Fewer people have watched it since. · The drop in viewers is a large one. (4) This is the conclusion. · The conclusion is presented as forced. · Henry was widely watched before the Wednesday move. · The reason was that it followed That''s Life. · The reason was not that people liked it. · People did not especially like the show.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The television show Henry was not widely watched until it was scheduled for Tuesday evenings immediately after That''s Life, the most popular show on television.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Henry is a television show."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Henry was not widely watched at first."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That changed when it was scheduled elsewhere."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The new slot was Tuesday evenings."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The slot came immediately after another show."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That other show is That''s Life."}, {"n": 7, "text": "That''s Life is the most popular show on television."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "During the year after the move, Henry was consistently one of the ten most-watched shows on television.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This covers the year after the move."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Henry was one of the most-watched shows on television."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It was among the ten most-watched."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It held that position consistently."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Since Henry''s recent move to Wednesday evenings, however, it has been watched by far fewer people.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Henry has now moved again."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The new night is Wednesday evenings."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The move was a recent one."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Fewer people have watched it since."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The drop in viewers is a large one."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "We must conclude that Henry was widely watched before the move to Wednesday evenings because it followed That''s Life and not because people especially liked it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The conclusion is presented as forced."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Henry was widely watched before the Wednesday move."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The reason was that it followed That''s Life."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The reason was not that people liked it."}, {"n": 6, "text": "People did not especially like the show."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s no causal relationship between time slot and popularity? What if Henry got a new writing team when it moved to Wednesday and the writing is now bad? What if the shows that replaced Henry are not seeing an increase in viewers?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q12", "qnum": 12, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 13, "slu