for a theorem that he claimed nonetheless to have proved.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Joseph is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He cites his encyclopedia."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Pierre de Fermat was a mathematician."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Fermat died in the year 1665."}, {"n": 5, "text": "He left behind no written proof."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The proof would have been for a theorem."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Fermat claimed to have proved that theorem."}, {"n": 8, "text": "He claimed it even so."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Probably this alleged theorem simply cannot be proved, since—as the article points out—no one else has been able to prove it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The theorem is only an alleged one."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It probably cannot be proved."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A reason is given for this."}, {"n": 4, "text": "No one else has been able to prove it."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The article points that out."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore it is likely that Fermat was either lying or else mistaken when he made his claim.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is Joseph''s conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Something about Fermat is likely."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One possibility is that he was lying."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other is that he was mistaken."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This concerns the time he made his claim."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Laura: Your encyclopedia is out of date.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Laura is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "She addresses Joseph''s encyclopedia."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The encyclopedia is out of date."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Recently someone has in fact proved Fermat''s theorem.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Someone has proved the theorem."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The theorem is Fermat''s."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The proof happened recently."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is offered as a fact."}]}, {"n": 6, "text": "And since the theorem is provable, your claim—that Fermat was lying or mistaken—clearly is wrong.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is Laura''s conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on the theorem being provable."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Joseph made a claim."}, {"n": 4, "text": "His claim was that Fermat was lying or mistaken."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That claim is wrong."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It is clearly wrong."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether Fermat was lying or mistaken.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Argument Part", "correct_answer": "B"}, {"citation": "PT112.S4.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 14, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q15", "prompt": "It is not good for a university to have class sizes that are very large or very small, or to have professors with teaching loads that are very light or very heavy. After all, crowded classes and overworked faculty cripple the institution''s ability to recruit and retain both qualified students and faculty.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Something is not good for a university. · Very large class sizes are not good. · Very small class sizes are not good either. · Teaching loads matter as well. · The loads belong to professors. · Very light loads are not good. · Very heavy loads are not good. (2) This gives the reason for that. · Crowded classes do damage. · Overworked faculty do damage too. · They cripple an ability of the institution. · The ability is to recruit and retain people. · Those people include qualified students. · They include faculty as well.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "It is not good for a university to have class sizes that are very large or very small, or to have professors with teaching loads that are very light or very heavy.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Something is not good for a university."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Very large class sizes are not good."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Very small class sizes are not good either."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Teaching loads matter as well."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The loads belong to professors."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Very light loads are not good."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Very heavy loads are not good."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "After all, crowded classes and overworked faculty cripple the institution''s ability to recruit and retain both qualified students and faculty.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives the reason for that."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Crowded classes do damage."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Overworked faculty do damage too."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They cripple an ability of the institution."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The ability is to recruit and retain people."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those people include qualified students."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They include faculty as well."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s no downside to light teaching loads and small class sizes? What if universities don''t care about recruiting and retaining qualified students and faculty?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q16", "prompt": "Sales manager: The highest priority should be given to the needs of the sales department, because without successful sales the company as a whole would fail.\n\nShipping manager: There are several departments other than sales that also must function successfully for the company to succeed. It is impossible to give the highest priority to all of them.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A sales manager is speaking. · The highest priority should go to certain needs. · Those needs belong to the sales department. · A reason is given for this. · Sales might not be successful. · Without successful sales the company would fail. · The failure would be of the company as a whole. (2) A shipping manager replies. · There are other departments besides sales. · Several of those departments exist. · They must function successfully too. · That is required for the company to succeed. (3) This continues the shipping manager''s point. · The highest priority could be given to a department. · It cannot be given to all of them. · Doing so is impossible.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Sales manager: The highest priority should be given to the needs of the sales department, because without successful sales the company as a whole would fail.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A sales manager is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The highest priority should go to certain needs."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those needs belong to the sales department."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A reason is given for this."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Sales might not be successful."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Without successful sales the company would fail."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The failure would be of the company as a whole."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Shipping manager: There are several departments other than sales that also must function successfully for the company to succeed.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A shipping manager replies."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There are other departments besides sales."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Several of those departments exist."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They must function successfully too."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That is required for the company to succeed."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is impossible to give the highest priority to all of them.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This continues the shipping manager''s point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The highest priority could be given to a department."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It cannot be given to all of them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Doing so is impossible."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether the highest priority can be given to departments on whom the success of the company depends.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q17", "prompt": "Researchers have found that people who drink five or more cups of coffee a day have a risk of heart disease 2.5 times the average after corrections are made for age and smoking habits. Members of the research team say that, on the basis of their findings, they now limit their own daily coffee intake to two cups.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Researchers have made a finding. · The finding concerns coffee drinkers. · Those people drink five or more cups a day. · They have a risk of heart disease. · Their risk is 2.5 times the average. · Corrections were made to the figure. · One correction was for age. · Another was for smoking habits. (2) Members of the research team say something. · They are on the team that made the finding. · Their findings are the basis for it. · They limit their own coffee intake. · The limit is two cups a day. · They do this now.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Researchers have found that people who drink five or more cups of coffee a day have a risk of heart disease 2.5 times the average after corrections are made for age and smoking habits.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Researchers have made a finding."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The finding concerns coffee drinkers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those people drink five or more cups a day."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They have a risk of heart disease."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Their risk is 2.5 times the average."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Corrections were made to the figure."}, {"n": 7, "text": "One correction was for age."}, {"n": 8, "text": "Another was for smoking habits."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Members of the research team say that, on the basis of their findings, they now limit their own daily coffee intake to two cups.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Members of the research team say something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are on the team that made the finding."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Their findings are the basis for it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They limit their own coffee intake."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The limit is two cups a day."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They do this now."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s no causal relationship between coffee consumption and risk of heart disease? What if something else, like stress, causes people to both drink a lot of coffee and to have a higher risk of heart disease? What if coffee is a home remedy for heart disease, so those who know they have an increased risk drink a lot of coffee?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q17", "qnum": 17, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 17, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q18", "prompt": "People who have political power tend to see new technologies as a means of extending or protecting their power, whereas they generally see new ethical arguments and ideas as a threat to it. Therefore, technical ingenuity usually brings benefits to those who have this ingenuity, whereas ethical inventiveness brings only pain to those who have this inventiveness.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about people who have political power. · They see new technologies a certain way. · They see them as a means to an end. · One end is extending their power. · The other is protecting that power. · This is a tendency rather than a rule. (2) This contrasts with the point about technologies. · The same people see something else differently. · The things are new ethical arguments. · New ideas are included too. · They see these as a threat to their power. · This is generally so. (3) This is the conclusion. · Technical ingenuity brings benefits. · The benefits go to those who have that ingenuity. · This is usually what happens. · Ethical inventiveness is set against it. · It brings pain to those who have that inventiveness. · Pain is all that it brings.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "People who have political power tend to see new technologies as a means of extending or protecting their power,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about people who have political power."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They see new technologies a certain way."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They see them as a means to an end."}, {"n": 4, "text": "One end is extending their power."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The other is protecting that power."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is a tendency rather than a rule."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "whereas they generally see new ethical arguments and ideas as a threat to it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This contrasts with the point about technologies."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The same people see something else differently."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The things are new ethical arguments."}, {"n": 4, "text": "New ideas are included too."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They see these as a threat to their power."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is generally so."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, technical ingenuity usually brings benefits to those who have this ingenuity, whereas ethical inventiveness brings only pain to those who have this inventiveness.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Technical ingenuity brings benefits."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The benefits go to those who have that ingenuity."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is usually what happens."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Ethical inventiveness is set against it."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It brings pain to those who have that inventiveness."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Pain is all that it brings."}]}], "the_read": "What if politically powerful people don''t reward things they think enhance their power and punish things they think jeopardize their power?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q18", "qnum": 18, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 18, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q19", "prompt": "Birds need so much food energy to maintain their body temperatures that some of them spend most of their time eating. But a comparison of a bird of a seed-eating species to a bird of a nectar-eating species that has the same overall energy requirement would surely show that the seed-eating bird spends more time eating than does the nectar-eating bird, since a given amount of nectar provides more energy than does the same amount of seeds.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Birds need food energy. · They need it to maintain their body temperatures. · The amount they need is very large. · Some birds spend their time eating. · They spend most of their time that way. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · A comparison is proposed. · One bird belongs to a seed-eating species. · The other belongs to a nectar-eating species. · The two have the same overall energy requirement. · The comparison would show the seed-eating bird spends more time eating. · The comparison is with the nectar-eating bird. · The result is claimed to be sure. (3) This gives the reason for that. · A given amount of nectar provides energy. · Nectar provides more of it. · The comparison is with the same amount of seeds.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Birds need so much food energy to maintain their body temperatures that some of them spend most of their time eating.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Birds need food energy."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They need it to maintain their body temperatures."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The amount they need is very large."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Some birds spend their time eating."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They spend most of their time that way."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But a comparison of a bird of a seed-eating species to a bird of a nectar-eating species that has the same overall energy requirement would surely show that the seed-eating bird spends more time eating than does the nectar-eating bird,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A comparison is proposed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One bird belongs to a seed-eating species."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other belongs to a nectar-eating species."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The two have the same overall energy requirement."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison would show the seed-eating bird spends more time eating."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The comparison is with the nectar-eating bird."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The result is claimed to be sure."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "since a given amount of nectar provides more energy than does the same amount of seeds.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives the reason for that."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A given amount of nectar provides energy."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Nectar provides more of it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with the same amount of seeds."}]}], "the_read": "What if it takes a lot more time to eat nectar than it does to eat the same quantity of seed?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q19", "qnum": 19, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 19, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q20", "prompt": "Consumer advocate: The introduction of a new drug into the marketplace should be contingent upon our having a good understanding of its social impact. However, the social impact of the newly marketed antihistamine is far from clear. It is obvious, then, that there should be a general reduction in the pace of bringing to the marketplace new drugs that are now being tested.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A consumer advocate is speaking. · New drugs are introduced into the marketplace. · Such an introduction should be contingent on something. · The condition is our having an understanding. · The understanding must be a good one. · What is understood is the drug''s social impact. (2) This is a contrast with what came before. · There is an antihistamine. · It has been newly marketed. · Its social impact is not clear. · It is far from clear. (3) This is the conclusion. · The conclusion is called obvious. · There should be a reduction in pace. · The reduction should be a general one. · The pace is that of bringing drugs to the marketplace. · The drugs are new ones now being tested.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Consumer advocate: The introduction of a new drug into the marketplace should be contingent upon our having a good understanding of its social impact.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A consumer advocate is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "New drugs are introduced into the marketplace."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Such an introduction should be contingent on something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The condition is our having an understanding."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The understanding must be a good one."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What is understood is the drug''s social impact."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "However, the social impact of the newly marketed antihistamine is far from clear.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is an antihistamine."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It has been newly marketed."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Its social impact is not clear."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is far from clear."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is obvious, then, that there should be a general reduction in the pace of bringing to the marketplace new drugs that are now being tested.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The conclusion is called obvious."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There should be a reduction in pace."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The reduction should be a general one."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The pace is that of bringing drugs to the marketplace."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The drugs are new ones now being tested."}]}], "the_read": "What if the new antihistamine is an outlier, and we do understand the social impact of most new drugs?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q20", "qnum": 20, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 20, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q21", "prompt": "Tina: For centuries oceans and human eccentricity have been linked in the literary and artistic imagination. Such linkage is probably due to the European Renaissance practice of using ships as asylums for the socially undesirable.\n\nSergio: No. Oceans have always been viewed as mysterious and unpredictable—qualities that people have invariably associated with eccentricity.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Tina is the first speaker. · Oceans have been linked with something. · The other thing is human eccentricity. · The link lives in the imagination. · That imagination is literary and artistic. · The link has lasted for centuries. (2) This explains the linkage. · The explanation is only probable. · There was a practice of the European Renaissance. · The practice used ships as asylums. · The asylums held the socially undesirable. (3) Sergio is the second speaker. · He rejects what Tina said. · Oceans have been viewed as mysterious. · They have been viewed as unpredictable. · They have always been viewed that way. · People associate those qualities with eccentricity. · They have invariably done so.", "category": "Controversy", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Tina: For centuries oceans and human eccentricity have been linked in the literary and artistic imagination.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Tina is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Oceans have been linked with something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The other thing is human eccentricity."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The link lives in the imagination."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That imagination is literary and artistic."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The link has lasted for centuries."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Such linkage is probably due to the European Renaissance practice of using ships as asylums for the socially undesirable.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This explains the linkage."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The explanation is only probable."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There was a practice of the European Renaissance."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The practice used ships as asylums."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The asylums held the socially undesirable."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Sergio: No. Oceans have always been viewed as mysterious and unpredictable—qualities that people have invariably associated with eccentricity.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Sergio is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He rejects what Tina said."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Oceans have been viewed as mysterious."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They have been viewed as unpredictable."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They have always been viewed that way."}, {"n": 6, "text": "People associate those qualities with eccentricity."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They have invariably done so."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether the link between oceans and human eccentricity predates the European Renaissance.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q21", "qnum": 21, "question_type": "Controversy", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 21, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q22", "prompt": "In a recent study, a group of subjects had their normal daily caloric intake increased by 25 percent. This increase was entirely in the form of alcohol. Another group of similar subjects had alcohol replace nonalcoholic sources of 25 percent of their normal daily caloric intake. All subjects gained body fat over the course of the study, and the amount of body fat gained was the same for both groups.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There was a study. · The study was a recent one. · One group of subjects took part. · The subjects had a normal daily caloric intake. · That intake was increased. · The increase was 25 percent. (2) This is about that increase. · The increase came in the form of alcohol. · Alcohol accounted for all of it. (3) There was another group of subjects. · Its subjects were similar to the first group''s. · Alcohol replaced something for them. · What it replaced was nonalcoholic sources. · Those sources supplied 25 percent of their intake. · The intake is their normal daily caloric intake. (4) Every subject gained body fat. · The gain happened over the course of the study. · A further point is added. · An amount of body fat was gained. · That amount was the same for both groups.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In a recent study, a group of subjects had their normal daily caloric intake increased by 25 percent.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There was a study."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The study was a recent one."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One group of subjects took part."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The subjects had a normal daily caloric intake."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That intake was increased."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The increase was 25 percent."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This increase was entirely in the form of alcohol.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about that increase."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The increase came in the form of alcohol."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Alcohol accounted for all of it."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Another group of similar subjects had alcohol replace nonalcoholic sources of 25 percent of their normal daily caloric intake.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There was another group of subjects."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Its subjects were similar to the first group''s."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Alcohol replaced something for them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What it replaced was nonalcoholic sources."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those sources supplied 25 percent of their intake."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The intake is their normal daily caloric intake."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "All subjects gained body fat over the course of the study, and the amount of body fat gained was the same for both groups.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Every subject gained body fat."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The gain happened over the course of the study."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A further point is added."}, {"n": 4, "text": "An amount of body fat was gained."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That amount was the same for both groups."}]}], "the_read": "An increase in body fat is not entirely determined by total calories consumed.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q22", "qnum": 22, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 22, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q23", "prompt": "When investigators discovered that the director of a local charity had repeatedly overstated the number of people his charity had helped, the director accepted responsibility for the deception. However, the investigators claimed that journalists were as much to blame as the director was for inflating the charity''s reputation, since they had naïvely accepted what the director told them, and simply reported as fact the numbers he gave them.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Investigators made a discovery. · There is a local charity. · The charity has a director. · The director overstated a number. · The number was of people the charity had helped. · He overstated it repeatedly. (2) This is what happened on that discovery. · The director accepted responsibility. · The responsibility was for the deception. (3) This is a contrast with what came before. · The investigators made a claim. · Their claim was about journalists. · Journalists were to blame. · They were as much to blame as the director. · The blame was for inflating a reputation. · The reputation is the charity''s. (4) This gives the reason for that claim. · The journalists accepted what the director told them. · They accepted it naively. · They also reported the numbers. · The director gave them those numbers. · They reported the numbers as fact. · They simply did that and no more.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "When investigators discovered that the director of a local charity had repeatedly overstated the number of people his charity had helped,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Investigators made a discovery."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a local charity."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The charity has a director."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The director overstated a number."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The number was of people the charity had helped."}, {"n": 6, "text": "He overstated it repeatedly."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "the director accepted responsibility for the deception.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is what happened on that discovery."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The director accepted responsibility."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The responsibility was for the deception."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "However, the investigators claimed that journalists were as much to blame as the director was for inflating the charity''s reputation,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The investigators made a claim."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Their claim was about journalists."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Journalists were to blame."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They were as much to blame as the director."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The blame was for inflating a reputation."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The reputation is the charity''s."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "since they had naïvely accepted what the director told them, and simply reported as fact the numbers he gave them.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives the reason for that claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The journalists accepted what the director told them."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They accepted it naively."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They also reported the numbers."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The director gave them those numbers."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They reported the numbers as fact."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They simply did that and no more."}]}], "the_read": "What if a journalist''s failing to fact check a source is not blameworthy?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q23", "qnum": 23, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 23, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q24", "prompt": "Telephone companies are promoting \"voice mail\" as an alternative to the answering machine. By recording messages from callers when a subscriber does not have access to his or her telephone, voice mail provides a service similar to that of an answering machine. The companies promoting this service argue that it will soon make answering machines obsolete, since it is much more convenient, more flexible, and less expensive than an answering machine.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Telephone companies are promoting something. · The thing is called voice mail. · They promote it as an alternative. · The alternative is to the answering machine. (2) Voice mail provides a service. · The service is similar to an answering machine''s. · Voice mail records messages. · The messages come from callers. · The recording happens when a subscriber lacks access. · What the subscriber lacks access to is a telephone. (3) The companies promote this service. · They make an argument about it. · Voice mail will make answering machines obsolete. · That will happen soon. (4) This gives the reason for that argument. · Voice mail is more convenient. · It is much more convenient. · It is more flexible. · It is less expensive. · The comparison is with an answering machine.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Telephone companies are promoting \"voice mail\" as an alternative to the answering machine.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Telephone companies are promoting something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The thing is called voice mail."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They promote it as an alternative."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The alternative is to the answering machine."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "By recording messages from callers when a subscriber does not have access to his or her telephone, voice mail provides a service similar to that of an answering machine.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Voice mail provides a service."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The service is similar to an answering machine''s."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Voice mail records messages."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The messages come from callers."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The recording happens when a subscriber lacks access."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What the subscriber lacks access to is a telephone."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "The companies promoting this service argue that it will soon make answering machines obsolete,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The companies promote this service."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They make an argument about it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Voice mail will make answering machines obsolete."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That will happen soon."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "since it is much more convenient, more flexible, and less expensive than an answering machine.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This gives the reason for that argument."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Voice mail is more convenient."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is much more convenient."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is more flexible."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is less expensive."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with an answering machine."}]}], "the_read": "What if there are huge downsides to voice mail that outweigh the upsides? What if there''s a key beneficial feature to answering machines that voice mail cannot match?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q24", "qnum": 24, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 24, "slug": "PT112_S4_Q25", "prompt": "The judgment that an artist is great always rests on assessments of the work the artist has produced. A series of great works is the only indicator of greatness. Therefore, to say that an artist is great is just to summarize the quality of his or her known works, and the artist''s greatness can provide no basis for predicting the quality of the artist''s unknown or future works.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a judgment that an artist is great. · That judgment rests on assessments. · The assessments are of the artist''s work. · The artist has produced that work. · The judgment always rests on them. (2) Greatness has an indicator. · The indicator is a series of works. · The works in the series are great. · It is the only indicator there is. (3) This is the conclusion. · Someone may say that an artist is great. · Saying that is only a summary. · What is summarized is quality. · The quality is that of the artist''s works. · Those works are the known ones. (4) This adds a second part to the conclusion. · The artist''s greatness provides no basis. · The basis would be for predicting quality. · The quality would be of other works. · Those works are unknown ones. · They may be future ones instead.", "category": "Parallel Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The judgment that an artist is great always rests on assessments of the work the artist has produced.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a judgment that an artist is great."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That judgment rests on assessments."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The assessments are of the artist''s work."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The artist has produced that work."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The judgment always rests on them."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "A series of great works is the only indicator of greatness.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Greatness has an indicator."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The indicator is a series of works."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The works in the series are great."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is the only indicator there is."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, to say that an artist is great is just to summarize the quality of his or her known works,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Someone may say that an artist is great."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Saying that is only a summary."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What is summarized is quality."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The quality is that of the artist''s works."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those works are the known ones."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "and the artist''s greatness can provide no basis for predicting the quality of the artist''s unknown or future works.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This adds a second part to the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The artist''s greatness provides no basis."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The basis would be for predicting quality."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The quality would be of other works."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those works are unknown ones."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They may be future ones instead."}]}], "the_read": "What if a great artist is only capable of producing great works?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT112.S4.Q25", "qnum": 25, "question_type": "Parallel Flaw", "correct_answer": "A"}]}]'::jsonb,
