  ),
  (
    'read-recall-colour-pt134-s3',
    'Read, Recall, Colour · PT134 Section 3',
    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, and react to it. Once you finish the stimulus, say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, then give it a reaction. After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears, and you recall the whole argument out loud from memory.", "rules": ["Read each piece silently. Everything you say back, including your reaction and final recall, is out loud.", "Read about a sentence at a time. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said.", "Then colour it: react to that specific piece with a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling.", "Repeat these steps through the whole stimulus.", "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and stays gone. Say the entire argument back from memory, with every detail you can still hold.", "Press space again to move on. The whole section records in one take, with no pauses or breaks."], "briefing": {"lede": "Read these instructions once before your first section. After that, come back when you need a reminder.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still reach the answers remembering almost none of it.", "body": ["You can follow a stimulus sentence by sentence and understand every part. Then you reach answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and that is what this drill trains.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. Understanding the stimulus is not enough if it is gone by the time you reach the answers.", "Most everyday reading trains you to reach the end, not retain every detail. You scan names, drop connecting words, and turn a list of three things into one vague label. That works for a phone bill. It does not work for the LSAT."]}, {"heading": "How the method works", "body": ["For each piece, read it, say it back, and colour it. Once you finish the stimulus, recall the whole argument. You decide how much to read at a time, and nothing on the screen decides for you.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it", "body": "Read about a sentence silently. Give every word enough attention to hear the sentence naturally in your head. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less. If you take on too much, the beginning is usually what disappears first."}, {"label": "Tell it back", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it", "body": "React to what you just said. Give it a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling. Keep it short, but make it specific. If the same reaction could fit any sentence, it does not count."}, {"label": "Recall it", "body": "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Say the entire argument out loud from memory, with every detail you can still hold. This is what gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter", "body": ["Do not give back only the gist or what seemed important. You cannot know which details will matter until you have read the whole stimulus. Filtering as you read is how useful information disappears.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. Names, numbers, and small words carrying the load, such as all, most, only, and probably, have to come back too.", "**Being efficient does not mean leaving information out.**"]}, {"heading": "When nothing comes to mind", "body": ["You may try to colour a sentence and find that nothing comes to mind. Usually, the hidden thought is that nothing feels good enough to say.", "Do not judge the reaction before you say it. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count when they connect to what you just read.", "The reaction makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away from the text", "body": ["The text stays visible while you say each piece back. You have to look away from it yourself.", "If you read while you recall, you stop using your short-term memory and slide back into the author''s exact words. Looking away forces you to rebuild the meaning in your own words.", "This is also the version that works on paper and on test day."]}, {"heading": "Build toward twenty-five minutes", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section. It only counts when you read every stimulus, say each piece back, colour it, and recall the whole argument. Finishing early by skipping parts is not finishing early. If you receive extra time on test day, use your full allotted time here too.", "The clock counts up so you can track your pace without watching your time disappear.", "Do the method correctly before you try to do it quickly. Go slower while it is new. Speed follows once the method becomes automatic."]}], "closer": "One section keeps the method steady. Improvement starts with the second."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, tells it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives The Recall of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Recall against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q1", "prompt": "\"Hot spot\" is a term that ecologists use to describe those habitats with the greatest concentrations of species found only in one place—so-called \"endemic\" species. Many of these hot spots are vulnerable to habitat loss due to commercial development. Furthermore, loss of endemic species accounts for most modern-day extinctions. Thus, given that only a limited number of environmental battles can be waged, it would be reasonable for organizations dedicated to preserving species to _______.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Hot spot is a term. · Ecologists use that term. · It describes certain habitats. · Those habitats have the greatest concentrations of species. · The species are found only in one place. · Such species are called endemic. (2) This is about the hot spots. · Many of them are vulnerable. · What they are vulnerable to is habitat loss. · The loss is due to commercial development. (3) This is an additional point. · It is about loss of endemic species. · That loss accounts for extinctions. · It accounts for most of them. · The extinctions are modern-day ones. (4) This is the conclusion. · It rests on a given. · The given is that only a limited number of battles can be waged. · The battles are environmental ones. · It would be reasonable for certain organizations to do something. · Those organizations are dedicated to preserving species. · The conclusion itself is left blank.", "category": "Fill In", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "\"Hot spot\" is a term that ecologists use to describe those habitats with the greatest concentrations of species found only in one place—so-called \"endemic\" species.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Hot spot is a term."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Ecologists use that term."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It describes certain habitats."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those habitats have the greatest concentrations of species."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The species are found only in one place."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Such species are called endemic."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Many of these hot spots are vulnerable to habitat loss due to commercial development.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the hot spots."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Many of them are vulnerable."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they are vulnerable to is habitat loss."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The loss is due to commercial development."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Furthermore, loss of endemic species accounts for most modern-day extinctions.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about loss of endemic species."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That loss accounts for extinctions."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It accounts for most of them."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The extinctions are modern-day ones."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Thus, given that only a limited number of environmental battles can be waged, it would be reasonable for organizations dedicated to preserving species to _______.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It rests on a given."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The given is that only a limited number of battles can be waged."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The battles are environmental ones."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It would be reasonable for certain organizations to do something."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those organizations are dedicated to preserving species."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The conclusion itself is left blank."}]}], "the_read": "Protect hot spots.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Fill In", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q2", "prompt": "Principle: If you sell an item that you know to be defective, telling the buyer that the item is sound, you thereby commit fraud.\n\nApplication: Wilton sold a used bicycle to Harris, knowing very little about its condition. Wilton told Harris that the bicycle was in good working condition, but Harris soon learned that the brakes were defective. Wilton was therefore guilty of fraud.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This states a principle. · It is conditional on selling an item. · You know the item to be defective. · You tell the buyer the item is sound. · Doing that commits fraud. (2) This applies the principle. · Wilton sold a bicycle. · The bicycle was used. · Wilton sold it to Harris. · Wilton knew little about its condition. · What Wilton knew was very little. (3) Wilton told Harris something. · Wilton said the bicycle was in good working condition. · This is a contrast. · Harris found something out. · Harris learned it soon. · The brakes were defective. (4) This is the conclusion. · Wilton was guilty of something. · What Wilton was guilty of is fraud.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Principle: If you sell an item that you know to be defective, telling the buyer that the item is sound, you thereby commit fraud.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This states a principle."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is conditional on selling an item."}, {"n": 3, "text": "You know the item to be defective."}, {"n": 4, "text": "You tell the buyer the item is sound."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Doing that commits fraud."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Application: Wilton sold a used bicycle to Harris, knowing very little about its condition.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This applies the principle."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Wilton sold a bicycle."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The bicycle was used."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Wilton sold it to Harris."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Wilton knew little about its condition."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What Wilton knew was very little."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Wilton told Harris that the bicycle was in good working condition, but Harris soon learned that the brakes were defective.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Wilton told Harris something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Wilton said the bicycle was in good working condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Harris found something out."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Harris learned it soon."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The brakes were defective."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Wilton was therefore guilty of fraud.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Wilton was guilty of something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What Wilton was guilty of is fraud."}]}], "the_read": "What if Wilton didn''t know the bicycle was defective?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q3", "prompt": "Engine noise from boats travelling through killer whales'' habitats ranges in frequency from 100 hertz to 3,000 hertz, an acoustical range that overlaps that in which the whales communicate through screams and squeals. Though killer whales do not seem to behave differently around running boat engines, engine noise from boats can be loud enough to damage their hearing over time. Therefore, _______.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about engine noise. · The noise comes from boats. · The boats travel through killer whales'' habitats. · The noise ranges in frequency. · The bottom of the range is 100 hertz. · The top of the range is 3,000 hertz. (2) The range is an acoustical one. · It overlaps another range. · The whales communicate in that other range. · They communicate through screams. · They also communicate through squeals. (3) This is a contrast. · Killer whales do not seem to behave differently. · This is around running boat engines. · Engine noise can be loud enough to do damage. · What it damages is their hearing. · The damage happens over time. (4) This is the conclusion. · The word therefore introduces it. · The conclusion itself is left blank.", "category": "Fill In", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Engine noise from boats travelling through killer whales'' habitats ranges in frequency from 100 hertz to 3,000 hertz,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about engine noise."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The noise comes from boats."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The boats travel through killer whales'' habitats."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The noise ranges in frequency."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The bottom of the range is 100 hertz."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The top of the range is 3,000 hertz."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "an acoustical range that overlaps that in which the whales communicate through screams and squeals.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The range is an acoustical one."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It overlaps another range."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The whales communicate in that other range."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They communicate through screams."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They also communicate through squeals."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Though killer whales do not seem to behave differently around running boat engines, engine noise from boats can be loud enough to damage their hearing over time.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Killer whales do not seem to behave differently."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is around running boat engines."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Engine noise can be loud enough to do damage."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What it damages is their hearing."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The damage happens over time."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Therefore, _______.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The word therefore introduces it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The conclusion itself is left blank."}]}], "the_read": "Boat noise may impair whales'' ability to communicate.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Fill In", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q4", "prompt": "Journalist: A manufacturers'' trade group that has long kept its membership list secret inadvertently sent me a document listing hundreds of manufacturing companies. A representative of the trade group later confirmed that every company listed in the document does indeed belong to the trade group. Because Bruch Industries is not listed on the document, it is evidently not a member of the trade group.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A journalist is speaking. · There is a manufacturers'' trade group. · The group keeps its membership list secret. · It has done so for a long time. · The group sent the journalist a document. · The sending was inadvertent. · The document lists manufacturing companies. · It lists hundreds of them. (2) A representative confirmed something. · The representative is of the trade group. · The confirmation came later. · Every company listed does belong to the group. · The companies meant are those in the document. (3) This is the reason given. · Bruch Industries is not listed on the document. · This is the conclusion. · Bruch Industries is not a member of the group. · This is marked as evident.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Journalist: A manufacturers'' trade group that has long kept its membership list secret inadvertently sent me a document listing hundreds of manufacturing companies.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A journalist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There is a manufacturers'' trade group."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The group keeps its membership list secret."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It has done so for a long time."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The group sent the journalist a document."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The sending was inadvertent."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The document lists manufacturing companies."}, {"n": 8, "text": "It lists hundreds of them."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "A representative of the trade group later confirmed that every company listed in the document does indeed belong to the trade group.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A representative confirmed something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The representative is of the trade group."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The confirmation came later."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Every company listed does belong to the group."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The companies meant are those in the document."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Because Bruch Industries is not listed on the document, it is evidently not a member of the trade group.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Bruch Industries is not listed on the document."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Bruch Industries is not a member of the group."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is marked as evident."}]}], "the_read": "What if the document doesn''t list every member of the trade group?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q5", "prompt": "Peter: Unlike in the past, most children''s stories nowadays don''t have clearly immoral characters in them. They should, though. Children need to learn the consequences of being bad.\n\nYoko: Children''s stories still tend to have clearly immoral characters in them, but now these characters tend not to be the sort that frighten children. Surely that''s an improvement.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Peter is the first speaker. · This is a contrast with the past. · It is about children''s stories nowadays. · Most of them lack immoral characters. · Those characters would be clearly immoral. (2) This is a contrast. · Peter says they should. · What they should have is such characters. (3) This is Peter''s reason. · Children need to learn something. · What they learn is consequences. · The consequences are of being bad. (4) Yoko is the second speaker. · Children''s stories still tend to have such characters. · The characters are clearly immoral. · This is a contrast about those characters. · The characters now tend to be different. · They are not the sort that frighten children. (5) This is Yoko''s conclusion. · The change is an improvement. · Yoko treats this as sure.", "category": "Controversy", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Peter: Unlike in the past, most children''s stories nowadays don''t have clearly immoral characters in them.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Peter is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is a contrast with the past."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about children''s stories nowadays."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Most of them lack immoral characters."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those characters would be clearly immoral."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "They should, though.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Peter says they should."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they should have is such characters."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Children need to learn the consequences of being bad.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is Peter''s reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Children need to learn something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they learn is consequences."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The consequences are of being bad."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Yoko: Children''s stories still tend to have clearly immoral characters in them, but now these characters tend not to be the sort that frighten children.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Yoko is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Children''s stories still tend to have such characters."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The characters are clearly immoral."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is a contrast about those characters."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The characters now tend to be different."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They are not the sort that frighten children."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Surely that''s an improvement.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is Yoko''s conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The change is an improvement."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Yoko treats this as sure."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether children''s stories today tend to have clearly immoral characters.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Controversy", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q6", "prompt": "Local resident: An overabundance of algae must be harmful to the smaller fish in this pond. During the fifteen or so years that I have lived here, the few times that I have seen large numbers of dead small fish wash ashore in late summer coincide exactly with the times that I have noticed abnormally large amounts of algae in the water.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A local resident is speaking. · This is the conclusion. · It is about an overabundance of algae. · The algae must be harmful. · What it harms is the smaller fish. · The fish are in this pond. (2) This is about a period of years. · The number is fifteen or so. · The resident has lived here that long. (3) The resident has seen dead small fish wash ashore. · The numbers were large. · The resident saw this only a few times. · It happened in late summer. · Those times coincide with other times. · The coincidence is exact. · The other times are when the resident noticed large amounts of algae. · The amounts were abnormally large.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Local resident: An overabundance of algae must be harmful to the smaller fish in this pond.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A local resident is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about an overabundance of algae."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The algae must be harmful."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What it harms is the smaller fish."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The fish are in this pond."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "During the fifteen or so years that I have lived here,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about a period of years."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The number is fifteen or so."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The resident has lived here that long."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "the few times that I have seen large numbers of dead small fish wash ashore in late summer coincide exactly with the times that I have noticed abnormally large amounts of algae in the water.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The resident has seen dead small fish wash ashore."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The numbers were large."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The resident saw this only a few times."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It happened in late summer."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those times coincide with other times."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The coincidence is exact."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The other times are when the resident noticed large amounts of algae."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The amounts were abnormally large."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s no causal relationship between the algae and the dead fish? What if pollution is causing both the algae growth and the fish deaths? What if dead fish cause algae to grow?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q7", "prompt": "Tanner: The public should demand political debates before any election. Voters are better able to choose the candidate best suited for office if they watch the candidates seriously debate one another.\n\nSaldana: Political debates almost always benefit the candidate who has the better debating skills. Thus, they don''t really help voters determine which candidate is most qualified for office.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Tanner is the first speaker. · This is Tanner''s recommendation. · The public should demand political debates. · The debates would come before an election. · This applies to any election. (2) This is Tanner''s reason. · Voters would be better able to choose. · They would choose the candidate best suited for office. · This holds if they watch the candidates debate. · The debating is serious. · The candidates debate one another. (3) Saldana is the second speaker. · Political debates benefit one candidate. · That candidate has the better debating skills. · This is almost always so. (4) This is Saldana''s conclusion. · Debates do not help voters. · What voters would determine is which candidate is most qualified. · The qualification is for office.", "category": "Controversy", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Tanner: The public should demand political debates before any election.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Tanner is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is Tanner''s recommendation."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The public should demand political debates."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The debates would come before an election."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This applies to any election."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Voters are better able to choose the candidate best suited for office if they watch the candidates seriously debate one another.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is Tanner''s reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Voters would be better able to choose."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They would choose the candidate best suited for office."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This holds if they watch the candidates debate."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The debating is serious."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The candidates debate one another."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Saldana: Political debates almost always benefit the candidate who has the better debating skills.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Saldana is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Political debates benefit one candidate."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That candidate has the better debating skills."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is almost always so."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Thus, they don''t really help voters determine which candidate is most qualified for office.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is Saldana''s conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Debates do not help voters."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What voters would determine is which candidate is most qualified."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The qualification is for office."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether political debates help voters decide who is more qualified for office.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Controversy", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q8", "prompt": "A recent study shows that those highways that carry the most traffic, and thus tend to be the most congested, have the lowest rate of fatal traffic accidents.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a study. · The study is recent. · It is about certain highways. · Those highways carry the most traffic. · They therefore tend to be the most congested. · Those highways have the lowest rate of accidents. · The accidents are fatal traffic accidents.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A recent study shows that those highways that carry the most traffic, and thus tend to be the most congested, have the lowest rate of fatal traffic accidents.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a study."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The study is recent."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about certain highways."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those highways carry the most traffic."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They therefore tend to be the most congested."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those highways have the lowest rate of accidents."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The accidents are fatal traffic accidents."}]}], "the_read": "What if people can''t drive particularly fast on these roads because of the heavy traffic, and fatal accidents tend to occur at high speeds?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q8", "qnum": 8, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 9, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q9", "prompt": "In some jurisdictions, lawmakers have instituted sentencing guidelines that mandate a penalty for theft that is identical to the one they have mandated for bribery. Hence, lawmakers in those jurisdictions evidently consider the harm resulting from theft to be equal to the harm resulting from bribery.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This holds in some jurisdictions. · Lawmakers instituted sentencing guidelines. · The guidelines mandate a penalty for theft. · That penalty is identical to another. · The comparison is with the penalty mandated for bribery. (2) This is the conclusion. · It is about lawmakers in those jurisdictions. · They consider two harms equal. · One is the harm resulting from theft. · The other is the harm resulting from bribery. · This is marked as evident.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "In some jurisdictions, lawmakers have instituted sentencing guidelines that mandate a penalty for theft that is identical to the one they have mandated for bribery.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This holds in some jurisdictions."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Lawmakers instituted sentencing guidelines."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The guidelines mandate a penalty for theft."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That penalty is identical to another."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with the penalty mandated for bribery."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Hence, lawmakers in those jurisdictions evidently consider the harm resulting from theft to be equal to the harm resulting from bribery.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about lawmakers in those jurisdictions."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They consider two harms equal."}, {"n": 4, "text": "One is the harm resulting from theft."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The other is the harm resulting from bribery."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is marked as evident."}]}], "the_read": "What if penalties for a crime have no relationship to the harm lawmakers think those crimes impose?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q9", "qnum": 9, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 10, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q10", "prompt": "People often admonish us to learn the lessons of history, but, even if it were easy to discover what the past was really like, it is nearly impossible to discover its lessons. We are supposed, for example, to learn the lessons of World War I. But what are they? And were we ever to discover what they are, it is not clear that we could ever apply them, for we shall never again have a situation just like World War I.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) People admonish us to learn history''s lessons. · They do this often. · This is a contrast. · What is conceded is that discovering the past might be easy. · Discovering its lessons is nearly impossible. (2) This is an example. · We are supposed to learn some lessons. · Those lessons are World War I''s. (3) This is a contrast with that example. · The argument asks a question. · The question is what those lessons are. (4) This is conditional on discovering them. · It is not clear that we could ever apply them. · This is stated as unclear rather than denied. · This is the reason given. · We shall never again have such a situation. · The situation would be just like World War I.", "category": "Argument Part", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "People often admonish us to learn the lessons of history, but, even if it were easy to discover what the past was really like, it is nearly impossible to discover its lessons.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "People admonish us to learn history''s lessons."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They do this often."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What is conceded is that discovering the past might be easy."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Discovering its lessons is nearly impossible."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "We are supposed, for example, to learn the lessons of World War I.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an example."}, {"n": 2, "text": "We are supposed to learn some lessons."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those lessons are World War I''s."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "But what are they?", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with that example."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The argument asks a question."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The question is what those lessons are."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "And were we ever to discover what they are, it is not clear that we could ever apply them, for we shall never again have a situation just like World War I.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is conditional on discovering them."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is not clear that we could ever apply them."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is stated as unclear rather than denied."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 5, "text": "We shall never again have such a situation."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The situation would be just like World War I."}]}], "the_read": "What if we can discover the lessons of history without knowing exactly what they are or applying them in identical situations?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Argument Part", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 11, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q11", "prompt": "Sigerson argues that the city should adopt ethical guidelines that preclude its politicians from accepting campaign contributions from companies that do business with the city. Sigerson''s proposal is dishonest, however, because he has taken contributions from such companies throughout his career in city politics.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Sigerson makes an argument. · He says the city should adopt ethical guidelines. · The guidelines would preclude something. · What is precluded is politicians accepting campaign contributions. · The politicians are the city''s. · The contributions come from certain companies. · Those companies do business with the city. (2) This is the conclusion. · Sigerson''s proposal is dishonest. · This is a contrast. · This is the reason given. · He has taken contributions from such companies. · He did so throughout his career. · The career is in city politics.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Sigerson argues that the city should adopt ethical guidelines that preclude its politicians from accepting campaign contributions from companies that do business with the city.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Sigerson makes an argument."}, {"n": 2, "text": "He says the city should adopt ethical guidelines."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The guidelines would preclude something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What is precluded is politicians accepting campaign contributions."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The politicians are the city''s."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The contributions come from certain companies."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Those companies do business with the city."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Sigerson''s proposal is dishonest, however, because he has taken contributions from such companies throughout his career in city politics.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Sigerson''s proposal is dishonest."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 5, "text": "He has taken contributions from such companies."}, {"n": 6, "text": "He did so throughout his career."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The career is in city politics."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because Sigerson has acted in a way that is counter to his proposal in the past, that doesn''t mean his proposal is dishonest?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q11", "qnum": 11, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q12", "prompt": "Some gardening books published by Garden Path Press recommend tilling the soil and adding compost before starting a new garden on a site, but they do not explain the difference between hot and cold composting. Since any gardening book that recommends adding compost is flawed if it does not explain at least the basics of composting, some books published by Garden Path are flawed.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about some gardening books. · Garden Path Press published them. · They recommend tilling the soil. · They also recommend adding compost. · This comes before starting a new garden. · The garden would be on a site. (2) This is a contrast. · The books do not explain something. · What they omit is a difference. · The difference is between hot and cold composting. (3) This is the reason given. · It is about any gardening book recommending compost. · Such a book is flawed under a condition. · The condition is not explaining the basics of composting. · At least the basics are required. · This is the conclusion. · Some books published by Garden Path are flawed.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some gardening books published by Garden Path Press recommend tilling the soil and adding compost before starting a new garden on a site,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about some gardening books."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Garden Path Press published them."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They recommend tilling the soil."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They also recommend adding compost."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This comes before starting a new garden."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The garden would be on a site."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "but they do not explain the difference between hot and cold composting.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The books do not explain something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they omit is a difference."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The difference is between hot and cold composting."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Since any gardening book that recommends adding compost is flawed if it does not explain at least the basics of composting, some books published by Garden Path are flawed.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about any gardening book recommending compost."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Such a book is flawed under a condition."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The condition is not explaining the basics of composting."}, {"n": 5, "text": "At least the basics are required."}, {"n": 6, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Some books published by Garden Path are flawed."}]}], "the_read": "What if an explanation of the basics of composting doesn''t need to include an explanation of the difference between hot and cold composting?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q12", "qnum": 12, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 13, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q13", "prompt": "Astronomers have found new evidence that the number of galaxies in the universe is not 10 billion, as previously believed, but 50 billion. This discovery will have an important effect on theories about how galaxies are formed. But even though astronomers now believe 40 billion more galaxies exist, many astronomers'' estimates of the universe''s total mass remain virtually unchanged.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Astronomers have found evidence. · The evidence is new. · It concerns the number of galaxies in the universe. · The number was previously believed to be 10 billion. · The number is not that. · The number is 50 billion. (2) The discovery will have an effect. · The effect is important. · The effect is on theories. · Those theories concern how galaxies are formed. (3) This is a contrast. · Astronomers now believe more galaxies exist. · The additional number is 40 billion. · Many astronomers make estimates. · The estimates are of the universe''s total mass. · Those estimates remain unchanged. · They are virtually unchanged.", "category": "Resolution", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Astronomers have found new evidence that the number of galaxies in the universe is not 10 billion, as previously believed, but 50 billion.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Astronomers have found evidence."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The evidence is new."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It concerns the number of galaxies in the universe."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The number was previously believed to be 10 billion."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The number is not that."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The number is 50 billion."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This discovery will have an important effect on theories about how galaxies are formed.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The discovery will have an effect."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The effect is important."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The effect is on theories."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those theories concern how galaxies are formed."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "But even though astronomers now believe 40 billion more galaxies exist, many astronomers'' estimates of the universe''s total mass remain virtually unchanged.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Astronomers now believe more galaxies exist."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The additional number is 40 billion."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Many astronomers make estimates."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The estimates are of the universe''s total mass."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those estimates remain unchanged."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They are virtually unchanged."}]}], "the_read": "What if galaxy mass is a negligible amount of the entire universe''s mass? What if astronomers now believe each galaxy has a much smaller mass than previously thought?", "the_read_type": "R", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Resolution", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 14, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q14", "prompt": "Newspaper subscriber: Arnot''s editorial argues that by making certain fundamental changes in government we would virtually eliminate our most vexing social ills. But clearly this conclusion is false. After all, the argument Arnot makes for this claim depends on the dubious assumption that government can be trusted to act in the interest of the public.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A newspaper subscriber is speaking. · Arnot wrote an editorial. · The editorial makes an argument. · It is about making changes in government. · The changes are fundamental ones. · Making them would eliminate social ills. · The ills are our most vexing ones. · The elimination would be virtually complete. (2) This is a contrast. · The subscriber''s conclusion is that Arnot''s conclusion is false. · The subscriber treats this as clear. (3) This is the reason given. · Arnot makes an argument for the claim. · The argument depends on an assumption. · The assumption is dubious. · The assumption is that government can be trusted. · It would be trusted to act in the public''s interest.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Newspaper subscriber: Arnot''s editorial argues that by making certain fundamental changes in government we would virtually eliminate our most vexing social ills.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A newspaper subscriber is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Arnot wrote an editorial."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The editorial makes an argument."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is about making changes in government."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The changes are fundamental ones."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Making them would eliminate social ills."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The ills are our most vexing ones."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The elimination would be virtually complete."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But clearly this conclusion is false.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The subscriber''s conclusion is that Arnot''s conclusion is false."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The subscriber treats this as clear."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "After all, the argument Arnot makes for this claim depends on the dubious assumption that government can be trusted to act in the interest of the public.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Arnot makes an argument for the claim."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The argument depends on an assumption."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The assumption is dubious."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The assumption is that government can be trusted."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It would be trusted to act in the public''s interest."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because the argument relies on a questionable assumption, that doesn''t mean it''s totally wrong?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q15", "prompt": "Columnist: Shortsighted motorists learn the hard way about the wisdom of preventive auto maintenance; such maintenance almost always pays off in the long run. Our usually shortsighted city council should be praised for using similar wisdom when they hired a long-term economic development adviser. In hiring this adviser, the council made an investment that is likely to have a big payoff in several years. Other cities in this region that have devoted resources to economic development planning have earned large returns on such an investment.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A columnist is speaking. · The motorists meant are the shortsighted ones. · They learn the hard way. · What they learn is the wisdom of preventive auto maintenance. · Such maintenance pays off. · It pays off in the long run. · It does so almost always. (2) This is about the city council. · The council is usually shortsighted. · This is the conclusion. · The council should be praised. · It used similar wisdom. · It hired an adviser. · The adviser is for long-term economic development. (3) Hiring the adviser was an investment. · The council made it. · The investment is likely to have a payoff. · The payoff would be big. · It would come in several years. (4) This is about other cities. · Those cities are in this region. · They devoted resources to economic development planning. · Those cities earned returns. · The returns were large. · The returns came on such an investment.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Columnist: Shortsighted motorists learn the hard way about the wisdom of preventive auto maintenance; such maintenance almost always pays off in the long run.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A columnist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The motorists meant are the shortsighted ones."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They learn the hard way."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What they learn is the wisdom of preventive auto maintenance."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Such maintenance pays off."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It pays off in the long run."}, {"n": 7, "text": "It does so almost always."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Our usually shortsighted city council should be praised for using similar wisdom when they hired a long-term economic development adviser.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the city council."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The council is usually shortsighted."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The council should be praised."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It used similar wisdom."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It hired an adviser."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The adviser is for long-term economic development."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "In hiring this adviser, the council made an investment that is likely to have a big payoff in several years.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Hiring the adviser was an investment."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The council made it."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The investment is likely to have a payoff."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The payoff would be big."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It would come in several years."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Other cities in this region that have devoted resources to economic development planning have earned large returns on such an investment.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about other cities."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those cities are in this region."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They devoted resources to economic development planning."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those cities earned returns."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The returns were large."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The returns came on such an investment."}]}], "the_read": "What if this city is not comparable to other cities that have seen large returns on the investment in an economic development adviser, so this city won''t see the same returns?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q16", "prompt": "Editorial: Cell-phone usage on buses and trains is annoying to other passengers. This suggests that recent proposals to allow use of cell phones on airplanes are ill-advised. Cell-phone use would be far more upsetting on airplanes than it is on buses and trains. Airline passengers are usually packed in tightly. And if airline passengers are offended by the cell-phone excesses of their seatmates, they often cannot move to another seat.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is an editorial. · It is about cell-phone usage. · The usage is on buses. · It is also on trains. · The usage annoys other passengers. (2) This is the conclusion. · It is suggested rather than proven. · There are certain proposals. · The proposals are recent. · They would allow cell phone use on airplanes. · The proposals are ill-advised. (3) Cell-phone use would be upsetting on airplanes. · It would be far more upsetting. · The comparison is with buses and trains. (4) This is about airline passengers. · They are packed in tightly. · This is usually so. (5) This is an additional point. · It is conditional on passengers being offended. · What offends them is their seatmates'' cell-phone excesses. · They cannot move to another seat. · This is often so.", "category": "Argument Part", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Editorial: Cell-phone usage on buses and trains is annoying to other passengers.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an editorial."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about cell-phone usage."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The usage is on buses."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is also on trains."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The usage annoys other passengers."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This suggests that recent proposals to allow use of cell phones on airplanes are ill-advised.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is suggested rather than proven."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There are certain proposals."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The proposals are recent."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They would allow cell phone use on airplanes."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The proposals are ill-advised."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Cell-phone use would be far more upsetting on airplanes than it is on buses and trains.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Cell-phone use would be upsetting on airplanes."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It would be far more upsetting."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The comparison is with buses and trains."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Airline passengers are usually packed in tightly.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about airline passengers."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They are packed in tightly."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is usually so."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "And if airline passengers are offended by the cell-phone excesses of their seatmates, they often cannot move to another seat.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional point."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is conditional on passengers being offended."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What offends them is their seatmates'' cell-phone excesses."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They cannot move to another seat."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is often so."}]}], "the_read": "What if cell phone usage on buses and trains are annoying because people are making phone calls, but people would only be allowed to use a phone to text on airplanes?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Argument Part", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 17, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q17", "prompt": "Science writer: The deterioration of cognitive faculties associated with Alzheimer''s disease is evidently caused by the activities of microglia—the brain''s own immune cells. For one thing, this deterioration can be slowed by some anti-inflammatory drugs, such as acetylsalicylic acid. Furthermore, patients with Alzheimer''s are unable to eliminate the protein BA from the brain, where it accumulates and forms deposits. The microglia attack these protein deposits by releasing poisons that destroy surrounding healthy brain cells, thereby impairing the brain''s cognitive functions.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A science writer is speaking. · This is the conclusion. · It is about deterioration of cognitive faculties. · That deterioration is associated with Alzheimer''s disease. · It is caused by the activities of microglia. · Microglia are the brain''s own immune cells. · This is marked as evident. (2) This is the first reason given. · The deterioration can be slowed. · Some anti-inflammatory drugs slow it. · Acetylsalicylic acid is given as an example. (3) This is an additional reason. · It is about patients with Alzheimer''s. · They cannot eliminate a protein. · The protein is BA. · They cannot eliminate it from the brain. · The protein accumulates there. · The protein forms deposits. (4) The microglia attack the protein deposits. · They attack by releasing poisons. · The poisons destroy brain cells. · Those cells are healthy ones. · The cells are the surrounding ones. · Doing so impairs the brain''s cognitive functions.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Science writer: The deterioration of cognitive faculties associated with Alzheimer''s disease is evidently caused by the activities of microglia—the brain''s own immune cells.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A science writer is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about deterioration of cognitive faculties."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That deterioration is associated with Alzheimer''s disease."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is caused by the activities of microglia."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Microglia are the brain''s own immune cells."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This is marked as evident."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "For one thing, this deterioration can be slowed by some anti-inflammatory drugs, such as acetylsalicylic acid.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the first reason given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The deterioration can be slowed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Some anti-inflammatory drugs slow it."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Acetylsalicylic acid is given as an example."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Furthermore, patients with Alzheimer''s are unable to eliminate the protein BA from the brain, where it accumulates and forms deposits.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional reason."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about patients with Alzheimer''s."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They cannot eliminate a protein."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The protein is BA."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They cannot eliminate it from the brain."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The protein accumulates there."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The protein forms deposits."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "The microglia attack these protein deposits by releasing poisons that destroy surrounding healthy brain cells, thereby impairing the brain''s cognitive functions.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The microglia attack the protein deposits."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They attack by releasing poisons."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The poisons destroy brain cells."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those cells are healthy ones."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cells are the surrounding ones."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Doing so impairs the brain''s cognitive functions."}]}], "the_read": "What if there''s no relationship between microglia and Alzheimer''s? What if something else causes Alzheimer''s? What if an Alzheimer''s diagnosis increases the amount of microglia in the brain?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q17", "qnum": 17, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 18, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q18", "prompt": "Lawyer: One is justified in accessing information in computer files without securing authorization from the computer''s owner only if the computer is typically used in the operation of a business. If, in addition, there exist reasonable grounds for believing that such a computer contains data usable as evidence in a legal proceeding against the computer''s owner, then accessing the data in those computer files without the owner''s authorization is justified.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A lawyer is speaking. · This states a necessary condition. · It is about accessing information in computer files. · The access is without the owner''s authorization. · Justification requires one thing. · The computer must be typically used in the operation of a business. (2) This adds a further condition. · There must exist grounds for a belief. · The grounds must be reasonable. · The belief is that the computer contains data. · The data would be usable as evidence. · The evidence would be in a legal proceeding. · The proceeding would be against the computer''s owner. (3) This is the consequent. · Accessing the data is justified. · The data is in those computer files. · The access to that data is without the owner''s authorization.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Lawyer: One is justified in accessing information in computer files without securing authorization from the computer''s owner only if the computer is typically used in the operation of a business.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A lawyer is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This states a necessary condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is about accessing information in computer files."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The access is without the owner''s authorization."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Justification requires one thing."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The computer must be typically used in the operation of a business."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "If, in addition, there exist reasonable grounds for believing that such a computer contains data usable as evidence in a legal proceeding against the computer''s owner,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This adds a further condition."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There must exist grounds for a belief."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The grounds must be reasonable."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The belief is that the computer contains data."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The data would be usable as evidence."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The evidence would be in a legal proceeding."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The proceeding would be against the computer''s owner."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "then accessing the data in those computer files without the owner''s authorization is justified.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the consequent."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Accessing the data is justified."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The data is in those computer files."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The access to that data is without the owner''s authorization."}]}], "the_read": "If a computer is not typically used in the operation of a business, then one is not justified in accessing information in the files without the owner''s permission.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q18", "qnum": 18, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 19, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q19", "prompt": "The conventional process for tanning leather uses large amounts of calcium oxide and sodium sulfide. Tanning leather using biological catalysts costs about the same as using these conventional chemicals if the cost of waste disposal is left out of the comparison. However, nearly 20 percent less waste is produced with biological catalysts, and waste disposal is a substantial part of the overall cost of tanning. It is therefore less costly to tan leather if biological catalysts are used instead.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a conventional process. · The process is for tanning leather. · It uses calcium oxide. · It also uses sodium sulfide. · The amounts are large. (2) Leather can be tanned using biological catalysts. · That costs about the same. · The comparison is with using the conventional chemicals. · This holds under a condition. · The condition is leaving out the cost of waste disposal. (3) This is a contrast. · Less waste is produced with biological catalysts. · The figure is nearly 20 percent less. · Waste disposal is part of the overall cost. · That part is substantial. · The cost is the overall cost of tanning. (4) This is the conclusion. · Tanning leather is less costly. · This holds if biological catalysts are used. · They would be used instead of the chemicals.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "The conventional process for tanning leather uses large amounts of calcium oxide and sodium sulfide.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a conventional process."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The process is for tanning leather."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It uses calcium oxide."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It also uses sodium sulfide."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The amounts are large."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Tanning leather using biological catalysts costs about the same as using these conventional chemicals if the cost of waste disposal is left out of the comparison.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Leather can be tanned using biological catalysts."}, {"n": 2, "text": "That costs about the same."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The comparison is with using the conventional chemicals."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This holds under a condition."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The condition is leaving out the cost of waste disposal."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "However, nearly 20 percent less waste is produced with biological catalysts, and waste disposal is a substantial part of the overall cost of tanning.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Less waste is produced with biological catalysts."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The figure is nearly 20 percent less."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Waste disposal is part of the overall cost."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That part is substantial."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The cost is the overall cost of tanning."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is therefore less costly to tan leather if biological catalysts are used instead.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Tanning leather is less costly."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This holds if biological catalysts are used."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They would be used instead of the chemicals."}]}], "the_read": "What if it costs much more to dispose of biological catalysts than to dispose of chemicals?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q19", "qnum": 19, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 20, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q20", "prompt": "One should not play a practical joke on someone if it shows contempt for that person or if one believes it might bring significant harm to that person.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) One should not play a practical joke. · The joke would be on someone. · Either of two conditions is enough. · One condition is that it shows contempt. · The contempt would be for that person. · The other condition is a belief. · The belief is that it might bring harm. · The harm would be significant.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "One should not play a practical joke on someone if it shows contempt for that person or if one believes it might bring significant harm to that person.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "One should not play a practical joke."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The joke would be on someone."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Either of two conditions is enough."}, {"n": 4, "text": "One condition is that it shows contempt."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The contempt would be for that person."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The other condition is a belief."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The belief is that it might bring harm."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The harm would be significant."}]}], "the_read": "If one should play a practical joke on another person, then the joke does not show contempt and the one playing the joke does not believe it might bring significant harm.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q20", "qnum": 20, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 21, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q21", "prompt": "Economics professor: Marty''s Pizza and Checkers Pizza are the two major pizza parlors in our town. Marty''s sold coupon books including coupons good for one large plain pizza at any local pizza parlor, at Marty''s expense. But Checkers refused to accept these coupons, even though they were redeemed by all other local pizza parlors. Accepting them would have cost Checkers nothing and would have satisfied those of its potential customers who had purchased the coupon books. This shows that Checkers''s motive in refusing to accept the coupons was simply to hurt Marty''s Pizza.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) An economics professor is speaking. · There are two major pizza parlors. · One is Marty''s Pizza. · The other is Checkers Pizza. · They are in the professor''s town. (2) Marty''s sold coupon books. · The books included coupons. · Each coupon was good for one large plain pizza. · The coupon was good at any local pizza parlor. · The cost fell on Marty''s. (3) This is a contrast. · Checkers refused to accept the coupons. · This is despite something. · All other local pizza parlors redeemed them. (4) Accepting them would have cost Checkers nothing. · It would also have satisfied some people. · Those people are its potential customers. · Those customers had purchased the coupon books. (5) This is the conclusion. · It is about Checkers''s motive. · The motive was in refusing the coupons. · The motive was to hurt Marty''s Pizza. · That was the only motive.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Economics professor: Marty''s Pizza and Checkers Pizza are the two major pizza parlors in our town.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "An economics professor is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "There are two major pizza parlors."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One is Marty''s Pizza."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other is Checkers Pizza."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They are in the professor''s town."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Marty''s sold coupon books including coupons good for one large plain pizza at any local pizza parlor, at Marty''s expense.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Marty''s sold coupon books."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The books included coupons."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Each coupon was good for one large plain pizza."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The coupon was good at any local pizza parlor."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cost fell on Marty''s."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "But Checkers refused to accept these coupons, even though they were redeemed by all other local pizza parlors.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Checkers refused to accept the coupons."}, {"n": 3, "text": "This is despite something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "All other local pizza parlors redeemed them."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Accepting them would have cost Checkers nothing and would have satisfied those of its potential customers who had purchased the coupon books.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Accepting them would have cost Checkers nothing."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It would also have satisfied some people."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those people are its potential customers."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those customers had purchased the coupon books."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "This shows that Checkers''s motive in refusing to accept the coupons was simply to hurt Marty''s Pizza.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about Checkers''s motive."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The motive was in refusing the coupons."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The motive was to hurt Marty''s Pizza."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That was the only motive."}]}], "the_read": "What if Checkers''s has a company policy to not accept coupons not issued by them and therefore the motive is to follow policy? What if it would have been super inconvenient and an administrative hassle for Checkers to accept the coupon?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q21", "qnum": 21, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 22, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q22", "prompt": "Science writer: Scientists'' astounding success rate with research problems they have been called upon to solve causes the public to believe falsely that science can solve any problem. In fact, the problems scientists are called upon to solve are typically selected by scientists themselves. When the problems are instead selected by politicians or business leaders, their formulation is nevertheless guided by scientists in such a way as to make scientific solutions feasible. Scientists are almost never asked to solve problems that are not subject to such formulation.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A science writer is speaking. · Scientists have a success rate. · The rate is astounding. · It is with problems they were called upon to solve. · That success causes a public belief. · The belief is that science can solve any problem. · The belief is false. (2) This is the correction. · It is about the problems scientists are called upon to solve. · Scientists themselves select them. · This is typically so. (3) This is the other case. · The problems are selected by someone else. · Either politicians or business leaders select them. · In that case scientists do not select them. (4) This is a contrast. · This is about their formulation. · Scientists guide the formulation. · The guidance has a purpose. · The purpose is to make scientific solutions feasible. (5) Scientists are almost never asked something. · What they are not asked is to solve certain problems. · Those problems are not subject to such formulation.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Science writer: Scientists'' astounding success rate with research problems they have been called upon to solve causes the public to believe falsely that science can solve any problem.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A science writer is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Scientists have a success rate."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The rate is astounding."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is with problems they were called upon to solve."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That success causes a public belief."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The belief is that science can solve any problem."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The belief is false."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "In fact, the problems scientists are called upon to solve are typically selected by scientists themselves.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the correction."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the problems scientists are called upon to solve."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Scientists themselves select them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "This is typically so."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "When the problems are instead selected by politicians or business leaders,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the other case."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The problems are selected by someone else."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Either politicians or business leaders select them."}, {"n": 4, "text": "In that case scientists do not select them."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "their formulation is nevertheless guided by scientists in such a way as to make scientific solutions feasible.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is about their formulation."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Scientists guide the formulation."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The guidance has a purpose."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The purpose is to make scientific solutions feasible."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "Scientists are almost never asked to solve problems that are not subject to such formulation.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Scientists are almost never asked something."}, {"n": 2, "text": "What they are not asked is to solve certain problems."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those problems are not subject to such formulation."}]}], "the_read": "Scientists may not have the same problem solving success rate if they did not choose or help to formulate the problems they are called upon to solve.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q22", "qnum": 22, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 23, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q23", "prompt": "Most auto mechanics have extensive experience. Furthermore, most mechanics with extensive experience understand electronic circuits. Thus, most auto mechanics understand electronic circuits.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about auto mechanics. · Most of them have experience. · The experience is extensive. (2) This is an additional premise. · It is about mechanics with extensive experience. · Most of those experienced mechanics understand electronic circuits. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about auto mechanics. · Most auto mechanics understand electronic circuits.", "category": "Parallel Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Most auto mechanics have extensive experience.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about auto mechanics."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Most of them have experience."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The experience is extensive."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Furthermore, most mechanics with extensive experience understand electronic circuits.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is an additional premise."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about mechanics with extensive experience."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Most of those experienced mechanics understand electronic circuits."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Thus, most auto mechanics understand electronic circuits.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about auto mechanics."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Most auto mechanics understand electronic circuits."}]}], "the_read": "What if we can''t use bad some/most conditional reasoning?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q23", "qnum": 23, "question_type": "Parallel Flaw", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 24, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q24", "prompt": "If one wants to succeed, then one should act as though one were genuinely confident about one''s abilities, even if one actually distrusts one''s skills. Success is much more easily obtained by those who genuinely believe themselves capable of succeeding than by those filled with self-doubts.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is conditional on wanting to succeed. · One should act a certain way. · One should act as though genuinely confident. · The confidence would be about one''s abilities. · This holds even in one case. · That case is actually distrusting one''s skills. (2) This is the reason given. · Success is more easily obtained by some people. · It is much more easily obtained. · Those people genuinely believe themselves capable. · The comparison is with those filled with self-doubts.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "If one wants to succeed, then one should act as though one were genuinely confident about one''s abilities, even if one actually distrusts one''s skills.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is conditional on wanting to succeed."}, {"n": 2, "text": "One should act a certain way."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One should act as though genuinely confident."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The confidence would be about one''s abilities."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This holds even in one case."}, {"n": 6, "text": "That case is actually distrusting one''s skills."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Success is much more easily obtained by those who genuinely believe themselves capable of succeeding than by those filled with self-doubts.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the reason given."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Success is more easily obtained by some people."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is much more easily obtained."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those people genuinely believe themselves capable."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with those filled with self-doubts."}]}], "the_read": "What if genuine self confidence cannot be gained from fake-it-til-you-make-it?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q24", "qnum": 24, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 25, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q25", "prompt": "Journalist: The trade union members at AutoFaber Inc. are planning to go on strike. Independent arbitration would avert a strike, but only if both sides agree to accept the arbitrator''s recommendations as binding. However, based on past experience, the union is quite unlikely to agree to this, so a strike is likely.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) A journalist is speaking. · Trade union members are planning something. · The members are at AutoFaber Inc. · They plan to go on strike. (2) Independent arbitration would avert a strike. · This holds under one condition only. · Both sides must agree to something. · They must accept the arbitrator''s recommendations. · They must accept them as binding. (3) This is a contrast. · The claim is based on past experience. · The union is unlikely to agree. · It is quite unlikely. · This is the conclusion. · A strike is likely.", "category": "Parallel Reasoning", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Journalist: The trade union members at AutoFaber Inc. are planning to go on strike.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A journalist is speaking."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Trade union members are planning something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The members are at AutoFaber Inc."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They plan to go on strike."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Independent arbitration would avert a strike, but only if both sides agree to accept the arbitrator''s recommendations as binding.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Independent arbitration would avert a strike."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This holds under one condition only."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Both sides must agree to something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They must accept the arbitrator''s recommendations."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They must accept them as binding."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "However, based on past experience, the union is quite unlikely to agree to this, so a strike is likely.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The claim is based on past experience."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The union is unlikely to agree."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It is quite unlikely."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 6, "text": "A strike is likely."}]}], "the_read": "What if just because arbitration was unlikely in the past, that doesn''t mean it''s unlikely now?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q25", "qnum": 25, "question_type": "Parallel Reasoning", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 26, "slug": "PT134_S3_Q26", "prompt": "Acquiring complete detailed information about all the pros and cons of a product one might purchase would clearly be difficult and expensive. It is rational not to acquire such information unless one expects that the benefits of doing so will outweigh the cost and difficulty of doing so. Therefore, consumers who do not bother to acquire such information are thereby behaving rationally.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about acquiring information. · The information would be complete and detailed. · It concerns all the pros and cons. · They are of a product one might purchase. · Acquiring it would be difficult. · It would also be expensive. · This is treated as clear. (2) Not acquiring such information is rational. · This holds unless one expects something. · The expectation is that benefits will outweigh. · The benefits are of acquiring it. · What they outweigh is the cost and difficulty. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about certain consumers. · Those consumers do not bother to acquire the information. · They are behaving rationally.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Acquiring complete detailed information about all the pros and cons of a product one might purchase would clearly be difficult and expensive.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about acquiring information."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The information would be complete and detailed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It concerns all the pros and cons."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are of a product one might purchase."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Acquiring it would be difficult."}, {"n": 6, "text": "It would also be expensive."}, {"n": 7, "text": "This is treated as clear."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is rational not to acquire such information unless one expects that the benefits of doing so will outweigh the cost and difficulty of doing so.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Not acquiring such information is rational."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This holds unless one expects something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The expectation is that benefits will outweigh."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The benefits are of acquiring it."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What they outweigh is the cost and difficulty."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, consumers who do not bother to acquire such information are thereby behaving rationally.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about certain consumers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those consumers do not bother to acquire the information."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are behaving rationally."}]}], "the_read": "What if the benefits of acquiring the information are always expected to outweigh the cost?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT134.S3.Q26", "qnum": 26, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "E"}]}]'::jsonb,
    'section',
    '{"standard": 25, "accommodation_53": 38, "accommodation_70": 50}'::jsonb,
    'PT134.S3'
  ),
  (
    'read-recall-colour-pt140-s1',
    'Read, Recall, Colour · PT140 Section 1',
    'A whole section, out loud, against the clock',
    '{"tagline": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, and react to it. Once you finish the stimulus, say the whole argument back from memory.", "task": "Read each piece silently. Look away, say it back in your own words, then give it a reaction. After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears, and you recall the whole argument out loud from memory.", "rules": ["Read each piece silently. Everything you say back, including your reaction and final recall, is out loud.", "Read about a sentence at a time. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less.", "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said.", "Then colour it: react to that specific piece with a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling.", "Repeat these steps through the whole stimulus.", "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and stays gone. Say the entire argument back from memory, with every detail you can still hold.", "Press space again to move on. The whole section records in one take, with no pauses or breaks."], "briefing": {"lede": "Read these instructions once before your first section. After that, come back when you need a reminder.", "sections": [{"heading": "You can understand every sentence and still reach the answers remembering almost none of it.", "body": ["You can follow a stimulus sentence by sentence and understand every part. Then you reach answer choice C and realise you are holding almost none of it. That is not a comprehension problem. It is a retention problem, and that is what this drill trains.", "Understanding is necessary for everything and sufficient for nothing. Understanding the stimulus is not enough if it is gone by the time you reach the answers.", "Most everyday reading trains you to reach the end, not retain every detail. You scan names, drop connecting words, and turn a list of three things into one vague label. That works for a phone bill. It does not work for the LSAT."]}, {"heading": "How the method works", "body": ["For each piece, read it, say it back, and colour it. Once you finish the stimulus, recall the whole argument. You decide how much to read at a time, and nothing on the screen decides for you.", "Space is the only key, and you press it twice per stimulus. The first press takes the text away and asks you for the whole argument from memory. The second press moves you to the next stimulus. Nothing advances on its own, so a pause to think costs you nothing but the clock."], "steps": [{"label": "Read it", "body": "Read about a sentence silently. Give every word enough attention to hear the sentence naturally in your head. When you are unsure how much to take on, take less. If you take on too much, the beginning is usually what disappears first."}, {"label": "Tell it back", "body": "Look away from the screen and say it back in your own words, like you are telling a friend what it said. Out of order is fine. Casual is fine. Laughing is fine."}, {"label": "Colour it", "body": "React to what you just said. Give it a thought, question, doubt, example, joke, or feeling. Keep it short, but make it specific. If the same reaction could fit any sentence, it does not count."}, {"label": "Recall it", "body": "After the final piece, press space. The stimulus disappears and does not come back. Say the entire argument out loud from memory, with every detail you can still hold. This is what gets scored."}]}, {"heading": "Simpler is not the same as shorter", "body": ["Do not give back only the gist or what seemed important. You cannot know which details will matter until you have read the whole stimulus. Filtering as you read is how useful information disappears.", "Fewer words is fine when the words cover the same ideas. Fewer ideas is not. Names, numbers, and small words carrying the load, such as all, most, only, and probably, have to come back too.", "**Being efficient does not mean leaving information out.**"]}, {"heading": "When nothing comes to mind", "body": ["You may try to colour a sentence and find that nothing comes to mind. Usually, the hidden thought is that nothing feels good enough to say.", "Do not judge the reaction before you say it. Say something obvious on purpose if you have to. \"Okay, sure.\" \"That seems like a stretch.\" \"Who measured that?\" All of those count when they connect to what you just read.", "The reaction makes the sentence yours instead of the author''s. Skip it and you are back to repeating words you do not own."]}, {"heading": "Look away from the text", "body": ["The text stays visible while you say each piece back. You have to look away from it yourself.", "If you read while you recall, you stop using your short-term memory and slide back into the author''s exact words. Looking away forces you to rebuild the meaning in your own words.", "This is also the version that works on paper and on test day."]}, {"heading": "Build toward twenty-five minutes", "body": ["The goal is twenty-five minutes for a whole section. It only counts when you read every stimulus, say each piece back, colour it, and recall the whole argument. Finishing early by skipping parts is not finishing early. If you receive extra time on test day, use your full allotted time here too.", "The clock counts up so you can track your pace without watching your time disappear.", "Do the method correctly before you try to do it quickly. Go slower while it is new. Speed follows once the method becomes automatic."]}], "closer": "One section keeps the method steady. Improvement starts with the second."}, "coach": {"drill": "a whole Logical Reasoning section run out loud, where the student reads each line of a stimulus, looks away, tells it back from memory in her own words, reacts to it, and gives The Recall of the whole stimulus before moving to the next one", "rubric": ["Judge by meaning, never by wording. Her own words are required here, so a good recall will never match the line''s phrasing. High overlap with the original is the failure, not the standard.", "Order is free. She is encouraged to deliver the ideas out of the order she read them in, and reordering must never cost her anything.", "Casual, unpolished, laughing delivery is expected. Never mark down for style, length or fluency.", "A detail merely gestured at is not carried. Naming the topic is not the same as saying what was claimed about it.", "Numbers, names, dates, quantities and connecting words are each their own detail. Never treat one as carried because the sentence around it was.", "A reaction that could be pasted onto any other sentence does not count as a colour. Judge whether it engages this content, never whether it is correct or well put.", "Score The Recall against the captured key for that stimulus, not against a retelling. A different name for the same move is fine; missing the move is not.", "Silence during a silent read is correct and expected. Only silence inside a recall or a colour counts against her."], "partial_rule": "Mark ''partial'' when the retelling is real but at least one detail went missing, which is the drill''s ordinary state. Mark ''incorrect'' only when the response is a gist or a topic label rather than a retelling."}}'::jsonb,
    'spoken-recall',
    '[{"order": 1, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q1", "prompt": "Several years ago, most of one country''s large banks failed and were taken over by a government agency. The agency is now selling these banks, aiming to strengthen the banking system in the process. But the banking system will not be strengthened if the former owners of these banks buy them back. So the agency is unlikely to achieve its goal, since _______.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This happened several years ago. · The banks in question were large. · The banks belonged to one country. · Most of those banks failed. · A government agency took them over. (2) This is about the agency. · The agency is selling the banks now. · The agency has an aim. · Its aim is to strengthen the banking system. · The strengthening would happen in the process of selling. (3) This is a contrast with what came before. · The banking system will not be strengthened. · That holds under a condition. · The condition is that the banks are bought back. · The buyers would be the former owners of these banks. (4) This is the conclusion. · The agency is unlikely to achieve its goal. · A reason is being given for this. · The reason itself is left blank.", "category": "Sufficient Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Several years ago, most of one country''s large banks failed and were taken over by a government agency.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This happened several years ago."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The banks in question were large."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The banks belonged to one country."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Most of those banks failed."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A government agency took them over."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "The agency is now selling these banks, aiming to strengthen the banking system in the process.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about the agency."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The agency is selling the banks now."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The agency has an aim."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Its aim is to strengthen the banking system."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The strengthening would happen in the process of selling."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "But the banking system will not be strengthened if the former owners of these banks buy them back.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The banking system will not be strengthened."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That holds under a condition."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The condition is that the banks are bought back."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The buyers would be the former owners of these banks."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "So the agency is unlikely to achieve its goal, since _______.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The agency is unlikely to achieve its goal."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A reason is being given for this."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The reason itself is left blank."}]}], "the_read": "The only possible buyers are the former owners.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q1", "qnum": 1, "question_type": "Sufficient Assumption", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 2, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q2", "prompt": "Accountant: The newspaper industry habitually cites the rising cost of newsprint to explain falling profits. But when corrected for inflation, the cost of newsprint is no more than it was ten years ago. Far from being victims of high costs, newspapers have been benefiting from cheap newsprint for decades. The real threats to their profitability are falling circulation and falling advertising.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The accountant is the speaker. · The newspaper industry cites a cost. · The cost is the cost of newsprint. · That cost is rising. · The industry cites it to explain falling profits. · The industry does this habitually. (2) This is a contrast. · The cost of newsprint is corrected for inflation. · Corrected that way, the cost is no more than it once was. · The comparison is with ten years ago. (3) Newspapers are not victims of high costs. · Newspapers have been benefiting instead. · What they benefit from is cheap newsprint. · They have benefited for decades. (4) There are real threats. · The threats are to the newspapers'' profitability. · One threat is falling circulation. · The other threat is falling advertising.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Accountant: The newspaper industry habitually cites the rising cost of newsprint to explain falling profits.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The accountant is the speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The newspaper industry cites a cost."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The cost is the cost of newsprint."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That cost is rising."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The industry cites it to explain falling profits."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The industry does this habitually."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But when corrected for inflation, the cost of newsprint is no more than it was ten years ago.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The cost of newsprint is corrected for inflation."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Corrected that way, the cost is no more than it once was."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The comparison is with ten years ago."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Far from being victims of high costs, newspapers have been benefiting from cheap newsprint for decades.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Newspapers are not victims of high costs."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Newspapers have been benefiting instead."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they benefit from is cheap newsprint."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They have benefited for decades."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "The real threats to their profitability are falling circulation and falling advertising.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There are real threats."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The threats are to the newspapers'' profitability."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One threat is falling circulation."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The other threat is falling advertising."}]}], "the_read": "What if the cost of newsprint and falling profits were problematic 10 years ago as well?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q2", "qnum": 2, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 3, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q3", "prompt": "Peter: Recent evidence suggests that moderate alcohol consumption has certain beneficial effects on health. In particular, alcohol creates an inhospitable environment in the human body for certain bacteria that can cause illness. Thus, alcohol consumption is, on balance, beneficial.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Peter is the speaker. · The evidence is recent. · The evidence suggests something. · Alcohol consumption has beneficial effects. · The consumption is moderate. · The effects are on health. · Only certain effects are beneficial. (2) This is a specific case of the point before. · Alcohol creates an environment. · The environment is inhospitable. · The environment is in the human body. · It is inhospitable to certain bacteria. · Those bacteria can cause illness. (3) This is the conclusion. · Alcohol consumption is beneficial. · It is beneficial on balance.", "category": "Classic Flaw", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Peter: Recent evidence suggests that moderate alcohol consumption has certain beneficial effects on health.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Peter is the speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The evidence is recent."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The evidence suggests something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Alcohol consumption has beneficial effects."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The consumption is moderate."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The effects are on health."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Only certain effects are beneficial."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "In particular, alcohol creates an inhospitable environment in the human body for certain bacteria that can cause illness.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a specific case of the point before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Alcohol creates an environment."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The environment is inhospitable."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The environment is in the human body."}, {"n": 5, "text": "It is inhospitable to certain bacteria."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those bacteria can cause illness."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Thus, alcohol consumption is, on balance, beneficial.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Alcohol consumption is beneficial."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It is beneficial on balance."}]}], "the_read": "What if the negative health effects of alcohol outweigh the positives?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q3", "qnum": 3, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 4, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q4", "prompt": "Consultant: Children taught using innovative new educational methods learn to think more creatively than children taught using rote methods such as drills, but they are less adept at memorizing large amounts of information. Most jobs at Grodex Corporation require the ability to think creatively but do not require a strong ability to memorize. So Grodex should probably conduct its employee-training seminars using the innovative methods, because _______.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The consultant is the speaker. · Some children are taught using certain methods. · Those methods are innovative and new. · The methods are educational ones. · Those children learn to think more creatively. · The comparison is with children taught by rote methods. · Drills are an example of rote methods. (2) This is a contrast. · This is about the children taught the new way. · They are less adept at memorizing. · What they memorize is information. · The amounts of information are large. (3) This is about jobs at Grodex Corporation. · Most of those jobs require an ability. · The required ability is to think creatively. · Those jobs do not require another ability. · The ability they do not require is a strong ability to memorize. (4) This is the conclusion. · Grodex should conduct its seminars a certain way. · The seminars are for employee training. · It should use the innovative methods. · This is only probably so. · A reason is being given. · The reason itself is left blank.", "category": "Fill In", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Consultant: Children taught using innovative new educational methods learn to think more creatively than children taught using rote methods such as drills,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The consultant is the speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some children are taught using certain methods."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those methods are innovative and new."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The methods are educational ones."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those children learn to think more creatively."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with children taught by rote methods."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Drills are an example of rote methods."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "but they are less adept at memorizing large amounts of information.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This is about the children taught the new way."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They are less adept at memorizing."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What they memorize is information."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The amounts of information are large."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Most jobs at Grodex Corporation require the ability to think creatively but do not require a strong ability to memorize.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about jobs at Grodex Corporation."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Most of those jobs require an ability."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The required ability is to think creatively."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those jobs do not require another ability."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The ability they do not require is a strong ability to memorize."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "So Grodex should probably conduct its employee-training seminars using the innovative methods, because _______.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Grodex should conduct its seminars a certain way."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The seminars are for employee training."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It should use the innovative methods."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is only probably so."}, {"n": 6, "text": "A reason is being given."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The reason itself is left blank."}]}], "the_read": "What is true of children''s teaching methods is true of adult teaching methods.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q4", "qnum": 4, "question_type": "Fill In", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 5, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q5", "prompt": "Essayist: If Earth''s population continues to grow geometrically, then in a few centuries there will be ten people for every square meter (approximately one person per square foot) of Earth''s surface. Some people have claimed that this will probably not be a problem, since humans will have learned by then how to colonize other planets. This would, however, be a temporary solution at best: if the population continues to double every 30 years, and if in the year 2500 half of Earth''s population emigrated to Mars, then by the year 2530 Earth would be just as crowded as it had been before the emigration.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The essayist is the speaker. · This states a condition. · Earth''s population might continue to grow. · The growth is geometric. (2) This is what would follow. · It would happen in a few centuries. · There would be ten people per square meter. · The square meters are of Earth''s surface. · That is about one person per square foot. · The square-foot figure is approximate. (3) Some people have made a claim. · Their claim is that this will not be a problem. · They say it probably will not be. · They give a reason. · The reason is that humans will have learned to colonize other planets. · Humans will have learned that by then. (4) This is a contrast with the claim. · The solution would be temporary. · It would be temporary at best. · A condition is introduced. · The condition is that the population keeps doubling. · The doubling happens every 30 years. (5) A second condition is added. · The second condition is set in the year 2500. · Half of Earth''s population would emigrate. · They would emigrate to Mars. · Something would follow by the year 2530. · Earth would be just as crowded as it had been. · The comparison is with how crowded it was before the emigration.", "category": "Conclusion", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Essayist: If Earth''s population continues to grow geometrically,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The essayist is the speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This states a condition."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Earth''s population might continue to grow."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The growth is geometric."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "then in a few centuries there will be ten people for every square meter (approximately one person per square foot) of Earth''s surface.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is what would follow."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It would happen in a few centuries."}, {"n": 3, "text": "There would be ten people per square meter."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The square meters are of Earth''s surface."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That is about one person per square foot."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The square-foot figure is approximate."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Some people have claimed that this will probably not be a problem, since humans will have learned by then how to colonize other planets.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some people have made a claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Their claim is that this will not be a problem."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They say it probably will not be."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They give a reason."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The reason is that humans will have learned to colonize other planets."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Humans will have learned that by then."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "This would, however, be a temporary solution at best: if the population continues to double every 30 years,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with the claim."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The solution would be temporary."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It would be temporary at best."}, {"n": 4, "text": "A condition is introduced."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The condition is that the population keeps doubling."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The doubling happens every 30 years."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "and if in the year 2500 half of Earth''s population emigrated to Mars, then by the year 2530 Earth would be just as crowded as it had been before the emigration.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A second condition is added."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The second condition is set in the year 2500."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Half of Earth''s population would emigrate."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They would emigrate to Mars."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Something would follow by the year 2530."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Earth would be just as crowded as it had been."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The comparison is with how crowded it was before the emigration."}]}], "the_read": "What if the rate at which we colonize other planets can outpace population growth? What if space constraints on other planets are different than those on Earth?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q5", "qnum": 5, "question_type": "Conclusion", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 6, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q6", "prompt": "A recent taste test reveals that most people like low-fat chocolate ice cream as much as its full-fat counterpart. Previous tests with vanilla ice cream found that people tended to dislike low-fat versions, complaining of a harsher taste. Chemists point out that chocolate is a very complex flavor, requiring around 500 distinct chemical compounds to produce it. Hence, this complexity probably masks any difference in taste due to the lack of fat.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There was a taste test. · The test was recent. · The test reveals something. · Most people like low-fat chocolate ice cream. · They like it as much as the full-fat version. (2) There were earlier tests. · Those tests used vanilla ice cream. · Those tests found that people disliked the low-fat versions. · People tended to dislike them rather than always doing so. · People complained about the taste. · They said the taste was harsher. (3) Chemists point something out. · Chocolate is a flavor. · The flavor is very complex. · Producing it requires chemical compounds. · The compounds are distinct from one another. · The number of compounds is around 500. (4) This is the conclusion. · The complexity masks a difference. · The difference is in taste. · The difference is due to the lack of fat. · This is only probably so.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "A recent taste test reveals that most people like low-fat chocolate ice cream as much as its full-fat counterpart.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There was a taste test."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The test was recent."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The test reveals something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Most people like low-fat chocolate ice cream."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They like it as much as the full-fat version."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Previous tests with vanilla ice cream found that people tended to dislike low-fat versions, complaining of a harsher taste.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There were earlier tests."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Those tests used vanilla ice cream."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those tests found that people disliked the low-fat versions."}, {"n": 4, "text": "People tended to dislike them rather than always doing so."}, {"n": 5, "text": "People complained about the taste."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They said the taste was harsher."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Chemists point out that chocolate is a very complex flavor, requiring around 500 distinct chemical compounds to produce it.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Chemists point something out."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Chocolate is a flavor."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The flavor is very complex."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Producing it requires chemical compounds."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The compounds are distinct from one another."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The number of compounds is around 500."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Hence, this complexity probably masks any difference in taste due to the lack of fat.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The complexity masks a difference."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The difference is in taste."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The difference is due to the lack of fat."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is only probably so."}]}], "the_read": "What if some other difference between chocolate and vanilla, like texture and color, accounts for the differing opinions?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q6", "qnum": 6, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 7, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q7", "prompt": "Ethicist: Robert Gillette has argued that because a thorough knowledge of genetics would enable us to cure the over 3,000 inherited disorders that affect humanity, deciphering the human genetic code will certainly benefit humanity despite its enormous cost. Gillette''s argument is not persuasive, however, because he fails to consider that such knowledge might ultimately harm human beings more than it would benefit them.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The ethicist is the speaker. · Robert Gillette has made an argument. · His reason involves knowledge of genetics. · The knowledge would be thorough. · Such knowledge would enable us to cure disorders. · The disorders are inherited ones. · There are over 3,000 of them. · Those disorders affect humanity. (2) This is Gillette''s conclusion. · Deciphering the human genetic code will benefit humanity. · It will certainly do so. · There is a cost to deciphering it. · The cost is enormous. · The benefit holds despite that cost. (3) This is a contrast with Gillette. · The ethicist says Gillette''s argument is not persuasive. · A reason is given for that. · The reason is that Gillette fails to consider something. · What he fails to consider is that the knowledge might harm human beings. · The harm might come ultimately. · The comparison is with how much the knowledge would benefit them.", "category": "Conclusion", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Ethicist: Robert Gillette has argued that because a thorough knowledge of genetics would enable us to cure the over 3,000 inherited disorders that affect humanity,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The ethicist is the speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Robert Gillette has made an argument."}, {"n": 3, "text": "His reason involves knowledge of genetics."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The knowledge would be thorough."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Such knowledge would enable us to cure disorders."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The disorders are inherited ones."}, {"n": 7, "text": "There are over 3,000 of them."}, {"n": 8, "text": "Those disorders affect humanity."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "deciphering the human genetic code will certainly benefit humanity despite its enormous cost.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is Gillette''s conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Deciphering the human genetic code will benefit humanity."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It will certainly do so."}, {"n": 4, "text": "There is a cost to deciphering it."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cost is enormous."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The benefit holds despite that cost."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Gillette''s argument is not persuasive, however, because he fails to consider that such knowledge might ultimately harm human beings more than it would benefit them.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with Gillette."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The ethicist says Gillette''s argument is not persuasive."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A reason is given for that."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The reason is that Gillette fails to consider something."}, {"n": 5, "text": "What he fails to consider is that the knowledge might harm human beings."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The harm might come ultimately."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The comparison is with how much the knowledge would benefit them."}]}], "the_read": "What if Gillette reasonably assumes that enabling the curing of 3,000 genetic disorders is the only goal of humanity, meaning no harm could reasonably outweigh that benefit?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q7", "qnum": 7, "question_type": "Conclusion", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 8, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q8", "prompt": "Many uses have been claimed for hypnosis, from combating drug addiction to overcoming common phobias. A recent experimental study helps illuminate the supposed connection between hypnosis and increased power of recall. A number of subjects listened to a long, unfamiliar piece of instrumental music. Under subsequent hypnosis, half the subjects were asked to recall salient passages from the musical piece and half were asked to describe scenes from \"the film they had just viewed,\" despite their not having just seen a film. The study found that the subjects in the second group were equally confident and detailed in their movie recollections as the subjects in the first group were in their music recollections.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Uses have been claimed for hypnosis. · Many uses have been claimed. · One claimed use is combating drug addiction. · Another claimed use is overcoming phobias. · Those phobias are common ones. (2) There is a study. · The study is recent. · The study is experimental. · The study helps illuminate a connection. · The connection is between hypnosis and power of recall. · The recall power is increased. · The connection is only supposed. (3) Some subjects listened to a piece of music. · The number of subjects is not given. · The piece was long. · The piece was unfamiliar to them. · The music was instrumental. (4) The subjects were hypnotized afterward. · Half of the subjects got one request. · They were asked to recall passages from the musical piece. · The passages were salient ones. · The other half got a different request. · They were asked to describe scenes from a film. · The request said they had just viewed that film. · Those subjects had not just seen a film. (5) The study made a finding. · It is about the subjects in the second group. · Those subjects were confident in their movie recollections. · They were also detailed in those recollections. · The comparison is with the subjects in the first group. · The first group''s recollections were of the music. · The two groups were equal on both counts.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Many uses have been claimed for hypnosis, from combating drug addiction to overcoming common phobias.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Uses have been claimed for hypnosis."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Many uses have been claimed."}, {"n": 3, "text": "One claimed use is combating drug addiction."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Another claimed use is overcoming phobias."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Those phobias are common ones."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "A recent experimental study helps illuminate the supposed connection between hypnosis and increased power of recall.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "There is a study."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The study is recent."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The study is experimental."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The study helps illuminate a connection."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The connection is between hypnosis and power of recall."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The recall power is increased."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The connection is only supposed."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "A number of subjects listened to a long, unfamiliar piece of instrumental music.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some subjects listened to a piece of music."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The number of subjects is not given."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The piece was long."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The piece was unfamiliar to them."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The music was instrumental."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "Under subsequent hypnosis, half the subjects were asked to recall salient passages from the musical piece and half were asked to describe scenes from \"the film they had just viewed,\" despite their not having just seen a film.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The subjects were hypnotized afterward."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Half of the subjects got one request."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They were asked to recall passages from the musical piece."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The passages were salient ones."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The other half got a different request."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They were asked to describe scenes from a film."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The request said they had just viewed that film."}, {"n": 8, "text": "Those subjects had not just seen a film."}]}, {"n": 5, "text": "The study found that the subjects in the second group were equally confident and detailed in their movie recollections as the subjects in the first group were in their music recollections.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The study made a finding."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the subjects in the second group."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those subjects were confident in their movie recollections."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They were also detailed in those recollections."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with the subjects in the first group."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The first group''s recollections were of the music."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The two groups were equal on both counts."}]}], "the_read": "People under hypnosis may \"remember\" things that did not actually occur.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q8", "qnum": 8, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "D"}]}, {"order": 9, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q9", "prompt": "Records from 1850 to 1900 show that in a certain region, babies'' birth weights each year varied with the success of the previous year''s crops: the more successful the crops, the higher the birth weights. This indicates that the health of a newborn depends to a large extent on the amount of food available to the mother during her pregnancy.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The records run from 1850 to 1900. · The records show something. · What they show holds in a certain region. · Babies'' birth weights varied. · The weights varied each year. · They varied with the success of the crops. · The crops were the previous year''s. (2) This spells out the pattern. · The crops could be more successful. · The birth weights were then higher. (3) This is the conclusion. · A newborn''s health depends on something. · It depends to a large extent. · What it depends on is the amount of food available. · The food is available to the mother. · The availability is during her pregnancy.", "category": "Method", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Records from 1850 to 1900 show that in a certain region, babies'' birth weights each year varied with the success of the previous year''s crops:", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The records run from 1850 to 1900."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The records show something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they show holds in a certain region."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Babies'' birth weights varied."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The weights varied each year."}, {"n": 6, "text": "They varied with the success of the crops."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The crops were the previous year''s."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "the more successful the crops, the higher the birth weights.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This spells out the pattern."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The crops could be more successful."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The birth weights were then higher."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "This indicates that the health of a newborn depends to a large extent on the amount of food available to the mother during her pregnancy.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "A newborn''s health depends on something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "It depends to a large extent."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What it depends on is the amount of food available."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The food is available to the mother."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The availability is during her pregnancy."}]}], "the_read": "What if, in this region, all crops are sold internationally, so more a successful crop doesn''t mean more plentiful food? What if birth weights were higher during years with more successful crops because of the increased oxygen that the crops put into the atmosphere?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q9", "qnum": 9, "question_type": "Method", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 10, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q10", "prompt": "Vincent: No scientific discipline can study something that cannot be measured, and since happiness is an entirely subjective experience, it cannot be measured.\n\nYolanda: Just as optometry relies on patients'' reports of what they see, happiness research relies on subjects'' reports of how they feel. Surely optometry is a scientific discipline.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Vincent is the first speaker. · No scientific discipline can study certain things. · The things it cannot study are those that cannot be measured. · Vincent gives a reason about happiness. · Happiness is an experience. · The experience is entirely subjective. · Happiness therefore cannot be measured. (2) Yolanda is the second speaker. · She draws a comparison. · Optometry relies on reports. · The reports come from patients about what they see. · Happiness research also relies on reports. · Those reports come from subjects about how they feel. (3) This is about optometry. · Optometry is a scientific discipline. · She treats this as obvious.", "category": "Controversy", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Vincent: No scientific discipline can study something that cannot be measured, and since happiness is an entirely subjective experience, it cannot be measured.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Vincent is the first speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "No scientific discipline can study certain things."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The things it cannot study are those that cannot be measured."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Vincent gives a reason about happiness."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Happiness is an experience."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The experience is entirely subjective."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Happiness therefore cannot be measured."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Yolanda: Just as optometry relies on patients'' reports of what they see, happiness research relies on subjects'' reports of how they feel.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Yolanda is the second speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "She draws a comparison."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Optometry relies on reports."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The reports come from patients about what they see."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Happiness research also relies on reports."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those reports come from subjects about how they feel."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Surely optometry is a scientific discipline.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about optometry."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Optometry is a scientific discipline."}, {"n": 3, "text": "She treats this as obvious."}]}], "the_read": "The speakers disagree over whether scientific disciplines can rely on things reported by patients.", "the_read_type": "C", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q10", "qnum": 10, "question_type": "Controversy", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 11, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q11", "prompt": "Although large cities are generally more polluted than the countryside, increasing urbanization may actually reduce the total amount of pollution generated nationwide. Residents of large cities usually rely more on mass transportation and live in smaller, more energy-efficient dwellings than do people in rural areas. Thus, a given number of people will produce less pollution if concentrated in a large city than if dispersed among many small towns.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This opens with a concession. · Large cities are more polluted than the countryside. · That is generally so. · The urbanization is increasing. · Increasing urbanization may reduce pollution. · What may be reduced is the total amount generated nationwide. (2) This is about residents of large cities. · They rely more on mass transportation. · They usually do so. · They live in smaller dwellings. · Their dwellings are more energy-efficient. · The comparison is with people in rural areas. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about a given number of people. · Those people will produce less pollution. · That holds if they are concentrated in a large city. · The comparison is with their being dispersed among small towns. · The towns would be many.", "category": "Argument Part", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Although large cities are generally more polluted than the countryside, increasing urbanization may actually reduce the total amount of pollution generated nationwide.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This opens with a concession."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Large cities are more polluted than the countryside."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That is generally so."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The urbanization is increasing."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Increasing urbanization may reduce pollution."}, {"n": 6, "text": "What may be reduced is the total amount generated nationwide."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "Residents of large cities usually rely more on mass transportation and live in smaller, more energy-efficient dwellings than do people in rural areas.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about residents of large cities."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They rely more on mass transportation."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They usually do so."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They live in smaller dwellings."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Their dwellings are more energy-efficient."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The comparison is with people in rural areas."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Thus, a given number of people will produce less pollution if concentrated in a large city than if dispersed among many small towns.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about a given number of people."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Those people will produce less pollution."}, {"n": 4, "text": "That holds if they are concentrated in a large city."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The comparison is with their being dispersed among small towns."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The towns would be many."}]}], "the_read": "What if other things unique to city life more than make up for the pollution saved by public transit and energy efficient dwellings?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q11", "qnum": 11, "question_type": "Argument Part", "correct_answer": "E"}]}, {"order": 12, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q12", "prompt": "Climatologist: Over the coming century, winter temperatures are likely to increase in the Rocky Mountains due to global warming. This will cause a greater proportion of precipitation to fall as rain instead of snow. Therefore, the mountain snowpack will probably melt more rapidly and earlier in the season, leading to greater spring flooding and less storable water to meet summer demands.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) The climatologist is the speaker. · This concerns the coming century. · Winter temperatures are likely to increase. · The increase would be in the Rocky Mountains. · The cause would be global warming. (2) This is what the increase will cause. · Precipitation will fall as rain. · A greater proportion of it will do so. · The alternative is falling as snow. (3) This is the conclusion. · It is about the mountain snowpack. · The snowpack will melt more rapidly. · It will also melt earlier in the season. · This is only probably so. · One result would be greater spring flooding. · Another result would be less storable water. · The water would be needed to meet summer demands.", "category": "Strengthen", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Climatologist: Over the coming century, winter temperatures are likely to increase in the Rocky Mountains due to global warming.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "The climatologist is the speaker."}, {"n": 2, "text": "This concerns the coming century."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Winter temperatures are likely to increase."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The increase would be in the Rocky Mountains."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The cause would be global warming."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "This will cause a greater proportion of precipitation to fall as rain instead of snow.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is what the increase will cause."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Precipitation will fall as rain."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A greater proportion of it will do so."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The alternative is falling as snow."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, the mountain snowpack will probably melt more rapidly and earlier in the season, leading to greater spring flooding and less storable water to meet summer demands.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is the conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the mountain snowpack."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The snowpack will melt more rapidly."}, {"n": 4, "text": "It will also melt earlier in the season."}, {"n": 5, "text": "This is only probably so."}, {"n": 6, "text": "One result would be greater spring flooding."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Another result would be less storable water."}, {"n": 8, "text": "The water would be needed to meet summer demands."}]}], "the_read": "What if the temperature on the mountain peaks won''t rise enough to get above freezing?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q12", "qnum": 12, "question_type": "Strengthen", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 13, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q13", "prompt": "Animal feed should not include genetically modified plants. A study found that laboratory rats fed genetically modified potatoes for 30 days tended to develop intestinal deformities and a weakened immune system, whereas rats fed a normal diet of foods that were not genetically modified did not develop these problems.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This is about animal feed. · Animal feed should not include certain plants. · The plants are genetically modified ones. (2) A study made a finding. · The finding is about rats. · The rats were laboratory rats. · The rats were fed genetically modified potatoes. · They were fed them for 30 days. · Those rats developed intestinal deformities. · They also developed a weakened immune system. · They tended to do so rather than always doing so. (3) This is a contrast with the other rats. · Some rats were fed a normal diet. · The foods in that diet were not genetically modified. · Those rats did not develop the problems.", "category": "Weaken", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Animal feed should not include genetically modified plants.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about animal feed."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Animal feed should not include certain plants."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The plants are genetically modified ones."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "A study found that laboratory rats fed genetically modified potatoes for 30 days tended to develop intestinal deformities and a weakened immune system,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "A study made a finding."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The finding is about rats."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The rats were laboratory rats."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The rats were fed genetically modified potatoes."}, {"n": 5, "text": "They were fed them for 30 days."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those rats developed intestinal deformities."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They also developed a weakened immune system."}, {"n": 8, "text": "They tended to do so rather than always doing so."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "whereas rats fed a normal diet of foods that were not genetically modified did not develop these problems.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with the other rats."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Some rats were fed a normal diet."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The foods in that diet were not genetically modified."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Those rats did not develop the problems."}]}], "the_read": "What if the rats fed GMOs didn''t exercise and the normal diet rats did, and that''s what caused the difference? What if it was the potatoes themselves, not the GMO nature, that harmed the rats?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q13", "qnum": 13, "question_type": "Weaken", "correct_answer": "A"}]}, {"order": 14, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q14", "prompt": "Some philosophers explain visual perception by suggesting that when we visually perceive an object, a mental image of that object forms in our mind. However, this hypothesis cannot be correct, since it would require an inner self visually perceiving the newly formed mental image; this would in turn require that the inner self have a mental image of that mental image, and so on. But such an infinite regress is absurd.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Some philosophers explain visual perception. · They explain it by a suggestion. · Their suggestion concerns when we perceive an object. · The perceiving is visual. · A mental image forms. · The image is of that object. · The image forms in our mind. (2) This is a contrast with the philosophers. · The hypothesis cannot be correct. · A reason is given for that. · The hypothesis would require an inner self. · That inner self would visually perceive the mental image. · The image is the newly formed one. (3) This is a further requirement. · The inner self would have to have a mental image. · That image would be of the first mental image. · The pattern continues without end. (4) This is a contrast. · The regress described is infinite. · Such a regress is absurd.", "category": "Parallel Reasoning", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some philosophers explain visual perception by suggesting that when we visually perceive an object, a mental image of that object forms in our mind.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Some philosophers explain visual perception."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They explain it by a suggestion."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Their suggestion concerns when we perceive an object."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The perceiving is visual."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A mental image forms."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The image is of that object."}, {"n": 7, "text": "The image forms in our mind."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "However, this hypothesis cannot be correct, since it would require an inner self visually perceiving the newly formed mental image;", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast with the philosophers."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The hypothesis cannot be correct."}, {"n": 3, "text": "A reason is given for that."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The hypothesis would require an inner self."}, {"n": 5, "text": "That inner self would visually perceive the mental image."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The image is the newly formed one."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "this would in turn require that the inner self have a mental image of that mental image, and so on.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a further requirement."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The inner self would have to have a mental image."}, {"n": 3, "text": "That image would be of the first mental image."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The pattern continues without end."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "But such an infinite regress is absurd.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The regress described is infinite."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Such a regress is absurd."}]}], "the_read": "What if the absurd is, in fact, true?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q14", "qnum": 14, "question_type": "Parallel Reasoning", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 15, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q15", "prompt": "Greatly exceeding the recommended daily intake of vitamins A and D is dangerous, for they can be toxic at high levels. For some vitamin-fortified foods, each serving, as defined by the manufacturer, has 100 percent of the recommended daily intake of these vitamins. But many people overestimate what counts as a standard serving of vitamin-fortified foods such as cereal, consuming two to three times what the manufacturers define as standard servings.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) Exceeding a recommended intake is dangerous. · The excess must be great. · The intake is the recommended daily one. · The vitamins are A and D. · A reason is given for this. · Those vitamins can be toxic. · They are toxic at high levels. (2) This is about vitamin-fortified foods. · It holds for only some of them. · Each serving of those foods has a certain amount. · The serving is defined by the manufacturer. · The amount is 100 percent of the recommended daily intake. · The intake is of these vitamins. (3) This is a contrast. · Many people overestimate something. · What they overestimate is what counts as a standard serving. · The serving is of vitamin-fortified foods. · Cereal is an example of such foods. (4) This is about how much those people consume. · They consume more than a standard serving. · The amount is two to three times as much. · The manufacturers define the standard servings.", "category": "Most Strongly Supported", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "Greatly exceeding the recommended daily intake of vitamins A and D is dangerous, for they can be toxic at high levels.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "Exceeding a recommended intake is dangerous."}, {"n": 2, "text": "The excess must be great."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The intake is the recommended daily one."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The vitamins are A and D."}, {"n": 5, "text": "A reason is given for this."}, {"n": 6, "text": "Those vitamins can be toxic."}, {"n": 7, "text": "They are toxic at high levels."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "For some vitamin-fortified foods, each serving, as defined by the manufacturer, has 100 percent of the recommended daily intake of these vitamins.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about vitamin-fortified foods."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It holds for only some of them."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Each serving of those foods has a certain amount."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The serving is defined by the manufacturer."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The amount is 100 percent of the recommended daily intake."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The intake is of these vitamins."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "But many people overestimate what counts as a standard serving of vitamin-fortified foods such as cereal,", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Many people overestimate something."}, {"n": 3, "text": "What they overestimate is what counts as a standard serving."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The serving is of vitamin-fortified foods."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Cereal is an example of such foods."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "consuming two to three times what the manufacturers define as standard servings.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is about how much those people consume."}, {"n": 2, "text": "They consume more than a standard serving."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The amount is two to three times as much."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The manufacturers define the standard servings."}]}], "the_read": "If consuming 200-300 times the daily recommended intake of vitamins is harmful, some people may put themselves in danger by eating two to three times the recommended serving of vitamin-fortified cereal.", "the_read_type": "I", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q15", "qnum": 15, "question_type": "Most Strongly Supported", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 16, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q16", "prompt": "At the end of 1997 several nations stated that their oil reserves had not changed since the end of 1996. But oil reserves gradually drop as old oil fields are drained and rise suddenly as new oil fields are discovered. Therefore, oil reserves are unlikely to remain unchanged from one year to the next. So most of the nations stating that their oil reserves were unchanged are probably incorrect.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This happened at the end of 1997. · Several nations made a statement. · They stated that their oil reserves had not changed. · The period of no change began at the end of 1996. (2) This is a contrast. · Oil reserves go down. · The dropping is gradual. · Reserves drop as old oil fields are drained. · Oil reserves also rise. · The rising is sudden. · Reserves rise as new oil fields are discovered. (3) This is a conclusion drawn from what came before. · Oil reserves are unlikely to remain unchanged. · The span considered is one year to the next. (4) This is a further conclusion. · It is about the nations that stated their reserves were unchanged. · Most of those nations are incorrect. · They are probably incorrect.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{"n": 1, "text": "At the end of 1997 several nations stated that their oil reserves had not changed since the end of 1996.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This happened at the end of 1997."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Several nations made a statement."}, {"n": 3, "text": "They stated that their oil reserves had not changed."}, {"n": 4, "text": "The period of no change began at the end of 1996."}]}, {"n": 2, "text": "But oil reserves gradually drop as old oil fields are drained and rise suddenly as new oil fields are discovered.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Oil reserves go down."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The dropping is gradual."}, {"n": 4, "text": "Reserves drop as old oil fields are drained."}, {"n": 5, "text": "Oil reserves also rise."}, {"n": 6, "text": "The rising is sudden."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Reserves rise as new oil fields are discovered."}]}, {"n": 3, "text": "Therefore, oil reserves are unlikely to remain unchanged from one year to the next.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a conclusion drawn from what came before."}, {"n": 2, "text": "Oil reserves are unlikely to remain unchanged."}, {"n": 3, "text": "The span considered is one year to the next."}]}, {"n": 4, "text": "So most of the nations stating that their oil reserves were unchanged are probably incorrect.", "details": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a further conclusion."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about the nations that stated their reserves were unchanged."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Most of those nations are incorrect."}, {"n": 4, "text": "They are probably incorrect."}]}], "the_read": "What if the nations discovered new oil fields with as much oil as had been consumed between 1996 and 1997?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q16", "qnum": 16, "question_type": "Necessary Assumption", "correct_answer": "B"}]}, {"order": 17, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q17", "prompt": "If a motor is sound-insulated, then it is quiet enough to use in home appliances. If a motor is quiet enough to use in home appliances, then it can be used in institutional settings. None of the motors manufactured by EM Industries are quiet enough to use in home appliances.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) This states a condition about a motor. · The condition is that the motor is sound-insulated. · Such a motor is quiet enough. · It is quiet enough to use in home appliances. (2) This states another condition. · The condition is that a 