ails": [{"n": 1, "text": "This is a contrast."}, {"n": 2, "text": "It is about Meyers."}, {"n": 3, "text": "Meyers had no good reason to think something."}, {"n": 4, "text": "What he had no reason to think is that the compost was anyone else''s property."}, {"n": 5, "text": "The compost was in the public garden."}, {"n": 6, "text": "A conclusion follows from this."}, {"n": 7, "text": "Taking the compost was not wrong for Meyers."}]}], "the_read": "What if taking compost is always wrong? What if we can''t use bad conditional reasoning?", "the_read_type": "L", "questions": [{"citation": "PT140.S1.Q21", "qnum": 21, "question_type": "Classic Flaw", "correct_answer": "C"}]}, {"order": 22, "slug": "PT140_S1_Q22", "prompt": "From time to time there is a public outcry against predatory pricing—where a company deliberately sells its products at prices low enough to drive its competitors out of business. But this practice clearly should be acceptable, because even after its competitors go out of business, the mere threat of renewed competition will prevent the company from raising its prices to unreasonable levels.", "credited_answers": [], "cumulative_after": [], "explanation": "The details your recall has to carry, by chunk: (1) There is a public outcry. · The outcry happens from time to time. · The outcry is against predatory pricing. · Predatory pricing is then defined. · A company sells its products at low prices. · The company does this deliberately. · The prices are low enough to drive competitors out of business. (2) This is a contrast. · The practice should be acceptable. · This is said to be clear. · A reason is being given. · The reason covers the time after competitors go out of business. (3) There is a threat of renewed competition. · The threat alone is enough. · The threat will prevent the company from doing something. · What it prevents is raising prices. · The prices would otherwise reach unreasonable levels.", "category": "Necessary Assumption", "chunks": [{